ck in with Al in beautiful Hawaii honoring servicemen and women in a fun and tasty way. But first, this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We are back. and headed out to Hawaii once again. We`re joined by a very special guest. Hi, Al.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Hey, guys. Good morning. We are here, as we`ve said, at Joint Base Pearl Harbor-Hickam. It`s the 12th Smucker, JM Smuckers mission breakfast and we are pleased to bring it back. We`ve had to keep it down because of COVID. And we`re here at the Joint Base.\par \par \uc2 And joining me is Command Master Chief Billy Smith. Sir, thank you.\par \par \uc2 BILLY SMITH, COMMAND MASTER CHIEF: Good morning, sir.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: We appreciate your service and the service of all the men and women here. This is the first live, big live event you guys have been able to do. What does it mean for the base to have something like this?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We`re getting back a sense of normalcy and I think everybody`s a little excited about it. We`re moving to the next stage and hopefully out and everything`s going to get better. I think this is a sense of pride. Everybody`s here to see this. And I think this is going to be a great thing as we move forward.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: One of your jobs is to check on morale of your servicemen and women. How have things been in the last several months as we come out of this?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It`s getting better. We were getting to a low point but we kept it up and we`re getting a lot better now. We`re coming out of COVID, I can`t see anything but positivity as we move forward.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And this breakfast, it`s a sponsored by \uc2 JM Smucker\uc2 . What does it mean to all the folks here?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We can`t appreciate that enough. Smucker is one of our great sponsors in DECA system and the commissary, and just a great sponsor. We sell a lot of their products. Everybody likes them been popular for years and years. Glad to have them.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: What is -- this Base, Pearl Harbor, you know, you`ve served in a lot of bases, but this place has a very special spot in I think in all of our hearts.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It sure does. Especially the Arizona Memorial and attack in Pearl Harbor. I think 1 million visitors a year says otherwise. This is just a great place for everybody. We take a lot of great pride every day\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And I talked about the fact that we were serving pancakes, but we also have your Waffles. This is Waffles, one of your morale officers.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: He sure is. He helps all the 93,000 people who live and work around here. He loves them. This is some of the workstations.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes. Probably you can see why everybody loves this girl.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Waffles, sit down.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: All right. Chief master, thank you so much. We appreciate.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you, Mr. Roker. Glad to have you here.\par \par \uc2 [08:55:04]\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And thank you all for your service. Thanks so much. We`ll be back with more, we`re going to talk to Team Smucker just a little bit later in our 3rd Hour. Guys?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: That`s Waffles?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: That`s Waffles.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, bring Waffles home for sure.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: This is Waffles. This is Waffles.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Say hello.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Sweet, sweet.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thank you, Al. Thank you, sergeant.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: You don`t need any syrup, you`re so sweet already.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Did you, Al?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thank you, Waffles.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Thanks, al.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: And coming up, in addition to as Al mentioned more from Mr. Roker there in Hawaii on the 3rd Hour. Oscar nominee Minnie Driver is going to join us. She`s going to open up about her very personal new project.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And later on Hoda and Jenna, celebrity makeup artists, Sir John, will show us how to pull off that Met Gala glow.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Exciting.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. And by the way, she`s going to be my co-host. She`s hilarious.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Wait, who?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yvonne Orji. She`s awesome. She`s a lot of fun. Y`all get a kick out of her.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: But first, your local news.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220503ei53000ul\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Oklahoma becomes the latest state to pass a bill banning abortion after six weeks\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Stephanie Gosk \par \uc2 414 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 April 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Another story developing tonight. Oklahoma becomes the latest state to pass a bill banning abortion after six weeks. It`s modeled after a controversial Texas law and comes ahead of a critical Supreme Court decision. Stephanie Gosk has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): As early as six weeks, that`s when a heartbeat can be detected. After that, the state of Oklahoma says abortions are banned except in medical emergencies or pregnancies resulting from rape or incest. The new law allows private citizens to sue doctors and anyone else who facilitates the procedures.\par \par \uc2 TODD RUSS, REPUBLICAN STATE REPRESENTATIVE, OKLAHOMA: We`re very excited and it means a lot to the state of Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Earlier this month, a bill was passed outlawing abortion altogether, with few exceptions.\par \par \uc2 The law that the governor signed last month is effectively saying to the Supreme Court overturn Roe v. Wade, right?\par \par \uc2 [19:10:01]\par \par \uc2 RUSS: It`s more complicated than just say overturn Roe v Wade. I feel -- certainly we all would like to see that happen. We do want to see the genocide of unborn babies ended in America.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: But that law won`t go into effect until August. And this new ban is immediate as soon as it`s signed. The Oklahoma Heartbeat Act is identical to a Texas bill passed last fall, forcing many women seeking abortion across state lines to Oklahoma, where there are only four clinics.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA GALLEGOS, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, TULSA WOMEN`S CLINIC: We had a tremendous increase in volume of patients.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Andrea Gallegos runs one of the clinics which will have to start turning women away.\par \par \uc2 GALLEGOS: This Oklahoma law not only impacts Oklahoma women, but it impacts women from surrounding states as well.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: So, in some cases, they may not just have to pass one state border, it may be two or even three.\par \par \uc2 GALLEGOS: Correct. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: In addition to Texas and Oklahoma, there are 10 states that have passed so-called heartbeat bans. But the laws are blocked or have failed to hold up in court. The landscape could change dramatically with an upcoming Supreme Court decision which will decide if Mississippi`s abortion ban after 15 weeks is constitutional, the most direct challenge to Roe v Wade in 30 years. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220503ei4t0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE NIGHTLY NEWS for April 29, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kelly Cobiella, Tom Costello, Kate Snow, Stephanie  Gosk, Vaughn Hillyard, Emilie Ikeda, Kristen Dahlgren \par \uc2 3,941 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 April 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the American killed fighting alongside Ukrainian forces.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voice-over): A 22-year-old Marine veteran now the first known U.S. citizen to die in the fight against Russia. What his family is saying.\par \par \uc2 In Mariupol dramatic video appearing to show Russian forces under fire near the steel plant where Ukrainians remain holed up. New images from the deadly strike on Kyiv amid word the Russian offensive is days behind schedule.\par \par \uc2 Also, tonight, that nosedive on Wall Street. The Dow plunging more than 900 points, the worst month for the NASDAQ and nearly 14 years. What`s causing it?\par \par \uc2 The Navy investigating the suicides of several sailors assigned to the USS George Washington. A former shipmate now speaking out. The pleas for help she says went ignored.\par \par \uc2 The new CDC report on a mystery hepatitis outbreak in children. Have health officials found a common link?\par \par \uc2 The star baseball pitcher hit with a longest-ever suspension out of the MLB is Domestic Violence Policy.\par \par \uc2 My exclusive inside the LAPD`s cutting-edge program training the next generation of officers in virtual reality.\par \par \uc2 And love is in the air. The surprise wedding at 37,000 feet.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening, everyone. The Pentagon tonight warning Americans against travel to Ukraine after the death of a 22-year-old U.S. Marine veteran who went there to join the fight. The mother of Willie Joseph Kancel saying he wanted to go over because he believed in what Ukraine was fighting for. T\par \par \uc2 hat fight this evening still focused in the east, but U.S. officials say the Russian advance there has been slow and uneven and taking longer than the Russians participated. The mayor of the besieged city of Mariupol, which is so critical to the Russian goal of dividing the country believes more than 20,000 and that city alone have died since the beginning of the war.\par \par \uc2 And the Russians once again looking over their shoulders turning some of their attention back to the capital. Missiles striking in Kyiv, the city where a major Russian armored assault was successfully repelled several weeks ago. Kelly Cobiella is in eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KELLY COBIELLA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): After more than two months of war and countless Ukrainians killed in battle, tonight, we`ve learned an American is now among the dead. 22-year-old ex-Marine Willie Joseph Kancel died Monday his family said, but where and how still unclear.\par \par \uc2 His family telling CNN Kancel was working for a private military contractor. He believed in what Ukraine was fighting for, his mother said. He was married with a 7-month-old son.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a senior US defense official says the Russians are making slow and uneven progress and are likely a few days behind schedule in their effort to take control of the East.\par \par \uc2 In Mariupol, city officials posting this video they say of Russian forces under fire near the steel plant where Ukrainian soldiers and civilians have been holed up. The families of those inside pleading for international help.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Is this price we need to pay for our freedom and independence? Many dead people, kids.\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA: The war taking a heartbreaking toll, this funeral for a 24-year- old Ukrainian soldier killed near Kharkiv.\par \par \uc2 Tell me about Vladimir. Who was he?\par \par \uc2 He was a wonderful person, his friend said. He helped everyone.\par \par \uc2 And in Donetsk, this grandmother lost her 14-year-old grandson when a Russian rocket hit their home. I wish I had died, and he had survived. I lived much more than him. I will never forgive myself for it, she says.\par \par \uc2 In Kyiv, they`re clearing the rubble after that deadly Russian missile strike yesterday. Russia says it was targeting a defense plant. NBC`s Erin McLaughlin is there.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): One of the missiles struck this factory, what Ukrainian officials believe was the intended target. The other struck this apartment building. The victim`s car parked right outside.\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA: Tonight, Kyiv`s mayor says the Russian strike was a middle finger to the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . coming just an hour after a visit by the Secretary-General.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Kelly joins us again from Eastern Ukraine. Kelly, we`re now getting a new prediction or assessment from \uc2 NATO\uc2  about how long this war could last.\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA (on camera): That`s right, Lester. NATO`s Deputy Secretary-General said that the next few days and weeks could be decisive. But also warned this war could last months, even years. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: It`s hard to imagine this is just getting started. All right, Kelly, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Here at home, a major sell-off on Wall Street today in the end of a brutal month for the markets. Tom Costello is following it. Tom, what`s going on?\par \par \uc2 [19:05:08]\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Yes, Lester, the sell-off has sold off has been led by tech stocks after some tech giants reported disappointing earnings. \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Intel\uc2  among them. The Dow Jones Industrials lost nearly 938 points today and almost five this month. The tech-heavy NASDAQ down four percent today, down 13 percent in April, its biggest loss since 2008.\par \par \uc2 The broader S&P down nearly nine percent this month, 13 percent this year. Hanging over the market, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  expected to raise interest rates by another half-point next week as it tries to tame skyrocketing inflation. But many analysts fear a recession could be near. The war in Ukraine is already showing that -- slowing rather, the global economy. And the supply chain crisis and China shows little signs of improving, Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A lot of moving parts. Tom, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the Navy says it`s making urgent changes to better support sailors on the USS George Washington after a string of recent suicides. Kate Snow spoke with a former sailor on the aircraft carrier who says toxic management and poor working conditions took a heavy toll.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KATE SNOW, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): The crew of the USS George Washington is in the midst of a crisis, five suicides in the past year, three in the span of a week this month. Mika`il Rayshawn Sharp, then Natasha Huffman, then Xavier Hunter Sandor.\par \par \uc2 NAUTICA ROBINSON, FORMER FORMER FIRE CONTROLMAN, USS GEORGE WASHINGTON: My higher-ups failed me and the same people that failed me on board that ship failed those sailors.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: 23-year-old Nautica Robinson joined the George Washington in 2019 to work on weapons systems, but the aircraft carrier was at port being overhauled, so instead she was painting and laying deck. Nautica began to struggle with her mental health and said she repeatedly asked for more support.\par \par \uc2 And what did people say?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: It was like, I said it and I spoke into like, thin air. Like, nobody listened.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: about a year ago, she tried to take her own life.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: I`m sad, I`m tired being here. I`m tired being on the ship. I`m tired dealing with this. Like, it shouldn`t be like this anymore.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: She was at her apartment and friends saved her.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: I was dying and nobody from my chain of command cared enough to even come to check on me.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: NBC News spoke with one other former sailor and two current sailors who described uninhabitable conditions on the ship and a culture where seeking help is not met with necessary resources. Though it`s impossible to know what led to individual suicides.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PRESS SECRETARY, PENTAGON: We don`t want to see any sailor harmed or hurt or lose their life, period.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: The commander of the Naval Air Force Atlantic says they`re working daily and aggressively to ensure support and resources are available to sailors in the shipyards, at sea, and at home. The Navy has added additional mental health services for the crew and is in the process of making temporary housing available to sailors who want it.\par \par \uc2 RUSSELL SMITH, MASTER CHIEF PETTY OFFICER, NAVY: I understand that we still have a problem.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Last week, the Navy`s senior enlisted person, Master Chief Russell Smith, spoke to the George Washington crew and acknowledged the overhaul work they`re doing is not pleasant, but said it could be worse.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: What you`re not doing is sleeping in a foxhole like a marine might be doing. What you are doing is going home at night, most night. The downside is some of the (BLEEP) that you have to go through logistically will drive you crazy.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: The reaction that I got was kind of like, are you serious?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: He seemed to be sort of saying, toughen up. This is the Navy.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Embrace the suck. It`s the Navy. There`s only but so much suck that you can kind of embrace and take.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Nautica left the Navy in February but still talks with sailors on the ship and says many are more scared to speak out now.\par \par \uc2 Why are you sharing your story?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Well, I want my story to help others and to, you know, get them to speak up and talk and to keep pushing for mental health. And maybe, just maybe, it`ll resonate with, you know, the higher-ups.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Kate Snow, NBC News Charlotte, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Another story developing tonight. Oklahoma becomes the latest state to pass a bill banning abortion after six weeks. It`s modeled after a controversial Texas law and comes ahead of a critical Supreme Court decision. Stephanie Gosk has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): As early as six weeks, that`s when a heartbeat can be detected. After that, the state of Oklahoma says abortions are banned except in medical emergencies or pregnancies resulting from rape or incest. The new law allows private citizens to sue doctors and anyone else who facilitates the procedures.\par \par \uc2 TODD RUSS, REPUBLICAN STATE REPRESENTATIVE, OKLAHOMA: We`re very excited and it means a lot to the state of Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Earlier this month, a bill was passed outlawing abortion altogether, with few exceptions.\par \par \uc2 The law that the governor signed last month is effectively saying to the Supreme Court overturn Roe v. Wade, right?\par \par \uc2 [19:10:01]\par \par \uc2 RUSS: It`s more complicated than just say overturn Roe v Wade. I feel -- certainly we all would like to see that happen. We do want to see the genocide of unborn babies ended in America.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: But that law won`t go into effect until August. And this new ban is immediate as soon as it`s signed. The Oklahoma Heartbeat Act is identical to a Texas bill passed last fall, forcing many women seeking abortion across state lines to Oklahoma, where there are only four clinics.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA GALLEGOS, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, TULSA WOMEN`S CLINIC: We had a tremendous increase in volume of patients.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Andrea Gallegos runs one of the clinics which will have to start turning women away.\par \par \uc2 GALLEGOS: This Oklahoma law not only impacts Oklahoma women, but it impacts women from surrounding states as well.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: So, in some cases, they may not just have to pass one state border, it may be two or even three.\par \par \uc2 GALLEGOS: Correct. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: In addition to Texas and Oklahoma, there are 10 states that have passed so-called heartbeat bans. But the laws are blocked or have failed to hold up in court. The landscape could change dramatically with an upcoming Supreme Court decision which will decide if Mississippi`s abortion ban after 15 weeks is constitutional, the most direct challenge to Roe v Wade in 30 years. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The Midterm Elections are here. And with the first primaries just days away, critical tests of Donald Trump`s power over his party. Vaughn Hillyard has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): The Trump shows second act facing major tests over the next month. This weekend, the former president in Nebraska campaigning for Charles Herbster, the candidate for governor accused of groping by eight women. Herbster denies those allegations.\par \par \uc2 This just days after holding a rally in central Ohio.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The greatest political movement in the history of our country.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Key primaries that will test his grip on the Republican party today.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They have my complete and total endorsement.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Trump also throwing his political weight behind a trio of high- profile candidates.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I endorsed another person today, Dr. Oz in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 David Perdue\uc2  is a great leader.\par \par \uc2 We have to put J.D. Vance in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . So, get your friends and get your family, get everyone you know.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R-OH), SENATE CANDIDATE: The best president of my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: But that`s a big shift from 2016 when Vance oppose Trump.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I`m a never-Trump guy.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: But since Trump endorsed him this month, a new poll showing a big boost for the controversial author of Hillbilly Elegy. But is it enough to sway enough Ohio voters by Tuesday?\par \par \uc2 Still thinking about it?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes. But I`m not sold on J.D. Vance. I`ll have to see.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  illegally spied on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Trump`s favorite candidates in these races, parroting his false 2020 election conspiracy. But Georgia is incumbent Republican governor targeted by Trump pushing back.\par \par \uc2 GOV. BRIAN KEMP (R-GA): Weak leaders blame everybody else for their own loss instead of themselves.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: And didn`t Pennsylvania, Trump choosing celebrity Dr. Mehmet Oz, despite prominent Republicans like Ted Cruz backing the different longtime conservative.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD (on camera): If Trump is not able to catapult his candidates to victory, it would suggest his influence has cooled. And other potential 2024 candidates like \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  and Ted Cruz are already on the campaign trail as well. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Vaughn, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, growing concern about those mysterious hepatitis cases in children. What parents should be looking for. And why a pitching star got a two-year suspension.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Major League Baseball has suspended \uc2 Los Angeles Dodgers\uc2  pitcher Trevor Bauer for two full seasons after a woman accused him of sexual assault. It`s the longest suspension ever out of the league`s domestic violence and sexual assault policy. The DA is Office has declined to press charges against Bauer saying there was insufficient evidence. Bauer denies any wrongdoing and vows to appeal.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn out of the unexplained outbreak of hepatitis causing severe liver damage in some children. The CDC out with a report today showing a potential link between cases. Emilie Ikeda explains.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:08]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Tonight, the mysterious outbreak of severe hepatitis in children could be to blame for a death in Wisconsin. At least nine states now with suspected or confirmed cases of liver inflammation among kids.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD PEBODY, TEAM LEADER, WHO HIGH THREAT PATHOGENS: These are children who previously have been fit and well. So, this is sort of an unusual phenomenon that we`re seeing.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: The typical causes have been ruled out including the Hepatitis B virus, which is what most children are vaccinated against. Stephanie Cuna is among those who want answers after her otherwise healthy 3-year-old rapidly went into liver failure last summer.\par \par \uc2 One possible theory for the global spread is adenovirus, something all nine of Alabama`s hepatitis patients tested positive for.\par \par \uc2 DR. MARKUS BUCHFELLNER, PEDIATRIC INFECTIOUS DISEASE PHYSICIAN, \uc2 UNIVERSITY OF ALABAMA AT BIRMINGHAM\uc2 : It was really unusual, and it was kind of scary too.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: The CDC detailing the Alabama infections in report today, all were age six and under, three patients developed acute liver failure and vomiting, diarrhea, and fever were among the most common initial symptoms.\par \par \uc2 How should parents know the difference between a bug and potentially hepatitis?\par \par \uc2 BUCHFELLNER: Specific signs for liver injury really is things like jaundice, things like severe abdominal pain, things like changes of the urine and of the stool.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (on camera): While health officials don`t believe COVID or the vaccine is behind the uptick, they are looking into the possible role lockdowns could have played with kids less exposed to common infections during the pandemic. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Emilie Ikeda, thank you. Up next, can virtual reality help police officers keep confrontations from turning deadly? I`ll go inside their training.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: back now with an NBC News exclusive. The cutting-edge new training tool being employed by police here in Los Angeles helping officers learn to deescalate confrontations before they can turn lethal. The \uc2 LAPD\uc2  showed us how it works. A warning some of the images may be disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: At first glance, this looks like a video game.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sir, step back, step back.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Police avatars responding to the W Hotel in the heart of iconic Hollywood Boulevard on the roof. A man in distress is threatening to jump.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If you can please sit down. Let`s talk. Put your hands-on top of your head.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: But these are actually real officers at the Los Angeles Police Academy.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW CULLEN, SERGEANT, LOS ANGELES POLICE DEPARTMENT TRAINING BUREAU: I can take a scenario that I might train one day and put that on Hollywood Boulevard. And then the next day, I can go ahead and say, you know what, we`re going to run a very similar scenario, but we`re going to put it in Skid Row.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Partnering with 3D animation firm V Arm (ph) to build a motion capture studio in the Academy`s gymnasium. Dozens of high-tech cameras and wearable sensors will transport officers and cadets to the next generation of police training.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Gun down.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MOORE, CHIEF, LOS ANGELES POLICE DEPARTMENT: The systems that we`re installing here actually will monitor the heartbeat, eye movements, the stress levels of the individual.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Chief of Police Michael Moore sees virtual reality as a game-changer, especially for deescalation training.\par \par \uc2 Does this deemphasize the gun as the primary tool of effective law enforcement?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: 1,000 percent.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: It comes at a critical time for policing with use of force tactics under intense scrutiny. The \uc2 LAPD\uc2  reported 37 police shootings last year, up from 27 in 2020. More than half involving individuals experiencing a mental health crisis.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: It allows us to as we look at critical instances and some of their outcomes to go back and replicate them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Victim down, victim down.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Incidents like this one at the \uc2 Burlington Coat Factory\uc2  in North Hollywood where police fatally shot an assault suspect, but also accidentally killed a teenage girl in a nearby dressing room.\par \par \uc2 It`s hard not to think of this training without thinking of what happened in the \uc2 Burlington Coat Factory\uc2  shooting. I know that`s still under investigation, but it was a complicated problem set. Is that the kind of thing that you might be able to enlighten officers in a new way?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, absolutely. We will look at -- every one of our situations where officers resorted to the use of deadly force as an opportunity for us to what can we learn from this?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What do we do now? Stop the bleeding.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: We see VR now as an opportunity to then take that investigation and then model it. We see it absolutely as a -- as a means of improving outcomes for instances that resulted in a tragic loss as the Burlington incident did.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We have two cops with the event.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A virtual classroom with no boundaries.\par \par \uc2 CULLEN: What we`re trying to point out to officers is you`re going to encounter people in crisis and that worst day doesn`t necessarily mean they`re going to try to hurt you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You`re doing great.\par \par \uc2 CULLEN: If you identify that, it might give you an opportunity to communicate with them better.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A new tool of modern policing.\par \par \uc2 Up next for us, walking down the aisle at 37,000 feet.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, it wasn`t how they planned it, but love was definitely the air for a couple from Oklahoma. Kristen Dahlgren with tonight`s "INSPIRING AMERICA."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN DAHLGREN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): It was meant to be in Vegas.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY SALDA, GROOM: Our plan was to land, head to the courthouse, grab the marriage license, and then head to the chapel. BadaBing BadaBoom.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Pam was already wearing the dress. But what was supposed to happen in Vegas almost stayed in Dallas. Their flight canceled. Then, as if by divine intervention --\par \par \uc2 PAM SALDA, BRIDE: The sweetest guy, Chris, walks up and he goes, well, I just want to let you know, I`m an ordained minister and I think I can help you out.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: The three found another flight.\par \par \uc2 P. SALDA: We had to even switch airports. So, we get in the Uber and we tell him to floor it, and he did.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Finally, onboard the Southwest plane --\par \par \uc2 J. SALDA: And the pilot standing there, and you know, he`s like, are you guys going to Vegas to get married? Pam goes, if it doesn`t happen, we`re going to do it on this plane. And he`s like, OK, yes, it sounds good. The next thing we know we`re in the cockpit doing wedding photos.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: The flight crew sprung into action, toilet paper streamers, the minister sash out of snack bags, flight attendant call buttons lighting the aisle. A professional photographer on board offered pictures and that ordained minister, Chris, just happens to work in broadcasting.\par \par \uc2 P. SALDA: He had with him lapel mics and cameras and lights and tripods. Not only did he marry us, he was the wedding planner and the producer of the show.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m going to cry.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: They now consider him a friend for life. As for their marriage --\par \par \uc2 P. SALDA: I can`t wait to be your copilot through life.\par \par \uc2 It was so fun and amazing and sweet. And as crazy as it was, it still felt like OK, this is what we`re supposed to do.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: A high flying start to the adventure of a lifetime. Kristen Dahlgren, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: You`re going to love when a plan comes together. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Friday. Thanks for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220430ei4t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 State Legislators In Oklahoma Have Just Approved A Bill That Would Prohibit Abortions After Six Weeks Of Pregnancy\b0\par\par\uc2 Craig Melvin, Blayne Alexander \par \uc2 637 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 April 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN: That will be a real good stuff. This morning, some new developments tied to an emotional fight that`s playing out across this country. State legislators in Oklahoma have just approved a bill that would prohibit abortions after six weeks of pregnancy. It`s modeled after a similar law in neighboring Texas. NBC`s Blayne Alexander joins us now with this story. Blayne, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Craig, good morning to you. You know what`s notable here is that there is no lag time between when this bill would be signed and when it goes into effect, meaning that it`s just a pen stroke away from becoming one of the most restrictive abortion laws in the nation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): With the showdown over abortion access being fought in states nationwide, this morning, Oklahoma is the latest battleground. State lawmakers there approved a bill dubbed the Oklahoma Heartbeat Act that would ban abortions after six weeks, the time doctors are able to detect a heartbeat but before most women even know they`re pregnant.\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. JULIE DANIELS (R-OK): This will not save just one life. This will save many, many lives.\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. JULIA KIRT (D-OK): This bill is incredibly intrusive. It reduces individual freedoms. And it`s just plain wrong.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): The bill now goes to the desk of Oklahoma`s Republican governor who earlier this month signed a different bill making it a crime to perform abortions in the state.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KEVIN STITT\uc2  (R-OK): We want Oklahoma to be the most pro-life state in the country.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): That bill goes into effect this summer. But this latest measure passed with an emergency provision, meaning it goes into effect as soon as it`s signed likely any day.\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. MARY BOREN (D-OK): It impacts every fertile woman`s health in the state of Oklahoma by passing this law.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): The president of Planned Parenthood calls the measure unconscionable and unconstitutional vowing to fight it in court. The impact stretches beyond Oklahoma`s borders into neighboring Texas, where a similar law has sent women seeking abortions to nearby states. According to one study, nearly half of them traveled to Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 The legislation is the latest in a flurry of strict abortion laws nationwide, including Florida where Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  recently signed a measure banning the procedure after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): We are here today to protect life.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): That law is modeled after a similar one in Mississippi, which is currently under review in the Supreme Court. It poses a direct challenge to the landmark Roe v. Wade ruling, setting up a potentially groundbreaking decision by the nation`s highest court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Now getting back to Oklahoma, right now, there`s no indication of exactly when Governor Stitt may sign that bill into law. But Craig, he has said that he plans to sign any anti-abortion legislation that hits his desk. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 [07:15:04]\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Blayne Alexander for us, Blayne, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: 7:14, we`re going to shift gears now. Tom, last night was like a dream maker series.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220430ei4t0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Struggle Some Cancers Patients Face And Why There Is Hope\b0\par\par\uc2 Sheinelle Jones, Kristen Dahlgren, Al Roker \par \uc2 11,418 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 April 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [09:10:00]\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES: It is Infertility Awareness Week. And this morning, some hope for the thousands of young women battling cancer and then facing infertility due to the very treatments used to save their lives. Our Kristen Dahlgren joins us with more. This such an important story.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN DAHLGREN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey. Good morning, Sheinelle.\par \par \uc2 Yes, and it is one that, of course, hits close to home for me. I feel so lucky that I had my one daughter before my diagnosis. Up to half of breast cancer survivors are less likely to conceive after treatment. So, think about it, you get this devastating diagnosis and then, on top of everything you need to make decisions about fertility preservation. But as you are about to see, it can have a happy ending.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voiceover): At 35 years old, things were falling into place for Betsy O`Brien Phillips. A new city, a promotion and big dreams.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (on camera): Did you always want to be a mom?\par \par \uc2 BETSY O`BRIEN PHILLIPS, CANCER PATIENT: Yes, I did. It was always something I hoped.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voiceover): But before she could start a family, she found a lump in her breast.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (on camera): You know, when you are 35 and hear the words, you have cancer, what goes through your mind?\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: I was definitely in shock. You are focused on the surviving aspect. And you don`t know what the rest of life looks like.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voiceover): Doctors told Betsy her immediate future would be chemo, surgery and radiation, followed by five to 10 years of medicine to suppress hormones in her body. Like roughly three quarters of cases, Betsy`s cancer was fueled by estrogen from her ovaries. But while the treatment was designed to keep her cancer at bay, it would also jeopardize her dreams of starting a family.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: At some point it came up, someone said, you know, are you going to freeze your eggs? I hadn`t thought about it.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voiceover): And with chemo about to start, she has only hours to decide. She met with Dr. \uc2 Mitchell Rosen\uc2  at \uc2 UCSF\uc2 `s Fertility Preservation Program.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:00]\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 MITCHELL ROSEN\uc2 , UCSF FERTILITY PRESERVATION PROGRAM DIRECTOR: I think having options available to you is really important. Because potentially it could be permanent. You know, if you don`t have that option and then it`s 10 years down the road, you can`t turn back the clock.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voiceover): Betsy is one of 13,000 women of reproductive age who are diagnosed with breast cancer every year. In a recent study of survivors trying to conceive, only 76 percent had ovarian function return after chemotherapy. Just half went on to give birth without fertility treatments. For the others, using eggs or embryos that were frozen before cancer had a high chance of success.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROSEN: About 70 percent of those that were not able to conceive naturally would be able to conceive with the tissues they have stored.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voiceover): Betsy decided to freeze her eggs, knowing the cancer treatments could damage her ovaries and likely prevent her from even starting to try for children until her mid-40s.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: Having the egg was a huge peace of mind in terms of getting on with my life and it gave me the gift of time to find the right person that I was going to, you know, end up with to use them. So --\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (on camera): Which you did.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: Which I did, yes.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voiceover): When she least expected it, Betsy met John. The two married in 2019 and started trying for a family. Finally, almost eight years after freezing her egg, Betsy returned to the same hospital to use them for IVF. She`s now eight months pregnant with a baby girl.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: I wouldn`t have obviously expected when I was diagnosed with cancer that it would end up this way.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (on camera): Do you think you are going to look at every little moment with that baby a little bit differently given what you have had to confront?\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: She`s just going to be such a gift. Being able to experience those moments that we weren`t sure were going to happen will make it that much sweeter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: So, the emphasis here is that there are options. But I think it is important to point out this isn`t just for breast cancer patients. You know, it can be men. It can be women. I even know of teens who are about to start some type of cancer treatment who are having to confront these fertility preservation decisions. So, it really can be a lot on top of everything else, Sheinelle.\par \par \uc2 JONES: And, Kristen, there`s another part we have to acknowledge, freezing eggs can incredibly expensive. What sort of insurance coverage is there for these types of procedures?\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Right. It is a great question. And Dr. Rosen says the cost is actually the number one barrier for many of his patients. Only 11 states require that insurers cover fertility treatments for cancer patients. So, it can be a huge, huge obstacle. But as you saw with Betsy, if you are able to do it, it can give you the gift of time.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: And, Kristen, how is your little girl doing?\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: She is amazing. And honestly, Craig, I think I can only handle the one. So, you know, the world works in mysterious ways. and I was given exactly what I could do.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s sweet. Well, Kristen, it was great to have you on this morning. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Good to see you, Kristen.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Good to see you, Kristen.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Coming up next, a business that began with some family recipes and has grown into something truly special. Meet the family serving up a taste of the Caribbean deep in the heart of Texas.\par \par \uc2 Then, later, co-stars Andrew Garfield and Daisy Edgar-Jones live here in Studio 1A talking true crime, dance parties and a whole lot more as the "3rd Hour of TODAY" continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JONES: Guess what?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: What?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: What?\par \par \uc2 JONES: The newest episode of "Family Style with Al Roker" drops today. And this one, hits close to home.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Well, you know, I love Caribbean food and I found a true taste of the islands in a spot you might not expect. I traveled deep into the heart of Texas to meet the amazing couple behind Tony`s Jamaican Food Truck.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TONY SCOTT, TONY`S JAMAICAN OWNER: Jerk chicken and --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you very much, sir.\par \par \uc2 T. SCOTT: Enjoy.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Austin is home to over 1,200 food trucks. But we`re here for one specific truck, We`re here for Tony`s Jamaican. Serving up fine Caribbean fair to Austin for more than 10 years.\par \par \uc2 ROKER (voiceover): Tony Scott moved to Austin in 2003. Today, his popular food truck invites customers to enjoy the flavors of Jamaica where his mom taught him how to cook.\par \par \uc2 ROKER (on camera): When did you start cooking? How young were you?\par \par \uc2 T. SCOTT: 10.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Really? What was it about cooking that you liked?\par \par \uc2 T. SCOTT: I don`t know. I liked food. That was that.\par \par \uc2 ROKER (voiceover): Tony worked a small food business in Jamaica serving jerk chicken and drinks to beach goers. But when tourism to the island stalled after 9/11, he moved to the States to find work. He landed a job painting houses where coworkers were blown away by his homemade lunches.\par \par \uc2 ROKER (voiceover): You bring in Jamaican food that you made and some of your friends taste and say, where did this come from?\par \par \uc2 T. SCOTT: Yes. You know. And they were like, oh, you should, you know, open a restaurant.\par \par \uc2 ROKER (voiceover): Tony pursued his dream. Opening his food truck in March of 2012. It was one of the first places in Austin to offer traditional Caribbean food.\par \par \uc2 ROKER (on camera): When you first opened up, was it successful right away?\par \par \uc2 T. SCOTT: I came out here 10:00 in the morning and I was all here until 3:00 the next morning. I make $37.\par \par \uc2 ROKER (voiceover): It took some time. But word of mouth plus a small write up helped give his business a boost. Tony`s wife, Kim, runs the operation with her husband. But she first fell in love with his food.\par \par \uc2 ROKER (on camera): What was the first meal he cooked?\par \par \uc2 KIM SCOTT, TONY SCOTT`S WIFE: Curry chicken and rice. And once I had it, I didn`t want to ask for me. You know how ladies are. We try to eat a little bit. Don`t want them to know that we that greedy but it was so good I asked for seconds.\par \par \uc2 ROKER (voiceover): Tony`s truck has been so successful, in 2018, he expanded his footprint.\par \par \uc2 ROKER (voiceover): You opened up a brick-and-mortar restaurant. Were you nervous about that?\par \par \uc2 T. SCOTT: A little bit. It was --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Let me rephrase. Kim, were you nervous about that?\par \par \uc2 K. SCOTT: Oh, yes. I`m so glad you asked me that question. Yes, I was. It was something totally different. And it was challenging but also it was fun.\par \par \uc2 ROKER (voiceover): Tony serves a lot of Jamaican classics, bus I was who is excited to try one specialty.\par \par \uc2 ROKER (on camera): My mother is Jamaican. And in our house, ox tail was king.\par \par \uc2 [09:25:00]\par \par \uc2 ROKER (voiceover): Tony frequently sells out of ox tails. So, it was time to get in the truck and find out why.\par \par \uc2 T. SCOTT: This is ox tail, the famous ox tail everybody go crazy over. And these are like Jamaican products seasoning that we use.\par \par \uc2 ROKER (voiceover): The ox tails are seasoned with a savory spice mix. Plus, a few chef secrets.\par \par \uc2 T. SCOTT: This is my product that I make. It have like onion, bell Scott`s (ph) blended pepper.\par \par \uc2 ROKER (voiceover): They`re marinated overnight, then slow cooked to fall off the bone perfection.\par \par \uc2 T. SCOTT: This is when it comes out to be.\par \par \uc2 ROKER (on camera): Oh, now we`re talking.\par \par \uc2 T. SCOTT: Preview to taste.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That`s why I came to Austin.\par \par \uc2 ROKER (voiceover): Tony`s passion for his Jamaican roots is in every bite.\par \par \uc2 ROKER (on camera): Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 T. SCOTT: This is black bean.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: When you opened that top, I go, wait a minute.\par \par \uc2 T. SCOTT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: This is my mother`s beans and rice. This is great.\par \par \uc2 T. SCOTT: My mama always tell me, don`t make nobody tell you you can`t do nothing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: There you go. And that is the amazing thing about Austin, is that there are all these incredible cuisines from all around the world.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And they are just growing by leaps and bounds. In fact, you can check out more flavors of Austin`s booming food scene in the full episode later this morning, "Family Style with Al Roker" airing today at 11:30 and 5:30 on our streaming channel "TODAY All Day."\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s an interesting face that you made in the picture there. Did you see that picture?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: You could have brought back some ox tail.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I`m saying.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: I could have, but --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Well, I did. But I ate them.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Of course. Of course.\par \par \uc2 Up next. True crime fans, listen up, Andrew -- oh, they`re here.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Hey.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Andrew Garfield, Daisy Edgar-Jones live here in Studio 1A to telling us all about their new series that`s based on a real-life mystery. We`re going to hear all about it when the "3rd Hour of TODAY" comes right back.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I think I`m fan growing. I don`t do that very often.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:30:00]\par \par \uc2 ROKER: This morning, we are joined by two incredibly talented actors who are teaming up in a new series. We, of course, all know Andrew Garfield from playing the webslinger "Spider-Man," also nominated for an Academy Award this year for his role in "tick, tick... BOOM!"\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes. And Daisy Edgar-Jones first caught our eye -- and I love it so much -- the romantic drama series, "Normal People." Now, they both star in a grizzly new true crime show, it`s called "Under the Banner of Heaven."\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Andrew plays a fictional detective named Jeb Pyre, a Mormon who has his faith challenged after the real-life murder of Daisy`s character Brenda Lafferty.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW GARFIELD, ACTOR: If you turn your back on heavenly father, I`m confident the forensics will have proved your guilt by tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I knelt with her in the temple. I brought her to church with my family. You look at these as signs of innocence, but they aren`t.\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: Did you broke your covenant so she threatened to leave you? Is that what happened? I can imagine the fights. Is that why you killed her?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Very rarely do you get three -- like that might be a first in "3rd Hour" history that you got three hmms.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Daisy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I mean, it`s true crime is a huge genre right now. Have you always been fans of the drama? Of the genre?\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: I love true crime.\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES, ACTRESS: I do too.\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes?\par \par \uc2 EDGAR-JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: It is a really compelling thing. It`s become weirdly like a kind of comfort genre for people I think during the pandemic, weirdly. I don`t know what that is. Maybe because -- well, I have no idea. But I find it kind of fascinating. But this story particular is quite a vital one because it is a true crime story and the character that Daisy plays, it`s, you know, a horrible thing that she goes through with her daughter. And the intention with this was really to tell a story that is really compel but also to honor, you know, Brenda Lafferty and her daughter, Erica.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s interesting because, you know, crime aside, the show takes place across two separate timelines, right, if you will. So, you have no scenes together. But you have such chemistry. How did you guys build that connection?\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: How do we find a thing to do together? That is my question.\par \par \uc2 EDGAR-JONES: I know, right. Me too. Yes. It was interesting because, obviously, we were filming in Calgary for about five months but we never had any scenes together. And it`s always fun when you are in something and there`s so of it that you`re not involved in that you can watch it with fresh eyes as if you were. So, I`m like so excited to see the scenes that I wasn`t in.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: We heard there were dance parties involved?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Really?\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: Daisy gets it going, yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Daisy gets down?\par \par \uc2 EDGAR-JONES: I`m dressed for disco right now.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s probably helpful though because the content can be, I`m sure, heavy.\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: So, they have a dance party. I`m sure, it was probably helpful.\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: Definitely.\par \par \uc2 EDGAR-JONES: Yes, you do need to find balance.\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: Yes. It was a strange thing. And we -- I`ve never had that one on a show before. Usually, I`m very kind of immersed in the thing and kind of I like taking my work home in a way because it enhances my life. But with this, I think all of us, we were in the trenches together and we had to have a lot of gallows, humor, we had to dance a lot, we have to play boardgames, we have to go on hikes, we had to remind ourselves.\par \par \uc2 JONES: To clear it.\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: Yes. In order to be able to dive back into the material the next day, I think.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I mean, your character, again, it`s a fictional one but it`s based on this true story, a book, 2003 by Jon Krakauer. As you know, at the time, there`s some controversy surrounding how the church who was portrayed in this particular book. Did that affect how you prepared for the role at all or no?\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: I think we wanted to be really sensitive. And luckily, the person who adapted the book from the great Krakauer book was Dustin Lance Black who is an ex-Mormon himself and he`s -- he was our showrunner and our director and our writer. And he has real affinity with the church still even though he left the church a bunch of years ago.\par \par \uc2 So, he has a real kind of kindness and a warmth and a connection to the Mormon church while also doing the really hard and important work of shedding light into the nooks and crannies of an organization that maybe hasn`t seen light in a while and to tell the truth about this organization. And I think the truth can only be a good thing to bring to light and the truth will always come out. And I think Dustin Lance Black has done kind of an amazing job here.\par \par \uc2 [09:35:00]\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes. Speaking of truths, Andrew, you were here recently, just before the "Spider-Man" movie, the latest "Spider-Man" movie came out. And you -- it was so wonderful seeing you and Toby.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Really good.\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: I don`t know what you are talking about. I`m not in that movie. I`m not.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Well, you know, you talked with Hoda and Savannah, and here is what you had to say.\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: Oh, dear.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: We do have to ask you about "Spider- Man."\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: Oh, yes. You know we have to.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Everybody is saying you may be in it, there`s --\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: Listen, I`m not in the film.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: And it is kind of shocking to witness how good I am at that.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: You just --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: You are such a good actor.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: You just talked about truth.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That`s what I`m saying. That`s why I brought that up.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Andrew Garfield is a liar?\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: The truth always comes out. And here we are, the truth --\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s so funny.\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: It`s always -- yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: So, I ask this next question knowing after just watching this video. Would there be any scenario where Andrew Garfield is in another spider suit or in some part of the Marvel Universe?\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: See my arms just curl.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes, you do that. That means yes. OK. So, move on. That means yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: I`m watching your nose.\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: I`m a really bad poker player, believe it or not.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That is so funny.\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: But like no matter what I say at this point, I am a known liar. So, like, it`s over for me.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: Like there`s nothing I can say that can, you know, going to satisfy you, Al. It`s done.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: You always satisfy me.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s funny. I know. Wait. Before you guys leave us this morning. Daisy, I don`t know if you saw our show earlier this morning, we talked about the fact that "The Crown" is looking for an actress to play Duchess Kate Middleton. And so, I don`t know if you`ve seen it. But all over \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , people are weighing in.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: There it is.\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 JONES: And so many people think that you should be Kate Middleton in "The Crown."\par \par \uc2 EDGAR-JONES: Oh, my gosh. That is the first I`ve heard of that.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Really?\par \par \uc2 EDGAR-JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh, my gosh. Oh, yes. People love you.\par \par \uc2 EDGAR-JONES: That`s cool.\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: What do you think?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Would you do it?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Well, I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 EDGAR-JONES: I mean, I wouldn`t say no. I`ll do it.\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: Oh, dear. That`s commitment. You just commit to "The Crown."\par \par \uc2 JONES: She did on national television.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Would it help if Andrew wore kind of a baldish wig as Prince William?\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: Hey. Why do you have to bring up his balding, just like it`s an affinity thing? You just sort of like --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Really quickly. Where the (INAUDIBLE)? What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 EDGAR-JONES: Well, yes. I mean, so it is coming out in July.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EDGAR-JONES: And, yes. I really hope the fans of the book will be happy with it and our adaptation. I think we`ve done a really good job honoring it. So, yes. I`m really excited forward people to see it.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: OK. Are you going to be in the next "Spider-Man" movie?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: I sense a thing.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That was good. That was good. You guys are a delight.\par \par \uc2 GARFIELD: You guys too. Thank you for having us.\par \par \uc2 EDGAR-JONES: Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Andrew, Daisy, thanks to both of you. FX`s "Under the Banner of Heaven" premiers tomorrow, April 28th, exclusively on \uc2 Hulu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Coming up next, the first look inside "People" magazine`s beautiful issue featuring some trailblazing women, Hollywood friendships and so much more.\par \par \uc2 And then, later, Chef Elena Besser is taking us for a spin in the kitchen when she shows us the right way to prep and store our fruits and veggies. To go buy some first. I`m kidding. "3rd Hour of TODAY" will be right back.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: You`re not kidding.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:40:00]\par \par \uc2 JONES: This morning, we are highlighting some remarkable women. "People" magazine`s "Beautiful" issue comes out today, celebrating success and empowerment in women of all ages. Earlier on "TODAY," we revealed Helen Mirren is on the cover this year. And now, we have more exclusive details about what`s inside. So, here to talk about it is editor-in-chief Liz Vaccariello.\par \par \uc2 Liz, welcome back.\par \par \uc2 LIZ VACCARIELLO, PEOPLE`S EDITOR-IN-CHIEF: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Hi, Liz.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s been quite the 90 days for you.\par \par \uc2 VACCARIELLO: I know.\par \par \uc2 JONES: My goodness.\par \par \uc2 VACCARIELLO: It`s beautiful.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I was just about to say, let`s start with the cover. It just warms my heart. Helen Mirren, she was here not too long ago in person in studio.\par \par \uc2 VACCARIELLO: I know.\par \par \uc2 JONES: She is stunning.\par \par \uc2 VACCARIELLO: She is stunning. And, you know what, for -- what "People" magazine wanted to do this year with "Beautiful" is talk about the 