s Wednesday, April 27th. Listen to this song.\par \par \uc2 HAGER (on camera): Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: This is Face on my Money by Walker Hayes. You know, listen, it`s rap and country in one. Wait, hang on.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: You love this song.\par \par \uc2 Hoda played this song for me in New Orleans. I remember it, right?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Put your Face on my Money, honey. If Walker Hayes -- yes, this is one of them. He`s got a bunch.\par \par \uc2 By the way, Walker Hayes is going to be here. He`s got a new book out. He is so cool. He was -- they were -- he has like six kids, they were down on their luck, had like a car with not enough seatbelts for all the kids and a friend came to his rescue. And it`s a beautiful story of friendship and love and all that stuff.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: We love him. We love his music.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You know, what we love more than all of it? Today`s Viola day. Viola Davis is going to be on our show. She is a national treasure.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: She is.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: She has written a book that will transform you and change you.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: It`s like the classic. I mean, that`s what so as a reader, as somebody that loves to read, she`s an incredible writer. I mean, I know so many people are going to read this and feel --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, if you think you know Viola Davis, you don`t know Viola. If you know her as the Oscar winner, as the Tony winner as this incre -- and she is -- she has all of those things. But the way she got there will be unlike any, any journey I think you`ve seen.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: You know what I love about this book? Is that the front and the back, Viola now and it`s the back as a little girl and it`s all about her. The whole book is about sort of finding that little girl.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Do you ever like when you think back to you?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You know what`s so funny? I was just thinking that the other day and I was always afraid to talk. I was the one -- they used to tell me in school, you should play ground voice, like, use your outside voice.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Wait, why?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Because I was just afraid of -- I don`t know. I think I was -- I don`t know if I was shy or just afraid to say something wrong. Or I didn`t want it?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: What was it -- did you feel --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I just felt like scared. I felt scared. I felt like, you know, like, don`t pick me, don`t look at me, don`t call on me. Like, you want to fade away.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Because if you feel different.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I think like a lot of kids who are from other places like my parents are from Egypt, you feel different. And you don`t even -- you can`t put your finger on it but you know you are, you know?\par \par \uc2 You know you are -- sometimes interactions with boys, you know you are in other things in life. But when you`re that little kid, you just want to be the same.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: The same. But isn`t that such a weird -- like, as an adult, the difference is are what make people great.\par \par \uc2 Like, when you find somebody that`s super unique. You`re like, oh, my gosh, you`re hilarious. You`re this, you`re that. You`re not the same as everybody else.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You`re not the same, right.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And how do we like, don`t you wish somebody had said to you, but what -- the coolness of you?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And I don`t -- you know what, I got to tell you though, at that age, I don`t know what you actually believe.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Could have said.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Because I think as a kid, you just have your 7-year-old brain. And the all you can compute is they`re having fun. And they`re all the same. And I`m over here.\par \par \uc2 Like, what does this feel like to be the one who`s over here? And I think a lot of probably immigrant kids have felt this too, you never quite in the beginning