autiful.\par \par \uc2 By the way, the People Magazine has a cool section. It`s called My Ride or Die. So, they pick two best friend.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Did they ask us to be in it? No.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You mean, you and Savannah? OK.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Maybe next -- wait, excuse me?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Why? I was going to ask if it was you and Savannah? I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: You`re also My Ride or Die. Like, if I had a little motorcycle, I`d want you both in the sidecar.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, I want to drive.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Your driving. I`ll be in the sidecar with popcorn. I`d love to be in the sidecar. I`ll play D.J.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Here`s a couple of besties, ready? Did you know Allison Janney and Octavia Spencer?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I mean, it makes so much sense because they`re both hilarious women.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: (INAUDIBLE). They say their friendship didn`t start off so swimmingly. It started off kind of rocky, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALLISON JANNEY, ACTRESS: She didn`t like me when she first met me.\par \par \uc2 OCTAVIA SPENCER, ACTRESS: Well, I didn`t realize we kind of were interested in the same guy.\par \par \uc2 JANNEY: And I didn`t know she was interested.\par \par \uc2 SPENCER: I didn`t know she was interested. And then, when I found out she was interested.\par \par \uc2 JANNEY: Like, I don`t like this girl.\par \par \uc2 SPENCER: I mean, she`s tall, statuesque and gorgeous.\par \par \uc2 JANNEY: And then, we just decided we liked each other better than the guy.\par \par \uc2 SPENCER: Better than the guy. And our friendship has lasted so much longer. Right?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Don`t you love that?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: 30 plus years.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: They made a good decision. Always get rid of the guy.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: This is -- this is going to be a theme.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I mean, I have one guy that I want to keep in. But I think like, I mean, I have a guy -- middle school was really the only time that ever happened to me. Because just for whatever reason, and I`m like, why did I choose the guy over her?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. And did you go back to being friends with her?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes, I dumped him pretty soon after he gave me the frog ring, you know?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You got a frog ring?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: A frog ring.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, my God. Do you remember when someone held your hand, what that felt like? Was it like the cool -- when I was -- when I was growing up, they would come up to you and say, will you go with me? And that meant will you go steady. Did he ask you if you would go with him? It`s like, that`s what --\par \par \uc2 HAGER: See, nobody -- so, one person asked me to go with them. And then they broke up with me bathe -- when they saw me in a bathing suit. I know that was a real -- that was a real -- and that was sort of like what the whole school knew too. Because I have a birthmark and you know, don`t look. I know, what a jerk. What a jerk.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And also, when the whole school knows something. OK. By the way, I don`t know if you guys knew this, but Octavia and Allison Janney are 11 years apart.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: No, I didn`t know that.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: But OK.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Who cares? I noticed that they asked us this morning, they said have you ever had a friendship with somebody that is a little older? I mean, hello.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I mean, we`re sitting here.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Hello. We were sitting there on the Zoom when they asked us that.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: In the same room, we were in the same room.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Next to each other. I was sitting next a garbage can where I always sit.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I mean, I think when it comes to age and doesn`t matter. I do think the most interesting people have friends of all ages.\par \par \uc2 Because number one, like the things we talk about have nothing to do with our ages.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Or even our cultural references. First of all, they`re the same. Second of all, we are in the same stage of life. Which, you know, matters in some ways, because I`m constantly asking you for children advice.\par \par \uc2 But also, I realized, like, when I`m out sort of a way on the road, like how much I talk about you, like I do, it`s kind of weird. It`s in like a -- but it`s because I like being with you, you know,?\par \par \uc2 Like, we sit and we`re talking and we`re crying and we`re laughing. And then, they`re like, it`s time to go to television.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And then, it continues in the television part.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes, it does.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, you guys, don`t forget today`s Viola day. We`re going to talk to Viola in a little bit.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, do you have trouble getting off the phone with a talker?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: We`ve got some advice where we tackle your social dilemmas.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:13:36]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Welcome back. All right, it is time for some Hoda and Jenna`s Social Dilemmas. All right.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: It`s a long graphic.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I like it. All right.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: OK, so our first one comes from our Today.com parenting page. And this is the question: Are gift registries for kids` birthday parties OK?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, apparently this is a new trend. What do we think?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I say no.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You say no to gift registries.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I say I think that teaches kids that they can get whatever they want, which is not, you know, what I want to teach my kids.\par \par \uc2 But also, haven`t you ever had a birthday party where you forget to say no gifts and there`s all this stuff, all this cake, all these things?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Do you usually do a no gift birthday?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Like, usually, I like to do a no gift, or just have a small party like Mile is having four friends.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, that`s different. You can afford gifts.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: But don`t you think they open the gift, they throw it on the ground.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The more gifts, the more that they discard. And I think the point about -- I was thinking about it from the parents who`s going to the party, you know, from (INAUDIBLE) it`s like, oh, good. Oh, gosh, do I have time to run -- do we have time to run to a store and throw wrapping paper on something and say, and this way. It`s like oh, boom, they got what they wanted. We don`t have to worry about it.\par \par \uc2 But as the receiver, yes, I think it does teach you that, you know, all you have to do is hit a button and you`ll get exactly what.\par \par \uc2 And then, the question after is why didn`t anyone get me the bla bla bla that I wanted as opposed to?\par \par \uc2 All right, so if we say no gifts.\par \par \uc2 [10:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HAGER: OK, well, according to Today.com poll 46 percent say tacky and 42 percent say useful and 12 are still undecided.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I don`t think it`s tacky. I think you may make the better point. It`s like your kids should not have everything on their wish list instantly gets delivered because \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has it.\par \par \uc2 All right, here`s the next one. My family members are known for talking on the phone for hours. I am the exact opposite. How do I get off the phone with my grandma?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Don`t get off with grandma.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK, you should pick another family member.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: You never know when grandma`s not going to be there. If it`s your sister.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, you can.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes, just say you got to go.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I got to go.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: But grandma, talk to her.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I know. Yes, I think that you`ve just got to just say and then what? And even put it on speaker and 