tic childhood including abuse and bullying.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): It is an incredible story. It`s a beautiful story. It`s haunting. It`s a story of resilience. Viola, welcome.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I mean, let`s just start with the basics. You put this book out, it is your heart and soul. It`s on a platter and you`re handing it out to the world saying here I am, this is me. How are you feeling in this moment?\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: I feel pretty good. I feel pretty liberated. You know, I feel that I was going through this existential crisis of meaning, you know? What does it all mean after the pandemic, sort of reaching success and that sort of glass ceiling that people say you have a life now, you`re famous, you did it, you make money and then, nothing.\par \par \uc2 The disillusionment of success comes and then I -- you know, I say it in the book tour, I realize that the final step is significance.\par \par \uc2 It`s sort of that ownership of every part of you and not just sort of putting on that mask of success, and moving through life as this other person.\par \par \uc2 I just wanted people to be introduced to Viola from Central Falls, and not by all of the celebrity that you hug and target parking lot, (INAUDIBLE). You know, and I appreciate it. I hug everybody. But this is me.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I mean, and that`s what we were talking about this cover, but it`s like you also had to go back and find little Viola and be with her, you know? You write about running. But you ran not only from literally from bullies who throw things at you and say horrible things. But did you run from her too? Did you run from that childhood?\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Well, I did run from her because I believed all those things that they said about me. You know, who tells you who you are? Your parents tell you who you are. Your culture tells you who you are. Your environment tells you who you are.\par \par \uc2 My environment said that I was Black, that I was ugly, that I was that inward. And so, I heard it and I believed it and I had drive, I went to college I went to Juilliard and realize, like I said in the book, my feet just stopped moving.\par \par \uc2 [10:25:12]\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: But I felt like my heart was still running from that past, being liberated from that little girl who I thought was uh-uh, I don`t want to be her. I don`t want to be the bed wetter, even though she`s a part of me.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And not only were you running, as Jenna was pointing out from those - - from those boys, when you -- there was not a safe place that was home.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes when you get home, you`re like, oh, at least I`m home. But your home as you described in this book so eloquently, was a place where there wasn`t food for you, you had to literally find it.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Yes, it was beyond food and security. It was just no food, you know? And when -- people have to realize when there`s no food, that`s all you`re thinking about. So, you`ll do anything for food, you go into garbage cans for food, you`ll steal for food, you`ll make friends for food, you`ll go up to strangers on the street and go, do you have any money, so I could get food.\par \par \uc2 And it`s constant negotiating about with deprivation, and then the violence in the home didn`t help. The poverty didn`t help. The bedwetting didn`t help. The constant deprivation didn`t help.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, it`s like Einstein says, imagination is more powerful than knowledge. And the reason why he says that is you can go to an ultimate reality within yourself, if you are a creative spirit.\par \par \uc2 When you need relief from your life, sort of like the Pensieve in Harry Potter, when you take out the bad memories, and you put it in the pot, when you need relief from it at times.\par \par \uc2 Imagination does the same thing. It sort of elevates you from your realistic realm world, and then you can redefine yourself and the world that you can create. I didn`t have that. I didn`t -- I didn`t think that I had it. Of course, when my sisters created, the characters of Georgie (PH) and Georgia (PH), I was like, maybe we had it a little bit.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And you had -- you write about like this age of 14. And a couple things happening that empowered you. I mean, you really, you empowered you, you had teachers that saw you. And that felt like something but you also kind of had a dream that you say was as valuable as the food and water that you could have.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Absolutely. It`s once again being elevated into this ultimate realm of redefinition of oneself. And you know, I say that about my life. I say that my whole life has been. Now, I see it as a relay race of the 6-year- old Viola, she ran her leg of the race, she survived. She had a lot of inappropriate expletives that came out of her mouth.\par \par \uc2 But that`s what we do when we`re in survival mode. And she passed the baton on to the 14-year-old Viola that realized that dreams can get you out of your reality, that passed it on to the 28th Viola, who said, you know what, if I want to go out in the world and feel joy and peace, then I got to work on myself, I got to go into therapy.\par \par \uc2 And then, that 28-year-old Viola passed the baton to the 34-year-old Viola, who got married. And you`re like, that`s an awesome responsibility. And now, I`m 56. And I have the baton.\par \par \uc2 And right now, I want to be liberated from labels. I want to be liberated from everything the world has taught me about myself. I want to be brave. That`s what I want to be. I dream about being brave.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, your dream is (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 HAGER: This is courage in a book, this is courage found, you know?\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Yes, but courage makes your knees shake. You know, it`s like, you know, I jumped out of a plane and I realized I jumped out of a plane for the exact same reason is to release myself of fear. And it`s interesting that as you ascend with the plane, you`re thinking to yourself, what am I doing?\par \par \uc2 And then, you get up to a certain, you know, point and you realize is only one thing that exists, you either jump or you don`t, there`s no in between.\par \par \uc2 You either jump or you don`t and you trust that the wind is going to carry you, you know that the parachute is going to carry you, you know. And that`s how I feel about my life. I feel like I just have to trust myself.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You know, you have gone from you describe your childhood and you describe finding love. And you know, when I think about love, your parents are the ones who show you how to speak, how to love, how to survive, how to be. They teach you all these life lessons.\par \par \uc2 You found this prints -- you found this prints. How did that come to be and how did you learn like not to seek out what you had seen not to mirror what you had to live?\par \par \uc2 [10:30:09]\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: I went to therapy. I did, I went to therapy. I remember my therapist, one of the things that she said was, you know, Viola, you wouldn`t know how to walk across the street if it weren`t covered, and poop and pee. You would think that there was something wrong with it.\par \par \uc2 And I thought to myself, Oh, my God, really? That`s me? But I felt like I had to do that work in order to handle the insurmountable task, which is marriage. That day to day living.\par \par \uc2 I actually Michelle Obama, quote, you know, she says, if you`ve been married for 50 years and 10 of those years were bad. You have a good marriage.\par \par \uc2 JENNA BUSH HAGER, NBC ANCHOR (on camera): Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: That day to day living, of accepting another human being into your life and nurturing that love, and sort of dying to yourself.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: You know? Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Can you read that little piece of your book, do you mind?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: That was just a beautiful phrase.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: This is like your prayer.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: When I got on my knees, and I prayed to God for Julius, I wasn`t just praying for a man. I was praying for life that I was not taught to live. But for something that I had to learn, that`s what Julius represented.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And what does your -- what does your daughter represent? Your 11- year-old, beautiful daughter.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: You know, I feel about Genesis the same way feel about myself. When I look at myself and I`m younger, I think it`s a great opportunity to grow and learn even about me, because I have to give her those affirmations. Every day I do it. Gee, your heart and your head are the two most important things in your life.\par \par \uc2 Genesis, Mommy`s going to love you more than anyone is ever going to love you even the love of your life. Genesis, life is going to be hard. I could give you everything, everything that I know, but it`s still going to be hard. You`re still -- I`m telling it to myself.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Telling yourself.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Telling it to yourself.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: You`re telling like, this little girl --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Viola --\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Wait, you can`t leave. We need more.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We know -- we never want you to leave, we just want to point that out.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes. If you want to stay longer, you can.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You can find -- you can find this book. It`s called Finding Me. It`s an incredible read, you won`t put it down, it will be read in one sitting.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Today.com/shop. And also, if you want to hear it the most beautiful way, Viola reads her own story.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And it`s a powerful, powerful.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Thank you, Viola.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Thank you, Viola. Stay longer.\par \par \uc2 We back right after this.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow, oh, wow. Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220428ei4r000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Meet the Press for April 17, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Amna Nawaz, Eugene Daniels, Matt Bradley \par \uc2 9,414 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 April 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC ANCHOR: This Sunday, Putin`s warning. As the war moves to the east...\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNINDENTIFIED MALE: Mariupol is in a dire situation. To save them, we need more weapons.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And Ukraine sinks a key Russian warship. The U.S. announces a new $800 million weapons package.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NED PRICE, STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: President Biden has said repeatedly that we don`t bluff.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: But Ukraine`s President Zelensky says his country still needs more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR, ZELENSKY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE (through translator): We need it sooner. We need it now. Is it enough? We don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Russia retaliates and warns the U.S. directly of unpredictable consequences.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BURNS, CIA DIRECTOR: None of us can take lightly the threat posed by a potential resort to tactical nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: What does it all mean for the U.S. and European response?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARL NEHAMMER, CHANCELLOR OF AUSTRIA: He believes he is winning the war.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: My guest this morning, Austrian Chancellor \uc2 Karl Nehammer\uc2 , the very first European leader to sit down face to face with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  since the Russian invasion began.\par \par \uc2 Plus COVID`s new phase.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ASHISH JHA\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE COVID-19 RESPONSE COORDINATOR: Virus is going to be with us for a very long time, probably forever.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: With cases up but hospitalizations still low, what will new Omicron subvariants mean for mask mandates?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You`re ready for the mandate to be over.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A year ago.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ll talk to the administration`s new COVID response coordinator, Dr. \uc2 Ashish Jha\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, whose party?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , 45TH PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We first have to defeat the RINOs and grandstanders in the primaries.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Another Trump impeacher announces he is leaving Congress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. FRED UPTON (R), MICHIGAN: Some of the folks here are so beholden to Trump that they don`t accept those of us that are willing to stand up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ll sit down with Republican Congressman \uc2 Fred Upton\uc2  of Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Joining me for insight and analysis are: PBS Newshour chief correspondent Amna Nawaz; Politico Playbook co-author Eugene Daniels; Matthew Continetti of the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 ; and Ruth Marcus, deputy editorial page editor for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is "Meet the Press" with Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Good Sunday morning. Happy Easter and happy Passover.\par \par \uc2 In Kyiv and across the country, between air raid sirens and emergency curfews, Ukrainian Jews gathered with their families this weekend for Passover seders, refugees celebrated across Europe having to say "next year in Ukraine." This morning in Saint Peter`s Square, the pope said the world is marking an Easter of war with Ukraine dragged into a cruel and senseless conflict. And there were processions through Lviv this morning as Ukrainians marked Palm Sunday. Orthodox Easter is celebrated next week.\par \par \uc2 But overnight, explosions rattled Kyiv and Kharkhiv. Missile strikes in retaliation after Ukranian missiles sunk the flagship of Russia`s fleet in the Black Sea. A real humiliating loss for the Russians. Now Russia is consolidating its forces in the east, staging attack helicopters at Ukraine`s eastern border and sending new soldiers and artillery for a long campaign. The governor of the Donetsk region says Mariupol has been wiped off the face of the earth by Russian forces.\par \par \uc2 And Russia is warning of unpredictable consequences directly to the United States through diplomatic channels if the United States and \uc2 NATO\uc2  continue to arm Ukraine. What is that a pretext for? All of this after the United States announced a new $800 million aid package that includes helicopters, Javelin missiles, and for the first time, much heavier artillery. But President Zelensky is still asking for more. In fact, he said this.\par \par \uc2 "When some leaders ask me what weapons I need, I need a moment to calm myself because I already told them the week before. It`s `Groundhog Day`."\par \par \uc2 NBC News foreign correspondent Matt Bradley is in Bucha this morning.\par \par \uc2 And, Matt, we thought this region was somewhat safe and then overnight cruise missiles hit Kyiv. What have you seen? What have you heard?\par \par \uc2 MATT BRADLEY NBC FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I mean, we`re talking about cruise missiles that are landing outside the capital city. You know, this city is still kind of lurching back to life despite some glimpses of the fighting still going on. The mayor of Kyiv just sort of shortened the curfew that had been in place for several weeks by a couple of hours, just as an indicator that things are kind of getting back to normal despite constantly hearing air raid sirens and the continued shelling outside the city.\par \par \uc2 I`m outside of Kyiv right now, and as you can see, they`re cleaning things up. I`m in something of a graveyard myself, but this one is for vehicles. If you can see just over my right shoulder here, Chuck, these are some of the Russian tanks that were recovered from the battlefield here in Bucha, totally burned out. But in front of me here are some of these civilian cars. The rest of these vehicles are for civilians. And, you know, a lot of this, you`re seeing signs of, you know, surrender. This is a white flag, and we`re seeing this over and over and over again, Chuck, white flags tied to cars that have been riddled with bullet holes, and in this case it looks like shrapnel holes, something, a large munition blew up right here and clearly sprayed this car with shrapnel.\par \par \uc2 This handwritten sign right here, it says, "deti.," that means "children." And we`re seeing the same thing on this car here. A lot of these vehicles are going to be figuring into investigations into war crimes in the coming weeks and months -- Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: An important crime scene that Matt Bradley is at right now. Matt, thank you.\par \par \uc2 This week Austrian Chancellor \uc2 Karl Nehammer\uc2  became the first European leader to sit down in person with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  since the Russian invasion began. Austria is one of six E.U. countries that are not in \uc2 NATO\uc2  and the country is facing questions about the official neutrality it has maintained since the Second World War.\par \par \uc2 Earlier I spoke with the chancellor and asked him about that Putin visit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You decided to visit Bucha before you went to the Kremlin. And you had said you wanted to be a firsthand witness and confront Mr. Putin by what you saw. Tell me what you told him.\par \par \uc2 NEHAMMER: Well, you know, I took the decision to visit first Ukraine and to meet President Zelensky, the prime minister, the defense minister, and the vice prime minister. And they showed us all of Bucha. And we saw the war crimes there. An Orthodox priest told us Russian soldiers shot the civilians.\par \par \uc2 And after the trip to Ukraine, I did a trip to Moscow to confront President Putin with what I saw. You know it was not a friendly conversation. It was a frank and tough conversation. And I told him what I saw. I saw the war crimes. I saw the massive loss of the Russian army. And I told him that there is a need for humanitarian coordinators for cities like Mariupol or Kharkhiv, for example. Civilians need water and we have to take care of the wounded -- of the wounded there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And what was his reaction? You called -- you accused him of -- of his soldiers of committing war crimes. What was his reaction?\par \par \uc2 NEHAMMER: He told me that he will cooperate with some international investigation on the one hand, and on the other hand, he told me that he doesn`t trust the Western world. So this will be the problem now in the future. I think international trust is the United Nations, an international investigation is necessary. So it was a tough discussion between each other. But I tried to convince him that, for example, the former Yugoslavian war showed us that international investigation is useful to prosecute the war criminals.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You came away pretty pessimistic. Why?\par \par \uc2 NEHAMMER: You know, we all can see that there is the preparation of a messy battle in the Donbas region. The Ukranian side -- Ukrainian side is prepared for that. The Russians are prepared for that. And we will see many losses of human lives there. And so this is the reason why I`m pessimistic on the one hand. On the other hand, both sides, President Zelensky -- I talked with President Zelensky about the trip to Moscow, and both sides, so President Zelensky and President Putin, mentioned the international -- the Istanbul peace talks. And maybe we have a little chance there for peace. And I also informed about that the president of turkey, President Erdogan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Do you get the sense that Vladamir Putin is viewing reality and what`s happening in Ukraine, or is he getting a dressed-up picture of the war?\par \par \uc2 NEHAMMER: No, I think he is now in his own war logic, you know? He thinks the war is necessary for security guarantees for the Russian Federation. He doesn`t trust the international community. He blames Ukrainians to -- for genocide in the Donbas region. So, well, he is now he is now in his world, but I think he knows what is going on now in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When you heard our CIA director talk publicly about the concerns that when Putin is cornered, he might do something like use a tactical nuclear weapon, the person you sat down with, do you think that`s a person that might use a nuclear weapon?\par \par \uc2 NEHAMMER: It`s a tough question. I think he knows that he has this weapon, and he knows the threat of this weapon. So I don`t know if he`ll really use it, but he knows that he can threaten the world with these weapons.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Does he believe he`s winning the war or losing the war?\par \par \uc2 NEHAMMER: No, I think he believes he is winning the war.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Did he give a rationale? I mean, did he explain what makes it seem as if things are going better than what it looks like to the rest of us?\par \par \uc2 NEHAMMER: No, I couldn`t say that. You know, he sent me clear messages about his concerns. What I think what is necessary, to confront him all the time with that what is going on in Ukraine. I think this is necessary. You know, at the end of our talks, he told me in German, it`s better the war ends earlier than later. So I think he knows exactly what`s going on now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to talk about additional punishments by the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , of which Austria is a member of. You have said you want sanctions that punish Russia and don`t punish Europeans. Obviously this has to do with the oil and gas issue. You`re very reliant on Russian gas. How quickly could Austria cut itself off from Russia without having an economic catastrophe?\par \par \uc2 NEHAMMER: I mean, we do it already, but it will take time because our dependency on Russian gas is 80 percent. It`s a similar situation to the Republic of Germany. And our industry and our private households need the gas. So now we try everything to get independent, but it is not possible today, tomorrow, but maybe in a few years we are independent from Russian gas.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Austria`s neutrality, it`s written into your constitution. Obviously as a member of the E.U., you have some protections of \uc2 NATO\uc2  nations if something were to happen to you. Given what you`ve seen the Russians do in Ukraine, I`m guessing it`s hard for you to be neutral in this war. You`re against this war. Do you feel the need to want to help Ukraine more?\par \par \uc2 NEHAMMER: Well, you know, first of all, the Austrian neutrality is deeply rooted in Austrian history. So in Austria, there is no discussion about neutrality. But we are neutral in a military way. We are not neutral if we see that we have to help to show solidarity, to help, and that we do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Would you like to see Ukraine become a member of the E.U.? Would you support that?\par \par \uc2 NEHAMMER: Well, I said also to President Zelensky and to the prime minister, I think the first step is that Ukraine needs fast and tough help like a new plan on how to rebuild Ukraine with the United States, with the United Kingdom, with the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . And after this is finished, there can be strong talks about membership in the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . But first things first, and first we have to help to rebuild Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Karl Nehammer\uc2 , the chancellor of Austria, I really appreciate you taking a few minutes during this holiday weekend to talk to us. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 NEHAMMER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`re going to discuss the Ukraine situation more a bit later in this show. But now we turn to the COVID-19 pandemic. It`s entering a new phase driven by fear, uncertainty, and apathy. In Europe and in New York, a new highly transmissible versions of the Omicron variant are on the rise. Philadelphia announced it is reinstating an indoor mask mandate. And the CDC announced masks will continue to be required on commercial flights until at least May 3rd. But hospitalizations are at their lowest point since the pandemic began, just 1,464 new daily hospital admissions this week compared to a seven-day average of more than 21,000 in January. Cases have ticked up across the United States over the past week. But take a look at where they were in January. They were at more than 800,000. So this is a different picture right now.\par \par \uc2 Across the country, though, differences in how states test are actually raising questions about whether case counts are an accurate metric. Just in this last week California tested more than 1,400 people out of every 100,000. New Jersey just 47 out of every 100,000. So joining me now is the new White House COVID-19 response coordinator, Dr. \uc2 Ashish Jha\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dr. Jha, welcome to "Meet the Press," sir.\par \par \uc2 JHA: Good morning. Thanks for having me here, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, I`m no longer asking you opinions, I`m asking you about policy here. So let me start with the current state of the pandemic. Are we in the middle of a new surge?\par \par \uc2 JHA: Yes, so, first of all, as you pointed out, Chuck, really important to take that bigger picture perspective. We are in a much, much better place than we have been for the last two years, certainly way better than we were in January, right? And that`s really good. Cases are ticking up, and we`re going to want to watch this carefully. It`s being driven primarily by BA.2, this highly contagious variant of Omicron. You know, so far, as you said, hospitalizations are largely flat, maybe inching up a little in a few places. We`re going to have to pay very close attention to this and see where this goes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you safe from these subvariants if you`ve been vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 JHA: Yes, the good news is our vaccines are holding up really well against BA.2, against all of the Omicron variants, especially if you have been boosted. So the key here is you`ve got to have the initial two shots and then you`ve got to have a booster. That`s what`s really protecting people at this moment.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So let me ask specifically about the Philadelphia decision. And I know you believe, look, local officials should make local decisions. I get all that. But are they using the right metric? Is the case count the correct metric? I mean, I just showed you right across the way from Philadelphia, New Jersey, you know, we don`t have an accurate picture of the case count in New Jersey. Does Philadelphia, and do they have an accurate enough one to make a decision like this?\par \par \uc2 JHA: Yes, it`s a great question. So if you look at the CDC guidance -- and by the way, something I`ve been enormously supportive of well before I came into my current role, the CDC guidance says basically three things should go into these kinds of decisions. Case count is certainly one of them. Hospitalization levels is another. And then hospital capacity is a third. You should be using all of those. That said -- I think so that`s a good framework for local officials. That said, you know, local officials do have a lot of local knowledge of where is it spreading, in which communities. They`re using other types of data. So I have been really supportive of local people making -- local leaders making local decisions, mayors and governors, and I continue to be supportive. I think that`s the right way to go.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you concerned, though, if you do something like this based on a case count, not on hospitalizations, that it -- it`s sort of a -- as I believe one of the doctors that \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  regularly has a columnist for, Dr. Wen, I think she said you risk a crying wolf situation.\par \par \uc2 JHA: Yes, look, how you communicate these kinds of decisions to your community is an enormous challenge that all local and federal leaders face, right? That is always important. And the question is, are the leaders in Philadelphia and other cities making decisions based on what they think is in the best interests of people there? I think they are. I think in general that`s what most of our political leaders are doing. They`re trying to get this right. And they`ve got to figure out how to communicate that in a way that people understand.\par \par \uc2 And when cases go up, you know, they`re going to look at their own local communities, figure out where is it spreading, and make those decisions. And I don`t think it`s important or I don`t think it`s useful for those of us sitting in Washington to second-guess those decisions.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Well, speaking of a Washington decision, the federal transportation mask mandate is something that`s a Washington decision, whether on airplanes or on other federally-funded public transportation. What metric are you looking at for this decision on May 3rd?\par \par \uc2 JHA: Yes, so, first and foremost, this is a CDC decision, right? And the CDC scientists, what they laid out was, they said, look, we need 15 more days. And the reason we need 15 more days is that cases are rising. We want to see is this going to translate into more severe disease, more hospitalizations, more deaths? That` why they asked. I think in 15 days we`ll have a lot more information. And then they are going to make a decision. They`re going to make a recommendation based on their assessment of the signs.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So you were an advocate of a vaccine mandate when you were on the other side of this, in your private capacity here. Is that something that can be implemented, a vaccine mandate for air travel?\par \par \uc2 JHA: That`s a really good question. I mean, I think, you know, when I was -- I have always believed, and certainly in the early days of the pandemic, once vaccines became available, I thought it was very important to get as many Americans vaccinated as quickly as possible. And mandates worked. Mandates do work. We are in a different position now obviously with more than 220 Americans vaccinated, 100 million people boosted. And so we really have to ask ourselves what is the role of mandates moving forward? And we`re going to need to assess each of these individual situations differently.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The -- would it be easier for the airlines to manage a vaccine mandate than a mask mandate?\par \par \uc2 JHA: Well, that`s a very good question. I think ultimately for -- for the airlines, I think the decision that the CDC is facing right now is do we extend that mask mandate moving forward. And that really has to be driven, I think, by the broader national situation decision, cases, hospitalizations, deaths, what`s happening with the state of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. A couple of vaccine questions. First, we still don`t have a vaccine for, essentially, birth to five. Is that -- are we basically not out of the potentially mask mandate world or even voluntary masks, if you will, until we figure out how to get folks under five vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 JHA: Yes, great question. First of all, I think we should let science and evidence drive when we`re ready to get kids under five vaccinated. \uc2 Moderna\uc2  has put out its press release. They`re going to be submitting the full set of data to the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , hopefully reasonably soon. I know \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  is working on this. So we`re going to have a lot more information. I don`t think we need to tie that to broader mask mandates. I think these are two separate issues, and I think we should keep them separate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The boosters, I`ll -- I`ll admit, my own doctor seems to have some confusion. Is the CDC recommending it for everybody 50-plus, or just approving it for 50-plus, consult your physician?\par \par \uc2 JHA: Yes, so, first of all, on boosters, let`s be very clear on one thing, Chuck, which is, everybody needs that third shot, the first booster. Every adult needs one. There is overwhelming evidence that that`s the best way to protect yourself both against infection but, much more importantly, against severe disease. On the second booster, on the second booster, the data out of Israel is pretty compelling that people over 60 who got a second booster after -- four months after their first booster, saw not just a reduction in infections but a reduction in deaths. So I think that`s what really prompted both \uc2 FDA\uc2  and CDC.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So...\par \par \uc2 JHA: I think...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JHA: I have elderly parents...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why 60 not 50?\par \par \uc2 JHA: ... I have recommended it -- sorry.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, I`m just curious. Why didn`t -- if 60 is where the science is, why did we lower it to 50?\par \par \uc2 JHA: Well, two sets of things. So the Israeli data is 60 and above. There are a lot of people in the 50s, especially if you have chronic disease, who are at high risk. So in my mind, as I read the evidence, people over 60 I think should be getting that booster. Again, that evidence is pretty compelling. Fifty to 59 you`re certainly eligible. It depends on your risk profile. That`s a place where I think it`s really important to talk to your doctor.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dr. \uc2 Ashish Jha\uc2 , welcome to the government, as they say. I appreciate you coming on and sharing the administration`s perspective on this.\par \par \uc2 JHA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the Republican Party is campaigning to take back the majority in Congress. Will it be a functioning majority party if all of its governing pragmatists are purged? Retiring Republican Congressman \uc2 Fred Upton\uc2  joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Last week Michigan Congressman \uc2 Fred Upton\uc2 , one of 10 House Republicans who voted to impeach \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , became the fourth to announce that he is going to retire. Five more are being primaried from the right by candidates Trump has endorsed. California`s David Valadao is the only exception so far. After Upton announced his retirement, Trump released this statement writing: "Upton quits. Four down, six to go."\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Fred Upton\uc2  joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Fred, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 UPTON: Nice to see you, always.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with what you told my colleague, Vaughn Hillyard. You said, this is on March 30th, not that long ago, you said that possibility of the perception of giving Trump a win by retiring was actually a motivator to run again, and yet here you`re deciding not to run. Did you give Trump a win?\par \par \uc2 UPTON: No, we didn`t. I don`t think we did. No, it would have been a doozy of a campaign. I would have loved it. I like campaigns. I really do.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You wanted Trump involved? You were ready to...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UPTON: I was ready to go.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 UPTON: But the final straw was they re-drew the district. So my hometown, my home community faces Lake Michigan, I can actually see it across the street where I live, it`s -- a lot of it now is with Lake Erie. So it`s literally 250 miles away, about 350,000 different voters than I had before. And so that was the final -- when the judges...\par \par \uc2 TODD: You test-drove a campaign though. You paid for some TV advertising.\par \par \uc2 UPTON: Yes, we were ready.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You did seem to test-drive it.\par \par \uc2 UPTON: We were -- we were ready.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Did you travel this new district?\par \par \uc2 UPTON: Yes, oh, absolutely, yes. No, I did. And we would have been welcomed in a lot of different places, but we would have had to raise, I don`t know, 5, 6 million bucks in a couple months. I could have done that, but at the end, you know, it`s time for family. I`ve got three grandkids now, one of them is actually saying, there`s papa. He`s two years old. He`s watching right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I am curious what you think is going to happen to a Republican- controlled Congress next year. \uc2 Don Bacon\uc2 , who is a colleague of yours, he`s a Republican from Omaha, he said this. "We can`t have this mindset of burn the house down. We have got to be a governing party when we`re in the majority. So we need people like Upton and folks like that so other people have to step up."\par \par \uc2 Look, you regularly vote to keep the government open. And you`re usually in the minority. And I believe the last time the debt ceiling was raised, I think now there is only going to be 33 members left.\par \par \uc2 UPTON: Maybe even less.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Who voted for it left in Congress. I mean, do you fear we`re going to -- that these House -- this next House Republican majority could lead us to default?\par \par \uc2 UPTON: Well, a lot depends upon what happens in November. I do believe that the House is going to flip. In fact, I talked to Charlie Cook earlier in morning. And he -- I think he believes that as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The math is hard to overcome.\par \par \uc2 UPTON: What is going to be the marginal? Is it going to be better than Pelosi, I think it will, a little bit, better than what it is today for her. But I don`t think it`s going to be this wild swing knowing that we picked up a lot of seats in the last election despite Biden winning. So, you know, what`s the over/under? So that`s going to make a big determination.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So a small majority, does that make it more likely if it`s a smaller Republican majority?\par \par \uc2 UPTON: Trouble, yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You think that`s more trouble?\par \par \uc2 UPTON: More trouble, which is why then the Problem Solvers Caucus, I`m one of the vice chairs there, is so important as we try to have some glue or fabric to really move forward on issues that we have got to deal with, whether it be immigration, whether it be energy, whether it be inflation, all thsoe different -- debt ceiling, obviously is going to be an early, early test in the next Congress.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got death threats for voting for infrastructure spending.\par \par \uc2 UPTON: I did.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You played these voicemails, we played these voicemails...\par \par \uc2 UPTON: That`s why I`m here today.\par \par \uc2 TODD: In some ways it is, isn`t it. I mean, it made it easier to say, I`m out.\par \par \uc2 UPTON: Yes, well, death threats, I mean, they never were like we had this last year, but it was pretty crazy. And remember, that was a Republican bill. I mean, literally a year ago this week, Governor Hogan brought a bunch of us up to his place in Annapolis. Republicans, Democrats, senators, governors, House members, both sides of the aisle. We defined what infrastructure ought to be and we decided how to pay for it. And it passed 69 to 30 in the Senate. It was the issue all last summer. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , Trump`s best friend, voted for it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So if you`re getting death threats, what is the likelihood of somebody watching what happened to you and going, yes, forget it, I`m not going to vote (ph)?\par \par \uc2 UPTON: Yes, you know what, it`s going to be a detriment getting good people to run, it really will be, because I have got a school board member who lives on my street, I think he got death threats, too, just over the -- the mask mandate. So...\par \par \uc2 TODD: We -- we have story out of Georgia where they can`t find anybody to be the elections person in Fulton County.\par \par \uc2 UPTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Because of threats.\par \par \uc2 UPTON: I believe it. I believe it. It`s -- you know, it`s -- it puts you at risk, particularly when they threaten not only you, and I like to think I`m pretty fast, but when they threaten your spouse or your kids or whatever, that`s what really makes it frightening.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask about if the Republicans are going to run on anything, and I`m curious what you would have done. While Kevin McCarthy says that the Republicans should run on an agenda this cycle...\par \par \uc2 UPTON: Yes, I think they should.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Here`s what \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If Republicans take back control of Congress after the midterms, what would be your agenda?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY), MINORITY LEADER: That is a very good question, and I`ll let you know when we take it back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: As a political strategist, this is probably the right call. It`s a lot easier to win an election by running against somebody than being for anything. But isn`t this what`s wrong with our politics?\par \par \uc2 UPTON: Yes, you have to be for something. And I think what Kevin has done, eight or nine different working groups and a whole bunch of different subjects, whether it be high tech or China or the deficit or, you know, energy, is a good thing. And I was around. Remember, I was Newt`s deputy whip, a deputy -- one of his deputy whips back in `94. That`s when we came up with the Contract with America. That was the huge effort. We didn`t say we were going to necessarily pass them, but it was going to be an agenda item for the next 100 days. I didn`t actually vote for all of them, but I signed the contract that we ought to have a debate. And some of them became law.\par \par \uc2 [09:30:01]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Can Kevin McCarthy both represent you and \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 UPTON: He can if he gets the margin. That`s why this over/under number is so important.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 UPTON: Are we going to be over or under 230? If it`s under 230, it will be --\par \par \uc2 TODD: You don`t think -- you think he may not become speaker if it`s under 230?\par \par \uc2 UPTON: It will be very hard to govern for Republicans if we`re under 230, knowing that we`ve got the MTG element that`s really not a part of a governing majority.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`ve had wild types of members of Congress since the history of this republic.\par \par \uc2 UPTON: Always have.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Okay. But if you --\par \par \uc2 UPTON: Remember Jim Traficant?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh, yeah. Look, beam me up."\par \par \uc2 UPTON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2 , and Marjorie Taylor Green, and \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2 , is that a different element than you`ve ever seen before?\par \par \uc2 UPTON: I don`t think we`ve had as many folks in that sort of wing of the party elected as we have before, but I think they`re very popular back at home. I mean, Boebert, she -- I mean, she being.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What does that tell you about -- what does that tell you about the Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 UPTON: Troubled waters I guess you could say, and that`s why the margin is going to be so, you know. Right now, look, Pelosi`s got the votes, particularly with the use of the proxy vote, she`s not going to lose a vote, and I don`t think she really has in the last year and a half.\par \par \uc2 We`re not going to have proxy votes. Kevin has made that very clear. None of us want that to happen, and it was put in place really because of the COVID issue, but a lot determines on what that level`s going to be. And I`ll be watching you in November.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I don`t mean to throw it very quickly at you, you were just in the region of Ukraine. Let me just ask this simple question: is the United States doing enough to help Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 UPTON: We have to do everything we can. Let me just tell you, the Ukraine folks, so brave, they are fighting for us. It was a bipartisan trip that I went on. We have to make sure that they have every tool, to make sure that they can survive.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It sounds like you think we can do a little bit more?\par \par \uc2 UPTON: We can.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Fred Upton\uc2 , thanks for coming on. I may bug you more even if you`re retired.\par \par \uc2 UPTON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, with the cost of living climbing, key parts of President Biden`s political coalition is falling away. Is there any way out of his domestic malaise? Panel is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:35:19]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Panel is here.\par \par \uc2 "PBS NewsHour" Chief Correspondent Amna Nawaz; "Politico Playbook" co- author Eugene Daniels; Matthew Continetti of the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  and author of the new book "The Right", it`s a fascinating book actually; and "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " deputy editorial page editor Ruth Marcus.\par \par \uc2 I say that as if I`m not used to books being fascinating. I don`t mean it - -\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTEPRISE INSTITUTE: Or books by me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Or books by you.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I`m still going to quote you though.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, you should. Put me on the back of the jacket.\par \par \uc2 Eugene, I want to start with you because you had an interesting interview with the president`s pollster.\par \par \uc2 EUGENE DANIELS, CO-AUTHOR, POLITICO PLAYBOOK: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And it sort of dovetails.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to put up a couple of poll numbers here. It`s really his overall job rating from our CNBC poll and the economic job rating. And the reason I`ve singled these two out is because they`re converging. They`re basically the same now.\par \par \uc2 His overall job rating I think is being seen through the lens of the economy, pure and simple, no other issue is popping through. And then you had this Anzalone interview. What did Biden`s pollster say?\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Yeah, he came on our podcast and, you know, he had a lot of interesting things to say. First and foremost, that this was the worst political environment he`s seen in his 30 years which, you know, is scary for --\par \par \uc2 TODD: `94 for the Democrats. We could go `10, `14, yeah.\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: That this was the worst environment.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: And, you know, there was a lot of the concerns that you hear from Democrats behind closed doors. They won`t say it on the record or in front of cameras, but they do worry about how the economy is affecting how people think about what they`re going to do in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 And I will say his -- when you talk about whether or not the administration`s able to pass the Build Back Better or call it what you want at this point, chunks of that, he said that if they`re able to do that, they`ll be able to break through. So if you touch health care, if you do something with the care economy, if you do something with climate change, he thinks that that might work through. But that`s hard to see happening at this point.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Amna, there`s one demographic group I want to single out, and it`s voters under the age of 35 --\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ, \uc2 PBS\uc2  NEWSHOUR CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: Oh, yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- who have never experienced inflation in their lifetime.\par \par \uc2 As I say, they`re used to, like, when, oh, there`s a new gadget, well, in six months, it`ll be cheaper.\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Not more expensive.\par \par \uc2 This is a whole different world.\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look at these numbers here. They think over -- by a five-point margin greater. Right now, 82 percent of the public thinks the economy`s only fair or poor. Adults under 35, it`s 87 percent.\par \par \uc2 Again, if you want to buy a house for the first time, you`re paying a higher interest rate than your parents have ever paid.\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Never mind food and gas. This is a huge part of the Dem coalition?\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: It`s not great. It`s not great. You`re talking about one of the key groups that not only helped Democrats win back control of Congress, you know, propelled Biden into the White House, but are also going to be in play in larger numbers than ever before in the upcoming midterms, right?\par \par \uc2 And the economy is front and center. And when we talk about the economy, let`s be specific. We`re talking about inflation, we`re talking about cost of living, because the White House will again point to things like unemployment being at record lows in 17 states, under 3 percent in 20 states, wage growth at the rate it`s been. But also the fact that wage growth is not keeping up with the cost of living, right?\par \par \uc2 So this generation, yeah, they stepped out into the world and were immediately hit with two back to back financial crises, right? You had the global financial crisis, then you have this really substantial crisis from the pandemic, and things are not getting better.\par \par \uc2 Even if you just go back a year, expectations were so high: vaccines were coming out, things were going to get back to normal. Their wages have been suppressed, they are forced into unemployment or underemployment, and they are pessimistic about the future.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Ruth, when America`s economy gets a cold, Nevada gets the flu, and I want to show these Nevada numbers here. Because two incumbents, Democratic incumbent \uc2 Steve Sisolak\uc2 , Democratic incumbent Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , both were behind, trailing their potential Republican opponents. There`s still some primaries, still a lot of work to do there.\par \par \uc2 But Neva -- but when you look at the Nevada electorate, this is pieces of the coalition you see fraying away from Biden, Hispanic voters starting to peel away a little bit, working class, non-college-educated whites.\par \par \uc2 This is a problem and Nevada may be the most acute.\par \par \uc2 RUTH MARCUS, DEPUTY EDITORIAL PAGE EDITOR, THE WASHINGTON POST: Nevada is a very acute problem, and it`s an indicative problem that as John Anzalone said, Democrats are facing a very dire situation in November.\par \par \uc2 And just to put some numbers on that, more than 7 in 10, 72 percent of Nevadans rated economic conditions fair or poor. This is a state that has gone for the Democratic candidate four times in a row, for Democratic presidential candidate. It`s a state that Biden won. Now his approval rating there is below the overall poll, it`s at 35 percent.\par \par \uc2 And most scary, and perhaps most ominous, is the peeling off of Hispanic voters in Nevada. Slump of approval rating from 73 percent a year ago to 52 percent now.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:02]\par \par \uc2 You just read that if you`re a Democratic candidate or pollster, you read it and, gulp, what is going on here?\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: And one of the biggest issues for Democrats is that they are talking about the things they`ve done, right? They`ve talked about the ARP, COVID money, the child tax credit, talking about the infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 But voters are thinking, Okay, but what are you doing now? What are you doing for me lately? And Anzalone said that, and the White House knows that.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I think there was a big myth in both parties in Washington, D.C. that Hispanic voters are single issue voters on the issue of immigration. That is not true. Hispanic voters are American voters, and they care about the same thing everybody else does.\par \par \uc2 How`s the economy doing? How are your schools? And are your streets safe?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: And it`s that trifecta of issues that`s powering the Republican wave this November.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS: And are you representing me and my concerns?\par \par \uc2 And so, when Hispanic -- Hispanic voters, Republicans have always said and with good reason, that they are naturally Republican voters. There`s a lot of social conservatism among the Hispanic voting bloc, to the extent that Democrats are not echoing that, and supporting the way that Hispanic voters think. They are just hurting themselves.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It -- you know, and the White House has to be more focused on Ukraine. I know politically where they have to be focused, but they need to be. And you can make an argument that successfully getting an end to this war sooner rather than later can actually help the economy.\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: Well, sure. I mean, the two issues are almost at odds, right? Because the longer that war goes on, the longer there`s going to be food shortages, the longer there`s going to be fuel prices going up. And we know of course, and we`ve seen in the data, yes, inflation had been going up even for several months before the war in Ukraine began, but we know a lot of that huge increase in inflation was because of the fuel price increase, because of the war.\par \par \uc2 And so voters get that. I think people understand that. But when they`re looking at who to blame in all of this, most, most Americans do look at the pandemic. And they say, okay, 69 percent of Americans say, I get it. That`s why the economy is where it is. Fifty-five percent blame Putin, but then 45 percent or so blame President Biden.\par \par \uc2 And they`re not going to be voting against the pandemic, and they`re not going to be voting against Putin. They`re going to have to decide about President Biden.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Gallup recently asked the public, what are your top concerns? Of course economy and the cost of living was number one. Second, strikingly to me, was government and poor leadership.\par \par \uc2 So other presidents have been able to recover from these sorts of setbacks, but I think the public has judged Biden, and judged him negatively, and it`s going to be hard for him to change that perception no matter what happens.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, as Anzalone will say, the one thing they hang their hat on is, Reagan had a bad first midterm, Clinton had a bad first midterm, right? And, Obama had a bad first midterm.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Things got better. For those presidents, conditions got better.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS: Kind of, that`s kind of clinging to faint hopes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`ve got to try something, right?\par \par \uc2 All right, let me pause here. When we come back, two more coaches have added their names to the \uc2 Brian Flores\uc2  lawsuit against the \uc2 NFL\uc2  alleging racial discrimination. The league is responding, but has it been enough? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:45:51]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. It has been 20 years since the \uc2 NFL\uc2  first instituted what`s known as "The Rooney Rule." It requires teams to interview at least one candidate of color for a head coaching job.\par \par \uc2 There were three Black head coaches, just three then. And today, there are still just three. Three of the league`s 32 head coaches are Black.\par \par \uc2 In February, former \uc2 Miami Dolphins\uc2  head coach \uc2 Brian Flores\uc2  filed a class action lawsuit alleging racial discrimination after being fired. Two more coaches have signed on since.\par \par \uc2 In a new report for our single-topic show "Meet the Press Reports", my colleague Blayne Alexander takes a look at the troubled legacy of race and the \uc2 NFL\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: In 2021, Black players made up the vast majority of the \uc2 National Football League\uc2 , with more than two- thirds identifying as either African American or mixed race. Yet among coaches, it`s a much different story. Currently, there are only three Black head coaches in the entire league. And throughout the league`s nearly 102- year history, only 25 Black men have ever held that position.\par \par \uc2 Among them, \uc2 Tony Dungy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I think the basic question is: Does the \uc2 NFL\uc2  have a race problem?\par \par \uc2 TONY DUNGY\uc2 , FORMER NFL COACH: We do have a problem. When you`ve got an organization that is 65-70 percent African American on the field but then you look in other portions of the business, the staff, the coaching staff, the front office, the support staff, and it`s not that, you have to question and say, well, what`s going on here?"\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: In the last month, the NFL`s league office has taken steps, announcing a new diversity committee to evaluate hiring practices. But, of course, it is the owners that eventually have to make these changes. You can see our entire "Meet the Press Reports" episode anytime on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, in anticipation of the Supreme Court`s decision on Roe, states are proposing and passing a wave of new abortion laws.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:51:27]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 We`ve had quite a few changes in abortion law pretty much since the start of this year, both on the restrictive side in the states and on the legal side as well. Look at this. We had some -- forty-plus states have seen more restrictive abortion laws introduced, and I believe we`ve had nine of them enacted. Eight of them had Republican governors. Just one didn`t, and that`s \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  on the Democratic side.\par \par \uc2 You had about 30 states that introduced bills to protect or expand abortion rights. You`ll be shocked to know, Ruth, that all of those states had Democratic governors --\par \par \uc2 MARCUS: Funny how that works.\par \par \uc2 TODD: - except for one, \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 . And guess what? \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , his veto was overridden by a Democratic legislature. \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , on the other side, his veto done on that.\par \par \uc2 But we`re all doing this, waiting for what happens in June from the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS: What`s going to happen in June?\par \par \uc2 TODD: You tell me.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS: June is not good news for abortion rights. But what`s really remarkable, I think, is the energy, the jump-the-gun urge among conservative states and Republican state legislatures. Guys, you`re going to win. It`s only a question of how much you`re going to win how quickly. You can`t even wait for the court? Apparently not.\par \par \uc2 We`ve had a situation in Texas where what is now a constitutional right has been allowed not to take effect for almost all pregnant women since September. We now see in Kentucky for the first time effectively abortion is outlawed there because they have passed a law that it is impossible to comply with. It requires abortion clinics to have mechanisms to dispose of fetal remains, but that is simply not possible.\par \par \uc2 So abortion is outlawed by state legislatures and so far allowed by the courts, even before the court acts. That`s pretty remarkable.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to play sort of from both sides of this debate how governors are messaging their decisions. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R), FLORIDA: This will represent the most significant protections for life that have been enacted in this state in a generation.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D), MICHIGAN: The assault on women`s privacy rights and bodily autonomy is no longer a theoretical risk.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KEVIN STITT\uc2  (R), OKLAHOMA: We want Oklahoma to be the most pro-life state in the country. We want to outlaw abortion in the state of Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JARED POLIS (D), COLORADO: No matter what the Supreme Court does in the future, people -- women in Colorado will be able to choose.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Matthew, I think a lot depends on how the Supreme Court rules on abortion, right? Because I think there is - if this is a debate about legal or not legal, Republicans might have a bigger problem than if this is a debate about 15 weeks or 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Yes. A couple things, Chuck, on this important issue.\par \par \uc2 The pro-life movement has had two major goals since Roe v. Wade. One is to reverse Roe v. Wade. That seems like it could happen or come close to happening with the huge space for states to regulate abortion in June.\par \par \uc2 The second goal is to ban abortion, right? And that`s the subject of the Human Life amendments that have been introduced throughout the history of the Roe era. That`s a harder goal, especially on the national level.\par \par \uc2 And I think what we`re seeing now on both the Republican and Democratic side is people preparing for the reintroduction of abortion law into the Democratic space. And I think that means that you`re going to get extreme bills actually on both sides: red state bills outlawing abortion, blue state bills legalizing it all the way through the nine months of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 [09:55:02]\par \par \uc2 TODD: What`s curious -- I picked out Florida and Arizona, Eugene, because they`re battleground states. And when it`s about legality, it`s a clear majority in favor of staying legal. It`s when you start to get into the details that things change here.\par \par \uc2 When you spoke to Anzalone, are they counting on a Roe ruling that energizes them in these midterms?\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Yes, and Democrats have been talking about this a little bit, is that they expect this to be something that sends people to the polls. The problem is depending on what the Supreme Court rules, you know, Roe seems like it`s going to be rolled back at least a little bit.\par \par \uc2 And so that`s already going to happen, so there`s no change that they can do other than to say, if you give us more Senate seats, if you give us more House seats, we can pass some type of federal law that allows abortion so we don`t have to go through this route. Because what that map showed, both of those maps, is that where we`re going, what we may have is a world in which there are two countries: where you can go in one state and get an abortion and literally a mile away cannot get an abortion. That`s something that --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, Missouri`s trying to figure out how to even prevent that --\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Exactly. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: - for what it`s worth.\par \par \uc2 Amna?\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: But that`s what we`ve seen happen, right?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: And Texas is a perfect example. We already saw the end of last year after I think it was a six-week ban in Texas that private citizens were allowed to enforce -- and that`s how they avoided court intervention- thousands of women, thousands of women left the state to be able to go to other places to have those same kinds of abortion care services. And already I mean, you don`t need to pass an outright ban to essentially limit and completely remove a woman`s right to a legal or safe abortion. You just have to pass enough rules that clinics can no longer be in compliance to do it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me get -- I want to get out of the specifics, and I want to get to the culture war issue, left and right. Is a culture war midterm election to the benefit of the Democrats this cycle or the Republicans, Ruth?\par \par \uc2 MARCUS: Well, it depends on which aspect of the culture war, which battle of the culture war you`re going to fight. If you`re going to fight about gay rights, about schools and things, I think it may end up being a pro- Republican issue in certain states. If you`re going to fight about abortion, it`s going to end up being in those perhaps same states a pro- Democratic issue.\par \par \uc2 However, it is really hard to see a culture war outcome, determinative battle when you`ve got inflation at the stage that it`s at.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say - Inflation will overcome everything.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: After the Texas law was passed, I was among the conservatives saying this might have thrown a lifeline to Democrats in the off-year election. That didn`t happen. Republicans did very well in the off-year election post-Texas law.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  tried to use it in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Right now, Abbott is leading his bid for reelection. So that backlash has not happened.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Again, I think we all wait for what the Supremes tell us in June.\par \par \uc2 Look, I need to pause here for a minute. Before we go, I want to note the passing of a longtime friend and a colleague for a lot of us here in Washington at NBC. Wendy Rieger, she worked at News 4 here in Washington as a reporter and anchor for 33 years. We shared a bureau together for decades with her.\par \par \uc2 Wendy was diagnosed with brain cancer almost a year ago. Before her death, in a note to her beloved newsroom, Wendy wrote this: As you know, I`ve lived my life big and loud. It is my nature, and I`ve had a blast. But a stillness has come over me that is profound and potent. I didn`t know I could be this quiet. Life is not always a test; it is a teaching. I must learn this lesson with grace, and I will.\par \par \uc2 Wendy Rieger was 65.\par \par \uc2 That`s all we have for today. Thanks for watching. Have a happy Easter and a happy Passover. We`ll be back next week, because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020220417ei4h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Florida`s Republican Governor \uc2 Ron Desantis\uc2  Banning Most Abortions After 15 Weeks\b0\par\par\uc2 Tom Llamas, Kerry Sanders \par \uc2 1,811 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 April 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS: All right. Back here at home. Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signed into law yesterday a measure to ban abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy. That makes Florida the largest State in the latest of tighten access to the procedure ahead of a \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  decision over abortion rights. NBC`s Kerry Sanders is in Fort Lauderdale with the reaction.\par \par \uc2 Kerry, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, good morning. This is a significant step by Florida`s Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  banning most abortions after 15 weeks. The law here is modelled after the Restrictive Abortion Law in Mississippi which is now before the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . And is considered by many to be the greatest challenge to the landmark decision of Roe V. Wade in decades.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voiceover): This morning, Florida is at the epicenter of the escalating battle over abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): We are here today to defend those who can`t defend themselves.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voiceover): Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signing a bill into law, Thursday, that bans most abortions after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: This will represent the most significant protections for life that have been enacted in this State in a generation.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voiceover): The new law, House Bill 5 is set to take effect in July. While it permits women to obtains an abortion if their health is in danger, or if the baby has fatal fetal abnormality, it contains no exceptions for rape or incest. Critics argue it`s unconstitutional and will have ripple effects beyond Florida.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (on camera): What is your reaction to this new law in Florida?\par \par \uc2 MAYTE CANINO, FLORIDA ALLIANCE OF PLANNED PARENTHOOD AFFILIATES: This is a very cruel law that has passed the State of Florida. Now, people will have to go to North Carolina in order to access abortion past 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voiceover): Abortion rights activists Stephanie Loraine Pineiro says, the law would have blocked her own access to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE LORAINE PINEIRO, ABORTION RIGHTS ACTIVISTS: As a person who had an abortion as a result of sexual assault. If I was pregnant right now, I wouldn`t be able to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voiceover): It`s the latest in a series of strict new abortion laws enacted in recent days, from Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KEVIN STITT (R-OK): We want to outlaw abortion in the State of Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voiceover): To Kentucky where the Republican led legislature overrode the Democratic governor`s veto to enact sweeping restrictions. Florida`s law is modelled after a Mississippi law banning virtually all abortions after 15 weeks. Now under review by the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  representing the most serious challenge to Roe V. Wade in nearly three decades.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (on camera): Well, back in December, a majority of the conservative justices on the Supreme Court indicated that they may indeed uphold the Mississippi Restrictive Abortion Law. A decision on that is expected in weeks. Meantime here in Florida, planned parenthood says, they will follow the new rules, the new law here in Florida, even though they disagree with it. Tom.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: A lot of States dealing with these new rules. All right. Kerry, we thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Let us get to a "Morning Boost" and some good news. You guys are going to like this one. Our beloved college basketball analyst Dick Vitale. The 82-year-old announcing that he is now cancer free. Vitale was diagnosed with lymphoma last year and he marked the end of his sixth chemotherapy treatment by ringing a bell at Sarasota Memorial Hospital in Florida.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DICK VITALE, \uc2 ESPN\uc2  COLLEGE BASKETBALL BROADCASTER: Hey, ring that bell, baby. Ring that bell.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. Still got that enthusiasm. He says, he was inspired by the words of his late friend, Jim Valvano, don`t give up. Don`t ever give up.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Dickie V.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Didn`t look like he`s given up.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Dickie V.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: No one better.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Like he`s got that energy back.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: He does.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: When, we come back here on this Good Friday, ahead of Easter, I caught up with one of our country`s most prominent faith leaders, Bishop T.D. Jakes. We talked about the power of faith in these challenging times. We`ll have that conversation for you right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:10:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:15:00]\par \par \uc2 Welcome back on this Friday morning. So, last night our little "TODAY" show family hit the town to support the newest inductees in the Broadcasting and Cable Hall of Fame. Our own Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb, Roker himself emceed the event. Honoring pioneers, innovators, and stars in our industry.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC`S TODAY WEATHER ANCHOR: Oh, I got to tell you, it was a well-deserved night to remember for our beloved dynamic duo. They were reflecting on their career so far. Hoda had a touching speech reminding us how important it is to speak up for ourselves.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: We need to know our worth. And when I speak about not knowing your worth, I`m kind of speaking to myself, too. I`ve spent lot of my career thinking I didn`t deserve all the things I got even after I worked so hard. I didn`t think I deserved to work at "Dateline" or sit with Kathie Lee, and certainly not the "TODAY" show.\par \par \uc2 Ask for what you want. Ask for the raise. Ask for the promotion. Ask for the family. Even if it is just a whisper. I`m standing here and I am 57 years old. I have two daughters. And I get to sit with the incomparable Savannah Guthrie. I`m not doing this. I know they took bets at the table if I would. I`m not. I am grateful to be inducted into the Broadcasting and Cable Hall of Fame. And this award is to all the women who felt not worthy. You are worth it, and so am I.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: So special.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Hoda --\par \par \uc2 DYLAN DREYER, NBC NEWS 3RD HOUR OF TODAY, CO-HOST AND METEOROLOGIST: Catching the second time around, too.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: -- she brought down the house.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: That was like a Ted talk. That was amazing.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s beautiful.\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES, NBC NEWS 3RD HOUR OF TODAY, CO-HOST: The -- it was beautiful but then Savannah comes in as a closer and we were all crying.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: No, I`m like, why did the --\par \par \uc2 JONES: No.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: -- why do I have to follow Hoda? That`s impossible.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Such a well-deserved honor.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: For folks who don`t know you, as well as some of us have come to know you aren`t just the face of the "TODAY: show. You are our North Star here.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh, you are so sweet. Well, it was really fun. It was so cool. And everybody showed up for us, which was neat.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: And --\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes, and --\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: -- and we`re not done yet.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Savannah, we`re not done with you because you had some inspiring words for anyone whose plan A didn`t quite work out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I enthusiastically started my job at this tiny station with a newsroom staff of four, including me. Right away I was shooting and reporting and editing. I was doing it. I was Butte, Montana`s Diane Sawyer for 10 days. That is when the management called a meeting and closed the station. My TV career was over and it hadn`t even lasted two weeks. That was my big break. And it was gone.\par \par \uc2 If you had told that girl lying on her bed crying, humiliated and kind of too broke to even get home, that one day she`d be here in this company receiving this honor, well, all the old rusty mining equipment in Butte, Montana could not have lifted her jaw off the floor. To Hoda, especially, I would hold hands and close my eyes and go anywhere with you. And I am so glad that here is where we are tonight.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: I can`t take it. It was so good.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Wow. Oh, come on.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It was so fun. It was really special. And to have Al do the honors, it was so cool.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: It was my honor, believe me. Being part of that and seeing all of us together was just really special. Not just at the "TODAY" show but other folks from NBC News. It was really terrific.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: I just want to add, I mean, for you and Hoda, you are -- it is so deserving to be recognized for something like this because you`re so good at what you do.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: But as friends, as mothers, like, you guys are just role models.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: And some -- some -- both women that I look up to and I just want to thank you for everything you do for everyone around you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, I love guys. Your such good friends.\par \par \uc2 JONES: So good.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We love you. All right. guys.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: How does it feel to be a hall of famer?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh, my gosh. It`s like too mush. Where`s Hoda? How could she leave me today? I`m like -- everybody like -- anyway, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: That`s so good.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And you cannot just emcee. He also does the weather. What Al -- again, Al not only carrying the show, carrying the network.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: No, no.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Day and night.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Stop it some more. Anyway, let`s show you what we have around the country for today. Sunshine up and down the East Coast. Few showers down in Florida. Severe storms later this afternoon in the Lower Mississippi River Valley. Record lows out West in the Pacific Northwest with some rain and snow. And the chill continues across the Northern Plains.\par \par \uc2 [08:20:00]\par \par \uc2 That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220420ei4f0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Home Edit Star, Clea Shearer Revealing Her Breast Cancer Diagnosis And Undergoing A Double Mastectomy\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie \par \uc2 1,653 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 April 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [08:10:00]\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: We are back with an emotional announcement from our friend, Clea Shearer. She`s the star and co-founder of the hit \uc2 Netflix\uc2  show "Get organized with the Home Edit". On Thursday, Clea revealed that she has been diagnosed with the breast cancer. On Friday, she underwent a double mastectomy. And in a moment, we`re going to talk to her best friend and her "Home Edit" co-star and co-founder, Joanna Teplin. But first, a bit more about Clea`s story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CLEA SHEARER, CO-FOUNDER OF "THE HOME EDIT": Hi, guys. I`m home from the hospital. I`m recovering in my own bed and so --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (voiceover): Clea Shearer, the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  reality star. Mother of two. And dear friend of "TODAY" posting an emotional update to her six million followers after undergoing a double mastectomy on Friday.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: I`ve been mobile. I`ve been walking around. I have some pain, don`t get me wrong, but you know, we can do hard things.\par \par \uc2 We are actually professional organizers, crazy people.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (voiceover): The home design expert known for organizing famous closet with best friend Joanna Teplin is now sharing a very personal journey after being diagnosed with stage 2 breast cancer. Posting on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , I found a lump myself the last week of February. I`ve been trying to make an appointment with my O.B. for several months and even when I told them I found a lump they couldn`t accommodate me. I had to request a mammogram from my general doctor, but I caught it early. Had I not taken this upon myself, I would be in a completely different situation right now.\par \par \uc2 DR. SU WANG, PRIMARY CARE DOCTOR AT COOPERMAN BARNABAS MEDICAL CENTER: What she really highlights is that, women do have to be their own advocates. And you have to know your own body. Early detection is really critical for breast cancer. One, it improves survival. But two, it can totally change the course and the kind of interventions you need.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: If anyone can handle cancer, it`s me.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (voiceover): Now, the 40-year-old is using her experience to inspire other women to do regular self-exams and schedule an annual memory gram.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Please prioritize your headline. I know how hard it is on a daily basis to be a mom, to be working, to have a hundred things always on your to-do list. But, I mean, what does it all matter if we`re not healthy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (on camera): And joining us now Joanna Teplin, the other half of "The Home Edit" duo and Clea`s very good friend who`s been by her side every step of this journey. Hi, Joanna.\par \par \uc2 JOANNA TEPLIN, CO-FOUNDER OF "THE HOME EDIT": Hi, Savannah.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s strange to see you under these circumstances.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: I know. I don`t know what do without bins.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, I know, but we`re not organizing but -- and your other half. I mean --\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: I know.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: -- really, Clea, is like -- she`s just half your heart.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: She really is. She`s an extension of me.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes. How is she doing.?\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: You know, she`s doing well. I mean, she is just so strong and such a pillar of strength that I can`t imagine anyone tackling cancer the way she has --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I know.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: -- and is.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: She`s been incredible. She just had the double mastectomy.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 [08:15:00]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: She revealed that actual -- they thought it was Stage 1 cancer but it`s actually Stage 2.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: That`s right. So, she`s, right now, recovering from the surgery which -- the surgery was very successful. And she`s home now, just waiting to find out any next steps.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: So, you guys were actually here --\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: We were.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: -- in February. You were here for a "TODAY" show segment.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And she noticed lump on herself. Do you -- what did she say? Did she say it right then to you?\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: Yes, she just said I -- you know, I think I found something. I`m not sure what it is. And, you know, we just decided we`re not going to spiral yet.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: You know, let`s get more information first. And so, she decided to try to make a -- try to get an appointment with her O.B. They couldn`t get her in for a long time. And so, she`s -- realized she had to keep pushing and figure out what to do. So, she reached out to her general practitioner, who was, thankfully, able to take get her in and then, kind of, the rest transpired.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, you know, I think if there`s anything to take away from this, it`s -- you need to be a Clea in a situation like this.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Because, if she hasn`t pushed it --\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: -- that mammogram wasn`t available --\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: No.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: -- for months and months. And this is a very aggressive form cancer.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: Exactly. So, thank God she`s such an advocate for herself. And she is now becoming an advocate for so many others to self-test and be aware from the start.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And how is she feeling? Like, how`s her -- she`s so strong.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: So strong.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: But does she -- I`m sure she has her moments. How is she handling all this?\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You know, her family, her kids. It`s all -- you know, it`s a lot to take on.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: She has the best attitude. I can`t imagine having the attitude that she has. But she`s just, like, if it`s going to happen to someone, why not me, instead of why me.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: And because she`s able to have that fortitude, and that way of thinking about, she`s able to help so many other people. And you know, she used -- she`s using this platform and her privilege of having help, having the support, having the emotional support. All of those things to bring awareness to others.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: She did -- was she, you know, kind of evaluating whether she wanted to be so public about it or did she say --\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: She only has one mode. She is only -- she`s only a public person.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: And she decided to make this no different. And she felt like, if she could do this for anybody and it could be, you know, if there was something to show for this horrible thing that she has to go through, then it was all worth being public for.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, it`s amazing. I`m sure knowing her she`ll be back to work, organizing --\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: Oh, we`re trying to keep her sitting down.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I know.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: She`s impossible. Yes, I told her I`m going sit on her like a chicken so that she stays put. But she -- you know, she`s having a tough time sitting but she`s just so incredible.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, she really is. Our hearts are with Clea. Sit down. Just rest. OK.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: Please.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: If you`re watching this. Joanna, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: Thank you, Savannah.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Keep us posted. Wrap your arms around Clea. Give her a big hug from us.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: I definitely will.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You know, we`re just -- we just love you guys.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: You guys are the best.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: Thank you know.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. 17 minutes after the hour. We`ll go to Al. Get a check of his forecast. He`s in Sashington this morning.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC`S TODAY WEATHER ANCHOR: We send her our love. All right. Thank you so much, Savannah.\par \par \uc2 Let`s show you -- right now into tonight, we`ve got this severe forecast, this severe weather going on. 13 million people at risk. Wind gusts of over 60. Damaging mile -- damaging hail. Tornadoes, likely in this area. We`ve also got hail from Little Rock down to Waco, Dallas, Fort Smith.\par \par \uc2 For tomorrow, we`re looking at 39 million people stretching from Des Moines down to Austin back to Little Rock, Indianapolis, Chicago. Few strong tornadoes. In fact, we could be looking at EF-2 or stronger from Des Moines down to Ardmore. We`re going to be watching that very closely.\par \par \uc2 And then we move into Wednesday. 62 million people at risk for damaging hail, strong tornadoes possible. And, again, damaging wind gusts of up to 60 miles per hour. So, we`re going to be watching all of this. Four weeks in a row if it comes to fruition of severe weather over the three days. That`s what`s going on around the country, here`s what happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And that is your latest weather. Now, it is best time of the morning. The way to start the week off, "POP Start."\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY, NBC NEWS HOST: Pumping us up in the Nation`s Capital even.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Boom.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Uncle Al, thank you for that. We`re feeling the love on a Monday morning.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220415ei4b000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Meet the Press for April 3, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Cornell Belcher \par \uc2 8,763 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 April 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [09:00:22]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST (voice-over): This Sunday, is Russia withdrawing or reloading?\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , SECRETARY OF STATE: There is what Russia says, and there is what Russia does. We`re focused on the latter.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ukrainians push the Russians away from Kyiv and the north. But retreating forces appear headed to Ukraine`s east.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: It`s clear the Russians want to reprioritize their operations in the Donbas area.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Real questions now about what \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is being told.\par \par \uc2 KATE BEDINGFIELD\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: We believe that Putin is being misinformed by his advisers about how badly the Russian military is performing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: My guest this morning, the Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  and Russia and Putin expert Masha Gessen of "The New Yorker." Plus, one-on-one with \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: To let an autocrat do what Putin decided to do is something that everybody has a stake in trying to stop.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ll talk to the former secretary of state about Putin, the war, and the Democrats` midterm headwind. Also, the government plans to end a Trump era COVID policy that limited immigration.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Every single day that passes it becomes more and more absurd for the administration to claim that Title 42 has any basis whatsoever in public health.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But now Mr. Biden is likely to hear criticism that he`s making illegal immigration worse. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  asked Putin for information on President Biden`s son, Hunter.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: So now I would think Putin would know the answer to that. I think he should release it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why is the former president again asking for help from a man waging war on an ally?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joining me for insight and analysis are Amy Walter, editor in chief and publisher of the "Cook Political Report," Democratic pollster Cornell Belcher, NBC News Capitol Hill correspondent Leigh Ann Caldwell, and Republican strategist Brad Todd.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s MEET THE PRESS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is MEET THE PRESS WITH CHUCK TODD.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Good Sunday morning. Have we reached a turning point in this war? Russia`s announcement that it was pulling its forces from Kyiv increasingly looks more like an act of necessity than actual diplomacy. Ukraine has pushed Russian troops away from the capital city and other areas of the north as well. It`s unlikely the Russians are suddenly scaling back its Kyiv offensive as a matter of goodwill. And more likely they`re having to reposition and resupply, focusing on the east where they`ve had more success.\par \par \uc2 Notably, Ukraine attack helicopters managed to go on offense fly 20 miles inside Russian territory and hit a Russian oil depot, then returned safely to Ukraine. A true embarrassment for Russian military. As Ukrainian forces have reclaimed territory they`re finding not only the burned-out wreckage of Russian tanks and equipments but also the bodies of dead civilians apparently murdered by Russian troops.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy also says the Russians have mined homes and equipment and even booby-trapped those dead bodies. The question now is what does the next phase of this war look like? Russia`s hopes of a lightning quick victory have clearly failed. So are we now in for a long, bloody war in the eastern part of the country?\par \par \uc2 I want to talk to the Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  here in a moment. We`re going to begin with our chief foreign correspondent Richard Engel who is in Kharkiv. Basically the largest city in the east there. So, Richard, big success for the Ukrainian military in and around Kyiv. Where do we go from here?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Well, massive success from a military perspective pushing out Russian forces from the entire area, the entire region around Kyiv. But as Russian troops pull back, and as you said, they`re saying that this was a withdrawal in order to allow diplomacy to have more success, they were pushed back, and it allowed Ukrainian troops to go in. But what they saw was evidence of atrocities.\par \par \uc2 There were bodies on the street, many of them with their hands tied behind their backs, mass graves. It appears, according to witnesses, the president`s office, the mayor`s office that some of them were shot at close range, execution style. They also described looting and rape. So -- and these are consistent with witness accounts and victims` accounts that we`ve been hearing in other battle locations where there are these -- always seem to be accounts of rape, looting and executions.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Richard, is the Ukrainian military capable of going on offense and truly pushing the Russians out of the east as well?\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: They went on the offense successfully in the north and they are already on the offense here in the east.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:05]\par \par \uc2 Every night for the last several nights here in Kharkiv, we`ve been hearing nonstop artillery fire coming out of this city going into Russian positions in the nearby towns. They`ve already taken one town on the edge of Kharkiv and they plan to take more. They want to go on the offensive now before Russia is able to reposition its troops, to bring some of that extra fire power down from Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 And they also are motivated because they saw what Russia has been doing in places where it has been occupying Ukrainian towns and villages, and they don`t want it to happen to their own homes. Because there is a big difference. These people are fighting to defend their families and their own villages.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, the scenes that we`re seeing in and around Kyiv, we can only imagine that and what we`ve already heard out of Mariupol as well. Anyway, Richard Engel, who is in Kharkiv, the largest city out east. Richard, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And joining me now is Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 . Mr. Secretary, welcome back to MEET THE PRESS.\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: Good morning, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with the news of the Russians pulling back from Kyiv. Focusing appears now on the east where they`ve had a bit more military success. Are we in a new phase of this war?\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: Well, Chuck, we may be. I think this is evidence that Russia`s original plans to take over the whole country, including Kyiv, have been dealt a devastating setback. They are regrouping. They may be focusing on the east. But let`s keep in mind they still have ability to wreak massive death and destruction, including in places like Kyiv, with air power and missiles.\par \par \uc2 And at the same time they may be regrouping. They may be recalibrating. We`re focused on what they`re doing, not what they`re saying. And if it`s a refocus on the east, there is still, you know, a tremendous amount that`s going to be ahead of us. As President Biden said recently, this could go on for some time, and the question is how much death and destruction Russia wreaks in the meantime.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are we going to help Ukraine reinforce sort of now the central and western part of the country, reinforce Kyiv so that essentially the Russians don`t even think about coming back?\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: Chuck, we`re doing that every single day. Just over the course of this administration, we`ve provided more than $2.3 billion in security assistance. In the last month alone, $1.6 billion. The very kinds of things that have made the Ukrainians incredibly effective in dealing with this Russian onslaught, in pushing it back, in setting it back.\par \par \uc2 And look, what we`ve already seen is a strategic setback and maybe even defeat for Russia. Russia had three goals going into this. To subjugate Ukraine to its will, to deny its sovereignty and its independence, to assert Russian power and to divide the west, divide the alliance. And on all three fronts it has failed.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine is now more united. It`s -- a sovereign independent Ukraine is going to be there a lot longer than \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is on the scene. Russian power has actually been vastly diminished, the military has greatly underperformed. Its economy is reeling. And, of course, \uc2 NATO\uc2 , the west are more united than in any time in recent memory. So, on those grounds alone we`ve already seen a dramatic setback for Russia.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You have just described a situation, and Richard Engel was telling me that there are people in Ukraine going, hey, we`ve got Putin in as weak of a spot as we`ve had him in a long time. This is actually not the time to suddenly negotiate a way for him to get out of this.\par \par \uc2 So -- you know, nobody wants to see more war. At the same time, nobody -- I don`t think a lot of people, including the president of the United States, wants to see Putin remain in power after this. So can we really end this with giving Putin some sort of gain in the Donbas?\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: Chuck, how this war ends is up to two things. It`s up to the Ukrainian people and their elected representatives, including President Zelenskyy. We`ll support whatever they want to do in terms of how this war comes to an end. And as to Mr. Putin`s future, that`s up to the Russian people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But do you acknowledge he`s in a weak moment? I mean, is this a moment -- is this a moment that we`re -- if he`s -- if we let him lie and he wreaks havoc again, are we going to regret not taking advantage of this moment?\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: Again, it`s up to, in the first instance, Ukrainians when it comes to what`s going on in Ukraine and Russians when it comes to what`s going on in Russia. And here`s the thing. Even though he`s been set back, even though I believe this is already a strategic defeat for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , the death and destruction that he is wreaking every single day in Ukraine, the images are on our TVs and on social media every single day, are terrible. And so, there`s also a strong interest in bringing those to an end.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to ask about the Zelenskyy -- potential Zelenskyy-Putin face to face in Turkey. How realistic is that? And does President Zelenskyy -- does he have the ability to negotiate sanctions relief with Putin?\par \par \uc2 [09:10:04]\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: So how realistic it is? You know, very hard to say. The Ukrainians have sought a direct engagement with President Putin for President Zelenskyy. President Putin has rejected that repeatedly. We`ll see if he goes forward with anything.\par \par \uc2 But again, our focus is on making sure that we`re doing everything possible to strengthen Ukraine`s hand at the negotiating table, including with a potential meeting between President Zelenskyy and President Putin. And that`s exactly what we`ve been doing.\par \par \uc2 We`ve been supporting Ukraine. We`ve been imposing extraordinary pressure on Russia. We`ve been solidifying our own alliance. All of that goes to strengthening Ukraine`s hand when it comes to any negotiations with Russia.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Again, I want to go back to what is -- can Zelenskyy negotiate sanctions relief at all here or not?\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: The entire international community that has come together to impose those sanctions on Russia will be looking to see what Ukraine is doing and what it wants to do. And if it concludes that it can bring this war to an end, stop the death and destruction and continue to assert its independence and its sovereignty, and ultimately that requires the lifting of sanctions, of course, we`ll have that.\par \par \uc2 The purpose of the sanctions, Chuck, is not to be there indefinitely. It`s to change Russia`s conduct. And as a result of negotiations, the sanctions, the pressure, the support for Ukraine, we achieve just that, then at some point the sanctions will go away. But that is profoundly up to Russia and what it does going forward.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The restrengthening of the ruble, and I understand there is some market manipulation there to do that, but there clearly is some signs that the ruble is strengthened. Obviously Europe -- there are plenty of European countries that continue to pay Russia for oil and gas. Is there more that could be done to tighten -- to tighten these sanctions again? Because it looks like he`s found some ways around them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, Chuck, first, virtually all of the forecasts show Russia`s economy contracting by 10 percent this year. That is dramatic especially at the time when other economies including our own are rebounding dramatically.\par \par \uc2 Second, when it comes to the ruble, it`s more than a little manipulation, it`s a lot of manipulation. People are being prevented from unloading rubles. That`s artificially propping up the value. That`s not sustainable. So I think you`re going to see that change.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen the most extraordinary exodus of virtually every leading business from around the world leaving Russia. Everything from, you know, McDonald`s to Toyota. And that`s having not just an immediate impact, but a long-term impact.\par \par \uc2 And then the export controls that we`ve imposed on Russia, denying it the technology it needs to modernize industry after industry. That`s going to have an increasing impact. Having said that, to your point, we`re working every single day with partners and allies around the world to make sure that we`re tightening the sanctions, closing any loopholes, adding new ones.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Whose side is time on here? If this is a six -- if this is going to drag out another six months, can Ukraine`s military hold up? Can the European alliance hold up? Can Putin survive economically?\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: Chuck, when it comes to the fundamentals, which is to say Putin`s objective in subjugating Ukraine of his will, on denying -- taking away its sovereignty and independence, time is certainly not on Vladimir Putin`s side. Because as I said, "A sovereign independent Ukraine has demonstrated it`s going to be around a lot longer than \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is on the scene."\par \par \uc2 The real question, though, again is, what happens in the meantime? If this goes on, how much death, how much destruction is there? And that is terrible.\par \par \uc2 So we have a strong interest. The Ukrainians have a strong interest in ending that. We`re all working toward that. The way to do that is to give Ukraine the strongest possible hand, to put as much pressure as we possibly can on Russia, while we`re strengthening our own defenses. That`s exactly what we`ve been doing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Secretary \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 , appreciate you coming on and sharing the administration`s perspective.\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: Thanks, Chuck. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And joining me now is Masha Gessen. Gessen is a staff writer for "The New Yorker" and has written extensively about Russia, and individually the rise of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . So if anybody can read Putin`s mind, it may be Masha Gessen. Masha, welcome back to MEET THE PRESS.\par \par \uc2 MASHA GESSEN, STAFF WRITER, THE NEW YORKER: Thank you. Good to be here, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to start with the intelligence that the U.S. released earlier this week, indicating that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  was not getting -- was not getting the truth from his advisers. And while that portion of the intel seemed believable, what`s not believable is that a former KGB agent would somehow be in the dark, unless they`ve chosen to be in the dark. What do you make of these reports?\par \par \uc2 GESSEN: Well, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  was never an extraordinary KGB agent. And he has been a tyrant for longer than he was a KGB agent. Dictators do tend to become isolated, and they become isolated not because they choose to become isolated, although I think that has happened with Putin as well, but because their aides are afraid to be messengers of bad news.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:07]\par \par \uc2 This is clearly what`s happening with Putin. It`s not news. It`s been happening for a long time. And it`s been exacerbated because of his isolation during COVID.\par \par \uc2 But Chuck, I agree with you that, at this point, if Putin is not seeing the footage from Bucha, from the outskirts of Kyiv, where retreating Russian troops have left the bodies of civilians with their hands tied behind their backs littering the streets, where they have left mass graves of civilians with their hands tied behind their backs, anybody who doesn`t see that footage is choosing not to see it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to ask you about negotiating with Putin. And if President Zelenskyy called you up and asked for some advice, what would you say? And before you answer that question, I want to play for you what former Ukrainian President Poroshenko said to me earlier this week on sort of his three rules for negotiating with Putin. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETRO POROSHENKO, FORMER PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE: Please, don`t trust Putin. I am the president who had a negotiation with Putin for five years. He never keeps his words. Point number two, don`t be afraid of Putin. And point number three, Ukraine never give up, and the territorial integrity of Ukraine cannot be the part of any compromise.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: First, good advice? And what would you add?\par \par \uc2 GESSEN: Excellent advice. What I would add is actually not advice, it`s an observation that, you know, \uc2 Petro Poroshenko\uc2 , the last president of Ukraine, and \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  are very different politicians in a country that was fairly polarized before the war, and where people held vastly different political positions depending on whether they supported one or the other. And what we`re seeing is what happens to a country that`s actually united in a true people`s war, where what you see the former president say is probably exactly what the current president would say, and exactly the advice that he should be following.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What should we make of the reports of the polling that came out, I`m sure you saw it over the last couple of days, that indicated Putin`s approval rating has actually gone up? That after what appeared to be some consternation about what he was doing, there is a rally around him effect taking place.\par \par \uc2 And I`m curious, is this because those that are educated are bailing the country and leaving and essentially, those that are left, a la what happened in Venezuela, your Cubas of the world, and those that are left have to support him because they have no other choice?\par \par \uc2 GESSEN: Russia at this point is a totalitarian society. That is, a society where the regime survives by terror. It is meaningless and irresponsible to be conducting opinion polling, and especially to be reporting on opinion polling in that country, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, the question, "Do you support the war," should really contain a second part, which is, "Do you support the war, or would you like to go to prison for 15 years for not supporting the war?" Right?\par \par \uc2 And I want to say something important about totalitarian societies. It`s not that people are hiding what they`re actually thinking. It`s that in conditions of terror people can`t even form their own opinions.\par \par \uc2 TODD: After Putin. First of all, do you have optimism that this -- you know, the history, particularly even in Russia, the history of overreaching has led to the eventual demise of that leader that overreached. Do you foresee that? And if so, what are -- what could happen post-Putin?\par \par \uc2 GESSEN: You know, at this point, when we talk about this apparent military failure bringing about the end of Putin`s regime, we`re engaging in magical thinking. But even more than that, I think this is the kind of self- reassuring thinking that gets us away from thinking about what the United States and the rest of the western world hasn`t done to prevent the kind of crimes against humanity that we have seen evidence of in the last 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 The United States has not supported closing the skies over Ukraine. The United States has not provided major military assistance that Ukraine has asked for. And Western Europe, and to some extent the United States, has not severed its dependence on Russian energy resources, which is the lifeblood of the regime.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So what you`re essentially saying is, until all that happens, let`s not fantasize about the end of Putin?\par \par \uc2 [09:20:05]\par \par \uc2 GESSEN: I think I`m saying let`s not fantasize about the end of Putin, and also let`s pay attention to the West`s responsibility to Ukrainians who are dying right now, instead of fantasizing about what`s going to happen in Russia after it`s over.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Masha Gessen, again, somebody who literally wrote the book on Putin. If you haven`t read it, it is definitely worth a read. Masha, really appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 GESSEN: Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And when we come back, more on Putin. I`m going to talk to former secretary of state and Democratic presidential nominee \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  about the war, Putin, and yes, a little politics, the Democrats` midterm headwinds. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  has a lot to say about \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . As secretary of state, she famously called for a reset in U.S.-Russian relations, but Putin was having none of it. And by the time Clinton left office in 2013, Putin repeatedly attacked her in very personal terms. That animosity may have played a role in Russia`s efforts to help \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in his 2016 campaign against Clinton. Perhaps he feared she`d have a much tougher policy against him. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  joins me now. Madame Secretary, welcome back to MEET THE PRESS.\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: Thanks very much, Chuck. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to pick up on -- I know you were listening to the interview with Masha Gessen. I want to pick up on Masha`s last point there, which seemed to imply that no matter when we`ve confronted Putin, and I could go back really all the way to when he came into power we`ve -- it seems as if we`ve always come up a bit short.\par \par \uc2 We`ve drawn a line not quite as far as others would like to draw you -- whether it was -- you`ve made points of this about Syria. Perhaps we should have drawn a thicker line on what happened in Crimea. Why is that, and are you concerned we`re maybe holding back now a little too much?\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: Well, no. Now I think we are really looking at this with our eyes wide open and seeing very clearly the threat that he poses not just to Ukraine, as we can watch every night on our news, but really to Europe, to democracy and the global stability that we thought we were building in the last 20 years.\par \par \uc2 So, Chuck, I really agree with both what Secretary Blinken said, that we`ve got to continue to keep the pressure on Putin and the Russian troops. We cannot, in any way, pause our efforts to support the Ukrainians. And I agree with Masha that we have to double down. There is more that can be done to increase pressure and stress, additional sanctions, more in the way of lethal aid. And now that there has been a pushback thanks to the brave Ukrainian military offensive action, there is time to resupply the Ukrainians so that they can continue to defend their country.\par \par \uc2 [09:25:07]\par \par \uc2 TODD: You heard Secretary Blinken essentially say, look, sanctions relief could happen. That all depends on the behavior of Russia. Can we really live in a world where Putin`s let back into the new world order?\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: Well, that`s not what I heard him say. What I heard him say was that, really, we are going to support the Ukrainians, the people and the government of Ukraine, as they try to figure out what for them is the best way forward, and that I think is exactly the right position for the United States, Europe and the West and other countries to take.\par \par \uc2 Your second part of that question, though, is a really important one. I would not allow Russia back into the organizations that it has been a part of. I think there`s an upcoming G20 event later in the year. I would not permit Russia to attend. And if they insisted on literally showing up, I would hope there would be a significant, if not total, boycott.\par \par \uc2 The only way that we`re going to end the bloodshed and the terror that we`re seeing unleashed in Ukraine and protect Europe and democracy is to do everything we can to impose even greater costs on Putin. There are more banks that can be sanctioned and taken out of the so-called SWIFT relationship. There is an increasing call for doing more on gas and oil.\par \par \uc2 Now, obviously, some of our strongest allies in Europe are desperately trying to get out from under their dependence upon Russian energy. We need to expedite, and I know the administration has been doing that, looking at more deliveries of liquefied natural gas, for instance.\par \par \uc2 So I think now is the time to double down on the pressure. And I agree completely with Masha. Let`s not fantasize about what comes next in Russia. Let`s focus on what we`re doing right now to help protect and defend the Ukrainian people`s right to be a free democratic nation and protect their sovereignty.\par \par \uc2 TODD Let me ask -- let me talk about the energy and gas front a minute here. One of the easiest ways, perhaps, to relieve some of the pressure on -- look, it`s not just this country that`s experiencing rising energy prices, it`s all of Europe, it`s really a lot of the world. If a country that we provide an extraordinary amount of defensive weaponry to, an extraordinary amount of support to, and I`m speaking of Saudi Arabia, who could essentially turn the dial tomorrow if they wanted to. We know this with OPEC nations. They`re choosing to essentially be on the side of Putin on this one. Should there be some consequences to this in our relationship going forward?\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: Well, I`m disappointed by that decision because I think it`s a very short-term one that is not in anybody`s interests, including theirs. So I would certainly do whatever I could that was available to me to try to be more persuasive, if you will and --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Carrots or sticks?\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: -- to talk about some consequences. But our real -- but our -- well, I think you have to do carrot and stick, Chuck. I mean, I think that we`re in a -- we`re in an existential crisis right now. And the only positive that comes out of this horrific, unjustified, unprovoked assault on Ukraine with the war crimes that are being committed on a daily basis by Putin`s military, is that the world is now, I think, awake. Some are more awake than others, but everybody has to pay attention.\par \par \uc2 The real challenge on the energy front is to do everything we can to help Europe wean itself off. You know, when I was secretary of state, I started something called the U.S.-E.U. Energy Council. And I`ll admit, I had a hard sell with our European friends that I kept saying, "Look what Russia`s already done." Because at that point they`d already used energy as a blunt force for power over Ukraine and Eastern Europe. They`d cut off gas supplies to, you know, force Ukraine and others to do their bidding.\par \par \uc2 And I made the case then, and now I`m delighted that everybody is back focused on this because the more we can get Europe to understand it has to lead the way. Now, there are other problems. We`ve got other big countries, China, India, for example, who are buying Russian energy, who are also, you know, trying to figure out which side to end up on this. So this is -- this is the slow but important work of diplomacy, which Secretary Blinken and others in the administration are pursuing.\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: But I think we need to be putting more sanctions more quickly on more things to up the ante on Russia and to try to bring more countries to the side of seeing that it`s in their interest to support that.\par \par \uc2 [09:30:11]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah. I want to turn to domestic politics here.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic Party is interesting - in an interesting moment that to me looks quite similar to a moment back in 1989 and 1990, you`re familiar with, where the Democrats are having - what does the party stand for? How far left? How far to the center? And you and your husband were on one side of that conversation.\par \par \uc2 Do you see some similarities to that period of Democratic hand-wringing in the early `90s?\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: Well, I don`t know. I do think hand-wringing is part of the Democratic DNA. That seems to be in style whether we`re in or out of power. We`re in power, and there still is hand-wringing going on.\par \par \uc2 From my perspective, President Biden is doing a very good job. I think that his handling of Ukraine, passing the American Rescue package, the huge infrastructure package. I`m not quite sure what the disconnect is between the accomplishments of the administration and this Congress and the understanding of what`s been done and the impact it will have on the American public, and some of the polling and the ongoing hand-wringing.\par \par \uc2 I`ve always thought that the best politics is doing the best job you can do. And there`s a lot that Democrats can talk about in this upcoming midterms. I`m well aware that midterms are obviously always difficult for the party in power. But we`ve got a great story to tell. And we need to get out there and do a better job of telling it.\par \par \uc2 And for those who, you know, say it hasn`t gone far enough, that`s always the chorus in Democratic Party politics.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: But I would add that in Republican Party politics, you have an even greater disconnect. Unfortunately, most of that party has now gone to the, you know, to the extreme and are saying and doing things that have no basis in reality.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve got a good case to make if we get our focus in the right place to do it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m curious, do you think it`s the -- a lot of speculation is that this trouble for President Biden politically started with the chaotic withdrawal in Afghanistan. Do you buy that?\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: I don`t think it helped. I think that is obviously the case. I think that the performance with respect to Ukraine has been excellent.\par \par \uc2 And one of the smartest things that has been done was releasing classified intelligence to prevent the false-flag kinds of operations that Putin was counting on.\par \par \uc2 And also, let`s remember, Putin has been quite effective in impacting public opinion, particularly in the last decade or so, both in the United States and in Europe. And this was largely stymied by releasing that intelligence.\par \par \uc2 So, I think a lot of lessons have been learned. But there is a lot of good accomplishments to be putting up on the board, and the Democrats in office and out need to be doing a better job of making the case.\par \par \uc2 And, frankly, standing up to the other side with their craziness and their calls for impunity and nuttiness that we hear coming from them, I don`t think the average American, frankly, wants to be governed by people who live in a totally different reality.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Former Secretary of State \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , really appreciate you coming on, sharing your respective -- perspective with us. It was good to hear from you.\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, immigration at the southern border has been a big political problem for President Biden. Is it about to get worse?\par \par \uc2 Panel is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:36:51]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Panel is here.\par \par \uc2 NBC News Capitol Hill correspondent Leigh Ann Caldwell, Democratic pollster Cornell Belcher, Republican strategist Brad Todd, and Amy Walter, editor- in-chief and publisher of "The Cook Political Report".\par \par \uc2 All right, I want to start with this Title 42 decision. And Title 42, what does this mean? It was a CDC rule, Leigh Ann, that essentially allowed for Border Patrol to -- anybody who was coming across the border -- to be sent back immediately due to COVID, due to health protocols.\par \par \uc2 We know immigration advocates are like, this is, stop this. This is terrible. You have others that say, well, okay, but we don`t have a plan to do with this in order to lift it now.\par \par \uc2 How thorny is this going to get?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Extremely thorny. And that is an understatement. So people who are running for reelection, especially this is going to be difficult for them. Democrats, you have Senator Mark Kelly in Arizona, \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  in Nevada, two key states as far as immigration is concerned.\par \par \uc2 And Senator Kelly said after this announcement, you did this without a plan. I had been pushing the administration to have a plan for immigration before this is rescinded and they didn`t do that.\par \par \uc2 And I was told that this could be a very huge problem for Democrats. And some Democrats are trying to spin it and saying, well, look, here is one thing that is good. These Democrats can try to differentiate themself from the president and show that they are trying to do what is best for their states.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Wow.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: And that is the only positive.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You pull a muscle with that spin.\par \par \uc2 Amy Walter, I want to put up -- she brought up Mark Kelly, and I had another senator in here, \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, THE COOK POLITICAL REPORT: Hassan, yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me put up here Mark Kelly: Unacceptable to end Title 42 without a plan and coordination.\par \par \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 : Ending Title 42 prematurely will likely lead to a migrant surge.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, we heard from Joe Manchin: Title 42 has been an essential tool.\par \par \uc2 This is a --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Immigration is not just a border issue, right?\par \par \uc2 TODD: No. New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Right, it becomes more --\par \par \uc2 TODD: They`re not worried about Maine.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: They are much -- this is much about the issue about competency, preparedness. You know, you and I -- well, everyone around this table, we watch a lot of campaign ads.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: I`ve been watching a lot of Republican primary ads. And it seems like immigration`s in every one of those ads. So I went back and I asked the folks who cover this --\par \par \uc2 CORNELL BELCHER, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Shocker.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Yes, but more so than ever.\par \par \uc2 So at this point, this is early March, more than 30 percent of all Republican primary ads mention the border or immigration. At this point in 2018, it was 12 percent.\par \par \uc2 It is an issue that is not just animating -- right now, it`s animating the Republican base. But it becomes an issue, as I said, much more about is the administration prepared to deal with the problems that go on their plate?\par \par \uc2 And it just seems that so often with the administration thus far, it is much more about wishcasting than it is preparation. Wishcasting about inflation, right? It`s going to be transitory.\par \par \uc2 Wishcasting that we`re going to get Manchin, we`re going to get Sinema, they`re going to just come around eventually. This immigration isn`t going to solve itself. The border isn`t going to solve itself.\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: Brad, we were talking about this literally I think an hour before the news came out.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:03]\par \par \uc2 I have talked to some officials that said, hey, it`s May 23rd. A new variant may come. And don`t be surprised if May 23rd`s not the real deadline."\par \par \uc2 BRAD TODD, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: Well, Jeh Johnson said on this show on Wednesday that they use it to keep the numbers down. That`s why it`s done.\par \par \uc2 You know, two senators or two Democrat members of Congress stood out to me on this. One was \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 . He`s a Democrat who represents a majority- Hispanic district on the Texas border. He`s in a real primary with a challenge from the left. And he`s encouraging the administration to keep Title 42.\par \par \uc2 The second one was Raphael Warnock. Unlike \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  and Mark Kelly, he said nothing. He said absolutely nothing.\par \par \uc2 And so, the smart Democrats are figuring out that they`ve got to run against 2021 to win in `22. The ones that don`t are going to get beat. And I think Raphael Warnock`s in that group.\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: Cornell, you buy that?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Let`s get real about immigration reform here, right? There`s a reason why that`s in almost every Republican ad, right? And look, we`ve seen this play before, right?\par \par \uc2 This is part of that more fuel for driving the tribalism that Republicans see as part of their political calculus as how they drive this. \uc2 Pew Research\uc2  is fairly clear on this. Dissatisfaction with immigration has been driven by Republicans and they`re feeding this.\par \par \uc2 Not so long ago I remember when a guy named \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  thought he had an immigration deal with the Gang of Eight. But it was in the Republicans` benefit to not have a deal, comprehensive immigration reform, that included border security, that included a pathway.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, most Americans think that you should pay a penalty, and there should be a pathway. And that`s not going to happen. You know why it`s not going to happen? Because Republicans in Congress don`t want it to happen.\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: But you know what`s interesting, Leigh Ann, I had somebody very smart say to me, you take that Gang of Eight bill right now, not only are you not going to get as many Republican supporters as you got, but you won`t get as many Democratic supporters either.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: No, the politics have completely changed on immigration. And that Gang of Eight bill will not pass.\par \par \uc2 That`s why Senator Cornyn, Senator Durbin, a couple others have been talking about immigration for the past many, many months. It is going absolutely nowhere.\par \par \uc2 And the problem is, is because, yes, immigration is a Republican base thing, but once there`s these images of the border being chaotic and people being hurt or killed in the desert because they were trying to be trafficked or the cartels were bringing them, that starts to impact the independent voters and it starts to sow, as you mentioned, a lot of discord.\par \par \uc2 B. TODD: DHS says this summer we may have twice the flow as we had last summer in a campaign year.\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: By the way, we have job openings. There`s an incentive. No, no, no, I mean, this is the thing. Like, there`s real incentives besides concern about their own personal safety.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: But, Chuck, it`s not about -- yes, we should as opposed to sending the Haitians back, how about putting them to work in these jobs that Americans don`t want right now? But that`s not going to happen because we are -- it`s a tribal fear, what`s happening in this\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: Amy, Maria Teresa Kumar reminded me yesterday that DACA recipients are in their mid-30s now. I mean, are we going to be debating DACA recipients when it`s time for Social Security for them?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: They probably will.\par \par \uc2 Right, it`s the easy things that we know could be fixed. But it`s too good of a wedge issue to fix it.\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: All right. I`m going to pause the conversation there.\par \par \uc2 Before we go to break, I want to let you know about our latest episode of our streaming show "Meet the Press Reports". This week, we look at the explosive growth of crypto and whether it becomes currency. NBC News` \uc2 Jo Ling Kent\uc2  reports on what it is, how it works, and whether crypto is a bubble ready to burst.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE]\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT\uc2 , NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: The crypto currency scene is booming. Advertisements dot the skies and fill our screens.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s way too late to try to ban Bitcoin. Everyone who says that Bitcoin should be banned is, in fact, revealing that they don`t understand what Bitcoin actually is.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If I buy Bitcoin, am I buying a share of stock or am I buying a pork belly? Or am I buying euros? What am I buying?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): Or are you buying air?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh wow, okay. You`re going in. What is it?\par \par \uc2 WARREN: I`m going in.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You can see our entire "Meet the Press Reports" story on crypto right now anytime, streaming on Peacock, or you can check it out on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When we come back, could a Supreme Court decision overturning Roe v. Wade change the dynamics of the midterms? Stick with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:47:13]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Data Download time.\par \par \uc2 This summer`s expected Supreme Court ruling on Mississippi`s 15-week abortion ban could result in the landmark decision of Roe v. Wade being struck down. And that could send a major shock to the political system, just as we head to the midterms. The fact of the matter is, there`s never been a lot of support to overturn Roe v. Wade. Over the last 30 years, the "don`t overturn" number according to Gallup has been near 60 percent. The "overturn" number has really never gotten even close to 40 percent.\par \par \uc2 We asked in our current NBC News poll about congressional candidates who are either for keeping Roe v. Wade or against it. Overall, 56 percent said they`re more likely to support a candidate who is for Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Now you think, is there a big gender gap? Actually, not really. Yes, slightly more women than men here. But as you can see, if you support Roe v. Wade the gender gap is not that big.\par \par \uc2 Now, among those who believe Roe v. Wade should be overturned, if you`re a candidate for Congress, that`s not a very popular thing to have, 58 percent said they`d be less likely to support a candidate who advocates overturning Roe. And again, not much of a gender gap here. Slightly more women saying that they wouldn`t at 63 percent, but still a majority of men say they would be less likely, too.\par \par \uc2 And if you`re wondering how this plays in the battleground states -- not well at all, 57 percent said they`d be less likely.\par \par \uc2 And look at this: This is a real potential problem for Republicans here, 75 percent of Independents say they would be less likely to support somebody who advocates overturning Roe.\par \par \uc2 Let`s see what the Supreme Court does. If they overturn it, this could enter in a whole new reset to the 2022 midterms.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, was it a mistake to take \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  off of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ? The answer may not be as obvious as you think.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:52:37]\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump is off \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , but he certainly hasn`t been quiet lately. In just the past couple of weeks, Mr. Trump urged \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to turn over dirt on President Biden`s son, Hunter. Now we`re at war with the guy.\par \par \uc2 He criticized \uc2 NATO\uc2  again, even as \uc2 NATO\uc2  has actually been more united than ever in the Ukraine issue.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, he went after Judge \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , claiming that she was disrespectful to Republican senators.\par \par \uc2 And last night, apparently called for going back into Afghanistan, which is, of course, going to be extraordinarily politically popular.\par \par \uc2 But it leads me to this question. And I`m going to start with you, Amy, which is this: does the general public know what Trump`s been doing?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: No.\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: And should they? And I sit here and it`s like, this is a reminder. Anytime you censor, even for the right reasons, do you get the unintended consequence?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: So if you look at the intensity around Democratic feelings about this election, or how engaged they are, much lower than 2020. And I hear this a lot in focus groups. I`m sure you guys hear this as well.\par \par \uc2 What Democrats say is after Trump left office, as soon as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was sworn in, they turned off the television, right? They don`t need to be on alert with their device and with watching the news all the time. He`s not there anymore.\par \par \uc2 Democrats want him to be there because he is the best motivational operation. He is their get-out-to-vote operation. The more he`s in front of us, the more people see him, the more that they can make the case that this election is not a referendum on Biden, but is a choice between a Trump-like Congress and the alternative. But he`s just not -- while he`d like to be, he is not in the front of the minds, even of the people who don`t like him.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, you know, Brad, I think this has become, I guess I would -- you could argue politically, is it the best of both worlds for your elected Republican? Because you`re not feeling the mainstream media pressure because it`s not in your face every day, and they don`t feel the discomfort constantly, which one could argue maybe something that these folks should. But they don`t feel the discomfort and they don`t have to cringe at the Trump thing, and they just ignore it. If it were more in the mainstream, would there be more elected Republicans having a Trump problem?\par \par \uc2 B. TODD: You know, Donald Trump`s main function in the Republican coalition is sort of as the testosterone booster, right? Our voters turn to him when they think other Republicans are not being confrontational enough for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 That`s not happening right now. The Republican Party is pretty united in opposing the administration on ideological grounds, and so the Republican base, I think, is not in turmoil. And when it`s not in turmoil, it doesn`t need an agitator. And so I think that that`s driving a lot of it.\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: You think his shtick`s wearing a little thin, too? It`s just repetitive?\par \par \uc2 B. TODD: No, I think the fact that he`s not spending all of his time posting up against the administration. If he were doing that, he would be seen as more relevant to the Republican base, because he talks a lot about 2020, that`s not where the base is focused right now. They`re focused on 2022.\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: Cornell, would Biden`s numbers be five points higher if he were on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  -- Trump were on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: You know, it`s funny that you say that. And one of the things I pick up in focus groups among Democrat voters is almost Trump has set us up. And he was in your face every day, all day. You always knew what was going on in Washington.\par \par \uc2 You know, Hillary said there`s a disconnect between what the administration`s been accomplishing and what American people know. And to your point, they`ve -- they`ve turned it off.\par \par \uc2 Trump has, I think, reset the game for politics in that, I think, you will suffer if you are not dominating social media. You will suffer if you`re not constantly taking your message to the people 24 hours a day. It is sad, but I think he has broken the system in a way that if you don`t follow in that path of constantly being on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , constantly being on social media, you`re failing. And I think that`s sad for politics.\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: Leigh Ann, do Republican electeds take comfort in the fact that he`s not? Yeah?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: You deal with him.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: You know I deal with him every day.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: So a Republican told me yesterday that they described the latest Trump statements as white noise. They don`t even really know what they are anymore. So they`re not really paying attention.\par \par \uc2 And this past week was a return of having to, for the first time in a very long time, having to ask a Republican what they thought about Trump.\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: Because of the Putin stuff.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Because of the Putin stuff. And we have not had to do that on Capitol Hill for a year and a half.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Two quick things, though. If -- you say that, but if he runs, he`s going to be the nominee. I`ll fight you, Brad, he`s going to be your nominee. If he runs, he`s going to be nominee, but also --\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: I don`t buy that he runs.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Why wouldn`t he run? It`s a money-generating --\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: It`s work.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Generate money, publicity, that`s what he`s for.\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: It`s work.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Fair. The other thing is, is that Trump`s a quintessential threat to our democracy. And whether you`re a Democrat or you`re a Republican, it was right for them to pull back. And I don`t want to play politics with it because he was a threat.\par \par \uc2 B. TODD: It is dead-level proof of how bad this administration`s political situation is that the number one thing they could hope for is another president from another administration coming back. Like they can`t talk about the situation or how they`re going to solve it, and that tells you why this is going to be 2006, frankly.\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: Well, it`s funny you say that. I agree with that because, Amy, I think the most fascinating thing about our poll is there`s not buyer`s remorse. People are not happy with Biden, but they don`t say, "Give me Trump."\par \par \uc2 WALTER: No.\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: They`ve disaggregated Trump from the GOP more than the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: More easily than Democrats would like to see it.\par \par \uc2 And the other fundamental challenge is that people voted against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  more than they voted for Biden. And that has been the problem for Democrats all along. They`ve been the party of who we aren`t, not the party of who we are.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Really quickly, Trump can`t solve our problems. We`ve got to do a better job of branding, especially with young people.\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: We`ll stop it right there.\par \par \uc2 That`s all we have for today. Thank you for watching. We`ll be back next week, because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020220403ei4300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 49th Anniversary Of The Historic Supreme Court Decision Roe Versus Wade That Gave Women Nationwide The Right To Have An Abortion\b0\par\par\uc2 Kristen Welker, Peter Alexander, Ali Vitali \par \uc2 3,483 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:40:19] KRISTEN WELKER: This morning, we`re going in depth on the 49th anniversary of that historic Supreme Court decision Roe versus Wade that gave women nationwide the right to have an abortion with a Supreme Court decision over its fate looming, both advocates and opponents are preparing for a future without it now.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER: And on Friday, anti-abortion activists gathered in Washington as they do each year to protest Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ali Vitali is here with more with what people on both sides of this issue are saying. Ali, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good morning, guys. Peter and Kristen, the whole country is waiting for the Supreme Court to decide on one of the most serious challenges to abortion protections that it`s heard since the Roe versus Wade decision nearly 50 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Now, groups on both sides of this battle are looking ahead to what comes next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voice over): On what could be Roe`s final anniversary as the law of the land, Yamelsie Rodriguez is preparing for a future without it.\par \par \uc2 YAMELSIE RODRIGUEZ, CEO, PLANNED PARENTHOOD OF THE ST. LOUIS REGION AND SOUTHWEST MISSOURI: We know firsthand what a post-Roe reality looks like because we live that reality every single day.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Her State is one of more than two dozen across the U.S. that have sought restrictions on abortion access, forcing patients to look elsewhere for care, oftentimes having to cross state lines to get it.\par \par \uc2 For thousands of women in Missouri, that means a trip to neighboring Illinois and growing logistical concerns.\par \par \uc2 RODRIGUEZ: People who have the means have always have and will always continue to have access to abortion. But the people that we are concerned about is the people that do not have the financial means. Do I pay the rent and feed my kids or do I save up all of this money in order to access abortion care?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: This weekend, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and their local partners are opening a new regional logistics center to ease that burden on patients, providing things like transportation, food, lodging, and cash assistance.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, abortion opponents nationwide are gaining strength. In just the past year, they introduced nearly 600 abortion restrictions and were able to pass more than 90 into law.\par \par \uc2 To counter it, reproductive advocates are channeling their resources into the states while the Supreme Court weighs whether or not to strike down or fundamentally alter the protections offered by Roe.\par \par \uc2 Is that what you guys are attempting to do then? You shore up the states where access is available to make up for the states where it`s not?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2 , CEO, PLANNED PARENTHOOD FEDERATION OF AMERICA: Yes, absolutely. We`re focusing on bolstering healthcare operations in regions that are going to be critical access points for patients.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Reproductive rights groups are also focused on expanding access to telehealth medication abortion for pregnancies up to 10 weeks. Another move to broaden access and limit the need for travel.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Friday, anti-abortion activist took to the national mall for the annual March for Life. Hopeful that when they gather at this time next year, Roe versus Wade will have been overturned.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WAGGONER, GENERAL COUNSEL, ALLIANCE DEFENDING FREEDOM: Prayers you`ve never prayed before that the Supreme Court will have the moral courage to do what is right.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: The high court`s verdict expected to come next June would impact nearly half of U.S. women of reproductive age about 36 million. Another milestone for a divisive and emotional issue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And Ali, clearly this is a very divisive issue. Where do Americans right now stand on it?\par \par \uc2 VITALI (on camera): Yes, Peter, a new poll shows that two thirds of Americans are against the court completely overturning Roe. And that if it were to be overturned, 59 percent say they`d want to see their state set more permissive abortion laws.\par \par \uc2 That said, another 40 percent said they`d want more restrictive legislation in their states. Once again, that clear stark divide, one that could play out at the peak of midterm season, Peter, Kristen.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Something we are going to watch very closely. Ali Vitali, great report on a really important issue. Thank you. Good to see you here in person.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Thanks, Ali.\par \par \uc2 We want to bring it in our friend Joe Fryer for a look at what is ahead this morning in POPStart. Joe, nice to see you this morning.\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Hi, good to see you. Coming up, and baby makes three. New details about Nick Jonas and Priyanka Chopra`s new bundle of joy, that`s after these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:47:08]\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We`re back on a Saturday morning with TODAY`s POPStart. "NBC NEWS NOW" anchor Joe Fryer is here with that. Joe, good morning.\par \par \uc2 FRYER (on camera): Hi, good morning. So, first up, a special delivery from the stork, it`s a Jonas. Nick Jonas and Priyanka Chopra just celebrated their third wedding anniversary. And now, they`re new parents.\par \par \uc2 Nick and Priyanka shared the happy news on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  writing: We are overjoyed to confirm that we have welcomed a baby via surrogate. No word yet on the baby`s name or gender but we send our congratulations to the Jonas family. Good for them, right?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: We are so excited for them. That`s so sweet. Good for them for saying it was delivered via surrogacy. And maybe we can have that rattle sent to them, Joe.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And maybe if it`s -- I mean, maybe it`s a girl, maybe it`s the Jonas sisters.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Maybe it`s a Jonas sisters, exactly. Get a new band together.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: There you go, a whole new generation.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`re going to keep the baby theme going here. So, we know that Prince William and Kate already have three kids. So, what are the odds that they`d grow their family and have a fourth child?\par \par \uc2 Well, I think we got our answer when the royal couple met with staff and volunteers at a charity in Northwest England that focuses on homelessness and mental health support. That`s when a little baby named Anastasia caught the Duchess` eye.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PRINCE WILLIAM, DUKE OF CAMBRIDGE: Don`t give my wife any more ideas. You can`t take her with you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Sounds like George, Charlotte and Louis should not be expecting a younger sibling anytime soon. Is that the impression you guys got from that one?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: No, Princess Anastasia I guess but you know, you could see William like a lot of dads, like, all right, let`s go this way.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes, the baby kind of had the same impression.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Exactly, exactly. So, I think we got our answer there.\par \par \uc2 All right. finally, all right, all aboard. Why is that? Well, Pete Davidson and Colin Jost, they`re making waves not just on SNL, they bought a boat, a big boat, bigger than this one.\par \par \uc2 The comedians are Staten Island royalty. Now, they`re the proud co-owners of a retired Staten Island ferry boat. They`re part of a larger group of New Yorkers who won the ferry in an auction and hope to turn it into an arts and entertainment venue.\par \par \uc2 And while you`re waiting for that ship to sail, you can catch Davidson and Jost on "SATURDAY NIGHT LIVE" tonight.\par \par \uc2 There`s a brand-new episode with former cast member Will Forte hosting and musical guests Maneskin and you can catch SNL tonight right here on NBC.\par \par \uc2 And that is your POPStart and I have to say, this is actually a pretty cool toy we found. I think Margot might like this one.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Oh, she would love that, Joe.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Wow.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That`s very cool. Nice hat. I have to say, it brings a whole new meaning to party boat.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: I was going to say maybe Kim Kardashian will be serving bar there. Joe, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Still to come, why this brand-new toy means so much to his happy little boy. We`re back right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:52:17]\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Still to come right here on TODAY, Tom Brady, Aaron Rodgers, the rest of the NFL`s top quarterbacks facing off this weekend as they dream of the Super Bowl. We`ll get you ready for the weekend`s big games.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Plus, why Jimmy Fallon had a little bit of fun with our friend Somara this week. It`s a good one, but first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:56:53]\par \par \uc2 KATHERINE UNDERWOOD, NBC 10 BOSTON, ANCHOR: Good morning, everyone. 7:56 on this Saturday, you`re watching NBC 10 Boston, thanks for being with us. I`m Katherine Underwood.\par \par \uc2 Listen, if you haven`t gone outside yet, we`ll tell you this. It is a chilly start to the weekend. You got to bundle up if you`re heading out.\par \par \uc2 Let`s check in with meteorologist Tania Leal with your First Alert Weather, a pretty shout out there at least, Tania.\par \par \uc2 LEAL: Yes, Katherine. Yes, it is very beautiful cloudy though into the Boston area because now we`ve got the wind speeds picking up just a bit more. And that`s allowing for a further increase in spread of those snow flurries making their effect from the ocean snow effect into Gloucester all the way towards Provincetown.\par \par \uc2 Now, looking into Providence, notice that Northern Rhode Island and portions of Connecticut are starting to get just a bit of flurries out there. The live activity will continue through the early afternoon. Boston`s being affected right now, Salem, Gloucester.\par \par \uc2 Temperatures will be reaching the upper 20s this afternoon. And we`ll be having more of a cloud increase as we head into tomorrow. Temperatures will be rising and we`ll be talking about our snow chances on NBC 10 Boston.\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: Tania, thank you.\par \par \uc2 As always, we are following the latest trends in the COVID-19 pandemic here in Massachusetts. Cases continue to trend downward.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, there were nearly 14,000 newly confirmed cases, but the seven- day average is just under 9,000. The average positivity rate is at 13.7 percent down from a peak of 23 percent.\par \par \uc2 Right now, police in Linn are looking for the driver who hit a 3-year-old boy and just took off. That boy is recovering at Boston Children`s Hospital. This happened Thursday night on Bulfinch Street. If you were in that area or you saw anything, you should give police a call.\par \par \uc2 All right, the \uc2 Bruins\uc2  play today at the Garden welcoming the \uc2 Winnipeg Jets\uc2 . The Bees will be without Brad Marchand though after he was hurt following a really hard hit in Thursday`s game against Washington.\par \par \uc2 Right now, no timeline on Marchand`s return. The puck drops today at 3:00.\par \par \uc2 Hey, thank you so much for watching NBC 10 Boston, we`ll be back in about a half hour.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:00:19] PETER ALEXANDER, NBC HOST (voice-over): Good morning. Frigid forecast. The southeast cleaning up this morning after another storm dumped several inches of snow in places and covered roads with ice. A plane even skidding off the runway in North Carolina and now millions from the deep south to the northeast are dealing with brutally cold temperatures. We`re tracking all of it.\par \par \uc2 Notebook confession. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  revealing writings found alongside Brian Laundrie`s body in which the 23-year-old admits to killing his fiancee Gabby Petito last year. Authorities now disclosing new details of that massive manhunt.\par \par \uc2 And clash of the quarterbacks. \uc2 Blockbuster\uc2  matchups in store this weekend, as the NFL`s top passer prepare to air it out for a ticket to the Super Bowl. Legends like Brady and Rodgers, and newcomers like Allen and Burrow ready to take the field starting later today, Saturday, January 22nd, 2022.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Hi, I`m Mira from Maryland.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m Ana from D.C.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And I`m Amanda from \uc2 Boston University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And we all love the TODAY show.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Congrats on 70 years.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And thank you for all you do.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Hi, I`m Michael from Long Island, New York, and there`s no better way to start my day than watching the TODAY show.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Good morning. We`re the Gonzalez family from Salem, West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ FAMILY: We love you, TODAY Show.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hi, Nana Grandma and Paul.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC HOST: Thanks to all of your plaza friends for starting their day with us and good morning and welcome back to TODAY on a Saturday morning. We`re here in our studio. You can feel the cold air coming through the windows. It`s just so chilly out.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, I was going to say, thank goodness we`re not on the other side of the windows.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Where it feels like right now in our area six degrees. That`s where we begin things today. The latest round on this winter weather. And it is frigid. Impacting folks up and down the East Coast. Icy road conditions in Carolina. A bitter blast of cold for millions.\par \par \uc2 Somara Theodore is watching it all for us this morning. Somara, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SOMARA THEODORE, METEOROLOGIST: Good morning. This is the type of weather that just looks better from indoors. Trust me on it. OK? When you take a look at these numbers downright cold. We`ve got this frigid air mass that`s moved into the northeast. Zero degrees, it`s zero degrees right now in Pittsburgh. Five-degree windchill in Washington, D.C. Minus 5 in Buffalo. (INAUDIBLE), Burlington, minus 14 degrees is what it feels like out there. That`s the actual temperature that way.\par \par \uc2 So it is a cold start, but it`s not just the northeast. Yes, you`re going to get a little bit of this, too, as you head down into the southeast and areas like Lake Charles, right into New Orleans. We`re looking at temperatures at or below 32 degrees. In fact some areas of Mississippi could be at 20 degrees this morning. We`ve got freeze warnings in effect.\par \par \uc2 And if it`s not the cold we`re talking, it`s the snow. So we had that storm system move out from the East Coast and we have another one that`s getting ready to impact the Great Lakes region. An Arctic front is bringing snow to the Great Lakes. We will see this through today. Rounds of snow making their way, even out towards the East Coast in the mid-Atlantic region, we could see a few flurries tomorrow evening. And that`s Sunday night.\par \par \uc2 As far as totals go, on the lower end widespread, one to four inches. But you get closer to the lake lines there in Grand Rapids, out towards Cleveland, you could see six to eight inches of snow. Peter?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Going to make it interesting when the Packers host the Niners tonight in Green Bay. Somara, thanks.\par \par \uc2 We`ll give you your full forecast for the rest of the country coming up in a moment.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And now we do want to get to the very latest on the coronavirus where there is some hopeful news now that we are on our way out of this latest wave. Health experts say they`re starting to see signs it is beginning to peak in some places. But hospitals out west are still inundated by omicron.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Kathy Park is outside of a hospital in New York with more on this.\par \par \uc2 Kathy, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 KATHY PARK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Kristen, good morning to you. We are starting to see numbers trend in the right direction, especially in portions of the northeast, including New York. COVID cases dropped 66 percent in just the past two weeks and just Friday the positivity rate fell under 10 percent. Something that we haven`t seen in quite some time.\par \par \uc2 And while we are starting to see these improvements, the rest of the country not necessarily on the same track. For example, in St. Louis, Missouri, some hospitals there are being inundated with COVID patients and dealing with staffing shortages so they are asking for federal assistance. Meanwhile, in San Diego, California, they`re dealing with an Omicron surge and they`re leaning on the help of field hospitals.\par \par \uc2 But there is some hopeful news released from the CDC yesterday when it comes to boosters. They are 90 percent effective at Omicron hospitalizations. But it is important to note that roughly 86 million Americans are eligible for these boosters, but less than half of the population has received that extra dose.\par \par \uc2 Kristen, back to you.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right. Kathy Park in New York. Thank you, Kathy.\par \par \uc2 [08:05:00]\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And some new details this morning in the investigation into the death of Gabby Petito. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  announced Friday that Petito`s fiance Brian Laundrie admitted killing her in a notebook discovered near his body in a Florida swamp. Investigators also confirmed that Laundrie had sent text messages to intentionally deceive people that the 22-year-old was still alive after he strangled her in late August. Petito`s body was found three weeks later in a Wyoming campground.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, we also want to tell you about a frightening close call in Florida this week that was caught on camera. Check out this Ring camera video. It happened very quickly. So you have to watch closely. A man out on his patio bravely pushes back a black bear who jumps over a small gate to attack his dog. Look at that. Walter Hickox had been hanging out with his dogs when the bear came dangerously close to them all.\par \par \uc2 Hickox told our NBC affiliate WESH that he suffered a few puncture wounds but did not need an ambulance. Those were some fast reflexes there.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Just a few puncture wounds. You said it happened quickly. That was way too --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Can you imagine that guy`s experience?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes. Any amount of time is too long. That`s a good point.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes. Staring down a bear. Time for a weekend morning boost which we could use right now.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Joe is back with that.\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, yes, so when it comes to gifts, the more personal presents are often the best, and one boy`s parents hit a home run with this one.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Wait, wait, wait. Wait, wait, wait, wait. Just a second. Wait, just a second. I`m going to --\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Yes, the boy just realized the stuffed toy looks familiar. He hurries back to his bedroom and then jumps for joy and screams when he sees why the stuffy looks just like one of his own drawings. A Pokemon character that he created. Other parents replied to this viral message showing the toys they had made from their children`s artwork and another parent commented, "Thanks for making the rest of us parents look bad."\par \par \uc2 Definitely raises the bar for parents everywhere there.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: That is the coolest stuffed animal idea ever, right, to take your kids` art. We`ve got it all over the walls, believe me, and turning it into something special that they can hold on to.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I was going to say, and he`s a pretty good artist, that little boy. That`s impressive.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The Pokemon folks are paying attention to that.\par \par \uc2 Joe, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead right here, Brady, Mahomes, Rodgers, you name them. The NFL quarterback ready to lay it all on the line this weekend.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Who will make it to the Super Bowl just a few weeks away now. We`re back just after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220124ei1m000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 TODAY SHOW for December 11, 2021, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Kristen Welker, Peter Alexander, Morgan Chesky, Somara Theodore,  Monica Alba, Emilie Ikeda, Pete Williams, Cecy Del Carmen, Jeff Saperstone,  Joe Fryer, Kathy Park \par \uc2 8,019 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 December 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC ANCHOR: Good morning. Breaking overnight:\par \par \uc2 Deadly tornadoes. Devastating twisters strike from Tennessee to Illinois, Missouri, Arkansas and Kentucky leaving a historic trail of destruction in its path. Around 50 people fear dead in Kentucky, many of them at a factory where the roof collapse.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It is tragic. Very hard. Really tough.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: We`re live with the very latest on the carnage and to forecast for the storm that`s not done yet. Now headed for the East Coast this morning.\par \par \uc2 Rising pain. A new government report finds groceries and gas prices more expensive than they have been in nearly 40 years.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We`re paying more for things they need than they had to pay before. That`s the bottom line.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: The president saying this is the peak and he hopes prices will go back down soon. But for the millions of Americans struggling to get by, will it be fast enough?\par \par \uc2 Soaring COVID cases. Health officials across the country sounding the alarm. New York`s Governor enacting an indoor mass mandate in an effort to keep residents in the state safe from COVID.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): And we could either flat toe here or cases could escalate beyond control.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: As the CDC releases some hopeful new information about the Omicron variant. All that, plus remembering One of the Monkeys.\par \par \uc2 Fans from around the world humming some of Michael Nesmith`s most popular songs this morning after The Monkey singer and guitarist passed away at the age of 78.\par \par \uc2 We`ll look back on his life and musical legacy today, Saturday, December 11, 2021.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News, this is TODAY with Peter Alexander, Kristen Welker and Dylan Dreyer.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Welcome to today and thank you so much for joining us on this Saturday morning. And Peter, we are waking up to a morning of really tragic breaking news.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC ANCHOR: Yes, it`s been awful news from overnight, a terrible night in the Southeast, in the Midwest, with these tornadoes leaving what is really an unfathomable amount of destruction behind. This morning, of course, our thoughts are with everybody in that area and with their loved ones there. This is what we know at this hour: Kentucky`s governor has declared a state of emergency in his state and speaking at a news conference just a couple of hours ago said at least 50 people could be dead in Kentucky alone. The highest death toll right now appears to be coming from a candle factory in Mayfield in Western Kentucky. There are several other states also reporting deaths at this hour. At least one person died at a nursing home in Arkansas where the roof collapsed.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: One of the eight tornado emergencies issued overnight came from a rare quad state tornado which means that one tornado, just one, spanned four states and traveled more than 200 miles: in Arkansas, Missouri, Tennessee and Kentucky. And there are fears that this deadly weather may not be over yet. We are going to speak to Kentucky`s governor in just a moment but we do want to begin with NBC`s Morgan Chesky who has been tracking the devastation overnight. Morgan, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, NBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Peter and Kristen, good morning and it will be a devastating day for so many communities following this massive storm outbreak. You mentioned Mayfield, Kentucky, the governor they`re saying at least 100 people were inside that factory when a tornado struck and 12 people may have perished at that one business alone. Meanwhile, the extent of this devastation only expected to rise with daybreak.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: More than 100 people were inside a candle factory when the tornado ripped through Mayfield, Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And it blew the house away. The only thing standing was the bathroom and the hall.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  believes at least a dozen people could be lost at that business alone.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): It`s very hard. Really tough. And we`re praying for each and every one of those families.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: Now, Kentucky officials believe this storm is one of the most severe in the state`s history.\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: We believe our death toll from this event will exceed 50 Kentuckians, probably end up closer to 70 to 100 lost lives.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: The powerful Twister demolished the (INAUDIBLE) Nursing Home in Arkansas, trapping 20 people inside overnight, at least one person is reported dead and five seriously injured. A massive emergency response rushed to the site and were able to bring the rest of the residents out safely. A second nursing home in Truman was damaged, power lines down there, the roads still blocked.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:19]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The nursing home is hit. The fire department`s been hit.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: Still, more damage in Leachville as the twister cut 100-mile-long path diagonally through Northeast Arkansas. Powerful wind swept through Illinois too, shredding an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse and trapping dozens of workers in Edwardsville. The fire department reports multiple injuries, anxious relatives fought through the storm to reach the sea.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, it was lightning bad, you know we lost power for about two hours now and then my phone died. So, it`s like, I have no idea how to get ahold of them. I have no idea what`s going on and I`m -- we`re just worried sick.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: And search and rescue operations are ongoing as we speak in multiple communities. The power of this storm incredibly clear, debris that was part of that Mayfield Tornado we`ve seen lifted up as high as 30,000 feet in the air. Peter and Kristen, we`ll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: One more staggering figure in a morning of so many and just an unbelievable death toll. Morgan Chesky. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We want to bring in Kentucky Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 . Governor, good morning to you and we want to start by expressing our deepest condolences to you and the people of your state.\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Thank you. We are hurting this morning. Once dawn breaks, we`re going to get a better idea of just how staggering this loss is. As we sit here right now, we know our death toll is going to exceed 50. Probably going to be closer to 70 to 100, and those are children of God irreplaceable in their community. So, a real tough morning in Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And Governor if you can give us a sense with the death toll that`s already staggering here, can you even tally how many people are still unaccounted for at this hour this morning?\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: No, we can`t because this happened in the dead of night. It is in dozens of counties that either one of these four tornadoes touched down and or we had real severe weather. I`ve been in our emergency operations center since about 1:00 a.m. in real time hearing, the family trapped in their basement, the factory that collapsed on so many individuals, my dad`s hometown. I know we`re going to have multiple casualties there and still trying to get family on the phone. So, morning will show us a whole lot more. At least one of our towns almost totally decimated, but we`re strong. We`re resilient. We will grieve, but we will rebuild.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Governor, help us to understand where the bulk of these fatalities may have been? You talked about that candle factory in Mayfield, presumably people working overnight as we get closer to the holidays. Is that where you think most of these fatalities you said possibly even 100 may have occurred?\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Well, that will be, we believe, the largest site, the largest place of loss. Graves County, which that`s in, in Mayfield, the city, lost people in multiple locations. This was part of a tornado that was on the ground for more than 200 straight miles in Kentucky, something we`ve never seen, but we have reports of lost life and more than five Kentucky counties. So, this thing is like something we have never seen before. Certainly that that candle factory was a difficult place. Dawson Springs, Kentucky, multiple losses. Marshall County, close by. Warren County, Bowling Green, we`ve lost too, so it`s going to be a hard day, all day. But I know that we will wrap our arms around those families will grieve together, and then we`ll move forward.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Governor, can you give us a sense of how much warning Kentuckians had that this massive tornado was coming?\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Well, we had warning that a tornado was coming, that we were going to have strong storms. But listen, we have strong storms a lot. In Kentucky, we flood 52 weeks a year. But we`ve never seen a tornado that touches down through four states, 227 plus miles and goes through the very middle of multiple towns. So, you can be prepared, people can know and it still be absolutely devastating.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And Governor, we can hear the emotion in your voice. We heard you say that you still have not been able to contact some of your own family members. Can you talk about the emotion, the weight this morning for all of you and the search and rescue efforts that are still underway?\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Oh, it`s hard. It is incredibly difficult. And to know that there are people who need help, that we have to clear the roads just to get to, why declared a state of emergency we`ve called out the National Guard, our Transportation Cabinet has their heaviest equipment out, clearing those roads. Division of Forestry cutting down the -- or chopping up the, the trees that are across our roadways. We`ve got EMS strike teams that are headed there; two tractor trailers full of water and, and the moment that the sky is clear enough, I`m going to be there too.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:35]\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  of the state of Kentucky. We`re thinking about you and we will be thinking with you, of you over the course of this day. Thanks so much for your time today.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Our condolences and thoughts and prayers to all of them. The tornado struck another five states overnight and that threat continues this morning. So, with that, we do want to turn to Somara Theodore for more on these storms and where they are headed next, Somara.\par \par \uc2 SOMARA THEODORE, NBC METEOROLOGIST: Yes, you know I want to put it into perspective. It`s Saturday morning, you have folks sleeping in. They still got another hour in the dark in parts of Tennessee and Kentucky and it`s the month of December. So, tornadoes may not be the first thing on their mind. I want you to call any friends and family you have that live in central Tennessee up into Kentucky in parts of Northern Alabama and just give them a heads up.\par \par \uc2 We`ve even got a tornado warning right now happening in Williamsburg Kentucky there and that runs right along I-75, five million people are waking up this morning to the threat of possible tornadoes stretching from Kentucky right on down through Tennessee in areas like Huntsville, Alabama. Now, this is the line of storms that wreaked havoc last night. They`re now moving and shifting as we head throughout the day towards the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 Now, these storms are going to stretch from the Gulf Coast to New England with the greatest threat for severe weather impacting areas like the Mid Atlantic down into the Carolinas this afternoon. And currently into areas like Columbus, Cincinnati parts of Central Kentucky as we just saw, they`re right on through Chattanooga and into Northern Alabama. 60 mile per hour wind gusts.\par \par \uc2 Tornadoes are still possible as we head throughout the day and the evening. So, that`s something we`re keeping an eye on. And the winds, especially, are going to be dangerous, because we`re looking at up to 120 million people impacted by strong winds today from two different storms. And we`ll talk about that West Coast storm a little later.\par \par \uc2 Focusing mainly on the East Coast, we could see 70 mile per hour wind gusts as we head throughout this afternoon. And that can be dangerous as well because we`re looking at power outages down trees through tonight. Wind advisories stretching from the Great Lakes region through the mid-Atlantic right on up into the New York Tri-State Area down into Nashville and areas like Northern Alabama, as far as when this is finally going to let up when we`re going to get some reprieve.\par \par \uc2 Not until tomorrow. By tomorrow. This clears out high pressure moves in and we`re quieting down. But the storm is not over yet. We still have to get through today. Again, timing on the East Coast, seeing that as we head through the afternoon and evening hours. So, things are about to get active even here in D.C., Kristen.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right, well, I know you`ll continue to track it, Somara. Thank you so much for that. And we will have much more on our next half hour including a conversation with a woman who had been trapped inside that candle factory that we were just talking about and she was able to make it out.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We have some other news to get to this morning. And the White House is acknowledging what most Americans are already feeling: the prices that we pay for everything from gas to groceries and a whole lot more are now at their highest point in decades. NBC`s Monica Alba is at the White House this morning with more on the worst inflation numbers in nearly 40 years. Monica, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MONICA ALBA, NBC CORRESPONDENT: Peter, good morning. The White House was already bracing for tough numbers. But the reality of soaring inflation has created economic and political fallout for the Biden administration as millions of Americans see their everyday expenses go up just weeks before Christmas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALBA: This morning, inflation rates not seen in nearly 40 years. Consumer prices up 6.8 percent in November compared to last year.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Inflation is affecting people`s lives.\par \par \uc2 ALBA: Gas, rent, used and new cars, all more expensive. With meat, poultry, fish and eggs up by one percent; pork by 2.2 percent. President Biden acknowledging the pain people are feeling.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: They`re paying more for things they need than they had to pay before. That`s the bottom line.\par \par \uc2 ALBA: While predicting costs will go down soon as supply chain backlogs ease.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It`s not gone down quickly enough. But I think it will\par \par \uc2 ALBA: The President making the case for his massive social spending and climate plan to bring down costs for things like child care, health care and prescription drug prices. But that legislation is far from a done deal with Senator Joe Manchin of West Virginia, pointing to high inflation as a reason for the government to limit its spending, not add to it.\par \par \uc2 President Biden`s scheduled to speak with him next week. As GOP lawmakers uniformly oppose the proposal. The Senate`s top Republican blasting Democrats for "More borrowing, more printing, more reckless spending," saying inflation is out of control on the Democrat`s watch. The challenge, all too real for Louis Hankins, Owner of El Teatro Restaurant in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 LOUIS HANKINS, OWNER, EL TEATRO RESTAURANT: We can survive something in the short term for sure. But is this going to continue through the next three months or six months?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [07:15:24]\par \par \uc2 ALBA: Now, the White House can`t see for sure how long rising inflation will last, with experts predicting it will go into next year. But senior officials point to gas prices already coming down in about 20 states with hopes the economic picture will continue to improve as the country navigates its way out of the COVID pandemic. Peter and Kristen.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Monica Alba at the White House this morning. Monica, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, with Christmas exactly two weeks away, health officials are getting more concerned about a COVID surge around the holidays. Today, the number of cases is expected to hit 50 million. And by the end of the weekend, COVID is likely to have killed 800,000 people.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: But there is some hopeful news when it comes to Omicron. NBC`s Emilie Ikeda is in Los Angeles this morning with the latest on that. Emilie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC CORRESPONDENT: Peter and Kristen, good morning. Overnight, Idaho reporting its first confirmed case of the Omicron variant. The infected individual experienced mild symptoms and was vaccinated. Health officials say, it underscores just how transmissible the variant is.\par \par \uc2 This morning, the U.S. quickly closing in on a grim milestone: 800,000 deaths from COVID-19 as Omicron extends its reach to the majority of states.\par \par \uc2 ROCHELLE WALENSKY\uc2 , CDC DIRECTOR: We will continue to closely follow this variant.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: The CDC reporting most of those with confirmed infections are young, nearly 80 percent were fully vaccinated, some with boosters and in a possible glimmer of hope, so far, no deaths. Symptoms including cough, fatigue, congestion, or runny nose. Health officials revealing, Friday, the new variant arrived in the U.S. earlier than initially thought, confirming a case from in November.\par \par \uc2 WALENSKY: We should remember that still over 99 percent of cases in this country right now, are caused by the Delta variants.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: This week, Maine and New Hampshire hospitals telling a record number of patients prompting the northeastern states to call in the National Guard. In Michigan, the health care system stretched thin, with Omicron detected there Thursday.\par \par \uc2 JAMES DOVER, SPARROW HEALTH SYSTEM PRESIDENT AND CEO: Our resources are so strange. We`re running at 100 percent capacity. Our ICUs are over full.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: And in New York, Governor Hochul mandating masks in doors with nearly three-dozen hospitals suspending non elective procedures.\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: We could either plateau here, or cases could escalate beyond control.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Would you say colder states are at a disadvantage right now?\par \par \uc2 ANNE RIMOIN, UCLA FIELDING SCHOOL OF PUBLIC HEALTH EPIDEMIOLOGIST: This virus loves cold environments where people are indoors in close proximity to each other. That is the best place for this virus to spread.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: And while the U.S. largely dodged a so-called Twin-demic last year, doctors warn this holiday season, we may not be so lucky.\par \par \uc2 RIMOIN: Testing is going to be critical at this moment to be able to make sure that you know what you have.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Studies show, Omicron is more skilled at sidestepping vaccines but Pfizer`s booster shot appears to provide stronger protection, fueling the debate over what the definition of fully vaccinated should mean. So far, more than 50 million Americans have been boosted. Peter, Kristen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right, Emilie Ikeda, thank you very much for that report.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Abortion providers in Texas are discussing what their next move will be this morning after their partial victory in fighting SB-8, the Texas Law that bans nearly all abortions after six weeks. The Supreme Court says they can challenge it in federal court. But for now, that law remains in effect. NBC News justice Correspondent Pete Williams has the very latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Texas abortion providers are considering their next steps this morning in fighting SB-8, the law the state tried to make impossible to challenge. The law bans most abortions after six weeks of pregnancy and allows anyone to sue abortion providers that violated. State officials don`t enforce it, so who can be sued?\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court ruled unanimously the challenges of the law cannot suit state court judges since they don`t enforce anything. With Chief Justice Roberts in the courts, three liberals dissenting, it ruled five to four that the challengers cannot sue state court clerks or the Texas Attorney General either. But it voted eight to one with only \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  dissenting, that the challengers can sue members of the State Medical Board just barely enough to let the lawsuits go ahead.\par \par \uc2 Even so, the court said the state can continue enforcing the law while the legal challenges play out a win for abortion rights opponents. But President Biden in his statement said he was "very concerned the law can be enforced," adding, "there is so much more work to be done to protect women`s rights."\par \par \uc2 NANCY NORTHUP\uc2 , CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS PRESIDENT AND CEO: I think this is going to go down as one of the most disgraceful decisions in Supreme Court history. It has allowed the state of Texas right now to nullify the Supreme Court`s decisions that there`s a constitutional right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 [07:20:00]\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So, abortion providers can still challenge the Texas law in federal court but from a much weaker position because they can`t sue anyone who actually has the power to stop enforcing it. Friday`s ruling was about the structure of the Texas law, not about abortion rights, a ruling on that and a separate case from Mississippi probably won`t come for several more months. For TODAY, Pete Williams, NBC News at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right, and we do want to go back to weather. Somara`s back with a look at the rest of the country`s forecast. And Somara, you`re tracking some weather out in the Northwest, some flooding.\par \par \uc2 THEODORE: yes. And this, the culprit behind that the atmospheric river that moisture high up in the atmosphere is going to be pumping bands of heavy rain, even some snow into parts of the mountain region in the northwestern part of the country, they`re going to be seeing winds gusting upwards of 45 miles per hour.\par \par \uc2 Now, here`s a look at the timing on this, and it begins today, but it pushes through 8:00 p.m. Sunday. And that`s why the threat for flooding is so high; it`s just going to be continuous throughout much of the weekend. Let`s go ahead and take a look at how much rain we could expect.\par \par \uc2 So, from Seattle to Portland -- Eureka, you got Northern California, Reno, Redding, they could see up to five inches of rain out that way. And because of that, flooding and flash flooding is a big threat through today. It`s not just the rain, it`s also the threat for snow as we head throughout much of the weekend.\par \par \uc2 You`re going to see you hit the Rockies areas like Utah, they could be seeing copious amounts of snow throughout the day there as well. So, here`s a look at the weather across the country. It`s the action going on in the northwest, then you start heading towards the middle parts of the country from Texas right on into the northern tier, dry and sunny skies.\par \par \uc2 But as you know, we have been tracking that threat for severe weather all night into tomorrow, these strong storms that impacted much of the Midwest and parts of the Great Lakes, that`s going to make its way to the east coast as we head through tonight. So, we`re going to be on high alert from Georgia all the way up into the mid-Atlantic in parts of the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 We`re also tracking some record warmth. It`s December, what`s going on? We`re going to talk more about this later for now back to you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: All right. Somara, thank you very much. And still to come right here, the very latest on those deadly -- we`re going to have the very latest on those deadly storms in the southeast and Midwest. We`re going to also speak live to one woman who was inside that candle factory in Kentucky when its roof collapsed.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But first, we`re going to go to your local news and weather.\par \par \uc2 CECY DEL CARMEN, NBC METEOROLOGIST: Strong gusty winds across the area associated with freezing drizzle this morning. These winds picking up this wave into the afternoon with mild temperatures. But in the meantime, we`re having to deal with slick roads. Poor visibility across much of the area during the next few hours.\par \par \uc2 We`re expecting for these temperatures to head into the 60s, in the meantime, around 44 this morning. Next 24 hours, these temperatures around a 60 as we head into the evening, the conditions are improving with blustery conditions for Sunday.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Coming up, we`ll have more on this devastating storms.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We`re back right after these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:25:13]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Still to come on TODAY, we`ll go live to Kentucky for the very latest on the overnight storms where dozens are feared dead.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Plus, we`re going to remember the life and contributions of Monkey`s singer Michael Nesmith, but first these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JEFF SAPERSTONE, NBC ANCHOR: Good morning, everyone. You`re watching NBC 10 BOSTON. I`m Jeff Saperstone, we have a number of headlines to get to this morning including this breaking story out of New Hampshire, a plane crashes just outside of Manchester, Boston Regional Airport. Police tell us the medical examiner was called in here, the twin engine plane crashed on the riverbank just across the airport. It`s not clear how many people are on board at the time. We are following this story. We`ll bring you any updates just as soon as we get them.\par \par \uc2 Back here at home, a grim milestone in the pandemic. Now, for the first time, the total number of cases in the state has surpassed 900,000. And here`s a look at the trend over the past few days, three in a row of more than 5,000 cases. And amid those rising cases, 93 percent of surgical beds and 87 percent of ICU beds here in the state are now full. Most of those patients are not COVID related. However, to try to combat this issue, the Department of Public Health says hospitals must reduce non-essential services and procedures by 50 percent. That goes into effect on December 15th.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get a check of weather right now here`s Cecy.\par \par \uc2 DEL CARMEN: Mild, blustery, and also the potential for some slick roads across central and northern portions of Massachusetts. And now, the wind gusts as we enter into this afternoon, this is when they`re really going to start to crank up, well surpassing 40-mile per hours across the southeast. Overspreading is waiting too early, even 43 miles per hour in Wooster. So, as we get into the overnight hours, yes, they will be dying down a bit but still, these wind gusts will remain quite blustery. As we head into this Sunday, 40 across the area with some light rain during the next 24 hours in Boston. Temperatures heading into and around this 60s.\par \par \uc2 SAPERSTONE: All right, Cecy, thank you. Thanks so much for watching NBC 10 BOSTON. We`re back with another update in 30 minutes.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:30:25]\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: We are back on this Saturday morning December 11th 2021. And we are following this devastating news from overnight where dozens of people are feared dead after severe weather ripped through six states as the sun begins to rise. Officials are only now starting to get a better sense of this devastating situation across that region.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yes, you can see the search and rescue efforts underway there.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Morgan Chesky has been following this all-night long. Morgan, what is the very latest as we wait for the sun to come up?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Peter, Kristen, the latest is that those search and rescue operations are ongoing and when the sun comes up today, it will be an absolutely devastating day for so many communities here across multiple states.\par \par \uc2 This storm system created one tornado that has now been confirmed to cross at least four states and it was on a path that could be more than 200 miles long.\par \par \uc2 The community that took the brunt of its wrath was Mayfield, Kentucky. That is where the Kentucky governor says at least 50 people could be presumed dead following that tornado that struck under the cover of darkness catching many people off guard.\par \par \uc2 We know that at least 100 people were inside a candle factory there where the roof collapsed. The governor telling us that 12 people may have perished inside that one business alone. And that is far from the only example of what this massive system left behind.\par \par \uc2 We know that there are confirmed deaths in Arkansas, in Tennessee, in Northern Mississippi from this storm system that created multiple tornadoes.\par \par \uc2 In Monette Manor, Arkansas, there was a nursing home that was struck, causing a partial collapse. First responders getting there as soon as they could but not before at least one fatality was confirmed.\par \par \uc2 They were able however to usher those elderly residents outside of that building to safety. But that story will be one that we hear many times as the sun comes up today as first responders are still having trouble going from damaged area to damaged area with powerlines debris still covering so many roads across the trail of this tornado, which as I mentioned before, hundreds of miles long.\par \par \uc2 This covered four separate states. We know that at least six states had reported tornadoes. And as of this hour right now, this is a storm system that could pose problems into the next 24 hours, Peter, Kristen.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right, Morgan Chesky, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We want to bring in Kyanna Parsons-Perez, she was inside that candle factory in Mayfield, Kentucky. Fortunately, she made it out safely.\par \par \uc2 Kyanna, we`re so glad to see you this morning and that you`re OK. If you can give us a sense, what was the scene like? What was it like inside that building where you were trapped at this candle factory for two hours as the storm hit?\par \par \uc2 KYANNA PARSONS-PEREZ, TRAPPED IN KENTUCKY CANDLE FACTORY DURING STORM: Well, good morning, it was extremely scary. It was extremely scary. Everything happened so fast. They had us in the area where you go in case there`s a storm, and we were all there and then the lights got to flicker in.\par \par \uc2 And then, all of a sudden, we felt a gust that we could feel the wind and then, my ears kind of start popping, you know, as they would as if you`re on a plane.\par \par \uc2 And then, we did like a little rock, like, this way and this way. And then, boom, everything came down on us. And all we heard was screams.\par \par \uc2 The factory has a lot of Hispanic people, there`s Puerto Rican, Mexican and Guatemalan and then there`s a lot of African-Americans and Caucasian. You know, it`s a -- it`s a diverse place.\par \par \uc2 But you can hear people screaming and praying in Spanish and just hollering and you know, trying to figure out what to do and I went live.\par \par \uc2 First, I called 911. But then later, I went live because I was trying to stay calm and keep everybody else calm and trying to get us as much help as possible.\par \par \uc2 So, if I went live, maybe, you know, more people would come to try to help us and get us out of there. But it was the absolutely the most terrifying thing I`ve ever experienced in my life.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And Kyanna, when you say something fell on top of you, was it a wall that fell on top of you and did you worry you weren`t going to make it out alive?\par \par \uc2 PARSONS-PEREZ: I did not think I was going to make it at all. I was so scared.\par \par \uc2 I was trying to stay positive. But after I think we were down there and I was where I was at for at least two hours.\par \par \uc2 And so, and I fell in a very awkward position. My back was against the wall and I was right by the air condition -- I mean, the water fountain.\par \par \uc2 [07:35:01]\par \par \uc2 PARSONS-PEREZ: And so, I didn`t know that behind the water fountain was an air conditioning unit. So, when the search and rescue person came in, I believe his name was Nate. When he came, he says -- I 