 we head into this Sunday 40 across the area with some light rain during the next 24 hours in Boston. Temperatures heading into and around in the 60s.\par \par \uc2 SAPERSTONE: All right, Cecy, thank you so much. We are back in 30 minutes with a full newscast. We will see you then.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020211211ehcb0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for December 10, 2021, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kristen Welker, Pete Williams, Miguel Almaguer, Guad  Venegas, Tom Costello, Stephanie Gosk, Andrea Mitchell, Kevin Tibbles \par \uc2 3,763 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 December 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOST, NBC NEW ANCHOR: Tonight the historic price surges with costs rising across the country and so many things we buy every day.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voiceover): Inflation soaring 6.8 percent in November, the fastest pace in nearly 40 years. Costs skyrocketing for rent food, gas, cars, clothing, and so much more. President Biden maintaining the crisis has hit its peak. What`s causing it and how much longer will it last?\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, the case Supreme Court ruling delivering a partial victory to challengers of Texas`s strict abortion law but leaving the law in place for now. Where the battle goes next?\par \par \uc2 The Omicron variant now in at least half of the U.S. The new CDC report, what it found regarding Omicron symptoms and the strict new mask mandate in New York, one of several states facing a potential winter surge.\par \par \uc2 The major winter storm on the move and 35 million under threat of severe weather and potential tornadoes.\par \par \uc2 The Delta flight diverted after an unruly passenger allegedly attacked a flight attendant and an air marshal. The video from inside the airplane.\par \par \uc2 Honoring \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2 . President Biden and Tom Hanks among those paying final tributes and services today.\par \par \uc2 From trees to T-shirts inside the fashion industry`s role in harming the rainforest.\par \par \uc2 And celebrating the singer-songwriter who also starred in a classic NBC sitcom.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening. We got one of those government reports today that tells you it`s not just you. Things are still getting more expensive. Inflation biting consumers hard right smack in the middle of the holiday buying season. Consumer prices rose in November compared to a year ago by almost seven percent. That`s the highest annual inflation jump since June of 1982.\par \par \uc2 Gasoline led the way up 58 percent. But virtually everything was up from cars to food, electricity, and housing. The trend is forcing some pretty tough conversations in many American households about making ends meet. President Biden offering vague reassurances that things will write themselves soon. It`s where we start tonight with Chief White House Correspondent Kristen Welker.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight Americans already slammed by soaring prices now getting hit even harder. New numbers showing inflation surging to its fastest pace in nearly 40 years, up 6.8 percent from a year ago.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I feel like there`s never enough money.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Christy Brown, a teacher and single mom telling us she`s struggling to pay higher rent, plus $150 more a month for groceries, buy winter clothes for her kids, and now they need a new car.\par \par \uc2 What`s the toughest part for you about dealing with these increased prices every day?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think it`s telling my kids that they can`t do things that they want to do.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Prices rising across the board, including gas prices up 58 percent over last year, fuel oil up 59 percent, beef up 21 percent, and used cars up 31 percent. All of it during the holidays. Deon King says his family has cut out restaurants and vacations and will celebrate Christmas without gifts this year because they cannot afford them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The prices has gone up dramatically. It`s just not in our budget to even go out and do anything for the holiday season of this Christmas.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And the spiking prices not only affecting families.\par \par \uc2 LOUIS HANKINS, RESTAURANT OWNER: Our tomatoes were up 40 percent two, three weeks ago. Our red onions were up 35 percent, and our guacamole, our avocado was up 30, 35 percent.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: At Louis Hankins` restaurant, El Techo, higher costs and the supply chain crisis forcing him to take items off the menu.\par \par \uc2 When you look at these increase in prices, what is your biggest fear?\par \par \uc2 HANKINS: My main concern is the customer coming, the gas prices rising, and everything else across the board rising, are they going to make money available in their budget and have tacos and margaritas?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Tonight, the Senate`s top Republican blasting Democrats for "more borrowing, more printing, more reckless spending, saying inflation is out of control on the Democrats watch." But with gas prices down eight cents in the last month, President Biden today touting progress.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It`s a real bump in the road. It does affect families. It matters to people when you`re paying more for gas, although in some states, we`ve got the price down below three bucks a gallon. But the point is, it`s not gone down quickly enough. But I think it will.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Kristen, the President today also saying he thinks this is the peak of the crisis.\par \par \uc2 WELKER (on camera): Well, Lester, while that may be the President`s view, White House officials acknowledge they cannot predict how long this inflation will last. Though, today, they said they are going to apply pressure to some industries including meatpackers to try to lower prices. Lester?\par \par \uc2 [19:05:09]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kristen Welker at the White House, thank you. Another major story we`re following tonight, the partial victory for opponents of the Texas law banning most abortions. The Supreme Court saying abortion providers can challenge the law in federal court but for now leaving it in effect. Here`s Pete Williams with more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEW JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): It`s a modest victory for supporters of abortion rights and a minor setback for Texas which tried to make SBA impossible to challenge. The law bans most abortions after six weeks of pregnancy and allows anyone to sue abortion providers that violate it.\par \par \uc2 State officials don`t enforce it, so who can be sued? The Supreme Court ruled unanimously the challengers of the law cannot sue state court judges since they don`t enforce anything. With Chief Justice Roberts and the courts three liberals descending, it ruled five to four that the challengers cannot sue state court clerk`s or the Texas Attorney General either.\par \par \uc2 But it voted eight to one with only \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  descending that the challengers can sue members of the State Medical Board, just barely enough to let the lawsuits go ahead. Even so, the court said the state can keep enforcing the law while the legal challenges play out.\par \par \uc2 NANCY NORTHUP\uc2 , PRESIDENT AND CEO, \uc2 CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS\uc2 : I think this is going to go down as one of the most disgraceful decisions in Supreme Court history. It is allowed the state of Texas right now to nullify the Supreme Court`s decisions that there`s a constitutional right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So, abortion providers can still challenge the Texas law and federal court, but they cannot sue anyone who actually has the power to stop enforcing it.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA MURRAY, PROFESSOR, NEW YORK UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF LAW: I`m not sure that the Supreme Court`s decision today provides them with the relief that they`re seeking because it doesn`t provide them with an avenue to have this law stopped on the ground.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS (on camera): Today`s ruling was about the structure of the Texas law, not about abortion rights. A ruling about that in a separate case from Mississippi probably won`t come for several more months. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Pete, thank you. Now, to the COVID crisis. The CDC saying today the Omicron variant has been found in at least 25 states and that the first cases were mostly mild and overwhelmingly among the vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Also, New York imposing a new statewide mask mandate as fears grow of a winter surge. Miguel Almaguer has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): As a new wave of COVID infections cascades across the country, the U.S. is now barreling toward unprecedented benchmarks. In the coming days, our nation will top 50 million cases. And as hospitalizations soar a staggering 40 percent, the grimace milestone 800,000 American deaths is now near.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: COVID pneumonia.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: COVID pneumonia.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Tonight, one of the greatest threats is in New Hampshire. The state with one of the lowest vaccination rates in the region now has the highest number of cases per capita. DR. MATTHEW GIBB, CHIEF CLINICAL OFFICER, CONCORD HOSPITAL: Our emergency rooms often have 50 to 60 people waiting. We often have upwards of 20 people waiting for admission in the emergency room.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: As the National Guard deploys to Maine, today, New York`s Governor reimplementing an indoor mask mandate with exceptions.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): We`re entering a time of uncertainty where cases could escalate beyond control.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: All of this comes as our nation battles delta and learns more about Omicron. Today, the CDC releasing new details about the 43 cases in 25 states. Most of those with confirmed infections are young. Nearly 80 percent were fully vaccinated, some with boosters, and with no deaths. Among the most promising sign, symptoms were commonly reported as cough fatigue, congestion, or runny nose.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROCHELLE WALENSKY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, CDC: Do what we know works against this virus. Vaccines work, boosting works, testing works, masking works, physical distancing works, washing our hands work, and proper ventilation works.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tonight, new insight into Omicron as Delta still devastates pockets of the nation. Miguel Almaguer, NBC New.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Horrific accident has killed dozens of migrants packed inside a semi- truck in Mexico, part of the ongoing flood of people making the dangerous journey to cross the U.S. border. We`re getting more from Guad Venegas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUAD VENEGAS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: A chaotic and deadly scene in the Mexican state of Chiapas. At least 55 people killed in over 100 injured when the truck they were crammed into crashed on its side. Survivors say it happened on a sharp curve.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (text): It flipped over.\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS: Today, Mexican officials say victims are migrants from several Latin American countries. Mexico`s president asking the U.S. to offer more legal opportunities for migrants. This as thousands risked their life traveling to the U.S. border overwhelming Border Patrol agents.\par \par \uc2 In Yuma, Arizona, the mayor now declaring a local emergency with thousands of migrants streaming into the city.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS NICHOLLS, MAYOR, YUMA, ARIZONA: It became a situation that wasn`t containable.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:03]\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS: This week, under court order, the Biden administration restarting the remain in Mexico policy which mandates asylum seekers wait in Mexico. Mexican officials tell us that move mistakenly led many asylum-seeking migrants to think it benefits them.\par \par \uc2 ENRIQUE VALENZUELA, COORDINATOR, CHIHUAHUA STATE POPULATION COUNCIL: For some people, it sort of sends a message that the U.S. is going to receive people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS (on camera): And tonight, federal and state authorities are searching for the driver of the truck who fled the scene. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Guad Venegas at the border tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 This evening, a pair of federal air marshals are being credited with stopping the latest in a series of dangerous in flight incidents after breaking their cover and intervening on board a Delta flight. We get more now from Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ARIEL PENNINGTON, ARRESTED DURING THE FLIGHT: You`re not scaring nobody no more.\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): It happened onboard Delta flight 342 D.C. to LAX. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says two Air Marshals broke their cover to detain an unruly passenger after he allegedly assaulted a flight attendant, then one of the Air Marshals who handcuffed him.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Stop resisting. Stop resisting.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: The plane diverted to Oklahoma City where police arrested 35- year-old Ariel Pennington.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: After a year of unruly, even dangerous behavior on both planes, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  says bad behavior is declining, but they had 5500 reports so far this year alone. The Attorney General recently ordered federal prosecutors to prioritize crimes that occur onboard planes.\par \par \uc2 And Air Marshals are teaching flight attendants self-defense tactics. But when they`re undercover and witnessed bad behavior, they face a difficult choice.\par \par \uc2 Air Marshals who train a mockup like this must decide whether that disturbance might really be meant to distract them from a hijacking attempt.\par \par \uc2 TSA Chief Admiral.\par \par \uc2 When should an Air Marshal get involved and intervene to stop this kind of activity?\par \par \uc2 DAVID PEKOSKE, CHIEF, TSA: We have protocols for Air Marshals breaking cover and intervening in a situation like this. And these are well-exercise protocols between the cabin crew, the flight attendants, and the Air Marshals. Interfering with the flight crew can bring up to 20 years in prison and fines of up to a quarter-million dollars.\par \par \uc2 Over Oklahoma, a pair of Air Marshals decided they had to intervene. Tom Costello, NBC News, Chicago.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In just 60 seconds, tracking that massive winter storm where it is headed tonight. And our eye-opening report and the cost of the clothes we wear on one of nature`s wonders.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A major winter storm is on the move tonight across much of the country. Winter weather alerts are up across 17 states stretching for nearly 1500 miles. Up to a foot of snow expected in the upper Midwest. And to the south, 35 million under the risk tonight of severe weather with the potential for tornadoes.\par \par \uc2 Now to our series Rescuing The Rainforest. Tonight, in partnership with the Pulitzer Center`s Rainforest Investigations Network, we look into a fabric you`ve probably never heard of but is in many of the clothes we wear. It`s often billed as eco-friendly, but critics say it`s putting the environment at risk. Stephanie Gosk investigates.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): It sleek, silky and most importantly cheap. Viscose rayon, one of the fastest growing fabrics and fashion in T-shirts, sportswear, and dresses, marketed as eco-friendly because it comes from renewable trees.\par \par \uc2 But environmental geographer Ruth DeFries explains that to make way for the tree farms that provide much of the world`s viscose, vast stretches of rainforest were cut down.\par \par \uc2 RUTH DEFRIES ENVIRONMENTAL GEOGRAPHER: If you fly over say, Southeast Asia, you will see these vast, vast plantations that have one single tree species in them. And that`s a very, very different from a diverse rainforest which has millions of species and habitat for lots and lots of different kinds of plants and animals.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: These images show how the Indonesian rainforest was decimated especially in the early 2000s to help produce palm oil, paper, and viscose.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: RGE is an $18 billion group of companies focused --\par \par \uc2 [19:15:07]\par \par \uc2 GOSK: One of the biggest players in the viscose business is \uc2 Royal Golden Eagle\uc2 , RGE, a global industrial group. They`ve supplied viscose to a host of companies including Adidas, \uc2 Abercrombie and Fitch\uc2 , and Esprit. According to an NBC News review of corporate disclosures, none of those retailers commented for our story.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The renewable plantations that underpin its core fiber businesses --\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Back in 2015, RGE pledged to halt deforestation, and has made significant progress. But according to experts in Norway in Indonesia who tracked deforestation, these Satellite images show some clearing of the rainforest may have continued.\par \par \uc2 Edward Boyda is a physicist and co-founder of the Environmental Research NGO Earthrise. He analyzed images for an area totaling 4200 square miles, controlled by companies that supply wood for RGE`s viscose. He estimates at least 30 square miles of rainforest had been cleared since late 2015.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD BOYDA, CO-FOUNDER, EARTHRISE: We can get imagery from 2015 where we can see healthy rainforest. We look again say 2016 or 17 some later year and we see signs that that forest has been destroyed. Looking later still, we can see the plantations for the pulpwood being put in place. And you`ve gone from one of the most you know bio rich places in the world to what`s essentially like a biological desert.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: RGE company in April said in a statement to NBC News, the vast majority of the land identified by Boyda had no deforestation, but rather normal legal plantation harvesting and replanting and small scale community agriculture. The company acknowledged a small amount of deforestation on land that it said had been encroached or damaged by third parties.\par \par \uc2 Johnny Bedika lives next to a large viscose plantation that supplies RGE and says there have been fires and flooding ever since the surrounding rainforest was destroyed in the years leading up to 2015.\par \par \uc2 It used to be easy to hunt pigs for food, he told us. Not anymore. Back in the U.S., concerns about the environment have driven some fashion brands to find alternatives to viscose.\par \par \uc2 DANA DAVIS, VICE PRESIDENT OF SUSTAINABILITY, MARA HOFFMAN: Mara Hoffman has made the decision to just --\par \par \uc2 GOSK: To move away move away from --\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Move away from viscose\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Dana Davis is vice president of sustainability for the designer Mara Hoffman.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: This is made from 100 percent recycled cotton.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Oh, wow. So, this was something else before it became these pants.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: The reality is for most shoppers, there`s such an enormous disconnect from grabbing something off the rack to the rain forests in Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Yes. I think that`s what most things that people do, right? They don`t stop to really think through where that might come from.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Where it comes from, the impact on the rainforest, and the people who live there. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, we`re back at a moment with the moving tributes to \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2 . How he was remembered today.\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: An emotional tribute today for \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2 . President Biden and both parties coming together for the funeral of the longtime senator and former GOP presidential nominee. And moving words from Tom Hanks and our own Savannah Guthrie. Here`s Andrea Mitchell.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): At Washington`s National Cathedral, a celebration to honor the long life of Robert J. Dole.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: God, what courage \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2  had.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Grievously wounded in battle but resilient, a political warrior who believed in compromise. Mentors to a freshmen Democrat named \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We disagreed. But we`re never disagreeable with one another. I found Bob to be a man of principle, pragmatism, and enormous integrity.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: His wife of nearly 46 years, former senator and cabinet member of Elizabeth Dole, a storied love affair for the ages, and daughter from a previous marriage, Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN DOLE\uc2 , DAUGHTER OF \uc2 BOB DOLE\uc2 : I love you, Dad. I promise you will never walk alone.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: His casket then driven to the World War II Memorial he helped create, a memorial to peace, he said, and to the half-million soldiers unlike him who did not return.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANKS, ACTOR: He was never able again to button a shirt or sign his name as he had. He saluted and he made your acquaintance with his left hand.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Family friend Savannah Guthrie.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: He stood for principle. He stood for dignity. And even when he couldn`t stand any longer, at the casket of an old friend, who could forget, still he stood out of respect and out of honor.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Taxes now sounding for the soldier of America, but we know as long as we see each other not as enemies, but as neighbors and colleagues, and taps will never sound for \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Tonight, an American patriots flying home to Kansas. Andrea Mitchell, NBC News, Washington.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And the music world is remembering Monkees singer, songwriter Michael Nesmith, whose death was announced by his manager today. The band exploded in popularity in the 1960s starring in The Monkees hit comedy series on NBC from 1966 to 68.\par \par \uc2 The band was known for hits like Daydream believer and last train to Clarksville. Nesmith was 78.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, why city officials in California tried to pull the plug on one fan`s light show tribute to a holiday classic.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, the holiday lights display inspired by a classic Christmas movie and how the fight that erupted over it ended up bringing a town together. Here`s Kevin Tibbles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN TIBBLES, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: With when the happiest holidays, hit La Mirada, California, outcome all things Griswold.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My reaction was, oh my god, this is awesome. He did a fabulous job.\par \par \uc2 TIBBLES: Lights, cameras, lots of action, as folks celebrate one neighbor`s love for National Lampoon`s Christmas Vacation, from the cop car to the motorhome.\par \par \uc2 JEFF NORTON, HOMEOWNER: I`m actually probably one of the biggest collectors on the planet of this movie. So, I have a lot of memorabilia.\par \par \uc2 TIBBLES: But this year`s display was almost unplugged by the city as Norton seeking to recreate the Griswold house built this false second floor without a permit.\par \par \uc2 NORTON: I mean, it`s a Christmas display. It`s a facade. It`s not real.\par \par \uc2 TIBBLES: Visitors started leaving donations to help battle the supposed Scrooge of Christmas presents.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They should definitely make an exception.\par \par \uc2 TIBBLES: A happy ending though. The spirit of Christmas prevailed when the two sides reached an agreement and his $100,000 display can light up the holidays till January 1.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And we`re going to have the happiest Christmas.\par \par \uc2 TIBBLES: Kevin Tibbles, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Honoring a classic. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Friday. Thanks for watching. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020211211ehca00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Ruling Delivering A Partial Victory To Challengers Of Texas`s Strict Abortion Law\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Pete Williams \par \uc2 471 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 December 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Kristen Welker at the White House, thank you. Another major story we`re following tonight, the partial victory for opponents of the Texas law banning most abortions. The Supreme Court saying abortion providers can challenge the law in federal court but for now leaving it in effect. Here`s Pete Williams with more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEW JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): It`s a modest victory for supporters of abortion rights and a minor setback for Texas which tried to make SBA impossible to challenge. The law bans most abortions after six weeks of pregnancy and allows anyone to sue abortion providers that violate it.\par \par \uc2 State officials don`t enforce it, so who can be sued? The Supreme Court ruled unanimously the challengers of the law cannot sue state court judges since they don`t enforce anything. With Chief Justice Roberts and the courts three liberals descending, it ruled five to four that the challengers cannot sue state court clerk`s or the Texas Attorney General either.\par \par \uc2 But it voted eight to one with only \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  descending that the challengers can sue members of the State Medical Board, just barely enough to let the lawsuits go ahead. Even so, the court said the state can keep enforcing the law while the legal challenges play out.\par \par \uc2 NANCY NORTHUP\uc2 , PRESIDENT AND CEO, \uc2 CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS\uc2 : I think this is going to go down as one of the most disgraceful decisions in Supreme Court history. It is allowed the state of Texas right now to nullify the Supreme Court`s decisions that there`s a constitutional right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So, abortion providers can still challenge the Texas law and federal court, but they cannot sue anyone who actually has the power to stop enforcing it.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA MURRAY, PROFESSOR, NEW YORK UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF LAW: I`m not sure that the Supreme Court`s decision today provides them with the relief that they`re seeking because it doesn`t provide them with an avenue to have this law stopped on the ground.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS (on camera): Today`s ruling was about the structure of the Texas law, not about abortion rights. A ruling about that in a separate case from Mississippi probably won`t come for several more months. Lester?\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020211211ehca0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 TODAY WITH HODA & JENNA for December 10, 2021, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Jenna Bush Hager, Willie Geist, Elizabeth Mayhew, Donna Farizan,  Lester Holt, Pete Williams, Peter Alexander, Morgan Chesky, Justin  Sylvester, Kelly O`Donnell, Chuck Todd \par \uc2 8,671 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 December 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [10:00:22]\par \par \uc2 JENNA BUSH HAGER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (voice over): Today, Santa`s helpers are coming to town, and we`ve got sacks full of surprises for a very deserving family.\par \par \uc2 Then, we`ll deliver Christmas cheer and tasty treats straight to your doorstep with the best mail order food gifts.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a revealing new memoir by Cagney and Lacey star Sharon Gless. The Emmy winner opens up with us, right now.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News, this is TODAY with Hoda Kotb and Jenna Bush Hager from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAGER (on camera): Hi guys, it`s Friday, December 10th. We are listening to the Middle by Zedd, Maren Morris and Grey. It`s actually one of Poppy`s favorite song.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Great song.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: She`s also a middle child. So, I feel like she`s real.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Oh, maybe that`s why.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: It`s sort of her theme song.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: The subconscious why she likes that song.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I don`t know why she loves it. And she`s the type of child that really is kind of like the middle child where Mila will say mommies -- I`m mommy`s favorite and Poppy is like, OK, you can be mommy`s favorite.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Oh, she gives it to her.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I know.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Wow.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: What do I do about that?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: I don`t know. Let her work through it. Time will take care of it. Plus, Maren Morris, great Texas girl like yourself.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Right? Do you ever listen to The Highway Women (PH)?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Don`t you love that band?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Love them. Love her.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And by the way, here -- look who`s here? It`s Willie. He`s rounding out the week for us. There`s one person clapping over there in the kitchen.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Again, you know the applause from the beginning of the week.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Oh, there`s somebody -- yes.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: I was going to say I`ve been grown actually they`ve gotten fewer.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes, there`s like a class here.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: It`s just sort of like an obligatory.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: People are tired. Wow, gee, thanks.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: I feel the same.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: You know what, Willie, the fact that you took the week, you ran from "MORNING JOE", you spent the week here talking about, you know, Cardi B`s whipped cream and meggings (PH) and all the wonderful thing we discussed around here, really means a lot.\par \par \uc2 But meanwhile, you`re like -- you`re a real journalist.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Sometimes.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And you have a big interview on Sunday, which everybody is looking so forward to.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Yes, this weekend on "SUNDAY TODAY", I have a conversation with former Secretary of State, of course, Democratic presidential nominee \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . She`s doing one of these master classes. We`ve seen a lot of well-known people do master classes where you kind of share some of your wisdom with people.\par \par \uc2 And the part that`s going to jump out to most people I think is this last episode where she reads her would-be victory speech from election night 2016.\par \par \uc2 So, she didn`t have a concession speech, she thought she was going to win.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: So, for the first time, she sat down in this masterclass, open and read the speech. And what you need to know about it is she ends the speech talking about her mother, who had a very, very difficult life was kind of sent out by her parents, put on a train when she was a little girl to go live on the West Coast with her grandparents. Her grandparents didn`t treat her well. Had a very hard life.\par \par \uc2 So, at the end of this speech, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  imagines walking down the aisle of that train when her mother was 8 years old on a train with her 3- year-old sister and saying, basically, it`s going to be OK, and your daughter became president of the United States. Here`s a part of that conversation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: I think the moment that is going to stick out to most people in this masterclass is an extraordinary one, where you sit down, and you read what you call the would-be victory speech.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: I dream of going up to her and sitting down next to her, taking her in my arms and saying, look at me, listen to me, you will survive. You will have a good family of your own and three children.\par \par \uc2 And as hard as it might be to imagine, your daughter will grow up and become the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is an NBC News Special Report. Here`s Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning, we`re coming on the air with breaking news. The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  has just issued a decision on the Texas law that prohibits abortion after about six weeks.\par \par \uc2 We want to go to our Justice Correspondent Pete Williams now who has the decision. Pete, good morning.\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Lester, the Supreme Court has just allowed lawsuits against S.B. 8, the Texas abortion law to go ahead in the lower courts.\par \par \uc2 Now, to be clear, this is not a decision that overturns S.B. 8. This simply opens the spigot now to allow lawsuits to proceed in state court in Texas, challenging the constitutionality of the law.\par \par \uc2 Remember what was unusual about this law, Texas could not simply on its own ban abortion after six weeks of pregnancy, because that would violate the current Supreme Court rulings that say you can`t ban abortion before the age of viability.\par \par \uc2 That issue by the way is what`s being debated now in the Mississippi case. But the court hasn`t ruled on that. So, the law of the United States right now is you can`t ban abortion after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 So, what Texas came up which was a novel plan to allow outsiders, anybody else, private citizens to file a lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 [10:05:07]\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So, the question for the Supreme Court was, can lawsuits go ahead challenging the structure of the Texas law? And the Supreme Court has just said, yes.\par \par \uc2 And I`m trying to decide now what the outcome of the court is. But it`s going to take a while to figure out what the vote is here, Lester, but the bottom line here is these lawsuits can now go ahead in Texas.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Pete Williams, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let me go to NBC News Chief White House Correspondent Peter Alexander.\par \par \uc2 Peter, clearly this is what the administration was hoping for.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that`s exactly right. We`ve heard from the president on this issue. He said within the last several months that this law in Texas unleashes unconstitutional chaos on women in the state of Texas and said that the -- that the government, the Biden administration would do everything in its power to see what it was able to do to help maintain those constitutional protections to an abortion for women in this country.\par \par \uc2 The president among other things directed recently a Gender focused Policy Council in the White House, the \uc2 Health and Human Services Department\uc2 , as well as the Justice Department to embark on what they described as a whole of government approach here to see what legislative measures, what other measures the government had control of independent of this.\par \par \uc2 Any ruling that would come from the court here, among the biggest concerns for President Biden and the Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , frankly, has been the impact that this law would have on women of color. 70 percent of the abortions taking place in the state of Texas are for women of color in that state.\par \par \uc2 So again, there`ll be -- they`ll be glad by this ruling as initially described by Pete Williams here, and we`ll try to get more details from them over the course of the next several hours.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Peter Alexander, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Joining us from Dallas, Texas is NBC News Correspondent who has been reporting on this case. Any official reaction from there, Morgan?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Lester, good morning. No official reaction just yet. But I can tell you that abortion rights advocates definitely seeing this as a victory. And the impact was felt immediately when this law went into effect back on September 1st, the Supreme Court deciding not to block it at that time.\par \par \uc2 There is a clinic just down the road from us in Fort Worth, Texas, who reported that their daily procedures went from 66 a day to 10.\par \par \uc2 And another abortion rights group in the state of Texas says that since this law went into effect, those numbers have dropped by 50 percent statewide.\par \par \uc2 As Peter mentioned, there was great concern for those women from lower socio-economic backgrounds, because in some cases, there have been multiple stories lesser of those individuals having to travel across state lines and not having the income or the funds to do so, putting essentially -- potentially their health at risk.\par \par \uc2 So, as it stands right now, the fight is not over for these abortion rights advocates. But as of this moment in time, there is still hope here in Texas, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Morgan Chesky, thank you. Let me go back to Pete now, you`ve had a chance to digest a little bit more about this decision. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So, the Supreme Court has whittled away at the kind of lawsuit that can be filed, the people that can be sued in Texas. The Texas had argued, look, you can`t sue -- you can`t sue us, you can`t sue state officials because we have nothing to do with enforcement of this law.\par \par \uc2 So, the opponent said, OK, we`ll sue state court judges and state court clerks. Well, the Supreme Court says you can`t do that.\par \par \uc2 So, they`ve narrowed down the kind of lawsuits that can be brought. Eight justices say that a lawsuit can nonetheless be proceed against that narrower base of potential defendants.\par \par \uc2 So, it does give the abortion providers the chance to go back to court. It does tie one hand behind their backs, however, but it does allow these lawsuits to proceed but not the lawsuit filed by the Biden administration, not the federal lawsuit. The Supreme Court said that one cannot go ahead.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Pete Williams, thank you. That concludes our coverage. We`ll be back on the air shortly to bring you live coverage of the funeral for Senator \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2 . We`ll have much more of course in the Supreme Court ruling tonight when I see you on "NBC NIGHTLY NEWS".\par \par \uc2 I`m Lester Holt in New York. Good day, everyone.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:13:21]\par \par \uc2 HAGER: It is Friday and Willie is in for Hoda. We`ve moved downstairs because we are going to play a new game. OK.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: OK.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I`m very excited about this. It`s a new game called Sip or Spill.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Should I be worried?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I think we should because each round, here`s how it`s going to work. We`re going to get a probing question to ask each other, OK?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: OK.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And then, after the question is read, we then have the option, answer the question or take a sip of what happens to be in front of us.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: And we don`t know what it is? It could be anything.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: We don`t know the questions. We don`t know the drink.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Got it.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: OK, so round one, will you please bring out the first sip.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: OK.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Here it is.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Oh, look how beautifully presented.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes, it sure is.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Thank you very much. OK. Should I reveal?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Might as well.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: OK, ready?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Its buttermilk.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Well, that`s not so bad.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Have you ever drink straight buttermilk?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: No, it`s for recipe, right?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I think you just put it in biscuits.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: All right, well, here`s the question. OK, which -- Willie, this is for you. Which of your features would you say your wife says is the sexiest?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Oh, that`s a good question. I think hair.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Wait, first, would you like to Sip or Spill?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: No, I`m going to -- I`m going 