par \uc2 HAGER: I`m sure that will fit.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Happy holidays everybody.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Thank you, Justin.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER: Anytime.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Justin, say hello to your mother.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER: I will.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Thanks, Justin. And we`ll be back with legendary "CAGNEY & LACEY" star Sharon Gless. After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: We are taking a walk down memory lane with legendary leading lady Sharon Gless.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes, there aren`t many actresses whose careers in Hollywood have lasted nearly five decades, but Sharon is one of them. And we`re going to chat with her in a moment, but first, let`s take a look back.\par \par \uc2 [10:50:05]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HAGER (voiceover): Sharon Gless is a Hollywood icon known for her brash wit, bold roles and brave choices. The two-time Emmy winner broke the mold with her legendary role as Sergeant Christine Cagney in the groundbreaking 80s female police procedural "CAGNEY & LACEY" alongside co-star Tyne Daly. The show averages staggering 30 million viewers a week.\par \par \uc2 SHARON GLESS, CHRISTINE CAGNEY, CAGNEY & LACEY CAST: I love burning the candle of both ends working for my partner all day long in the City of New York all night. It makes me feel needed, you know?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: After starting with Rosie O`Donnell in the critically acclaimed Queer as Folk, she was nominated for 10th Emmy Award as a supporting actress in 2010 for Burn Notice. Now, the Hollywood actress is opening up about her 50-year career in her new memoir "Apparently There Were Complaints," pulling back the curtain and sharing stories from both classic and current Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Sharon, good morning. It`s great to see you.\par \par \uc2 GLESS: Good morning. This is fun, exciting.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: We`re so happy you`re here.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: It`s exciting for us --\par \par \uc2 GLESS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: We can`t wait to ask you about the title that There Were Complaints. I need to know more about that. But we were just discussing as we watched that, 30 to 50 million people watched "CAGNEY & LACEY," that`s -- that long ago it`s a different universe now. When you look back on that series and the impact it had, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 GLESS: Well, I think how very fortunate I was to be in it. I think at the time when we were doing it, we did not know the power that we had or the -- as you say the impact we were having on the women of Los Angeles. We were lucky enough to shoot it, but it wasn`t until later that we realized what had happened and the changes we`ve made.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes, I mean --\par \par \uc2 GLESS: It was the first drama to ever star two women.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: That`s unbelievable when you think about that. And the fact you also handled themes that were groundbreaking in the day. Your character dealt with alcoholism?\par \par \uc2 GLESS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And that was something -- yes. And that was something that wasn`t normally spoken about, it was still very much taboo.\par \par \uc2 GLESS: Never, never, and that one changed lives.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLESS: But we dealt with child abuse. We dealt with breast cancer. We dealt with sex in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLESS: And we dealt with all of the things that are going on now.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: You write about the series in the book, "Apparently There Were Complaints," which raises the obvious question, what were the complaints and who was making them?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GLESS: I came up with the title first. I used that expression -- I was in Hazelton, and I was alcoholic. We didn`t know it at the time when I was doing "CAGNEY & LACEY" that a lot of people say were those scenes -- were you loaded when you shot those scenes? I was not.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLESS: But anyway, I was put in Hazelton, there was a lot of press about it, a lot of scandal, a lot of -- anyway, when I got out, somebody said to me, you were in Hazelton? It`s like the Harvard of the rehabs.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLESS: And I said, yes, I was. And she said, why were you in Hazelton? And I was trying to make light of it, you know, so I said, apparently, there were complaints.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I feel like that`s going to be my new slogan.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: That`s good.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Right?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Did you feel some sense of catharsis as you wrote through this, was it painful at times, did it feel better afterward?\par \par \uc2 GLESS: Any of the complaints about me, some were painful, some were funny, but I had already gone through the pain, you know, when I lived them, and I never forgot them. So, it was easy to recall them, putting them on the page and making them fuller, and making them interesting to you. Because I`ve lived with them, but I`m very happy with the result.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Well, you should be. Sharon, it`s a beautiful, beautiful book. And to check it out.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Apparently There Were Complaints, great tittle. Just scan the QR code, you see on the screen or head to today.com/shop.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And we`ll be back after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is an NBC News Special Report: REMEMBERING \uc2 BOB DOLE\uc2 . Here`s Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Good morning, everyone. We`re coming on the air to bring you to live coverage of the funeral of Senator \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2 , who passed away earlier this week at the age of 98. President Biden is among those expected to speak this morning at the service being held at the Washington National Cathedral. There`s a live view. Folks gather, and Senator Dole was a giant in American politics. For decades, the former Senate Majority Leader and the Republican Presidential Nominee in `96, ultimately losing to incumbent \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  who is also in attendance today.\par \par \uc2 [10:55:14]\par \par \uc2 Dole was a recipient of two Purple Hearts after he was severely injured in World War II. That experience would go on to really shape his life of public service. He was a key supporter of the Americans with Disabilities Act. And once he left office, he helped create the World War II Memorial in D.C., where he`ll be honored later today. We mention that President Clinton in attendance. Also there, are former Vice Presidents Cheney, and Pence, and Quayle.\par \par \uc2 Actor Tom Hanks is there, and we`ll be hearing from him in another service of -- at the World War II Memorial a bit later on. Members of the Leadership of the House and Senate also in attendance. And you saw my colleague Savannah Guthrie who`ll be, again, speaking later with that other memorial. We want to go right to NBC Senior White House Correspondent Kelly O`Donnell who`s at the National Cathedral. She covered Dole for a very long time, and I`m interested to hear your thoughts and observations. Kelly?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL NBC NEWS HOST: Good to be with you, Lester. One of the things that comes to mind when we see the nation saying goodbye to \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2  is that in the span of nearly a century of life, 98 years, he contributed so much. As a young man in World War II, suffering a tremendous injury in Italy. He won the Bronze Star. He was awarded the Purple Heart. He was a true war hero. And then, he turn that service after a very long convalesces into public service, local politics and then national politics. And then he had big ambitions to become president, to become vice president. He was on the ticket with Gerald Ford in `76. He ran three times himself. Ultimately becoming the nominee in 1996, losing to \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  in a very hard-fought battle.\par \par \uc2 But even in defeat, \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2  then turned that disappointment, painful disappointment into another kind of public service. He left the elective office. Put the campaigns behind him and then worked on advocacy and public contribution in other ways. One of the big lasting achievements is the work he put behind the World War II Memorial here in Washington, where there will be that public service later today. And in his later years, he spent so much time welcoming with those honor flights that diminishing generation of the greatest generation, those World War II veterans who would come to see their monument. Often, you would find \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2  there, leading them, talking with them.\par \par \uc2 We had a long personal relationship from covering his campaign. There would be times my cellphone would ring when I was covering another political event, and it would be \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2  checking in to give me his take on some of the matters of the day.\par \par \uc2 And in some ways, his passing is not only the passing of one remarkable American life, but of an era, a World War II veteran who served and ran for President. And now, really, a different kind of politics, he was a fierce partisan. He was very much in the hard scrabble world of Republican politics. But he also was about governing and has a long list of accomplishments that do affect the lives of Americans, the Americans with Disabilities Act, an international treaty for persons with disabilities. He worked to try to expand food stamps and to help with those who suffered poverty because of growing up in Kansas, he understood some of the real hard life experiences of the Dust Bowl and the great depression.\par \par \uc2 And along with his partner in life, Elizabeth Dole, who was a Senator herself from North Carolina and leader of the \uc2 American Red Cross\uc2 , public service was his legacy, and Lester, as you know from the many times, you dealt with him, his wit. He was always so funny and a dry sense of humor. And poked fun at his own defeats, his own shortcomings, and always brought humor to the political arena, as well. And he was someone who, once you were in his circle, you stayed in his circle, and that`s part of why this church behind me here at the National Cathedral is so full today.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Yes. A remarkable man, and you`re right, I spoke to him a few years ago at the World War II memorial. He reflected on his life, but in the end, he said, I`ve had a great life. I`ve had a great life. We`re also joined by NBC News Political Director and moderator of "MEET THE PRESS," Chuck Todd. Chuck, Kelly a moment ago talked about a diminishing generation with reference to the World War II generation. But this is also going to remind us of a diminishing generation within government, within the tone of government. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC NEWS HOST: You know, Lester, when I -- when I heard Kelly say that, first of all, I thought, you know, this is what I think has made Bob Dole`s passing a little bit harder for some of us.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020211211ehca00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Weighs Major Shift On Abortion\b0\par\par\uc2 Willie Geist, Ali Vitali \par \uc2 1,487 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 December 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [08:15:00] WILLIE GEIST: In January of 1973, the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  struck down a Texas statute that banned abortion. Setting a precedent in the case of Roe v. Wade and igniting legal and cultural battle that continues need.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, nearly a half century after that decision, the Supreme Court heard oral arguments in a challenge to a Mississippi law that bans most portions after 15 weeks of pregnancy. For Americans opposed to abortion, justices offer hope that the long-held goal of overturning Roe now may be within reach.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ali Vitali has our "Sunday Focus."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): A woman`s right to an abortion, now front and center at the Supreme Court where anti-abortion activists have wanted it for years. And where prochoice advocates have long-feared it would end up. The latest and perhaps more viable challenged, the landmark Roe v. Wade decision coming Dobs v. Jackson Women`s Health. Stemming from a 2018 Mississippi law that banned abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy. Challenging the standard set in Roe that allows abortion up to 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 REP. BECKY CURRIE; (R-MI): Life is so precious, and these babies have a chance now at 48 years ago they may not have.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): It comes as the court is majority conservative, transformed by three justices bitterly fought over but eventually appointed by Former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This confirmation process has become a national disgrace.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): The court now seeming poised to overturn or undermine Roe`s sweeping protections. Some justices signaling an openness to allow more restrictive measures without banning the practice altogether.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JUSTICE \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , \uc2 SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES\uc2 : If it really is an issue about choice, why is 15 weeks not enough time?\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): Others probing whether the court should assess this question at all.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE \uc2 BRETT M. KAVANAUGH\uc2 , \uc2 SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES\uc2 : The constitution is neither pro-life nor pro-choice on the question of abortion and they would say, therefore, it should be left to the people, to the states or to Congress.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): Liberal justices meanwhile warning of changing 50 years` worth of precedent and how a final decision could affect the court`s credibility as a nonpolitical body.\par \par \uc2 [08:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE \uc2 SONIA SOTOMAYOR\uc2 , \uc2 SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES\uc2 : Will this institution survive the stench that this creates in the public perception that the constitution and its reading are just political acts? I don`t see how it is possible."\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): Should Roe fall, it would trigger restrictive laws in more than half the states across the U.S. Effectively creating a reality where some women have access to care and others don`t. That disparity would hit low income and women of color hardest. Something the late Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  knew well.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE \uc2 RUTH BADER GINSBURG\uc2 , \uc2 SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES\uc2 : It hurts women who lack the means to go someplace else.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): The renewed debate comes as multiple states like Texas and Missouri issue restrictive abortion laws. Even as a majority of Americans say they favor at least some legal abortion. Democrats have responded by trying to shore up reproductive protections at the federal level.\par \par \uc2 Rep. Pramila Jayapal\uc2  (D-WA): We need to codify this into law.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (on camera): But it doesn`t look like that`s going to happen in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: I -- you know, I was on a text chain yesterday with members who feel like there is real possibility.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): Republican senator, \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 , back at the center of this debate after voting to confirm Justice Kavanaugh.\par \par \uc2 SEN. SUSAN COLLINS (R- ME): I do not believe that \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  will overturn --\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): Telling NBC News this week, she`s open to federally protecting abortion acts albeit in a more limited way.\par \par \uc2 These December Supreme Court arguments teeing up a bombshell springtime decision right in the middle of the 2022 midterms\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: I think that element of people trying to control our bodies is something that is going to reverberate with people across the country and into the ballot boxes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: And Ali joins me now live.\par \par \uc2 Ali, good morning.\par \par \uc2 As I said, this has been a cultural and political fight for generations. So, you touched on the midterm elections coming up next year. How might a sweeping change in abortion laws play out there?\par \par \uc2 VITALI (on camera): It could have a big impact, Willie, because in the midterms, Democrats believe this could galvanize their base, especially if Roe were to fall or its protections were to be chipped away at. For Democrats, they think it will underscore for voters why it`s so critical for them to keep and grow their congressional majorities.\par \par \uc2 But conservative voters could also see the fruits of their electoral labor in this too because Trump and Republicans have for decades run on putting conservative justices on the courts. And when it comes to the precedent this could set, there are also some who argue this could have ripple effects beyond abortion. Connecting this battle for the private right to choose to other court-decided issues like same sex marriage. Willie.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: We will find out in the span of the next few months. Ali Vitali, thank you so much. Ali, we appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next here, a "Sunday Sitdown" with Oscar winner, Javier Bardem, on playing Desi Arnaz to Nicole Kidman`s Lucille Ball in the anticipated new movie, "Being the Ricardos." And how that academy award for "No Country for Old Men" changed his life.\par \par \uc2 And then, a life well lived. The beloved golfer who broke down an old at the Augusta National Golf Club clearing a path for Tiger Woods to win the Masters two decades later.\par \par \uc2 And as we head to break, our "Photo of the week," a breathtaking site in the sky. A total solar eclipse visible only in Antarctica. The moon passed in front of the sun for just a few minutes to the delight of scientists there and presumably the penguins living in Antarctica.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:25:00]\par \par \uc2 JEFF SAPERSTONE, NBC10 BOSTON ANCHOR: And good morning, everyone. You`re watching NBC10 Boston. It is 8:26. I`m Jeff Saperstone. And here are some of your headlines right now.\par \par \uc2 This morning, Massachusetts has its first confirmed case of the Omicron variant. We are now joining more than a dozen states. Health officials say a woman in her 20s for Middlesex County recently traveled out of state. She has only experiencing mild symptoms. Experts anticipate seeing more cases in the state in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 Today is the final round of COVID vaccinations for kids at the Museum of Science. Children five to 11 that have already signed up will get the shot as well as two passes to the museum. Kids are encouraged to wear superhero costumes as well.\par \par \uc2 This morning, six adults and six children are without a home after a fire in Dorchester and we also know two firefighters were hurt trying to rescue a cat. It started around 8:30 last night on Geneva Avenue. The fire spread to all three floors. It`s not clear what sparked it. The firefighters have minor injuries.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get a check of the forecast. And here`s Tania.\par \par \uc2 LEAL: OK. Well, a high-pressure system will be in control this afternoon and that would be bringing dry conditions and temperatures that will be allowed to reach the 40s throughout the day. We`ll be watching a mix of sun and clouds. The winds remaining calm for the afternoon but gaining force as we get closer to the evening hours because we`re expecting changes. A warm front will be pushing in.\par \par \uc2 Temperatures will rising to the 60s, expecting moisture and some rain for the first half of the day tomorrow. Then we`re expecting a slight break, with another round of precipitation that could become wintry and windy through the afternoon. Another round of storms is expected for Wednesday and through the end of the week. This is your exclusive 10-day forecast. We`ll have all the details for you on NBC10 Boston.\par \par \uc2 SAPERSTONE: All right. Thanks so much for watching. We`ll see you back here in 30 minutes.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:30:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020211206ehc5000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Meet the Press for December 5, 2021, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Ashley Parker, Eugene Daniels, Errin Haines \par \uc2 9,667 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 December 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [09:00:22]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST (voice-over): This Sunday, the fate of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Life is so precious and these babies have a chance now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The Supreme Court signals it`s ready to allow an abortion ban at 15 weeks. Is it going to weaken or overturn Roe v. Wade?\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JUSTICE \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , \uc2 U.S. SUPREME COURT\uc2 : Is it really an issue about choice, why is 15 weeks not enough time?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Lawyers defending Roe argue that fetal viability should remain the standard --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mississippi`s ban on abortion two months before viability is flatly unconstitutional under decades of precedent.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- and liberal justices warn of damage to the court`s reputation. My guests this morning, Democratic Senator \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2  and Republican Senator \uc2 Mike Braun\uc2 . Plus, Omicron.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I`m going to fight this variant with science and speed, not chaos and confusion.\par \par \uc2 TODD: New travel restrictions amid concerns about transmissibility and how effective vaccines will be.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Because of the number of mutations that are seen, the concern is vaccines won`t work as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ll talk to the director of the National Institutes of Health, Dr. Francis Collins. Also, the parents of the boy accused of killing four high school classmates in Michigan are themselves charged with involuntary manslaughter.\par \par \uc2 KAREN MCDONALD, OAKLAND COUNTY PROSECUTOR: There was absolute reason to believe this individual was dangerous and disturbed.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They are now in jail for hiding from authorities after failing to appear in court. The entire family behind bars.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joining me for insight and analysis are "Washington Post" White House Bureau Chief Ashley Parker, "Playbook" co-author, Eugene Daniels, Errin Haines, editor-at-large of "The 19th," and Stephen Hayes, founder of "The Dispatch."\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday, it`s MEET THE PRESS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is MEET THE PRESS WITH CHUCK TODD.\par \par \uc2 TODD: A good Sunday morning. We`re going to take a look at the Supreme Court abortion case from two perspectives today, the practical and the political. What was clear from the arguments this past week is that a six- vote majority is ready to uphold Mississippi`s abortion ban at 15 weeks before viability. But what`s still at issue is will the court just weaken or will they fully overturn Roe v. Wade?\par \par \uc2 Roughly half the states are certain or very likely to make abortion illegal if it`s a full overturn and it unravels nearly 50 years of constitutionally protected abortion rights in the country. And abortion rights advocates concede the fight to win back those rights can be counted in decades, not years.\par \par \uc2 At the same time there are political consequences as well. The case could actually brighten what is a grim outlook right now for Democrats in 2022. The court has long been an energizing issue for Republican voters. Could the neutering of Roe now energize angry Democrats and women in general as we head toward the midterm elections?\par \par \uc2 The abortion debate is perhaps the longest running example of an increasingly divided America, split over guns, voting rights, education, climate, and lately vaccine requirements. But none of those divisive issues have the emotional power and relevance of abortion. And none so rested on one election. All elections have consequences, but three of the justices ready to undo Roe were appointed to the court by President Trump, reminding us that the 2016 election will continue to have consequences in American law for decades to come.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD (voice-over): Activists are bracing for a new battle over one of the nation`s starkest political divides.\par \par \uc2 NANCY NORTHUP\uc2 , PRESIDENT, \uc2 CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS\uc2 : This case is about is whether the Supreme Court is going to adhere to an almost 50-year precedent of respecting the individual liberty to make decisions about pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 CAROL TOBIAS, PRESIDENT, NATIONAL RIGHT TO LIFE COMMITTEE: Unborn babies are human beings, and they should be protected as any human being would.\par \par \uc2 TODD: In arguments on Wednesday, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  signaled he might uphold the Mississippi law without explicitly overturning Roe.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: If it is an issue about choice, why is 15 weeks not enough time?\par \par \uc2 TODD: But he appeared to be alone as other members of the court`s conservative majority went much further. Republican Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  has defender her vote for Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEN. SUSAN COLLINS (R-ME): He noted repeatedly that Roe had been upheld by Planned Parenthood versus Casey, describing it as precedent on precedent.\par \par \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2 , JUDGE, U.S. COURT OF APPEALS: I said that it`s settled as a precedent of the Supreme Court, entitled the respect under principles of stare decisis.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:03]\par \par \uc2 TODD: But now --\par \par \uc2 KAVANAUGH: If we think that the prior precedents are seriously wrong, if that, why then doesn`t the history of this court`s practice with respect to those cases tell us that the right answer is actually a return to the position of neutrality?\par \par \uc2 TODD: On Wednesday, the Democratic appointees on the court tried to appeal to their colleagues` concerns about the Court`s political standing.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE \uc2 SONIA SOTOMAYOR\uc2 , U.S. SUPREME COURT ASSOCIATE JUSTICE: If people actually believe that it`s all political, how will we survive? How will the Court survive?\par \par \uc2 TODD: A majority of Americans believe abortion should be legal in all or most cases. Just 32 percent favor overturning Roe, 58 percent oppose. If Roe is overturned, 12 states have explicit trigger laws which would immediately ban abortion. Experts say some two dozen states are either certain or very likely to make the procedure illegal and more could do so.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What would happen if the Supreme Court overturned Roe, for you?\par \par \uc2 SARAH HAEDER, ABORTION CLINIC NURSE: For us that would mean that we would be closing within the next 30 days.\par \par \uc2 TODD: With the future of Roe in doubt, the consequences of the 2016 election loom even larger.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (R), FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We`re going to appoint judges who will support, defend and uphold the constitution.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2  (D), FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Do we want him appointing our judges?\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  cemented his legacy with three new Supreme Court justices. Now, the court`s decision will come just months before the midterm elections and could reshape a political environment which now favors Republicans.\par \par \uc2 RICH LOWRY, EDITOR, NATIONAL REVIEW: The reaction to an overturn will be thermo nuclear. And it is the foreseeable event that has the greatest chance of changing the trajectory of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JEANNE SHAHEEN (D-NH): If you want to see a revolution go ahead, outlaw Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joining me now is Democratic Senator \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2  of Minnesota. Senator Klobuchar, welcome back to MEET THE PRESS.\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR\uc2  (D-MN): Thanks, Chuck. Great to be on again.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`ve been on the front lines of the Judiciary Committee of all of these actors that were inside the Supreme Court. So, I want to start with there`s - I think, from your perspective two potential bad outcomes that may come out of this. And I guess the question is, which of the bad outcomes, in your view, would you prefer? A total overturn of Roe, or what Chief Justice Roberts seems to be hinting at -- something that keeps Roe in name only or keeps some provision, but still allows the 15-week ban?\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: Ok. First of all, I don`t think that`s going to happen. It appeared to me, listening to that argument, that the conservative justices were in a different place than Justice Roberts, that they clearly are headed toward overturning Roe. And in either case, even if you go with Justice Roberts` idea, so 14 weeks, 13 weeks, 12 weeks, it`s a mess. You literally are going to go back to a time, if we don`t do anything -- I`ll get to that in a minute -- you`re going to go back to a time of back alley abortions. You`re going to go back to people -- people are going to be busing from one state to another.\par \par \uc2 Seventy-five percent of Americans believe that this decision should be made between a woman and her doctor, 75 percent of Americans. This wasn`t a case where the court was going with the changing mores of society, no. They are on their own there.\par \par \uc2 Raw political power pushed those new justices onto the court, and this is going to be the outcome. And I would agree with your run up to this. This is going to be about Americans standing up to assert their rights.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You don`t believe if they keep -- if they allow the Mississippi law to stand, within the framework of Roe, do you believe that means Roe is still -- is still legitimate - is still -- is still relevant case law at that point?\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: Technically, yes. But in reality what we`ve seen with these states -- I mean, in Texas, they`ve literally enacted a law that says that neighbors can spy on neighbors and can be bounty hunters going after women just for exercising their right for healthcare. That`s not the America that 80 percent of Americans want to see right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What do you make of Justice Kavanaugh`s argument that the Court should be neutral on abortion and essentially saying it should be up to the politicians? And I want to point something out. There`s really only three countries in the world that have abortion rights that were sort of given to them by the courts. It`s us, Canada, and Mexico. Most other --\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: Pretty significant countries.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No doubt -- most other countries, though, have done it either via their legislatures or via referendum. Should this be done that way? Is leaving it to the courts put us in a more vulnerable position on this?\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: I would turn this the other way. Fifty years of precedent, as Elena Kagan pointed out, 50 years of decisions and court decisions, part of the very fabric of women`s existence in this country. This is how our country protected rights. And now they`re willing to just flip it on its head.\par \par \uc2 And so, what is the answer? The answer may well be doing it through the political process now. I don`t think that`s the right thing to do, but it may be the way to do it. And I think the best way to do it is not a patchwork of state laws, but to put it, codify Roe v. Wade, put it into law. And we even have some pro-choice Republicans that have signaled interest in doing that.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:03]\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to put up the Minnesota law. And I`m just curious if you think this is a way to start as a baseline. The restrictions that are most notable in Minnesota which has, I think, the state constitution guarantees a right to abortion in Minnesota. That state-directed counseling, followed by a 24-hour waiting period, parental notification for minors, and I believe it`s 20 - I don`t know if I get that right - 24 weeks that you`re allowed to have an abortion. Is that a -- is that what you think the federal baseline should be essentially?\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: Actually, I think the federal baseline is the bill that we`ve put together, the Women`s Health Protection Act, which would basically put Roe v. Wade into law. But Minnesota, I am glad that that is in our law. I think we have long believed that people have the choice. We have one of the highest rates of international adoption. That`s one choice. Another choice is being a mom. Another choice is actually making a decision with your doctor that you`re going to terminate a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Those are choices in our state. But I don`t think that having state by state by state -- and literally you know very well, Chuck, based on what we`ve seen out of Texas and Alabama, that a huge swath of the country women are going to be denied that right to make their own choice. And I don`t think that`s the answer. I think the answer is putting it into federal law. Of course, the ultimate answer would be to uphold 50 years of precedent and the court doing the right thing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. This bill needs 60 votes in the Senate. Is there a way, besides getting rid of the filibuster, to lower that threshold? Could you - - could you find a way with Medicaid or Medicare funding, different ways to make it a 50-vote threshold?\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: Well, as you know, we`re looking at the rules of the Senate right now, and in the context of the all important issue of the Freedom to Vote Act, the bill I lead that has every Democrat in the Senate supporting it. And one of the things we`ve realized is that -- I would abolish the filibuster but even if you keep the filibuster in place, over time there have been 160 some carve-outs to the filibuster. The rules have changed over and over. Even Robert Byrd himself said that rules should change to fit the circumstances of their time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, how would you change the rule for codifying Roe?\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: Well, this would be some change to the Senate rules and it most likely would not be this particular issue. Right now, we`re really focused on voting rights.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You think voting rights is the carve-out (CROSSTALK) --\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: But it would be -- it would be a standing filibuster, a way to actually put this out there for the American public so that people would be required to be in the chamber and actually debate things, and come up with an outcome at some point. Because right now, if the Republicans want to go into this election, which I think it does look at where we`re heading, the American people are going to decide.\par \par \uc2 Do they want to go with a political party that has gotten them through this pandemic, that has gotten the vaccines out, that believes in science over misinformation on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 ? Or are they going to go with the political party that`s trampling on the freedom of women`s rights, trampling on the freedom to vote, will not vote to reduce prescription drug costs, will not be there for your childcare? There are going to be - all of this is going to be on the ballot in this election.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you, though, some of the political ramifications. This is something you said during your presidential campaign about pro-life Democrats. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: I believe we`re a big tent party and there are pro-life Democrats and they are part of our party. And I think we need to build a big tent. I think we need to bring people in instead of shutting them out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, I think one of the reasons why we were -- had stability on this issue is that, in the `70s and `80s, a third of Democrats were pro- life, a third of Republicans were pro-choice. And there was this sort of, you know, freeze if you will. Now, there`s a pretty stark partisan divide. Does that make it harder to have pro-life Democrats in the party?\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: I don`t think so. We still have people -- they come up to me all the time in my state, saying, I don`t agree with you on that issue, but I agree with you on this issue. I still think there is clearly room in our party for pro-life Democrats. You also have people -- many people -- that is their personal view, but they don`t believe that they should impose it on other people, that people should be able to make their own choices.\par \par \uc2 So, my view on that hasn`t changed. But what I think you`ve seen change is the Supreme Court in a very dramatic fashion, when raw politics got these last justices on and you see the raw political outcome.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you now more open to packing the court?\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: I`ve always been open to looking at the numbers of justices on the court, but I think the most sane route to get this done right now would be to bring this up before the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  to codify Roe v. Wade into law.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is that something that you think is a debate that`s going to happen sooner rather than later? Or do you have to wait until June?\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: Well, let`s see what the court does first.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Senator \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2 , Democrat from Minnesota. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: It was great to be on, Chuck. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Thanks very much. And joining me now, from the other side of the aisle, is Republican Senator \uc2 Mike Braun\uc2  of Indiana. Senator Braun, welcome back to MEET THE PRESS, sir.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MIKE BRAUN\uc2  (R-IN): My pleasure, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, let me start -- I`m going to start with the abortion laws in your home state. Some of the most -- some would argue some are somewhat restrictive from the perspective of a person seeking an abortion.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:05]\par \par \uc2 But I want to put them down here as a baseline. In Indiana, there is state- directed, in-person counseling. You can`t do telemedicine. There`s an 18- hour waiting period. There`s a physical exam and an ultrasound. Parental consent for minors. And the ban begins at 22 weeks, two weeks shorter than in many other states that are at 24.\par \par \uc2 You have said you are 100 percent pro-life. Is the law in Indiana, in your view, strong enough to support your pro-life position?\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: Chuck, I think it begs the question, in general, whether our state is where it needs to be, whether Mississippi, whether New York, Illinois. You hit it on the head earlier, political or practical. When it comes to issues like this that divide our country in a way to where we`re never going to get the 60 votes on any of this stuff, I think the practical solution is, when it`s not enumerated, return it to the states.\par \par \uc2 The beauty of our system is that it`s federal. It`s got all of these different ideas. And when you try to nationalize, federalize the way the other side of the aisle is doing on more than just this, I think you`re constantly in that area of contention. And when I got here three years ago, of course we`ve gone through two impeachments, we`ve gone through the biggest health care crisis, a lot of these issues don`t even get discussed, like the high cost of health care, maybe climate, maybe budgeting. How do we pay for all of this?\par \par \uc2 When it comes to things like abortion, I think it`s clear it`s time to turn it back to the states, let the diversity of this country show forth. It eliminates a lot of the contention to where we become the Hatfields and McCoys on many of these issues.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, should -- when do you believe abortion should be available? Because you`re going to have to vote on these issues now. Every elected official in the country is going to have to state very specifically now. You know, a lot of times elected officials have been able to hide behind Roe. Either you`re for it or against it. But now you`ve got to get to specifics. So, in your view, when should abortion be legal or be available?\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: So, in my case, when you believe in the sanctity of life, you want abortions to be eliminated from the landscape if you can. You also, in the context of reasonability, and the fact is, regardless of what any one individual, maybe with the point-of-view of mine or of Amy`s, you`re not going to settle it in a homogenized way. And that needs to reflect itself throughout states. And whatever one`s opinion is, whatever you`re trying to do politically, why try to do it at the national level?\par \par \uc2 We generally don`t do things well here anyway. And on something as contentious as this, it seems like it would make common sense, it would be practical and not political, to send it back to the states and let every state do what they want to do and live with it.\par \par \uc2 And if you don`t like it, then go to work within those legislatures. It was never enumerated in the constitution that this would be something that would be done at the federal level. And it takes the decibel level, the caustic nature of politics as we see it here in D.C. takes it to another place.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I understand it. You don`t believe, though, there should be a baseline protection of a woman being able to get -- have an access -- have access to abortion? You don`t believe there should be any baseline protection for that?\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: I think it`s a difference, again, without the specificity of this issue, is when you come down to things like this why you want to nationalize that in, say, your Bill of Rights, so to speak. It was never intended.\par \par \uc2 When you have a judicial ruling like occurred back in `73 and `92, you know, that was a judicial ruling. And now it`s being said to be constitutional. And that`s where you get into the contention, into the highly vitriolic nature of politics. And I think that ought to be the domain of the states. That`s the way the constitution intended it to be.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. So you want to see a ban on abortion. How would you enforce a ban on abortion?\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: You`re going to let that up to the individual states. And you might find that right mix. I`m not saying we got it right in Indiana. I`m saying -- it`s not right in New York. Maybe you find that happy medium, that place where more people are going to say --\par \par \uc2 TODD: What`s your place? What`s your idea? What`s your idea?\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: My idea is that you -- is with what`s happened with technology and when you look at all the things that we know now about the unborn, it needs to be different. It needs to be different from where it is.\par \par \uc2 And I don`t have the silver bullet. I don`t intend to put it out there. I just think you`ve got a better idea of getting where you need to be not trying to homogenize it at the national level.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It sounds like you`re uncomfortable with figuring out how you would enforce the ban because I think that`s a question.\par \par \uc2 [09:20:02]\par \par \uc2 Do you criminalize abortion? Would you criminalize it?\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: I`m perfectly comfortable with doing it just not at the level where everybody`s got to live with the same thing. And when you talk about criminalizing it then all you`re doing is taking this to a logical extreme that you`ll never get to anyway. We just need to take it off of where it is, send it back to the states. Let`s find that right way to address it. And this applies to many other issues as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I want to ask because one of the issues -- look, I originally invited you on to talk about the vaccine mandate. And you`re -- you`re a -- you`re a vigorous opponent of the federal government`s private sector vaccine mandate. And you`re worried about the liberty of the unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 What about the liberty of the woman who doesn`t want to carry a pregnancy to term? Why should the government force that? You don`t want the government to force people to get a vaccine. You`re essentially advocating for the government to force a woman to carry a pregnancy to term that she may not want to do.\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: You might try to create that as an issue of equivalency; I don`t. In this case of a vaccine mandate -- and I think clearly the turf I chose to pursue would be doing it through the Congressional Review Act. That was set up for the intended purpose of an executive branch that got into overdrive. And I chose to go that route and got all other Republican senators on board quickly.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s bipartisan. \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  decided to come on and not do it tacitly, to where he`s going to be outspoken about it. To me, that is different. And if you try to make those equivalent I think you`re going to get into that current paradigm we`re in to where you`re arguing about things that just divide us.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right --\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: In this case, it does go to -- and when you -- when you couch it -- and just a moment --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: When you couch it as getting the vaccine or lose your job, and we talked about it the other day. Well, you`ve got the ability to be tested weekly. In certain parts of the country, those are one and the same, along with the ultimatum that goes along with how this was done through OSHA.\par \par \uc2 In what other issue does this draw us into down the road unrelated to where you have the heavy hand of government? Here I`m saying on the abortion issue, take it back to the states where I think the constitution intended it to be.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Finally, though, on the vaccine mandate front, you are worried about forcing the unvaccinated in that situation. What about protecting the vaccinated? What about the freedom of the vaccinated to get through this pandemic?\par \par \uc2 And the only way we`re going to get through it is if we get more people vaccinated. And the mandates have worked. I know you don`t like them, but they`ve worked.\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: Well, the mandates have worked if you think that -- in my case, when I look at how this all started back in March of 2020 and we had that heavy hand, even at many local levels, of you`re an essential business, you`re not. I was clear upfront, lucky we`ve got a vaccination, of course there were arguments about who was going to orchestrate it. We got it out quickly. And transmission itself and everything we`ve learned about COVID. It is a formidable foe.\par \par \uc2 Businesses took it seriously from the get-go, small, medium, and large. They wanted to protect their customers, wanted to keep their employees safe. Again, it comes back to do you want things orchestrated at the federal level, where we don`t create results that are sustainable, or do you want to bring this back to a lower level of authority? And that has nothing to do with what you think about the vaccine or the disease and how you fight it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator Braun, I appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective, a reminder that there is still a sort of basic divide in this country, more government versus less government. I appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us, sir.\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: I`d agree with that. Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, I`m going to talk to Dr. Francis Collins. He`s head of the \uc2 NIH\uc2 , about the new variant. How soon before we know how dangerous Omicron really is?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:26:15]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The new Omicron coronavirus variant has now been identified in 16 states across the country, as scientists try to learn how transmissible and dangerous it actually is. A new study says Omicron may have picked up some genetic material from the virus that causes the common cold. And that could create greater transmissibility, but perhaps it makes the variant less likely to cause severe symptoms. It`s a question. One result of Omicron`s emergence? A surge in demand for vaccines, including a six-month high on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the Director of the National Institutes of Health. It`s Dr. Francis Collins. Dr. Collins, good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 DR. FRANCIS COLLINS, DIRECTOR, NATIONAL INSTITUTES OF HEALTH: Nice to be with you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with what researchers are learning. Every day, it seems like we learn a little something new about Omicron. So, this development, this idea that it is fused with the common cold it -- I can`t believe I`m saying this, could this be good news, weirdly enough?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Well, it`s a bit of a stretch.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes. OK. I`ll take good news right now but I`m not sure I can claim that this is going to be reliable. It is a bit of a stretch to look at the letters of the instruction book, the genome, of this Omicron variant and try to predict how it`s going to behave when it infects people around the globe.\par \par \uc2 But it is interesting. It looks as if this is a recombinant that has both part of the SARS-CoV-2 that`s undergone a lot of mutations, and maybe also it`s crossed over and picked up a bit of another virus.\par \par \uc2 I`m not sure everybody buys this particular conclusion that popped out in a pre-print but it`s an interesting observation. Much more important, though, is to find out what`s really happening. In the real world, does this in fact turn out to be less dangerous? And how good are our vaccines and our boosters in providing protection? Because we think they`re probably going to be useful, but just how useful? That`s an answer everybody wants.\par \par \uc2 The scientists are all over this. We were kind of expecting that this would come along at some point. People are hard at work, 24/7, to get those answers.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It seems that a lot of scientists seem to be surprised at how much this virus has mutate -- or how fast this virus has mutated. So, I guess what`s your sense of where we`re headed? You know -- look our -- I know we need to be vaccinated more around the world. But realistically, are we -- should we expect essentially a new dominant variant every six months? I mean, we got Delta before that. I mean, are we in that kind of pattern here?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: It`s certainly possible that this is not the last emerging variant that will attract a lot of attention and a lot of concern. This one does have the largest number of mutations that we`ve seen so far, Omicron, with about 50 mutations, compared to the original Wuhan virus. And it looks as if they probably arose in an immunocompromised individual, this is a hypothesis but it seems plausible, who wasn`t able to completely fight off the virus. And so, it remained in the system, maybe for months, in that person until they finally got over it.\par \par \uc2 And that is, of course, a perfect situation for the virus to be able to pick up additional mutations along the way. To the extent that that`s going to keep happening if we don`t have adequate immune protection across the globe, yes, we`re probably going to see something and we`ll have to use some of the other letters in the Greek alphabet.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We are approaching obviously an extraordinarily busy travel season. Given the variant and given the fact that we have a Delta surge in the north, I should show some headlines here to our viewers. I know you`re fully aware of it. This week alone on the Delta surge, Michigan hit a new record for hospitalizations. They ran out of beds at a hospital in Boston.\par \par \uc2 [09:30:00]\par \par \uc2 We`re at our third federal team to assist Minnesota hospitals with COVID- 19. There`s clearly -- we`ve got a Delta issue.\par \par \uc2 Is there anything about our travel -- about our travel over the next four weeks that has you concerned? And would you advise folks to maybe rethink their travel plans?\par \par \uc2 DR. FRANCIS COLLINS, DIRECTOR, NATIONAL INSTITUTES OF HEALTH: Well, I think people need to pay close attention to all those recommendations that maybe we`ve gotten a little sloppy about.\par \par \uc2 In terms of domestic air travel, I think with masks required on all travel conveyances, whether it`s air or whether it`s taxis or trains, that`s going to be a very important thing to stick to. But I think it`s more where you`re going to be when you travel and what kind of gatherings are going to happen.\par \par \uc2 And here`s where the CDC`s recommendations, which are still in place for 80 percent-plus of counties, is that if you`re indoors, you should be wearing a mask with other people who may or may not be vaccinated. That`s the best way to protect yourself.\par \par \uc2 We had a little holiday party on my cul-de-sac last night. And the people who organized it said you`re not coming to our house unless you prove you`re vaccinated and you got a test that day. So, yes, here`s my test. I`m glad to say I was negative.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well --\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: That is maybe the kind of thing for holiday gatherings we should be doing more of as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, let me tell you -- ask you about testing. I put together a mash of all the promises we`ve been made about rapid, fast testing that was going to be available. Let me play a little bit of it for you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIPS)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved the first at-home COVID- 19 test kit.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE\uc2 , FORMER VICE PRESIDENT: And we continue to approve new tests, including an at-home test.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I`ll use the Defense Production Act to increase production of rapid tests, including those that you can use at home.\par \par \uc2 This winter, we`re going to make free at-home tests more available to Americans than ever before.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIPS)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, testing has been -- I feel like it`s, it`s, you know, the anchor that we`ve been dragging from the very beginning here. But what has made it so difficult to fulfill that promise?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Well, we have come a long way, let`s be fair, Chuck. And \uc2 NIH\uc2  has been in this in a big way. There are now eight home tests that are out there, approved by \uc2 FDA\uc2 , available. Go to your pharmacy. You`ll see them on the shelves. And those are pretty easy --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Pretty pricey.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: -- like the one I just showed you. Fifteen minutes and you get an answer.\par \par \uc2 They are pricey and that`s one of the things the president`s trying to do about this right now; announcing this week that basically if you get one of these home tests, you can file with your insurance company and get reimbursed for it. And if you don`t have that kind of coverage, some 50 million tests being distributed out in places like food banks and community health centers to make it possible for people to have access to them for free.\par \par \uc2 We at \uc2 NIH\uc2 , we`re running pilot tests in seven states to see what happens if you just make it possible for people to order these for free from \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . We`ll see how that plays out.\par \par \uc2 I get it. We ought to have testing as easily accessible as possible and as cheap as possible, because it is a good way to protect yourself.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And I want to ask you about the issue of international travel versus domestic travel. If someone flies to Washington, D.C., over the holidays, it`s a popular tourist destination, from Singapore where 96 percent of the eligible population is vaccinated, that person has to show proof of vaccination and a negative test.\par \par \uc2 But someone flying to D.C. from Idaho, where less than half of the population is fully vaccinated, doesn`t have to do either. How is that at all logical about protecting our community?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Well, you know, I think, Chuck, we`re trying to be practical here. If you tried to impose those kinds of restrictions on domestic air travel, that would be extremely onerous for people who are trying to get around the country for things like holidays.\par \par \uc2 And I don`t know how much we`d gain by it. If we`re worried about whether you`re trying to protect some community from Delta, well, Delta`s all over the place right now. There`s not some chance that you`re going to see it spread by air travel that`s not happening already in communities.\par \par \uc2 So, I think we`ve got it about right. I do think we have an investment in making sure people aren`t bringing new cases, especially when we have things like Omicron to worry about. Hence, the importance of requiring international travelers to be vaccinated and to show they`ve had a negative test. I think that`s the right balance.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dr. Francis Collins, really appreciate you coming out and sharing your expertise with us, sir.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Glad to be with you, Chuck. Thanks for getting the word out there. Everybody, get vaccinated. And if you are already vaccinated, get boosted. It`s time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Please listen to him. He`s a doctor.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Dr. Collins.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, back to the abortion case. Could overturning Roe overturn our assumptions about the midterm elections? Panel is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:37:33]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Panel is here: "Washington Post" White House Bureau Chief Ashley Parker, Playbook co-author Eugene Daniels, Stephen Hayes, founder of The Dispatch; and Errin Haines, editor-at-large of "The 19th." And if you don`t know about "The 19th," you should, but it is an independent news organization dedicated to covering women in politics.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 I want to start with the Senator Braun interview because -- and Steve, I`m going to start on the right side of the aisle on this. What I sensed from Senator Braun was a discomfort on the issue as a whole.\par \par \uc2 He is -- he is 100 percent pro-life. He campaigned on it as a senator. But he did seem uncomfortable being definitive.\par \par \uc2 What does that tell us about the concern on the right about the politics of this?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN HAYES, CEO & EDITOR, THE DISPATCH: Yeah. I agree with you that he did seem uncomfortable talking about it. I don`t know that that necessarily represents a broader lack of willingness or eagerness among conservatives to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 When you look at the polling on the issue, look at Gallup Polling from May, 48 percent of the country describes itself as pro-life, 49 percent as pro- choice. You`ve got Republicans and conservatives who have been making the case for this moment, for the challenging of Roe v. Wade, because they believe that Roe v. Wade is bad law, that these are made up rights, and are perfectly willing to have that argument.\par \par \uc2 I think when you get into some of the details of the questioning and what comes next in a post-Roe world is where Republican policy makers are less comfortable talking about it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Errin, I got the sense, like you`re describing -- what he`s hoping for, I`m, like, "You`re kind of describing what Roe has allowed for," this uneasy peace that we have on this issue.\par \par \uc2 ERRIN HAINES, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yeah. The thing is, watching that interview, yes, knowing where the rest of the country is on this issue, I mean, you have that polling showing that, you know, the majority of Americans feel like there should be, you know, abortion should be allowed, with some exceptions. That, you know, it should not be illegal, or illegal with few exceptions.\par \par \uc2 Most people are not in favor of that. And so when you have that, I think that may be perhaps what some of his hesitancy is on being more vocal about it. But it`s not so much what Republicans are saying, it`s what they`re doing, right?\par \par \uc2 This is what conservatives have been pushing for. This was the long game on abortion, that is absolutely what their voters, you know, use their voice at the ballot box to push for, and what the former president pushed for. And what we`re seeing now is kind of the fruition of that.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:01]\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY PARKER, NBC NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: And the Braun interview is fascinating, but in some ways, a more interesting case study is someone like Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 , right, who voted to approve Justice Kavanaugh, said she believed his promises when -- promises, of course, when he was trying to earn a Supreme Court seat, and will say what the senators want to hear, that Roe was settled law. Then when she was asked about the oral arguments of the Court, she said she didn`t see them, which sort of feels like the version of, "I didn`t see the tweet," the Supreme Court version of that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: And now, she has come out and said she believes in Roe, she believes it should be codified. She does not sign on to support a Democratic bill which would do just that because it goes too far. But she has said she is working for other bipartisan options. She will introduce her own legislation.\par \par \uc2 But the way Congress is divided right now, that legislation - bipartisan, her own - does not have the votes to pass.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m guessing she`s glad that she`s up in `20 and wasn`t up in `22.\par \par \uc2 HAINES: I mean, and you saw, when she was up, what does she do? She votes against Amy Coney Barrett, which is absolutely an issue in her Senate rate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Eugene, the thing that interests me about a post-Roe world is how many elected officials got to duck their position on abortion for basically my entire lifetime. They got to basically say they`re for Roe or against Roe. Now, you have to find out he didn`t want to - well, do you criminalize it? You know, it`s when you start to get at - I didn`t get to the morning after pill. I didn`t have time to get to that in the interview, but you start to go into the specifics of reproductive rights law. This is where I think you start to open the Pandora`s Box.\par \par \uc2 EUGENE DANIELS, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Right. And that`s why it`s so complicated. The place where he was most uncomfortable when you guys were talking was what happens next. And that is exactly what people have been talking about who are abortion advocates, is that, okay, so you don`t back Roe v. Wade, what happens after that? And what does the country look like?\par \par \uc2 It looks bifurcated. You look in one state where you can get an abortion, and another where a woman could be arrested for getting an abortion.\par \par \uc2 There`s also the question that`s even more complicated than it was in the `90s when Roe seemed like it might be on the chopping block, which is the abortion pill, right? You can mail an abortion pill to women. So if a woman gets an abortion pill in a state that doesn`t allow abortion, who`s going to look in that mail and say, oh, this is a problem?\par \par \uc2 And for folks like conservatives who`ve talked a lot about, you know, not having a police state, I talked to some abortion advocates over this week, they say, well, isn`t that exactly what they say they`re against, a police state where people are checking the mail for abortion pills?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: But I don`t think the politics of this are that clear. I mean, look at what happened in Virginia, right? Look at the election there, the gubernatorial election there. You had \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  run a ton of ads, expensive ads, spent a lot of money making abortion a big issue. He said it was going to be one of the number one issues. I would argue that he mischaracterized Glenn Youngkin`s position quite a bit.\par \par \uc2 He makes this argument. Exit polls suggest that only 8 percent of Virginia voters thought it was the most important issue when they cast their ballots.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Fifty-nine percent of those voted for Glenn Youngkin.\par \par \uc2 This was in the context of a big discussion of the Texas abortion ruling. This is in the context of what we knew was going to be this case coming up. I`m not sure it`s as cut and dry or pro-Democrat or not.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But see - what this brings up, Errin, is that what we`ve seen is the right is motivated.\par \par \uc2 HAINES: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The left hasn`t been.\par \par \uc2 What is it going to take to motivate the left on this issue? The actual overturn itself?\par \par \uc2 HAINES: Yeah, that`s a really good question, Chuck, because to your point, I think that Democrats were lulled into this kind of false sense of security because you didn`t have a conservative court for so long. They thought that this was settled law, thought that this was precedent.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, you had people like Kennedy to uphold it. You know, it looked like --\par \par \uc2 HAINES: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TODD: - it was bipartisan. And those - by the way, that was in the era of a third of Democrats were pro-life in the elected Congress and a third of Republicans were pro-choice in the elected Congress.\par \par \uc2 HAINES: Yeah. And that`s the thing, right? I mean, elections have consequences. I think Republicans certainly understand that. Democrats are kind of beginning to understand what that means, especially around the Supreme Court, whether you`re talking about abortion, whether you`re talking about issues like voting rights, whether you`re talking about gun rights. Democrats are starting to see that when you have, you know, a Court that is more conservative, that is absolutely going to challenge some of these things that they thought were kind of settled law.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, I`m a little long, so I`m not going to play the bite. But Justice Kagan`s argument about overturning precedent was - she was saying, there has to be a groundswell, and that`s missing here. And she`s worried about the politicizing of the Court.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the left is now panicked that Justice Breyer is going to not retire, right? That conversation doesn`t help the politicizing issue, does it?\par \par \uc2 PARKER: It sure doesn`t. And the Court has been politicized from former President Trump, talking about "my justices," very clearly making the argument that he wanted the 2020 election to get kicked to the Supreme Court because his justices would hand it to him.\par \par \uc2 TODD: His justices.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: His justices would hand it to him. So whether or not the Court is politicized, you saw the justices in oral arguments raise that concern. You see polling that shows the public believe it. At a certain point, it`s something like the Court perception is reality.\par \par \uc2 HAINES: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah. You know what? The minute you allowed a president to appoint Supreme Court justices, you politicized the court. It`s kind of our Founders sort of decided that.\par \par \uc2 [09:45:02]\par \par \uc2 Anyway, when we come back, why do more than half of younger Americans think our democracy has failed or is in trouble?\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Data Download time and a look at our country`s future as seen by the folks that will shape it, younger Americans.\par \par \uc2 Harvard`s Kennedy School released a poll of 18 to 29-year-olds this week. Let`s just say there was not a lot of youthful optimism to be found. Look at their views on democracy.\par \par \uc2 Is democracy working well? Only 24 percent, one in four young Americans, believe that is true. 64 percent disagree with that statement.\par \par \uc2 As for the U.S. democracy in particular, is it healthy and somewhat functioning? Just 34 percent of voters under 30 think that.\par \par \uc2 Is it failed or in trouble? A majority of young folks think that`s the case in the U.S. democracy. I`m guessing a lot of you out there may agree with them.\par \par \uc2 As for President Biden approval rating, this is where we can show you that, even among voters under 30, there is an education divide like there is all across the electorate. If you have a college degree or you`re in college, you have a net positive rating on the president. By the way, this 54-44 isn`t a great number for the president, but it`s a net positive among young voters with a college degree. If you have no college degree, you disapprove of this president 57 percent, very similarly to older parts of the electorate.\par \par \uc2 There is a mild branding problem here for the Republican Party. When you look at this whole group of voters, more are comfortable identifying as conservative than they are as Republican, an eight point difference there. President Trump, by the way, not very popular among anybody in this age group. If you look down on the left side of the electorate here, 39 percent identify as liberal, 39 percent as Democrat. A little more of cohesion there.\par \par \uc2 There is one issue that cuts across ideological lines and it appears to be the issue of climate change, and is it going to impact some of the decisions you make in your life. Among all 18 to 29-year-olds, 70 percent of college degrees say it, 57 percent of folks in college, and a majority of folks not -- who don`t have a college degree also say climate change is going to impact some of the decisions that they make. So there, a bit of unanimity.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, a mass shooting at a high school.\par \par \uc2 [09:50:01]\par \par \uc2 The parents of the alleged shooter charged with involuntary manslaughter. We`re going to discuss this sad case and a rare prosecution next. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAREN MCDONALD, OAKLAND COUNTY PROSECUTOR: While the shooter was the one who entered the high school and pulled the trigger, there are other individuals who contributed to the events on November 30th. And it`s my intention to hold them accountable as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 That was Karen McDonald, the Oakland County, Michigan prosecutor who charged the parents of the boy accused of shooting and killing four students with involuntary manslaughter.\par \par \uc2 You know, Ashley, it`s interesting here with this school shooting. There`s always been talk of, you know, should parents be held accountable for when kids get a hold of guns. This prosecutor feels like she found enough evidence to actually do it. But we`ve not seen something like this. This feels like we are entering new territory.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: That`s right. It`s incredibly rare. I think an important point is she did not charge them simply because they were the parents of the shooter. She charged them because there was a whole litany of evidence, from buying him that gun for Christmas, taking him to the shooting range, when he got caught looking at ammo and guns at school, the mother texting him basically saying: LOL. I`m not mad at you. Just don`t get caught next time.\par \par \uc2 But you`re right. The overall point remains, which is this will be incredibly closely followed because, regardless, even if this is a rare instance, it will send such a message either way, depending on what that final outcome is.\par \par \uc2 [09:55:05]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Everyone -- a school shooting that gets a lot of attention, Eugene, gets at least some new rhetoric on the Hill to do something. I`m going to guess the safe gun protection law might be about the one place that you could get somewhere, right?\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: No, absolutely, because you`ve talked about, like, responsible gun owners, right? That is the place in which Republicans and Democrats can find some common ground. It`s still hard to see how you have a conversation about gun control or gun safety with this current Congress and to go anywhere, especially in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 But the lack of federal gun legislation has forced prosecutors, like in this case and others, to try to figure out ways to make people be more responsible, figure out ways to have some type of run-around on the lack of gun --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, in Michigan, there is no safe keeping law. That`s why --\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- they had to do involuntary manslaughter.\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Steve, I want to put up with Congressman Thomas Massie put up yesterday. The timing couldn`t have felt worse, couldn`t have felt more ill-timed. This is -- it`s gotten a lot of social media attention, Thomas Massie and his family all holding their gun of choice there and saying, P.S., Santa, bring more ammo.\par \par \uc2 Do we have sort -- is gun culture sort of -- are we at a bad place? Are we too celebratory of gun ownership on the right, Steve --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So I don`t think that represents gun culture. I mean, Thomas Massie gave an interview a couple years ago in which he said that his election and the rise of \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  and Ron Paul actually signaled this rise of sort of a libertarian-style Republican Party. And he later said the election of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  suggested that the craziest person is going to win.\par \par \uc2 I think that`s what you`re seeing here. I think he`s playing to the crazy people there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He`s not serious about this, but he`s playing to the crazies, is what you`re saying?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yeah, it`s a troll. He`s trying to get attention for doing it. I think most responsible gun owners wouldn`t want this kind of gun fetish display, just as most responsible gun owners, I think, would condemn the way that the parents handled the use of the gun and the lack of safe keeping. It sort of violates everything that responsible gun owners believe.\par \par \uc2 HAINES: And I think hearing responsible gun owners really pushing back in this moment and rejecting things like, you know, the tweet that Thomas Massie posted, which by the way, is at 60,000 likes and counting on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , and yes, I know that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is not America. We do know that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Thank goodness actually.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, imagine if it were.\par \par \uc2 HAINES: Absolutely. But no, there is an audience for this. And it just needs to be kind of underscored that for gun owners and for folks that are in gun culture who are, like, no, this is not who we are, hearing some of those voices in this conversation I think is going to be important too.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, there`s another part of this, Ashley. It`s what the school did and did not do. And, look, I have family that is in -- works in school systems. The pressure school administrators are feeling from parents.\par \par \uc2 And this set of parents said, don`t take my kid out of school. And administrators are afraid. You know, parents are angry. They`re in a tough place too.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: They absolutely are. They`ve already ordered up an investigation of what`s happened. My understanding is that, as rare as it is to charge parents, it is incredibly rare for a school to actually be held accountable. Now, that doesn`t mean there won`t be lawsuits and other --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Maybe individuals get held accountable --\par \par \uc2 PARKER: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: And other steps against them.\par \par \uc2 But it is an incredibly tricky position. And you also find that schools, that parents, that all of these things in the margins are coming under question because the big issue, which is should kids who are mentally troubled have access to guns, Congress simply can`t address that.\par \par \uc2 HAINES: But teachers are also in a tricky position too, right? What about the teacher, you know, that said, oh, Ethan Crumbley is monitoring ammo on his phone? She tried to raise the alarm. That didn`t --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Not enough people listened.\par \par \uc2 HAINES: Yeah, go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, we had a lot of light subjects for you today: abortion, guns, and COVID. But thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back next week because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020211205ehc500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for December 1, 2021, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Tom Costello, Pete Williams, Kate Snow, Peter  Alexander, Miguel Almaguer, Jacob Soboroff, Joe Fryer \par \uc2 3,941 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Tonight, the first case of the Omicron variant identified here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voiceover): The first patient, a traveler in California who recently returned from South Africa where the new variant was first reported \uc2 Dr. Anthony Fauci\uc2  saying the patient is fully vaccinated and had mild symptoms.\par \par \uc2 Concerns growing tonight. The new travel restrictions the U.S. is considering during the busy holiday season.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, the Supreme Court hearing the biggest challenge to abortion rights in decades. How the justices signaled they could rule.\par \par \uc2 The first court appearance of the 15-year-old suspected in that deadly Michigan high school shooting. Charged as an adult with murder and terrorism. It comes as a toll rises, a fourth student dying. And could the suspect`s parents be charged too?\par \par \uc2 The bombshell new claims that then-President Trump tested positive for COVID days before his first debate with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . How Trump is responding.\par \par \uc2 The deadly Beverly Hills home invasion, the wife of a music legend killed.\par \par \uc2 The shocking body cam of a man on a wheelchair allegedly armed with a knife. The officer firing nine shots into his back and side.\par \par \uc2 And time to shine. Your front row seat for tonight`s beloved holiday tradition right here in Rockefeller Center.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Good evening. The Omicron COVID variant has now officially been identified in the United States. The White House today confirming the discovery in California of the country`s first known infection with a puzzling and highly concerning strain.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what we know. The infected person identified as a recent traveler from South Africa, fully vaccinated and having suffered only mild symptoms. Tonight, the Biden administration which for days has been braced for the arrival of this variant is set to tighten COVID testing requirements for arriving international travelers including Americans.\par \par \uc2 Much of the focus tonight remains on the effectiveness of vaccines. 197 million Americans, 59 percent of the population, are considered fully vaccinated. But officials strongly urging booster shots as a stronger line of defense against Omicron. 21 percent of vaccinated Americans boosted so far. Let`s get late details now from Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The CDC says the first confirmed case in the U.S. of the Omicron variant has appeared now in San Francisco, a traveler who returned from South Africa on November 22, then started showing symptoms.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER TO THE PRESIDENT: The individual is self-quarantining and all close contacts have been contacted and all close contacts thus far have tested negative. The individual was fully vaccinated and experienced mild symptoms which are improving at this point.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: California health experts concede they have more questions than answers.\par \par \uc2 DR. GRANT COLFAX, HEALTH DIRECTOR, SAN FRANCISCO: We don`t know how infectious it is although there is a strong likelihood that it is more infectious than Delta. We don`t know how sick it will make people but that is being studied furiously right now across the world.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: With health experts still researching the Omicron threat, the White House is set to require every international traveler including Americans arriving from foreign airports to test negative no more than 24 hours before departure. The U.S. currently requires a negative test within 72 hours of flying.\par \par \uc2 The White House has already banned non-Americans from flying to the U.S. from southern Africa where Omicron is spreading fast. The CDC today ordered airlines to share contact information for anyone traveling from the region for future contact tracing.\par \par \uc2 Despite the omicron threat, the Delta variant remains the most dominant and dangerous strain. The CDC`s most recent transmission map showing 66 percent of America`s counties reporting high transmission rates, the Upper Midwest and Northeast most affected. Southern states report the lowest transmission rates.\par \par \uc2 While nearly 60 percent of Americans are fully vaccinated, it`s not clear how effective those vaccines will be against the new strain. Vaccine makers are looking into catering another booster to Omicron but \uc2 Dr. Anthony Fauci\uc2  says people should not wait for those boosters.\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Get boosted now. We may not need a variant-specific boost.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: The good news most people who`ve contracted the Omicron variant have demonstrated mild symptoms. Tonight though, the \uc2 world health organization\uc2  says banning only certain international travelers will not stop the spread of Omicron.\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL RYAN, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, WHO HEALTH EMERGENCIES PROGRAMME: Does the virus read your passport? Does the virus know your nationality or where you`re legally resident? So, therefore there are contradictions on this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [19:05:01]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Tom, there`s late word tonight that the transportation mask mandate may be extended. What can you tell us about that?\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (on camera): Multiple sources tell NBC News that tomorrow the Biden administration will extend the mask mandate probably until mid-March. It was going to expire in January, you`ll have to keep the mask on airports, planes, buses, trains probably until mid-March, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Tom, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Dramatic arguments at the supreme court today as it considers the most significant challenge to abortion rights in at least three decades the case is a direct challenge to the landmark ruling of Roe v Wade. With more, here`s Pete Williams.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Hundreds gathered outside the court, both sides vividly demonstrating what`s at stake. Mississippi defended its law passed in 2018 but blocked by lower courts that would ban virtually all abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy, at odds with nearly 50 years of Supreme Court rulings.\par \par \uc2 BECKY CURRIE, MISSISSIPPI REPUBLICAN STATE REPRESENTATIVE: Life is so precious. And these babies have a chance now as 48 years ago they may not have.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Starting with Roe v Wade, the Supreme Court has repeatedly ruled that states cannot ban abortion before the age of viability, around 24 weeks into a pregnancy. That`s why Mississippi`s law is a direct challenge to Roe.\par \par \uc2 NANCY NORTHUP\uc2 , PRESIDENT AND CEO, CENTER FOR REPRODUCTION RIGHTS: This is an individual liberty interest for women because it has such a profound effect on our health and our lives and our future.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The more liberal justices warned today that overturning Roe would undermine public confidence.\par \par \uc2 SONIA SOTOMAYOR\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICES OF THE U.S. SUPREME COURT: Will this institution survive the stench that this creates in the public perception that the constitution and its reading are just political acts.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  suggested he would uphold the Mississippi law without explicitly overturning Roe.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , CHIEF JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: If it really is an issue about choice, why is 15 weeks not enough time?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But several of the court`s conservatives appeared willing to rule for abortion opponents who say Roe was wrongly decided and should be overturned even though abortion rights have been the law of the land for almost 50 years.\par \par \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICES OF THE U.S. SUPREME COURT: If we think that the prior precedents are seriously wrong why then doesn`t the history of this court`s practice with respect to those cases tell us that the right answer is actually the return to the position of neutrality?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: If the Supreme Court did overturn Roe, it wouldn`t ban abortion. It would leave it up to individual states. Experts on local laws say 24 states are either certain or very likely to make the procedure illegal. Some already have bans that would go into effect the moment Roe was overturned.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Pete, when can we expect a decision?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS (on camera): Well, we probably won`t get it until late June, Lester. And if the court does uphold the Mississippi law and say that abortion can be banned after 15 weeks, then other states will undoubtedly try to ban it after even shorter periods.\par \par \uc2 We`re also waiting for a ruling on whether lawsuits can go ahead against the Texas law that bans abortion after six weeks, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Pete Williams at the Supreme Court tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 If the Supreme Court does make a change to the precedent of Roe v Wade after today`s arguments, the impact could be felt quickly. Kate Snow explains.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KATE SNOW, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: As Supreme Court Justices were hearing arguments today, nurse Sarah Haeder was busy seeing patients.\par \par \uc2 SARAH HAEDER, NURSE, RED RIVER WOMEN`S CLINIC: I would say there was probably 15 protesters outside today.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: That happens every Wednesday at the Red River Women`s Clinic in Fargo, North Dakota, a state that already has some of the strictest abortion laws in the nation. North Dakota is one of five states that has just a single abortion clinic operating and it is one of 12 states with a law already passed that would ban abortion if the Supreme Court overturns Roe.\par \par \uc2 What would happen if the Supreme Court overturned Roe.\par \par \uc2 HAEDER: For us, that would mean that we would be closing within the next 30 days. No more patience in 30 days.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: how high are the stakes for you?\par \par \uc2 HAEDER: Unbelievably high. The reason why I do this work is I believe strongly that women deserve to make their own choices. And if women are not allowed to do that, they become second-class citizens.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Haeder started working at this clinic after having an abortion here in her late 20s. But as a kid, she attended demonstrations against abortion with her grandparents. She understands what advocates like Carol Tobias at the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2  think.\par \par \uc2 CAROL TOBIAS, NATIONAL RIGHT TO LIFE COMMITTEE: Unborn babies are human beings and they should be protected as any human being would. There are a couple thousand pregnancy resource centers around the country who will help a woman through a difficult pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: One of Haeder`s patients today asked what could be done to keep this clinic open but that decision is in the hands of the highest court. Kate Snow NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [19:10:06]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In Michigan, a 15-year-old became the fourth to die from that shooting at a high school in a Detroit suburb as new details emerged about the teenage suspect and the victims. Meagan Fitzgerald is there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m going to confirm the name of the suspect this one time only during this press conference. It`s Ethan Crumbley, age 15.\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD: Authorities say the 15-year-old suspect opened fire at Oxford High School in Michigan yesterday, killing four and injuring seven others.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Charging this person as an adult is necessary to achieve justice and protect the public.\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD: He`s being charged with one count of terrorism causing death, four counts of first-degree murder, seven counts of assault with intent to murder, 12 counts of possession of a firearm. His father allegedly purchased the gun and prosecutors say they`re considering charging Crumbley`s parents.\par \par \uc2 The victims being remembered tonight. 17-year-old Justin Shilling was a member of the bowling and golf teams. Hannah St. Juliana was just 14 years old. She was on the volleyball team. 17-year-old Madisyn Baldwin wanted to be an artist. She was set to graduate this spring. And 16-year-old Tate Myre, a high school running back.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Four kids are gone. Four kids don`t get to go home tonight and see their parents. Four kids don`t get to take that math test that they dread.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We don`t know how to move on from a shooting.\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD: Chilling video of the shooting emerge showing students barricading themselves inside a classroom.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I shouldn`t be living in fear as, you know, going to school.\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD: Logan Sweeney was inside another classroom where he did the same.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I didn`t know if I was ever going to see my parents again. It was truly terrifying.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD (on camera): And tonight, the suspect pleaded not guilty. Prosecutors allege that he planned this attack long before it was carried out. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Meagan Fitzgerald in Michigan tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In just 60 seconds, the deadly encounter, a suspect in a wheelchair shot to death by a police officer.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Let`s get to that stunning new revelation about Former President Trump. NBC News confirming that he tested positive for COVID just three days before his first debate with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  last fall, though another test that same day was negative. Peter Alexander has details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: It was an explosive presidential showdown.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The question begins --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Why won`t you answer that question?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because the question -- the question is -- will you shut up, man.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Tonight, the new revelations from President Trump`s former Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  that Mr. Trump first tested positive for COVID three days before the debate against then then-candidate \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In his upcoming book, according to \uc2 the Guardian\uc2 , Meadows writes, nothing was going to stop Trump from going out there. Meadows adds that Mr. Trump received a negative result from a separate test hours later. And tonight, three sources familiar with the matter confirmed that timeline to NBC News.\par \par \uc2 After those results, Mr. Trump continued to a rally, met with gold star families, and hosted a COVID briefing.\par \par \uc2 Weeks later, repeatedly pressed by Savannah Guthrie, Mr. Trump denied testing positive before the debate.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Did you test the day of the debate?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I don`t know. I don`t even remember. I think it was Thursday evening, maybe even late Thursday evening I tested positive. That`s when I first found out.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: President Biden today dismissed a question about whether his rival put him at risk.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I don`t think about the former President.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Tonight, in a statement, Mr. Trump said the story that he had COVID before or during the first debate is fake news, but he did not address whether he had received a positive test. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Peter Alexander, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Beverly Hills, police are searching for suspects in the home invasion that left the wife of a music legend dead. 81-year-old philanthropist Jacqueline Avant was fatally shot inside her home overnight. Avant is the wife of Clarence Avant, the music executive known as the godfather of Black music. She is also the mother-in-law of Netflix CEO Ted Sarandos. Police are asking anyone with information to come forward.\par \par \uc2 And another shocking case. A police officer in Arizona was terminated after he was captured on bodycam fatally shooting a man in a wheelchair suspected of shoplifting. Miguel Almaguer has more. But first, a warning the video is disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I have a male suspect just shoplifted, left in front of me. He pulled a knife on me.\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The shoplifting suspect who police say had a knife was in a motorized wheelchair moving away from officers as he began to make his way into a home improvement store. Moments later, with the suspects back still to officers, the deadly encounter captured on body camera.\par \par \uc2 Richard Lee Richards was shot nine times in the back and the side. Officer Ryan Remington opened fired.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS MAGNUS, CHIEF, TUCSON POLICE: To be very clear, I am deeply disturbed and troubled by Officer Remington`s actions.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Moving quickly to terminate the Tucson, Arizona Police officer, the police chief says the Pima County Attorney`s Office will review the case which has the authority to pursue charges. Shortly before the shooting, police say Richards was accused of stealing a toolbox from a \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , then pulled a knife when confronted by Remington who followed him through a parking lot. The officer`s attorney says there shouldn`t be a rush to judgment.\par \par \uc2 MIKE STORIE, ATTORNEY OF RYAN REMINGTON: And at every moment, this officer was trying to use the least amount of force as possible which is verbal commands, talking with this individual, trying to de-escalate it.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Still, the mayor calls the incident unconscionable and indefensible. Now the shocking shooting in the hands of local prosecutors. Miguel Almaguer NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Up next, as we continue tonight our inside look at the critical supply chain link you might not know about, and how automation could be a game-changer.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Back now with our series Race to Deliver, and a rare look at how one of America`s largest railroads has been impacted by the supply chain crisis and the high-tech solutions to avoid future gridlock. We get more from Jacob Soboroff.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: If you`re waiting for something to arrive that you bought online, there`s a good chance it`s sitting parked in a container right here at the Union Pacific Terminal in Joliet Illinois just outside Chicago. But locomotive engineer Tom Moses says it`s not because his trains are late.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s because the other processes will have us to have to slow down or pick up because we`re always ready.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: For you to get there on time, you need the other pieces to be working?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Luke Slawson who runs operations here gave us a tour to explain.\par \par \uc2 Are we looking at more cargo containers in this parking lot than normal?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Current state, we`re a little bit above average right now. You want to turn a parking spot just like you would turn a table at a restaurant.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: And are they being turned as fast as possible these days?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Not as fast as possible.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Union Pacific`s Kristen South told us there aren`t enough truck drivers or space and warehouses to get cargo out fast enough.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN SOUTH, SENIOR DIRECTOR OF CORPORATE COMMUNICATIONS, UNION PACIFIC: Almost 50 percent of international cargo will hit Chicago on its journey at some point.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: That`s not always how things happened here. A generation ago, this area was a manufacturing hub employing thousands of workers, but the factories closed as manufacturing moved overseas. Now, what`s produced there ends up back here.\par \par \uc2 So, this place wouldn`t even exist were it not for the basically the contraction of domestic manufacturing and the move to international trade.\par \par \uc2 SOUTH: You`re exactly right.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: 40 percent of goods traveling long distances in the U.S. move by rail. And the Department of Transportation expects total freight demand to grow by 30 percent by 2040, which is why \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2  is banking on new autonomous technology including the 75 foot tall crane.\par \par \uc2 This is it?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is it.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: This is going to be a 100 percent humanless operation.\par \par \uc2 Can you see how tight I`m actually grabbing the railings right now? Wow. So, the trains are going to come in right below here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Correct. So it`s all going to continuously move around all day long.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: And the only humans to work on the crane won`t be anywhere near it.\par \par \uc2 You`re telling me that the potential solution to the supply chain crisis could look a lot like playing a video game.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Maybe not the full solution but it`ll be a lot more efficient.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: But just as when the factories went overseas and the supply chains lengthened, the changes here have their own consequences. Eric Pulley has been hauling cargo as a truck driver since his grandfather taught him the trade.\par \par \uc2 They want to automate, so they -- I mean, they want to get you guys in and out as fast as possible.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You want to hear the bad side about that?\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Yes, tell me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You lost your jobs.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Right.\par \par \uc2 Inside his union pacific locomotive, Tom Moses has faith everything will work out.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: What do you want the American people to know that are watching me and you right now thinking about their Christmas presents showing up on time?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would tell them they`re on their way as soon as possible. Have no fret.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Tom is on his way.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We`re on the highway.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Jacob Soboroff, NBC News Joliet, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And up next, the great light show about to happen right here at 30 Rock.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Well, I`ve stepped outside where a joyous tradition that began long ago here at 30 Rock returns tonight with the lighting of the Rockefeller Christmas Tree. Joe Fryer has a close-up look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Sure, it`s a great setting for a picture, but this 79-foot Norway Spruce is more than a backdrop, it`s rock centers rock star.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s just famous that everyone wants to see it every year.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Everyone from everywhere.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Vienna, Austria, Chicago, Seattle.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: And every age.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of the tree?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think that it looks sparkly.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: It does look sparkly, doesn`t it?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And I like the star.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: That star covered in three million Swarovski crystals, beneath it a 12-ton tree wrapped in 50,000 multi-colored lights. It`ll be illuminated tonight.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Three, two, one, light it up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think it really just brings everybody, puts everybody in the Christmas spirit.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: This tree tradition has deep roots. It was started in 1931 at the height of the great depression by construction workers building Rockefeller Center, the custom that continues.\par \par \uc2 I wanted to make sure you could see me. Most of all, I wanted to make sure you could see this streak.\par \par \uc2 I was on board and also sparkly when this year`s tree pulled into the heart of Manhattan making the 140 mile trip from Maryland donated by the Price Family.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We never thought a tree on our property would be selected for Rockefeller Center. Nope, not in a million years.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Now, adorned and adored, this rock star is ready to shine. Joe Fryer, NBC News New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And as you can hear, they`re warming up for the big moment. Our special coverage of the tree lighting begins just minutes from now right here on NBC. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020211202ehc100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Meet the Press for November 28, 2021, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Danielle Pletka, Eugene Robinson, Hallie Jackson, Jake  Sherman \par \uc2 9,361 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 November 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [09:00:22]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST (voice-over): This Sunday, the new coronavirus variant.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This variant has a large number of mutations and some of these mutations have some worrying characteristics.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The U.S. and Europe imposing restrictions on travel from southern Africa.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We don`t know a lot about the variant except that it is a great concern. It seems to spread rapidly.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , DIRECTOR, NATIONAL INSTITUTE OF ALLERGY AND INFECTIOUS DISEASES: Something that I`m concerned about, you want to be honest.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This as cases are already spiking in much of this country. My guest this morning, \uc2 Dr. Anthony Fauci\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plus, the Supreme Court is about to hear arguments on Mississippi`s new law that bars most abortions after 15 weeks. It could lead to the redefining or overturning of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TATE REEVES\uc2  (R-MS): It makes sense for the court to review their decisions from the past and this is a vehicle in which for them to do it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ll talk to Mississippi`s Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Also, Donald Trump`s former personal attorney \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  is free after serving time for breaking tax and campaign laws. I`ll talk to him about the investigation into Mr. Trump`s financial dealings --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL COHEN\uc2 , FORMER ATTORNEY FOR \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : They`re working on a daily basis in terms of bringing this indictment.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- and the former president`s political ambitions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joining me for insight and analysis are NBC News senior Washington correspondent Hallie Jackson, "Washington Post" columnist Eugene Robinson, Danielle Pletka of the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 , and Jake Sherman, founder of "Punchbowl News."\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s MEET THE PRESS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is MEET THE PRESS WITH CHUCK TODD.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, a good Sunday morning. I hope you`re enjoying the rest of your Thanksgiving Day weekend. Well, it appears the country and the world may be suffering from its own version of long COVID. Each pause in cases seems to be followed by a spike. And now there is Omicron. This is the name of a troubling new coronavirus variant that the WHO is calling a variant of concern, the first one they`ve labeled that since the Delta variant. We know how that turned out.\par \par \uc2 This was first identified in southern Africa. But as we`ve learned, when a variant shows up anywhere, it winds up everywhere. The reaction so far has been swift despite real uncertainly over how serious this variant actually is. There`s been jitters on Wall Street over the news and it caused the Dow to drop more than 900 points on Friday, the single worst day of the year. All this comes amid a sharp rise in cases in the U.S. and across Europe and this is pre-Omicron.\par \par \uc2 Here at home the seven-day average of cases has grown since the end of the summer wave with the recent spike especially sharp in the northeast and midwest as more people go inside due to the cold weather. Ultimately, this is also a political problem. President Biden ran on taming the pandemic and he prematurely declared independence from the virus back in July. At the same time we can see a backlash against all those Republican politicians who`ve been fighting mandates and playing down the need for vaccines. So where are we headed?\par \par \uc2 Well, joining me now is the director of the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases, it is \uc2 Dr. Anthony Fauci\uc2 . And, of course, he`s the president`s chief adviser on COVID. Dr. Fauci, welcome back to MEET THE PRESS. So --\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Good to be with you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- I did some calendar math here. The Delta variant was labeled a variant of concern in May. Obviously it didn`t really hit us until mid July. And, of course, we had a terrible surge there for about two months.\par \par \uc2 Here we are post November. So we are already experiencing a surge. Now we have this. Are we headed for a bleak winter here, sir?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: You know, Chuck, a lot of whether or not we`re headed into a bleak or bleaker winter is really going to depend upon what we do. And I think what you`re seeing is just the manifestation of what we`ve been talking about, why it is so important for people to get vaccinated and for those who are fully vaccinated to get boosted. Because even when you have variants like this, and there`s a lot of unknowns about this variant, we know from experience that when you get a level of protection with vaccine and particularly now with the extraordinary increase in protection you get with the booster, even when you have variants of concern, you do well against them. It may not be as good in protecting against initial infection, but it has a very important impact on diminishing the likelihood that you`re going to get a severe outcome from it.\par \par \uc2 So this is a clarion call as far as I`m concerned of saying let`s put aside all of these differences that we have and say, if you`re not vaccinated, get vaccinated. If you`re fully vaccinated, get boosted and get the children vaccinated also. We now have time. Thank goodness the South Africans are really extraordinarily good in what they did.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:03]\par \par \uc2 They were completely transparent right from the beginning. We were on the phone getting real time information from them on Friday. We`re going to be talking to them again today.\par \par \uc2 So we have an advantage of this. We have an up on it. We know what`s going on. We`re getting more information in real time.\par \par \uc2 When you -- when you diminish or stop or block travel from a particular country there`s a reason for that. It`s to give you time to do things. So don`t let this decision that was made about blocking the travel from certain countries go without a positive effect. And the positive effect is to get us better prepared, to rev up on the vaccination, to be really ready for something that may not actually be a big deal, but we want to make sure that we`re prepared for the worst. And that`s what we should be doing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK. What is it about this variant that you`ve seen so far that has everybody so alarmed that the other variants that we`ve had in between Delta and this one -- you know, Delta alarmed folks. This one alarmed folks. Lambda and Mu, for instance, haven`t. What makes Omicron so concerning to you?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Yes. Well, right now what we have is we have the window into the mutations that are in this new variant. And they are troublesome in the fact that there are about 32 or more variants in that very important spike protein of the virus which is the business end of the virus. And there`s about 10 or more of these mutations that are on that part of the virus. We call it the receptor binding domain that actually binds to the cells in your nasopharynx and in your lung.\par \par \uc2 In other words, the profile of the mutation strongly suggests that it`s going to have an advantage in transmissibility and that it might evade immune protection that you would get, for example, from a monoclonal antibody or from the convalescent serum after a person has been infected and possibly even against some of the vaccine-induced antibodies. So it`s not necessarily that that`s going to happen, but it`s a strong indication that we really need to be prepared for that.\par \par \uc2 That, together with the fact that it just kind of exploded in the sense that when you look in South Africa, you were having a low level of infection and then all of a sudden there was this big spike. And when the South Africans looked at it, they said, "Oh, my goodness. This is a different virus than what we`ve been dealing with."\par \par \uc2 So it`s clearly is giving indication that it has the capability of transmitting rapidly. That`s the thing that`s causing us now to be concerned, but also to put the pressure on ourselves now to do something about our preparation for this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What is it about -- what do you need to know to find out if our vaccines are working correctly or if they need to be tweaked or if the boosters need to be tweaked? We never tweaked the boosters for Delta. Do you expect us to do that for this one?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: We`re preparing to, but we might not have to. That is the critical question that you asked, Chuck. So what you do is you get the virus, either the whole virus itself or a modification, what we call a pseudovirus. And you get that and you take antibodies from a person who has been vaccinated and you see if those antibodies can neutralize this particular virus.\par \par \uc2 And if they can at a high titer of antibody, we`re in pretty good shape. If it looks like even at a high titer of antibody it doesn`t, then what you`ve got to do is you got to change and modify what the vaccine is going to be, which you can do pretty easily with the kinds of vaccines that we have.\par \par \uc2 So the critical questions now are, do the antibodies block this well? And what is the seriousness of the disease? So there are enough people right now in South Africa that our South African colleagues are following to determine, is this highly transmissible but does it really give a severe disease? Or does it really give the same kind of severity that we`ve seen with Delta and other variants? All of these are gaps in our knowledge that we are all over and going to find them out pretty quickly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So I got my booster five days ago. And now I`m wondering, did I get my booster too soon? And there`s going to be a whole bunch of people out there that are thinking about their booster.\par \par \uc2 I see you shaking your head. And I assumed you would. But I`d like you to clarify that. Because there are going to be people who sit there and say, "Well, maybe I should wait until the booster is tweaked." What do you tell them?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: If you -- right. If you are six months or more from the second dose of an mRNA, either the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  or the Moderna, get boosted. If you`re two months or more following the single dose of J&J, get boosted. Don`t try to play mind games of saying, "Maybe I should wait a little bit longer." Get boosted now. The one thing we do know, Chuck, that`s really good news is that when you get boosted, the level of your antibody goes way, way above what the level at its peak was after the second dose.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:10]\par \par \uc2 So the booster not only gets you back up to where you were, it gets you way, way, way up. And that`s the reason why we feel even with variants like Omicron that if you get boosted you`re going to get a level of antibody that is high enough that it is likely you`ll be able to get at least some degree and maybe a lot of protection against this. So as, again, I said in the beginning of the interview, Chuck, if ever there was a reason for the people who were vaccinated to get boosted and for those who are unvaccinated to get vaccinated, it`s now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We are a country that was trying desperately to get back to normal. We`re almost at pre-pandemic travel levels for the Thanksgiving holiday. Does the rise of this new variant and our continued stubborn unvaccinated population that we have in this country make you think we should have a different policy regarding holiday travel in a month?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Well, you know, if people follow the recommendations of the CDC and all of us about what to do in traveling, in getting vaccinated, in getting boosted, we could have a situation where you do continue to veer towards getting to some degree of normality. Chuck, I say it so many times, it`s within our own capability to do that. And that`s the thing.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got to put aside all of these things that are getting in the way of good public health practices. This is not rocket science. We know exactly what we can do and what we should be doing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Should we have a vaccine mandate for domestic air travel?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: You know, Chuck, I`m not going to make any pronouncements about what we should have about vaccine mandates for travel. We know that we evaluate these things literally in real time all the time. You know, everything is discussed and everything is on the table.\par \par \uc2 Right now we hope that people who get vaccinated and who follow the appropriate recommendations, that air travel will continue to be safe. And right now air travel is. When you say travel in general causes a risk of increased transmission, it`s the whole process of traveling, going to the airport, being in the airport in congregate settings, people taking their masks off, that`s the issue that we`ve got to make sure that we adhere to more closely and more firmly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How soon do we change the definition of fully vaccinated, considering now it seems like you`re saying, if you haven`t gotten the booster, get the booster? Is fully vaccinated going to have to be the booster shot included?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: You know, Chuck, I think there`s a little semantics there because when you say fully vaccinated with regard to certain regulations or can you get into a certain place, can you be in a certain position or in a job, and then there`s what`s the optimal for you for your own health. Right now there`s not going to be a change, certainly not in the immediate future, of what is the definition of fully vaccinated. And that`s still two shots of an mRNA and one shot of a J&J. But if you want to optimize your protection as an individual, clearly, that`s where we`re saying go get your booster.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is it pretty clear this is now an endemic? I mean, how does this end any other way?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: When you`re saying this, you`re talking about what we`re dealing with now in our own country namely the Delta and what we`re doing? You know, Chuck, as I`ve said --\par \par \uc2 TODD: That we`re going to have this virus circulating perpetuity.\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: You know, we certainly are not going to eradicate it. We`ve only eradicated one virus and that`s smallpox. Elimination means there`s none of it in the country like we have now with polio and with measles. I don`t think we`re going to be there with this. But what I do think we will be able to do is get a level of control that`s low enough that it doesn`t interfere with our function. It doesn`t have a major impact on society and what we do.\par \par \uc2 It`s not going to go away. The lower we get it, the better off we`ll be. And you get it that low when you get the overwhelming majority of the population vaccinated and boosted.\par \par \uc2 So like I`ve said multiple times, Chuck, it`s in our own grasp of how we`re going to be able to live with the virus. The lower we get it, the lower the dynamics of virus in the community, the lower the risk to everyone, including vaccinated people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dr. Fauci, I hope you had as enjoyable a Thanksgiving as you could. Considering the breaking news over the week, I`m guessing it was a bit interrupted, but thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Good to be with you, Chuck. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And joining me now is the governor of Mississippi. It`s Republican \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2 . Mississippi`s COVID caseload, by the way, is way down from its summer peak in late August. The cases there have also began to bump up a bit again. So, Governor Reeves, welcome to MEET THE PRESS.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:02]\par \par \uc2 And I know, for what it`s worth, we invited you to talk about the Mississippi abortion case, and I do want to get to that. But let me start with COVID. You let your state of emergency expire on COVID last Saturday. Given the news of the last week, do you have a metric in mind in your own head on when you might reinstitute that state of emergency?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, Chuck, thanks for having me on this morning, I really do appreciate it. I hope you did have a happy Thanksgiving, as well.\par \par \uc2 We`re certainly monitoring this new variant. We don`t have all the data that we need to make decisions at this time. Obviously, as Dr. Fauci was very clear and pointed out correctly, our cases are down significantly. We`re at a seven-day moving average of about 235 cases. That`s down over 90 percent from where we were in mid to late summer.\par \par \uc2 You look at total hospitalizations in Mississippi, we have 175 Mississippians in hospital beds. We have only 59 Mississippians -- and this is a state of 3 million, only 59 Mississippians in ICU beds. We have 28 Mississippians on the ventilator today.\par \par \uc2 So our case numbers are way down. Our vaccination numbers continue to rise. We have 1.6 million Mississippians that have been vaccinated. That`s not enough.\par \par \uc2 But in talking to our state health officers, we believe that somewhere between 80 to 85 percent of Mississippians have some level of immunity, either natural immunity or immunity from having taken the shots. And I think one of the things that we`ve all got to start thinking about is the booster shots, and encouraging, not mandating, but encouraging our fellow Mississippians to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you accept the idea, though, that the campaigns, like yourself and other Republican governors have had against vaccine mandates have sent the wrong message about getting the vaccine?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: I actually believe that the president`s decision to try to mandate vaccines a decision that, by the way, flies in the face of what he said throughout the campaign. And that`s one of the reasons his poll numbers are so low is because he ran for office saying one thing and then he`s decided to govern in a different way. But I actually think those mandates actually are hardening those individuals who were not interested in getting vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 If you go around our state and you talk to those individuals that have not gotten vaccinated, in large measure a lot of them have gotten the virus and so they believe they have some natural immunity. And they were thinking about getting vaccinated as they got beyond the 90 days or got beyond the 180 days, but the president`s insistence on mandating them have hardened them against the vaccine. So I actually think it`s the president`s policies that have made it more difficult in rural states like Mississippi to get more and more people vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 But we have continued to see an uptick in the numbers. As I mentioned earlier, over 1.6 million vaccinations -- fully vaccinated Mississippians. And we`re doing about 50,000 vaccines a week, and so we want to continue on that trend.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to continue to encourage it. You know, the only thing I know to do -- I`ve encouraged vaccines from the beginning. I actually took my shots on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  Live and I don`t like shots very much, Chuck. But that was my way in which of telling the people of Mississippi that, "I think this is the best way for you to protect yourselves." But I also believe in individual liberties, and I believe in freedoms, and I believe individuals can make their own decision, what`s best for them after they talk to their physician.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So let me switch gears here, speaking of some individual freedom that I think people might have a difference of opinion on. This week the Supreme Court`s going to hear some moral arguments in the case involving a law, a Mississippi law that would ban abortion -- all abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Do you hope that the Supreme Court makes this law legal within the framework of Roe v. Wade or overturns Roe v. Wade completely? I know your views on Roe v. Wade, you`d like to see it overturned or you believe it was wrongly decided. Do you think it needs to be overturned for this law to be enacted? Or do you think they can redefine Roe v. Wade to allow this law to be enacted?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, that`s a complicated question. But clearly, I think that this law can be enacted within a changing confinement of Roe v. Wade. But I also believe that Roe v. Wade was wrongly decided. I believe, in a simple reading of the United States Constitution, that when Roe was decided in 1973 there is no fundamental right in our United States Constitution to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 And furthermore, Chuck, I believe very strongly that if you read the Constitution there is nowhere in the Constitution that prohibits individual states, states like Mississippi, to limit access to abortions. And so I think Roe was wrongly decided.\par \par \uc2 I also believe that some 20 years later in 1992, Casey was incorrectly decided. And if you look at the Casey ruling, what you find in my opinion is a ruling that was not based upon fundamentals of the Constitution but a ruling that was determined based upon what the perceived political perception was at that time.\par \par \uc2 [09:20:13]\par \par \uc2 And I don`t think the judicial branch of government should ever allow politics to play into their decision making and I think they did in Casey.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If Roe is overturned to allow your law to be enacted, do you plan on pursuing even more restrictions or are you going to stop at 15 weeks? Because there`d be a tighter -- there is a tighter restriction at one time, a trigger law that was on the books. Do you want to see something like that if Roe is overturned?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, I certainly would like to do everything we can to protect unborn children. But let`s put this in perspective, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 You know, in Europe there are 42 countries that allow elective abortions. If Roe is overturned and this 15-week ban in Mississippi is allowed to go into effect Mississippi will still have a law on the books in which 39 countries, 39 out of 42 in Europe, have more restrictive abortion laws than what I believe to be one of the most conservative states in the United States. Thirty-nine countries in Europe restrict abortions earlier than 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 And so the reality is that the U.S. abortion laws are not in conformity or are not even in the realm of what we see in other parts of the country. When you look at, for instance, abortion laws in California and New York, they are much more similar to those abortion laws in China and North Korea than they are to Europe or many other countries around the world.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to play something that you said about the vaccine mandate, and ask you why it doesn`t -- why this same philosophy doesn`t apply here. Let me play what you said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REEVES: This is a power grab by the federal government. We`ve seen this time and time again by the Biden administration. And now we`re seeing their strong desire to try to make decisions on behalf of individual Americans. We believe in freedom and individual liberty.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: "Freedom and individual liberty." Why should the state of Mississippi tell a woman what they should do with their body? Why shouldn`t they have that individual freedom on their body particularly in the first 20 weeks?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, this is a prime example. And the far left loves to scream, "My body, my choice." And what I would submit to you, Chuck, is they absolutely ignore the fact that in getting an abortion there is an actual killing of an innocent, unborn child that is in that womb.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what we know about babies that are 15 weeks. We know that they have a heartbeat. We know that those babies at 15 weeks actually can open and close their hands. We know that they have developing lungs. And we know that those babies at 15 weeks can feel pain.\par \par \uc2 And so when you talk -- the difference between vaccine mandates and abortions is vaccines allow you to protect yourself. Abortions actually go in and kill other American babies. And let`s just put this in perspective, Chuck, the fact is --\par \par \uc2 TODD: But, Governor, vaccines are not about yourself -- Governor, hang on a minute. A vaccine is about protecting a larger community. A vaccine is about preventing spread. You could argue a vaccine mandate is a pro-life position.\par \par \uc2 REEVES: You could certainly argue that, Chuck. But even if you listen to Dr. Fauci`s interview with you earlier today he made it very clear that the vaccine may not keep you from getting the virus. It may not keep you from spreading the virus. But it can keep you from ending up in the hospital. That`s what`s been proven during this Delta surge that we`ve seen in America is that the virus is continuing to being spread even amongst those who are vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Conversely, when you`re talking about the pro-life position of protecting unborn babies -- let`s put it also in perspective. The fact is that during this very horrible and challenging time since I was sworn into office in January of 2020, Chuck, we`ve had 800,000 American lives lost because of COVID. And my heart aches for every single one of those individuals that have died because of COVID. And over 10,000 Mississippians. My heart breaks for every single one of them.\par \par \uc2 But since Roe was enacted, 62 million American babies have been aborted and are therefore have been killed. And that`s why I think it`s very important that people like myself and others across this country stand up for those unborn children, because they don`t have the ability to stand up for themselves.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2 , Republican of Mississippi, I appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us, sir. Thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, the Ahmaud Arbery murder case.\par \par \uc2 [09:25:00]\par \par \uc2 What the guilty verdicts may tell us about justice in America. The panel is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The panel is here. NBC News senior Washington correspondent Hallie Jackson, "Washington Post" columnist Eugene Robinson, Jake Sherman, founder of "Punchbowl News," and Danielle Pletka of the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All right. Actually, the big news pre-Thanksgiving was the Ahmaud Arbery verdict. I want to play the 911 call because there`s a line in there, Gene, that other people have pointed out that that might be the most important line of this trial. Let me play it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 911 OPERATOR: 911, what`s the address of your emergency?\par \par \uc2 GREGORY MCMICHAEL, CALLER: I`m out here at Satilla Shores. There`s a Black male running down the street.\par \par \uc2 911 OPERATOR: Satilla -- where at Satilla Shores?\par \par \uc2 MCMICHAEL: I don`t know what street we`re on. Hey drop that! Dammit. Stop! Travis!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So the emergency, Gene, was that a Black man was running down the street.\par \par \uc2 EUGENE ROBINSON, NBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes. Black male running down the street, yes. That`s the line --\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s the line.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: -- that jumps out. And that was the emergency they perceived. I mean it was -- it was -- the thing is, of course, we wouldn`t know any of this and there wouldn`t have been a trial had not that video been released and gone viral. Because the initial decision by prosecutors was -- local prosecutors was to do nothing. Was, you know, "OK, we`re good." Right? You know, you just killed this man in cold blood. We can now say officially you murdered this man. But it was OK because there was a Black male running down the street.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That had the feeling, Hallie, of the, you know, your small town, "Oh, I know who those guys are. I know them," and they all know each other. And it becomes one of those things. And it`s like, well, they were going to get protected from committing this crime.\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, NBC NEWS SENIOR WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: So, I think that Gene is bringing up such an important point. I spoke with Ahmaud Arbery`s father just a couple of hours after that verdict came down this week. And we talked about the idea that if there hadn`t been a video, what would have happened. And he said, "There would`ve been no accountability for my son." He strongly believes that, that it was the video that was the piece that cracked this open.\par \par \uc2 I was also struck by something that Ahmaud Arbery`s mother said in the in the immediate aftermath of the verdict coming in which was she had prayed for justice. She had prayed for the right prosecutor to come in because, as Gene notes, there was a lot of sort of back and forth about this. And the discussion came up of, you know, as a person of faith, you can pray. Is that what you have to do? Should it have to come down to prayer, right? Shouldn`t the system be better than that? And I feel like that`s where so much of the conversation is going especially with Mr. Arbery`s family.\par \par \uc2 His attorneys are talking about, "Yes, this is a verdict. This is an important one. It is not justice, but it is accountability." But there are so many other cases, there are so many other systemic changes that this issue is shining a light on.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dany, do you think bringing charges against the local prosecutor for not bringing charges is a way to fix the system?\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PLETKA, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I think it sends a signal. Look, I think for a lot of us who saw and believe that justice was ultimately done the fact that it almost wasn`t even addressed is possibly the most troubling part of the rule-of-law question here.\par \par \uc2 And so, if it sends a signal to other lawmakers, to other prosecutors, to other D.A.s around the country that you will get caught, that there is accountability, that in fact justice will be done by you as well.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there is an investigation. There`s been a grand jury. I`m actually impressed with the fact that they have followed up on this in a way that is extraordinarily aggressive, which is right. You want people not to have to pray. You want people to trust --\par \par \uc2 EUGENE ROBINSON, NBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Exactly. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 PLETKA: -- the justice system.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SHERMAN, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: And I also say we`re shifting into this new era of cellphone accountability. I mean, I think we`ve been in it for a long time but I think that also puts people, puts prosecutors, puts law enforcement officers on notice that this kind of stuff will be filmed, and accountability will happen.\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, NBC NEWS SENIOR WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Well --\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC NEWS MODERATOR, "MEET THE PRESS": Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: I was going to say, one thing I`ve also, you know, heard from folks on this though is to be cautious a little bit about drawing too many lessons or signals from this case, because look what it comes on the heels of, which was Kyle Rittenhouse and the verdict there that angered a lot of folks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But, but and then but many, some would argue on the right, well, jury trials worked in both cases.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 PLETKA: But anger -- anger --\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Well, you know --\par \par \uc2 PLETKA: -- is not a litmus test for how justice is done.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s fair.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Right. I mean, look, you, you know, you, the thing about the justice system is you have to look at the facts of any one case. And you also -- we also saw the trial unfold. And it is, you know, were prosecutors in the Rittenhouse case as good as the prosecutors in the Arbery case? No, they weren`t. I don`t think they were.\par \par \uc2 And the judge explained the law to the jury in a different way. There was a judge in the Rittenhouse case who was well known for favoring the defense side over the prosecution side in almost every case.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, I don`t want to say that a thumb was on the scales of justice there. If you want the justice system to err, you want it to err on the side of the defendant. But I think he was guilty of something, right? I mean, I think --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I think the question was there -- was there the wrong laws or the wrong charges, right?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Right. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It was bad prosecution, you know.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Exactly. But you said, you know --\par \par \uc2 PLETKA: But --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Go ahead, Dany.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: You know, bring a gun that you don`t really know how to wield, or to handle, or keep yourself safe, or keep others safe. And so, you know, three people shot, two of them dead.\par \par \uc2 PLETKA: Right. But if your argument is with the laws, then that`s one argument.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PLETKA: But as you rightly said, I think one of the complaints that so many of us have is that people -- too many people end up in prison. The complaint -- too many Black men are in prison in the United States.\par \par \uc2 You want judges that are, that are going to err on the side of the defense. You want judges that are impartial when it comes to the application of rule of law, and they`re not worried about what the anger is, the reaction is going to be. And you don`t want people to be influenced by the notion that there are going to be riots. That`s wrong.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: I think from a larger perspective, I mean we just saw, and from the legislative perspective, I think this is going to have a huge impact. I think on Capitol Hill we saw two very well-respected members of Congress, Tim Scott, \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  fail in their attempt to get any criminal justice reform passed.\par \par \uc2 I think these incidents put together, and just the charged nature of both of these incidents, is just going to make it more difficult for both --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, we`re about to see. Waukesha is about to, I think, become the issue in every Wisconsin race at a minimum, if not nationally, Gene.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Yeah, it`s, it`s going to, it`s going to feed into this whole debate over cash bail, over the, you know, over the fact that -- again, you look at every individual case. And in this anecdotal instance, this guy was out on a $1,000 bail for committing serious offenses. And so --\par \par \uc2 TODD: We can see the TV ads already being made. In fact, we saw them made in 1988.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Exactly. But you, you know, you can, you can say that cash bail is used unfairly to keep people -- and at the same time say that but when somebody is a violent offender like this, it needs to be more than 1,000 bucks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Wow, I have a packed show. We`ve got a lot of issues to get to. So I`m going to pause here.\par \par \uc2 When we come back -- this will be an interesting one -- I`m going to talk to Donald Trump`s former personal attorney, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , about the investigation into Mr. Trump`s finances and why he thinks Mr. Trump`s not going to run for president ever again.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:37:18]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 The criminal investigation into former President Trump`s family business is reaching a critical phase. The outgoing Manhattan DA, \uc2 Cyrus Vance Jr\uc2 ., is narrowing his focus to whether Mr. Trump fraudulently inflated the value of his assets to obtain better financing.\par \par \uc2 One person with a lot of insight into Mr. Trump`s financial dealings, particularly pre-2017, and perhaps the direction of this case overall in New York, is his former personal attorney and fixer, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 . Cohen, who pled guilty to charges including arranging hush money payments to porn star Stormy Daniels, ended his three-year sentence last week, much of which was spent at his home. Cohen also has written a memoir of his time with Mr. Trump called "Disloyal".\par \par \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cohen, welcome to "Meet the Press", sir.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL COHEN\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP ATTORNEY: Nice to see you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So let me start with what you know about the Manhattan DA. We`re not far from the end of the calendar year. He`s termed out. We know this. We know the new DA hasn`t been read into the investigation yet, since he`s not been sworn in.\par \par \uc2 Should we expect to see actual indictments before the New Year?\par \par \uc2 COHEN: You know, I generally try not to talk about the DA, which now also includes the attorney general`s civil case in the same exact matter. So I really try not to talk about it because it`s their investigation, nor do I want to tip off Trump or the Trump Organization`s people about what is actually happening.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, I would rather just not answer that specific question, other than to say that you can bet your bottom dollar that Allen Weisselberg is not -- and I truly say, I mean this -- Allen Weisselberg is not the, you know, the key to this. They are going after Donald. They`re going after Don Jr., Eric, Ivanka, a whole slew of individuals -- family, you know, family as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You just referred to Allen Weisselberg, the chief financial officer. He was indicted. It looked to a lot of observers that this was the type of indictment that was brought against you essentially, which was -- in the previous cases -- which was to squeeze you and hope that you would cooperate.\par \par \uc2 Were you surprised Allen Weisselberg hasn`t cooperated?\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Yeah, so they didn`t really do to Allen Weisselberg what they did to me. You know, the threat against me was that they were going to file an 85-page indictment that was going to include my wife. They were going to say she was a co-conspirator to the hush money payment, which is absolutely nonsensical.\par \par \uc2 And, look, I`m married now 27 years. I`m with the same woman for 29 years. There was no chance in the world that I was going to put her at risk with these animals. The way they came down on me is nothing like what they`re doing to Weisselberg.\par \par \uc2 They should be squeezing, right now, Barry Weisselberg, who worked for the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , and they should be squeezing Jack Weisselberg, who is one of only two organizations that made loans to the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  that we still know.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:13]\par \par \uc2 You know, when you talked about whether or not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  inflated or deflated his assets, every single word that I had said about that is 100 percent accurate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you their best -- are you their only witness on this, or have they found other witnesses? Because it does seem as if they have this hypothesis, but you`ve got to prove it. You`re one, you`re one witness. They`re going to want somebody in the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  actively, too. Do they have it?\par \par \uc2 COHEN: So again, I don`t want to get into the investigation at all. But rest assured, I am not their only witness.\par \par \uc2 And most importantly, what I gave to them are thousands and thousands of documents. And as you know in law, there`s something called documentary evidence. I`m not asking anybody to believe me.\par \par \uc2 This is no different than when I testified before the House Oversight Committee. Every statement that I make, I`ve backed up with documentary evidence. I truly believe that they could indict \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tomorrow if they really wanted, and be successful.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you believe -- considering what they`re investigating, are you confident you did help \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  commit crimes?\par \par \uc2 COHEN: I can assure you that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is guilty of his own crimes. Was I involved in much of the inflation and deflation of his assets? The answer to that is yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I take it then you`ve been given some level of immunity from the DA and the attorney general in New York, have you not?\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Yeah, my case is over. It was over when I had pled guilty. It was all part of one big, giant package.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So everything here --\par \par \uc2 COHEN: And it`s amazing that I`m the only one. You know, it`s amazing that I`m the only one that ended up doing prison, right? And indicted, incarcerated out of his entire group.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s funny you bring --\par \par \uc2 COHEN: I mean, the documents speak for themselves.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s funny you say this because I wonder, here you have, you`ve been cooperative to the government both on a federal level, on a state level. Some might argue, oh, you could`ve been more cooperative at different times. But let`s set that aside. You have.\par \par \uc2 No one else has been either held accountable or has come forward. Why do you think that is?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Because the system is broken. I did over 400 hours of cooperation. I didn`t get a day off. I didn`t get a minute off. I didn`t get a second off of my sentence. They ran me door to door on this, you know, which basically was nothing more than campaign finance violation.\par \par \uc2 All of the allegations that were centered around tax evasion are just not true. And, you know, again when they squeezed me, they squeezed me so hard that all -- and mine was in 48 hours. I had from Friday to Monday to plead guilty.\par \par \uc2 But I don`t even want to go into that. That part of my life, I`m trying to put behind me. I`m trying to move forward. Now the goal is to ensure that those people who are responsible become responsible for their dirty deeds.\par \par \uc2 I should not be responsible for Donald Trump`s dirty deeds. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the one who was involved with the campaign finance violation, as was Allen Weisselberg, as was \uc2 Don Trump Jr\uc2 ., Ivanka, Eric, you know, and several other individuals. They need to be held accountable. And I, like everybody else, am waiting for both Cyrus Vance Jr.`s District Attorney case, as well as Tish James` civil case to move forward, and start moving forward a little quicker.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you now believe that the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  is a criminal enterprise?\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Yeah, I don`t even know how to answer that. Are they a criminal enterprise? Let`s just say that they committed crimes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And why are you so convinced he`s not going to run, when it seems as if one could argue \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  humiliating him at that White House Correspondents` Dinner in 2011 motivated him to run. I know you think humiliation is the reason why he may not want to run and lose again. But we`ve seen him use that as a motivator before.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Yeah. So this should become a documentary, and it should be called the greatest grift in U.S. history.\par \par \uc2 Look, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has made it very clear, right, that he is grifting off of the American people, these supporters, these individuals that are just sending money to him at record levels. So, one of the biggest problems for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is that he makes this statement, right, that, "I`m thinking about it. I`m thinking about it." That`s only to keep the grift growing and to keep the grift going.\par \par \uc2 It`s really amazing that people don`t see exactly what the guy is doing. I talk about his sociopathy throughout "Disloyal". I talk about it on my podcast, "Mea Culpa", ad nauseam.\par \par \uc2 Please understand, Chuck, and this is really important for all of the viewers as well, one of the things \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has done is grift off of the big lie that the election was stolen from him in 2020.\par \par \uc2 [09:45:10]\par \par \uc2 It was not stolen from him. If he loses, which he will in 2024, what happens to the big lie? The big lie disappears. He can`t now be like the boy who cried wolf. Oh, they stole it from me in 2020. They now stole it from me in 2024, right?\par \par \uc2 Now, that goes out the door and there goes his money. There goes the big grift. So like I said before, it`s not going to happen. He`s going to run it like he did in 2011, right to the very, very last second.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , you`ve spent a lot more time with him than I have and many others who have watched. We`ll invite you back and see what we think soon. Thanks for coming on and I hope you had a good Thanksgiving holiday.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: All righty, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right.\par \par \uc2 When we come back --\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Thank you, you as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- how all that travel on this Thanksgiving Day weekend may have busted you out of your safe COVID bubble and your political bubble as well.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Data Download time.\par \par \uc2 A look at how traveling just a short distance during the holidays can actually put you outside of your political bubble and, of course, that comes with some health impacts as well, and combine with the ongoing pandemic. So I`m going to take a look at Thanksgiving travel. So far, the number of Americans traveling this year has been down, but it is way up.\par \par \uc2 But there`s 27 percent of the country is unvaccinated. Sixty percent of that unvaccinated is Republican. But just because it`s blue state, red state, you can live close together and end up essentially crashing into each other`s blue and red bubbles. Take North Carolina. Mecklenburg County, a big Biden county, has an also higher vaccination rate. Just a 30-minute drive away is Stanly County, a big Trump county with a vaccination rate of under 40 percent.\par \par \uc2 So, just a 30-minute drive actually gets you outside not only your political bubble, but your COVID bubble. And that itself is going to not just roil political debates this holiday season, but also some health debates.\par \par \uc2 All right. When we come back, that Mississippi abortion law heading to the Supreme Court. It`s the biggest abortion case frankly in my lifetime since Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:53:41]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: Welcome back. It`s been a busy show.\par \par \uc2 As we said, we`ve done abortion, we`ve done Trump, we`ve done COVID. I want to go back to abortion, though, because this is, Hallie, going to be the biggest abortion case, the Mississippi case, since Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 I think there`s two -- I don`t feel like there`s a debate about whether the Mississippi law is going to be allowed to go forward. I think really the question is does it go forward under Roe v. Wade, or not?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: And the political impact, too, of whatever the decision that happens, which we won`t know for months, of course, after their arguments happen on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 What is so interesting is this question that has come up that I hear a lot, which is, how much of a galvanizing issue is abortion going to be? I don`t have to tell you that there are typically smaller but very vocal minorities in the parties. So activists, for example, on both sides who want to make this an issue.\par \par \uc2 But what we`ve seen traditionally is that for voters especially in the midterms and in a presidential, it`s just not, right? It`s the economy, right now it`s COVID. We`ve seen that in these polling numbers.\par \par \uc2 But right now, you have some of these laws, so not Mississippi, but when you look at Texas and what we saw there, in some ways, outpacing where the electorate is, even in the Republican Party, right?\par \par \uc2 When you look at some numbers out, for example, from NPR showing that more people -- you know, more Republicans don`t support the various provisions in the Texas law. And so, there`s this -- there`s this gap here between, I think, the laws that are being put in place, and then what the populist even on the conservative side, actually wants to see.\par \par \uc2 [09:55:03]\par \par \uc2 And so, I mean, that`s really coming to a head, I think, this week when you see these arguments.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Gene, is it about Roe actually getting overturned before the left gets galvanized?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: I think probably, yeah. And I think Roe will be overturned. I see -- I see no indication that there isn`t a majority on this court --\par \par \uc2 TODD: You don`t think \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  -- is going to, whatever promise he made to Susan Collins, who swears that he said he wasn`t going to overturn. That he doesn`t find a way to allow the Mississippi law within the -- that he writes an opinion that keeps Roe alive?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: No. I don`t think so. I mean, maybe he does, but I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: I mean, listen, Democrats I could tell you, I mean, facing still political headwinds in 2022 in the midterm election, see this as a galvanizing force for them. They do. I mean, they don`t think they have much help --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: And they want it to be.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: They want it to be. Yeah. They think this is a base turnout operation. I mean, they think it`s a big deal for them. I see you shaking your head.\par \par \uc2 But they don`t think that they have a lot to talk about in 2022. And they think that abortion will help them out.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dany, if they catch the car. Overturning Roe. This is sort of like catching the car on the right. Then what?\par \par \uc2 PLETKA: Well, the discussion I often have with my dog, "What are you going to do then?"\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 No, I don`t think it`s galvanizing, for exactly the reason that Hallie said, which is that at the end of the day, the public has a much more nuanced view. I think a lot of conservatives have concerns about Texas not related to abortion. It`s not about the underlying issue. It`s about the way the law was written. And that has nothing to do with the question underlying it, but the question of empowering individuals to have --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Private citizens, yeah.\par \par \uc2 PLETKA: -- a right of action, right, which I think a lot of people are uncomfortable with.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, we have these conversations in a bubble here in Washington. The reality is that, while there is a very substantial majority in this country that supports a right to abortion in the first trimester, there`s a very substantial majority in this country, in fact, slightly greater in the last poll I saw, that doesn`t believe that abortion should happen after the first trimester.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Here`s the thing if Roe gets overturned, though. One of the things, there are a lot of elected politicians who have been able to say what they want on abortion and never have to vote on it. If you overturn Roe, I don`t care if you`re running for the statehouse or the U.S. House, everyone`s going to actually -- because your guy`s going to vote on something now. You`re going to have to legislate --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: You`re going to have to be pinned down on a position of how you would actually legislate --\par \par \uc2 TODD: So that has a trickle effect on the entire campaign messaging then.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: I mean, you`re right. But again, I think it goes back to what Dany`s talking about. How much does it actually move people when they`re going to the polls to vote you in?\par \par \uc2 I think you`re hitting the nail on the head when it comes to the idea that people up until now -- I shouldn`t say that. In many instances, there are people who are more perhaps in the center of the spectrum on either side of the aisle who can give some nuanced answers on abortion, and maybe some people in their constituency don`t like it, some people do.\par \par \uc2 But this is going to be something where you will have to be more pinned down, right? There is no -- there is no way to run away from the issue at this point.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Look, if Roe goes, I think this is a galvanizing issue for the Democratic base. Now, whether that`s enough for Democrats, that`s another question. But I think --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, Democrats are in charge of Congress still. What are they going to do if Roe`s overturned? I assume they`re going to make some symbolic effort to try to codify it, right?\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: And they already have. They have in the House. It can`t get through the Senate. It`s not going to get through the Senate in any way.\par \par \uc2 And I will also say if you look at this Congress or any Congress in the last ten years and think that they have the ability for political stability or just the heft to deal with a weighty issue like abortion, I don`t know what you`re looking at.\par \par \uc2 PLETKA: Remember gun laws in the Obama administration?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah. Just cue the \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  stuff.\par \par \uc2 All right. Unfortunately, we`re out of time. That`s all we have for today. Thank you for watching. Big shout out to the Maret Frogs State Champs, Class A Football. Good job, Coach Engelberg.\par \par \uc2 Enjoy the rest of your Thanksgiving weekend and we`ll be back next week because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020211128ehbs00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Supreme Court Set To Take Up That Controversial Ban On Most Abortions In Texas Today\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Pete Williams, Hoda Kotb \par \uc2 556 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: Also this morning, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  is taking up two challenges to the most restrictive abortion regulations in the country, the Texas law that`s virtually ended abortions in that state. NBC News justice correspondent Pete Williams has the story for us. Pete, good morning,\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Savannah, good morning. The justices agreed to take these Texas cases on a fast track ramping up the drama over this Supreme Court term. It`s turning out to be one of the most important ever for the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS (voice-over): The court is considering what`s to become of two lawsuits challenging SB 8, the Texas law that has stumped nearly all abortions of the state. The law says they can`t be performed after six weeks of pregnancy before most women know they`re pregnant.\par \par \uc2 AMY HAGSTROM MILLER\uc2 , WHOLE WOMAN`S HEALTH CLINIC FOUNDER: It is meant to intimidate, to threaten, to make us afraid of providing the abortion care that people need.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS (voice-over): The Supreme Court has long said state officials cannot ban abortion that early so the Texas legislature handed off enforcement to private individuals making it harder to challenge the law in court. It allows anyone to sue any doctor who performs an abortion or helps out. Those suing can collect at least $10,000. Supporters say it works.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLYN SCHWARTZ, TEXAS RIGHT TO LIFE DIRECTOR OF MEDIA AND COMMUNICATION: We know that the Texas Heartbeat Act saves about 100 lives per day from abortion.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS (voice-over): Texas is urging the Supreme Court to rule it because none of its officials do anything to enforce the law. They cannot be sued. But abortion providers in the state and the Justice Department both say their lawsuit should go forward. And state court clerks and judges should be blocked from doing anything to carry out the law. The Biden administration says Texas should not be allowed to use its novel law to nullify a constitutional right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Just pass the law empowering complete strangers become bounty hunters going after women who exercise their right to choose.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS (voice-over): And a month from now, the court here is an even bigger case, a challenge from Mississippi to Roe v. Wade itself of decades of rulings on the right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: A Supreme Court decision on the Texas law is probably months away and in the meantime SB 8 is likely to remain in effect. Savannah?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Pete Williams at the court for us. Pete, thank you.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: All right, Craig joins the table now as we turned to a trial that`s being closely watched all around the country. Hey Craig, good morning.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020211102ehb10002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for November 1, 2021, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Pete Williams, Peter Alexander, Anne Thompson, Tom  Costello, Stephanie Gosk, Cynthia McFadden \par \uc2 3,919 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Tonight, the Supreme Court showdown over the controversial abortion law right here in Texas.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voice-over): The justices hearing nearly three hours of arguments over the law banning most abortions in the state. The critical questions the court`s two newest conservative justices asked. And the signals on where the case heads now.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight President Biden on the world stage while his spending plan suffers a new blowback home. What moderate Joe Manchin now says he wants before he`ll back the bill.\par \par \uc2 The President at the U.N. Summit in Scotland urging the world to act on climate change, warning the eyes of history are watching. And President Biden`s apology for something his predecessor \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did.\par \par \uc2 American airlines canceling more than 2000 flights in four days. What the airline is blaming for the chaos.\par \par \uc2 The battle over vaccine mandates. 9000 New York City workers now on unpaid leave for failing to get the shot including police and firefighters. Will it impact emergency response times? And just hours from now, the key CDC advisory meeting on vaccines for children. Will it give the green light?\par \par \uc2 And kicking off our journey across America here in Austin. Why it`s now one of the most popular places to move in America.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt Across America reporting tonight from Austin, Texas.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Good evening from the Texas Capitol AS we begin our Across America journey tonight in Austin. The skyline behind me has come to symbolize one of the stories that brings us here, this city`s blistering growth attracting major tech firms and an explosion of people relocating here for lifestyle and opportunity. We`ll get to that shortly.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the state of Texas is at the center of our top story. The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  today hearing two challenges to the most restrictive abortion regulation in the country, the Texas law that has virtually ended abortions in this state and empowered citizens to enforce it.\par \par \uc2 A number of justices today signaling reservations about the law and its potential broader implications. Pete Williams has details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Opponents of abortion rallied outside the Supreme Court in support of SB8, the Texas law that bans abortion after doctors can detect a fetal heartbeat. That`s at about six weeks of pregnancy before most women know they`re pregnant.\par \par \uc2 REP. SHELBY SLAWSON (R-TX): There is no question that a heartbeat signifies life. And that that beating heart in a womb is the life of a human child.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The Supreme Court has long said state officials cannot ban abortion that early in a pregnancy, so the Texas legislature handed off enforcement to private individuals, allowing anyone to sue any doctor who performs an abortion or anybody who helps out. The state says abortion providers should have to wait until they`re sued before challenging the law.\par \par \uc2 But Texas ran into a wave of skepticism from even some of the court`s conservatives including Amy Coney Barrett and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  who earlier voted against temporarily blocking SB8. Justice Barrett said she doubted that making the challengers wait to be sued would give them a fair shot at defending their rights.\par \par \uc2 AMY CONEY BARRETT, ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: I`m wondering if in a defensive posture in state court, the constitutional defense can be fully aired.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And some members of the court worried that other states could copy the Texas model to limit a host of constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 ELENA KAGAN, ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: We would be like open for business. There`s nothing the Supreme Court can do about it, guns, same-sex marriage, religious rights, whatever you don`t like, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Justice Kavanaugh said a state could allow private lawsuits against the guns it wanted to ban.\par \par \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: Say, everyone who sells an AR-15 is liable for a million dollars to any citizen.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Today`s case was not about abortion itself. The court will take that up a month from today when it hears Mississippi`s challenge to Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: So, Pete, in the meantime, as all this plays out, what becomes of SB8 can it remain in effect for now?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS (on camera): Yes, it can. It remains in effect until the Supreme Court rules. And normally, that would take several months but because the court took these cases on a fast track, it`ll probably be quicker. And then the abortion providers can try again in Texas to get the law blocked, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Pete Williams, thank you. In Scotland today, President Biden told a global summit that climate change is an existential threat to human existence as we know it. Peter Alexander is with the President in Edinburgh tonight.\par \par \uc2 Peter, there`s new resistance back home to the President`s spending plan.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Lester. President Biden is pushing for urgent action here in Scotland. But new criticism from a prominent Democrat is now threatening to slam the brakes on his agenda.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:07]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Tonight, President Biden on the global stage warning this is a decisive decade to combat the ravages of climate change, hoping to showcase American leadership.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We`ll demonstrate to the world the United States is not only back at the table but hopefully leading by the power of our example.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: But back home today, the President facing a new surprising setback in his ongoing struggle to unite Democrats around his $1.75 trillion social and climate spending plan. Today, key moderate Democrat Joe Manchin firing a warning shot saying he`s still not on board.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): This is a recipe for economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And demanding progressives stop delaying a vote on the separate bipartisan infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: The political games have to stop. Holding this bill hostage is not going to work in getting my support.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The White House tonight quickly responding saying, "We remain confident the plan will gain Senator Manchin`s support. The President`s Sunday optimistic.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: God willing, going to be voted on as early as sometime this coming week.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The House`s top progressive tonight saying she trusts the president to handle Manchin`s concerns.\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL\uc2  (D-WA): We intend to pass both bills through the House in the next couple of days.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Tonight, for the president whose approval rating has dropped to 42 percent, his legacy-defining agenda again in doubt. Peter Alexander NBC News Edinboro, Scotland\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CHIEF ENVIRONMENTAL AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voice- over): I`m Anne Thompson in Glasgow where more than 190 nations are meeting to confront the devastating reality of climate change.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 , PRIME MINISTER, UNITED KINGDOM: It`s one minute to midnight on that doomsday clock and we need to act now.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Trying to speed up the reduction of greenhouse gas emissions that made wildfires, floods, hurricanes, and heat waves worse and more deadly this year.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We meet with the eyes of history upon us.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  with an extraordinary statement about his predecessor \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I do apologize for the fact that the United States, in the last administration, pulled out of Paris Accords and put us behind eight-ball.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: The U.S. is now back in the Paris Agreement. And today, the President promising a climate financing package to help the most vulnerable countries like Tuvalu, a small island nation.\par \par \uc2 If the United States and other countries don`t live up to their promises, what happens to your country?\par \par \uc2 SEVE PAENIU, FINANCE MINISTER, TUVALU: It will be gone. Science tells us in the worst-case scenario, in the next 30 to 50 years, our land will disappear.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Biden vowing to cut U.S. greenhouse gas emissions in half by 2030. A promise that depends on over $500 billion in climate investments in that spending bill Congress has yet to act on. Undeterred in Glasgow, President Biden`s domestic climate chief Gina McCarthy insisting America is all in despite a reluctant Congress and a Supreme Court that will now review the EPA`s authority to limit greenhouse gases.\par \par \uc2 GINA MCCARTHY, WHITE HOUSE NATIONAL CLIMATE ADVISOR: I`m still pretty confident that the Clean Air Act is all about addressing pollution and I`m pretty sure that people are convinced that carbon is a pollutant.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Anne, there`s been talk about trying to make sure the earth doesn`t warm more than 2.7 degrees by the end of the century. Is that`s even still possible?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (on camera): It is, Lester. But right now, the world is on track to warm nearly double that amount by the end of the century with catastrophic consequences. So scientists say we need faster and bigger emissions cuts. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Ann Thompson tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Here at home, \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  says it`s getting back to normal after canceling thousands of flights over the last three days affecting more than 200 thousand passengers. Tom Costello is at Reagan National Airport. Tom, what went wrong?\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, the airline is blaming high winds and reduced runway capacity in Dallas late last week, and then a series of cascading problems that just got worse and worse. In fact, very quickly, it found it didn`t have enough flight attendants. They were out of position.\par \par \uc2 It canceled 2200 flights since last Friday, 20 percent of flights on Sunday alone. Today, about eight percent of flights canceled. That`s nearly 500 flights affecting tens of thousands of passengers. The airline says about 1800 flight attendants who have been on leave are now coming back. It expects to be back to normal operations tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 But the pilots union is saying that corporate mismanagement is to blame here. And the pilots are warning this could be a rough holiday travel season. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tom Costello, thank you. The battle over COVID vaccine mandates is still playing out tonight among municipal workers in the nation`s largest city as the CDC gets closer to authorizing the vaccine for younger children. Stephanie Gosk now with the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: The \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine for small children is out the door. The White House says shipments started as soon as the \uc2 FDA\uc2  recommended emergency use authorization.\par \par \uc2 JEFF ZIENTS, WHITE HOUSE CORONAVIRUS RESPONSE COORDINATOR: We expect that several million doses are already in route to sites around the country.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:06]\par \par \uc2 GOSK: A CDC committee meets tomorrow, one of the last steps towards the EUA.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I want to be able to do the things with them that we used to be able to do without fear that they`re going to get sick or that they`re going to get anybody else sick.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: The children at the front of the line would be fully vaccinated by Christmas. But some parents are unwavering. They don`t want their kids in line at all.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: To me, personally, I just -- I don`t want to take the risk for them.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Meanwhile, in New York City today, the clock ran out on unvaccinated city workers. No shot means no job and no paycheck. Before sunrise this morning, the heads of the FDNY union were sounding the alarm about firefighter shortages.\par \par \uc2 People were worried this morning that response times.\par \par \uc2 BOBBY EUSTACE, VICE PRESIDENT, UNIFORMED FIREFIGHTERS ASSOCIATION: Absolutely. They will be. That`s why we`re out here at 6:00 in the morning basically to alert the public to be warned. We`re concerned.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: The FDNY Fire Division has a vaccination rate of 77 percent according to the city. But overall, more than 91 percent of city workers have gotten the shot. Today, there are 9000 employees on leave without pay. According to the mayor, city services still ran smoothly. But with police officers, firefighters, and sanitation workers looking at smaller crews and longer hours, many worry that won`t last. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In just 60 seconds, we`ll take you inside this boomtown here in Austin. Why so many people are moving here and how some tell us they`re being priced out.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Back now in Austin, the nation`s 11th most populous city and one of its fastest-growing welcoming a surge of transplants including some very big companies helping to make Austin one of the country`s premier tech hubs and a place so many Americans now want to be.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Austin, basking in its status as one of America`s biggest boom towns. New construction everywhere. It`s famous live music scene buzzing. A national survey showing it`s the most popular place for Americans to move during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Like Josh and Jesse Rubico who came to the area with their two small children from California just five months ago telling us the pandemic prompted their decision.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We were like, there`s got to be a better way. How can we buy back time to spend more time with family?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And this affords that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, for sure.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It has so far. Yes. We`re really, really happy with the move. We kind of felt like we were running the rat race in California because of the cost of living and, you know, just trying to keep up with everything.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Incredibly, in their Austin suburb, they`re one of at least six families just on their block who have moved to Austin from out of state since last year.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Rock, paper, scissors, shoot.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Including next-door neighbor Ozzie Hanley.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Ultimately, decided in Austin because it`s young, it`s hip, it`s very interesting, lots of sports for kids, also much cheaper than California. Across town, Jessica Stovall says the way Texas dealt with the pandemic impacted her decision to move from New Orleans two months ago.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The laws in Texas i felt like handled things a lot better than the laws in Louisiana. We were shut down for almost a year and a half where we couldn`t even work or you know, do our livelihood.\par \par \uc2 Austin`s boom has also been boosted by its surging tech industry. Apple is constructing a massive campus here. \uc2 Samsung\uc2  is considering building a new facility. And just last month, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  announcing he`s moving tesla`s headquarters here from California.\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, CEO, SPACEX: You know, factory is like five minutes from the airport, 15 minutes from downtown. And we`re going to create an ecological paradise.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tahera Rahman covers business for our Austin station KXAN. Why do the tech companies find Austin so attractive right now.\par \par \uc2 TAHERA RAHMAN, NEWSCASTER, KXAN: Warm climate. You`re seeing it right now. Also the low taxes. It`s considered very business-friendly.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Are there concerns here in Austin about the pace of growth that maybe things are moving too quickly?\par \par \uc2 [19:15:03]\par \par \uc2 RAHMAN: Absolutely. And right now, I think city and county leaders are seeing it as more of an opportunity to manage that growth in an equitable way.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Some of those challenges, public transportation issues and a rise in homelessness. And like so many American cities this year, homicides here are spiking up 80 percent though Austin`s total crime rate is down. Then there are the rising prices. Austin`s job growth has made a hot housing market even more competitive. The median sales price for a home $450,000. That`s up more than 28 percent from last year.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: People were coming in with offers that were like 10 to 15 percent above list price.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Abigail Chua has been here since 2018. Her fiance, Andrew, came in 2020. The couple had been looking to buy a home for eight months, then stopped feeling priced out, but still saying they have no plans to leave.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There`s plenty of outdoor activities, there`s tons of opportunity. Who wouldn`t want to be in Austin?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): A lot of this city feeling optimism about the future. Up next for us, a state divided inside the controversial new voting law here in Texas and what it could mean for voter access.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`re back now with our brand new NBC News poll that finds just two- thirds of registered voters believe their votes will be counted accurately, and that`s down from 85 percent a year earlier. It comes as several GOP-led states including Texas have passed new voting laws. Texas Republicans say the changes are popular among most voters here, but in heavily Democratic Harris County, many are blasting them. Here`s Cynthia McFadden.\par \par \uc2 [19:20:18]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Wow, it`s just beautiful.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, it is perfect.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA MCFADDEN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Representative Jim Murphy, head of the Texas House Republican Caucus helped lead the charge for the new Texas voting law.\par \par \uc2 JIM MURPHY, REPUBLICAN TEXAS STATE REPRESENTATIVE: We are in a fight to save our democracy.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: 50 enraged Democrats tried to scuttle the bill by fleeing to D.C. It passed anyway. Provisions include new voter ID requirements to vote by mail and elimination of all voter drop boxes. The law also scraps two recent changes in Houston`s Harris County, 24-hour voting and drive-through voting, though people with disabilities can still vote from their cars.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: These laws are intended to get more people to vote and to make sure the people that do vote have their votes counted.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: But Houston`s Democratic Mayor \uc2 Sylvester Turner\uc2  could not disagree more.\par \par \uc2 You`ve called it Jim Crow 2.0\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER TURNER\uc2 , MAYOR, HOUSTON: Right. Underscore underline, put quotation marks around it, and then circle it because it`s serious.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: So, who`s right?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: We have actually added days to early voting and more hours on those days.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: He is right about that. There`s already a two-week early voting period in Texas. And the new law adds two days to that and extends daily voting times by at least an hour. But the mayor says such improvements are just window dressing covering up changes that will make it harder for Black, Brown, and poor people to vote.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Over 50 percent of the people who participated in the 24-hour voting were people of color.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Like Sylvia Pina a working mother of five who says without it, she could not have cast her ballot.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s not allowing people who want to vote to go vote. I think it`s suppressing their voice and what they want to change.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Not so says representative murphy. He says the new law will restore voter confidence and prevent cheating. But is there widespread voter fraud? Records from the Texas Attorney General`s Office reveal since 2004, only 154 people have been charged with voting illegally out of 94 million votes cast.\par \par \uc2 Are you trying to solve a problem that doesn`t exist?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: It`s sort of like locking your house at night. What`s the chance of getting broken into? Really small. But you go ahead and do these things to secure things. And I don`t think there`s anything we want secured more than our election process.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The intent is to lock in those who are already in power and they attempted to do that by restricting and suppressing people`s right to vote.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Not true says Representative Murphy, pointing out his party dropped a proposal to eliminate Sunday morning voting popular with many African Americans. Meanwhile, here in Houston`s Harris County, many believe the new election laws which go into effect next month is going to make voting harder, not easier. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Cynthia, thank you. Up next, we meet the two Austin sisters who turned a food truck into one of the hottest tickets in town all during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Welcome back to Austin. Earlier, we told you about the tech boom here. But the growth has also been a huge lift for many small businesses including one we found that has just the right ingredients for success. We`re inside one of Austin`s most popular kitchens. Rayna and Maritza Vasquez with the taco secret taking this city by storm. Weekends at Veracruz all-natural mean lots of lines.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: These are the best tacos I think in the city, maybe in the country.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: But it wasn`t always that way for the sisters who came here from Mexico. Did you have success right away?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well it took like about three years to really make money for ourselves. We only sold like probably $80.00 a day from seven to seven.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It was frustrating because, you know, no money and just stay there waiting and waiting. So, it was hard.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: But now they`ve gone from a single food trailer to six locations in Austin, four food trucks and two restaurants. And when so many businesses were crushed by pandemic lockdowns, it was those outdoor food trucks that saved them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: People were more prone to come to a food truck than a restaurant.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Today, Reyna and Maritza showing us how to make one of their most popular tacos, Migas, a breakfast taco filled with scrambled eggs, pico de gallo, fresh tortilla chips, and a few other ingredients.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We grew up with my mom in the kitchen. I found out that i love being in the kitchen like her, so -- and business is something that we really enjoy.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: That family tradition now expanding to L.A. where they just opened a food truck called hot tacos.\par \par \uc2 What do we have?\par \par \uc2 Two sisters hoping to export their amazing Austin success.\par \par \uc2 It`s wonderful.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Funny I never leave Texas hungry. And our journey across America continues tomorrow in St. Louis in American city seeing a surge in violent crime. We`ll take you inside the effort by the community to stem the surge an issue so many cities are facing across the country.\par \par \uc2 That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Monday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. And so long from Austin.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020211102ehb100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for October 29, 2021, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Tom Costello, Gabe Gutierrez, Peter Alexander,  Stephanie Gosk, Miguel Almaguer, Jake Ward, Morgan Radford \par \uc2 4,065 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Breaking News tonight. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  authorizing the COVID vaccine for emergency use in children.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voice-over): The \uc2 FDA\uc2  clearing pediatric doses of Pfizer`s vaccine for some 28 million kids ages five to 11. The final decision up to the CDC. How soon could shots go into arms? Also, the deadline for New York City workers to get vaccinated. Will it mean fewer police and ambulances on the street.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, President Biden overseas without a deal and his agenda back home. The President that the Vatican today. What \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  told him amid calls from us bishops to deny him communion over his abortion stance.\par \par \uc2 Also, the first face-to-face meeting with the President of France since the rift with the U.S. over a nuclear submarine deal. What President Biden admitted during their meeting.\par \par \uc2 The Mid Atlantic bracing for the worst coastal flooding in nearly 20 years. Will it move out in time for Halloween this weekend?\par \par \uc2 Growing concerns about Queen Elizabeth`s health. What doctors have now advised her to do.\par \par \uc2 The crew member in charge of the guns on Alec Baldwin`s movie set breaking her silence about the deadly shooting. What she says about that fatal live round.\par \par \uc2 And he ran the very first New York City Marathon. Now, on the 50th running, he`s returning to the starting line.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening and thank you for joining us. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  tonight has given the OK for kids ages five to 11 to receive Pfizer COVID shots. The first injections likely just days away. With 28 million children eligible, today`s Emergency Use Authorization is a potential game-changer in the fight against the pandemic, especially in the nation`s beleaguered schools, and could narrow the vaccination get that tonight leaves fewer than 58 percent of Americans fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 All of this follows the recommendation of a safety panel which determine the child-size dose to be safe and effective. The CDC expected now to follow up shortly with its endorsement. Tom Costello now with late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): This afternoon`s \uc2 FDA\uc2  news means hospitals and clinics have just days to prepare for another wave of vaccinations. At Inova Hospital in Fairfax, Virginia, they`re prepping to vaccinate 500 young children each day, even bringing in therapy dogs and Star Wars characters.\par \par \uc2 How do you make this clinical setting less stressful for kids?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE GENTILE, CHILD LIVE SERVICE DIRECTOR, INOVA FAIRFAX HOSPITAL: Lots of things. We give kids stress balls, things to hang on to, distraction. We have costumed characters. We use the word poke instead of shots. We let kids sit on mom`s lap. We let them watch a video for distraction.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: With full CDC approval expected soon, needles could be going into young arms as soon as the middle of next week, critical to controlling the pandemic and getting kids back to a more normal life. 28 million 5 to 11- year-olds could be eligible for the shot. Already, 8300 children in that age group have been hospitalized with COVID, 146 have died. The eighth highest killer.\par \par \uc2 Pfizer`s vaccine offered a nearly 91 percent efficacy in preventing severe infection in children. With only a third of the adult dose offered to kids, the side effects were mostly mild. There were no cases of the extremely rare heart inflammation seen in some older boys and young men.\par \par \uc2 DR. PAUL OFFITT, COMMITTEE MEMBER, \uc2 FDA\uc2  ADVISORY: I think what parents can expect if they vaccinate their 5 to 11-year-old that a certain percentage will have fever, headache, muscle ache, joint pain. The good news is that that just lasts a couple days and then it goes away on its own.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Children`s hospital in Colorado was a vaccine trial center. Now, it`s preparing for mass vaccinations and urging parents to sign their kids up.\par \par \uc2 DR. BRANDI FREEMAN, PEDIATRICIAN, CHILDREN`S HOSPITAL COLORADO: We`ve hospitalized over 2000 children since the start of the pandemic. And so, what`s it`s really important to realize is this is happening to children. Children are getting sick. Children are being hospitalized and it`s a serious thing for them.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: With children making up 20 percent of the population, experts believe vaccinating them is critical to the country achieving so-called herd immunity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Tom, government approvals aside, the fact remains a lot of parents are still nervous about getting their kids vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (on camera): Yes. But experts make the point that more children have been hospitalized with COVID than with the flu. And they`re very worried about long-term effects on kids, brain fog, muscle aches, headaches that can last for weeks, months, maybe even longer. And they really believe the benefits far outweigh the minuscule risks associated with a vaccine, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right. Tom Costello, thank you. Here in New York, a sweeping vaccine mandate for municipal workers took effect tonight, while numerous other states are suing to block federal vaccine requirements. Gabe Gutierrez now has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Lay today, as New York City`s COVID vaccine mandate for municipal workers kicked in, the unions for the nation`s largest fire department warned of the potential for close firehouses and longer emergency response times.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:11]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: New York City was going to come to a crisis on Monday morning.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Earlier today the city said just 67 percent of its firefighters had received at least one dose. Those who don`t get the shot face unpaid leave starting Monday. Tonight, six firefighters had been suspended for driving a ladder truck while on duty to a state senator`s office and allegedly threatening the senator staff over the COVID vaccine mandate.\par \par \uc2 From coast to coast, more showdowns over shots. Los Angeles County Sheriff \uc2 Alex Villanueva\uc2  is calling his area`s COVID vaccine mandate an imminent threat to public safety, saying it`s causing a mass exodus within his department. Also, in California popular burger chain In and Out just shut down more restaurants rather than comply with mandates.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I can see where they`re coming from. That it`s not their job to do, as they put it, the vaccine police.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Today, 10 states filed a new lawsuit against the Biden administration`s vaccine requirement for federal contractors. Iowa`s governor signed a new law allowing workers fired for non-compliance with COVID vaccine mandates to receive unemployment benefits.\par \par \uc2 And next Tuesday, a new deadline for the Air Force. Up to 12,000 personnel have rejected orders to get fully vaccinated despite a Pentagon mandate. Still, the vast majority of active-duty airman, more than 96 percent are at least partially vaccinated. And tonight, word that the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  won`t block a COVID vaccine mandate for healthcare workers in Maine.\par \par \uc2 Back in New York City, officials say the last-minute push is working. \uc2 NYPD\uc2  now reporting more than 80 percent compliance up from 70 percent last week.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think we`re heading in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (on camera): And with the rise of vaccinations and the fading Delta variants, some encouraging news. New York City just reported a COVID test positivity rate of less than one percent. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Gutierrez here in New York, thank you.\par \par \uc2 At the Vatican today, the President and the pontiff. President Biden meeting with \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 . Then, Mr. Biden making a striking admission while speaking to the French president. Peter Alexander is at the Vatican.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): For President Biden, a day of devotion and diplomacy. America`s second-ever Roman Catholic President having a private audience with \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  with whom he`s built a personal bond, giving him a ceremonial commander coin and accomplishment.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You are the most significant warrior for peace I`ve ever met.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: President Biden was later asked whether the issue of abortion came up.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, it didn`t. we just talked about the fact that he was happy I was a good Catholic and I could keep receiving communion.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: That has been a source of controversy with some U.S. bishops recently arguing Catholic politicians who support abortion rights including the president should be denied communion. Just last month \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  called abortion homicide, but he`s never denied anyone communion. And the Vatican statement today notably made no mention of the highly contentious topic.\par \par \uc2 Hours later, President Biden look to rebuild trust with America`s oldest ally meeting with French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  for the first time since Macron recalled France`s ambassador from Washington, furious the U.S. took a multibillion-dollar submarine deal away from France without notice.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden admitting to Macron his administration made a mistake.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think what happened was, to use an English phrase, was clumsy, wasn`t done with a lot of grace.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Macron looking to move past the dispute.\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL MACRON, PRESIDENT, FRANCE: What really matters now is what we will do together in the coming weeks, the coming months, the coming years.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The President arriving in Europe without the political win he`d been hoping for, despite privately pushing Democrats at the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  Thursday to get behind the $1.75 trillion framework for his social spending and climate plan, even arguing his presidency is on the line.\par \par \uc2 But while progressives endorse that plan, they still refuse to vote for it or the trillion-dollar bipartisan infrastructure bill until they`re convinced moderate holdout Senators Joe Manchin and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  are on board too.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): We need a little bit more than an IOU. We need to know what it is that we`re going to commit to.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Peter joining me now from the Vatican. And Peter, climate is going to be on the President`s agenda all this week.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (on camera): Yes, Lester. That`s right. The G-20. Summit gets underway here tomorrow with climate a top priority. During that hour and 15 minute private meeting today, President Biden says that \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  emphasized to him the moral responsibility to protect the planet. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Peter Alexander, thank you.\par \par \uc2 New details tonight about the groping case against former New York Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 . The Albany County Sheriff says there`s overwhelming amount of evidence against Cuomo, who faces a misdemeanor charge of forcibly touching a female aide. He`ll be arraigned in mid-November. His lawyer says he never assaulted anyone.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, another pro sports league is under fire. The \uc2 NHL\uc2  being shaken by a sexual abuse scandal that has caused a head coach and two top executives their jobs. Stephanie Gosk now with the leaked details.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:16]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): It`s one of the biggest scandals the \uc2 NHL\uc2  has ever faced. Resignations mounting. Florida Panthers coach Joel Quenneville is gone. GM for the Chicago Blackhawks and a senior VP for the team also gone. Fallout from the release of an independent investigation that concluded \uc2 Blackhawks\uc2  management ignored a sexual assault allegation against one of its coaches in 2010.\par \par \uc2 KYLE BEACH, NFL PLAYER: I felt like I was alone and there was nothing I could do and nobody I could turn to for help.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Kyle Beach was 20 years old, called up to the \uc2 Blackhawks\uc2  as an alternate during the playoffs. That`s when he says the video coach Brad Aldrich threatened to hurt his career if he didn`t engage in sexual acts. A week after the incident, according to the law firm`s report beach told the team.\par \par \uc2 REID SCHAR, INVESTIGATION ATTORNEY: After being informed of Aldrich`s alleged sexual harassment and misconduct with the player, no action was taken for three weeks.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: According to the report, Quenneville, the coach at the time, wanted to focus on the finals. When they won, Aldrich still had his job and his name on the Stanley Cup. These photos included in the report.\par \par \uc2 BEACH: It made me feel like I didn`t exist. It made me feel that I wasn`t important.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: A month later, Aldrich resigned. In 2013, he pleaded guilty to a sexual misconduct charge involving a minor. Aldrich`s attorney says his client denies the report`s allegations. The \uc2 Blackhawks\uc2  and the \uc2 NHL\uc2  issued apologies. The team calling it inexcusable, adding no championship is more important than protecting our players.\par \par \uc2 Quenneville expressing his sorrow admitting he and the team failed the young player.\par \par \uc2 The NHL Commissioner says he is appalled that beach was so poorly supported.\par \par \uc2 Have you seen any indication that beyond this very specific investigation, the league is looking to address a larger cultural issue?\par \par \uc2 NANCY ARMOUR, SPORTS COLUMNIST, USA TODAY: No, and I think that they will be quite happy to just move on from this. And you know, if you don`t address these problems, you`re not fixing a bit.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Beach says he hopes the league will choose to focus less on its image and more on keeping this from happening again.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Gosk, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In just 60 seconds, new developments in that deadly shooting on a movie set. What lawyers for the woman in charge of the guns are saying about her role. And a new update on the health of Queen Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Just in tonight, Maryland declaring a state of emergency as a severe storm pushes through the mid-Atlantic and northeast. Heavy rain causing flooding in and around Washington D.C. 18 million under alerts on what could be the worst coastal flooding since 2003. Up to three feet in some areas. The storm is expected to clear out by Halloween on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, the crew member in charge of the guns on the Alec Baldwin movie set where a fatal shooting occurred is breaking her silence about the live round in Baldwins gone in what she calls unsafe working conditions. Here`s Miguel Almaguer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Saying she`s been slandered and falsely portrayed, today, Hannah Gutierrez-Reid, the 24- year-old in charge of guns on the set of Rust is sharing her side of the story. In a statement, her attorneys write, Hannah was hired on two positions on this film, which made it extremely difficult to focus on her job as an armorer. She fought for training, but ultimately was overruled by a production and her department.\par \par \uc2 But tonight, sources close to the production say no one was looking to cut corners and she never worked in both positions at the same time.\par \par \uc2 ADAN MENDOZA, SHERIFF, SANTA FE COUNTY: Although we appreciate the statement given by Miss Gutierrez-Reid, I think it raises more questions than it does answers.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:05]\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Investigators say a single bullet that should have never been on set was placed inside a revolver like this one before Alec Baldwin pulled the trigger and killed cinematographer Halyna Hutchins. Hannah has no idea where the live rounds came from, her attorney say. Hannah and the prop master never witnessed anyone shoot live rounds with these guns.\par \par \uc2 STEVE WOLF, FIREARMS AND PYROTECHNICS EXPERT: How could the armor not know that there was live ammo being put into these guns? That`s exactly what the armors job is to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (on camera): The sheriff has said the investigation into what happened here could take weeks even months. In the meantime, more witnesses are expected to step forward and share their side of the story. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Miguel Almaguer in New Mexico, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Queen Elizabeth has been advised by her doctors to rest for at least the next two weeks, Buckingham Palace said today. The 95-year-old Monarch was briefly hospitalized last week. The Queen will not receive any official visits and has canceled her appearance at Britain`s Festival of Remembrance on November 13.\par \par \uc2 Up next for us tonight, the sky-high prices, the delays. What to know about renovating your home in the Price You Pay.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: If you have been looking for a new home, then you know the market remains red hot. The median home price soaring about $400,000 for the first time. Many are choosing to renovate instead but that`s tough as well. Here`s Jake Ward.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Sit right here.\par \par \uc2 [19:20:02]\par \par \uc2 JAKE WARD, NBC NEWS TECHNOLOGY CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Homeowner and mom Amber Bustanami saved for years to renovate her home in Seattle.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And our kids are teenagers and they`re growing.\par \par \uc2 WARD: You budgeted for this one way, and I`m assuming that the price has changed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The lumber prices obviously have gone up.\par \par \uc2 WARD: For contractor Josh McDowell says he turns away roughly 10 jobs a week. Now, clients may need to wait for lower lumber prices.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, they`ve already been waiting six to eight months to have their project done, and then you have to tell them they now need to wait another year or so.\par \par \uc2 WARD: In fact, the backup and the workload is so intense Bustanami just took a job working with McDowell.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, new homes remain hard to come by. Construction fell by 1.6 percent in September as supply chain failures continue to choke the market.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You can understand why now house costs $700 to $800,000 if it costs 200 Just to add an addition to it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There you go.\par \par \uc2 WARD: At Stuart Lumber, basic plywood cost about 25 percent more today than it did pre-pandemic.\par \par \uc2 RYAN YOUNG, STEWART LUMBER AND HARDWARE CO.: You don`t want people walking out of your store because prices are just too high.\par \par \uc2 WARD: Materials like these were bought at a high price, and as a result, retailers tell us they can`t afford to sell them to you at a loss.\par \par \uc2 Even glue costs more if you can get it at all.\par \par \uc2 YOUNG: You will get it in on a Tuesday, it will run out by Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 WARD: Experts say you`ll want to wait until next year at least to get started on a new project. Adjust your designs to use less wood if you can and plan for these new prices because they may not fall.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The plywoods and stuff like that, they`ll never come back down to where they were.\par \par \uc2 WARD: Jake Ward, NBC News Seattle.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The holiday known as Day of the Dead is about to be celebrated. And for many, it`s taking on an added meaning this year with a toll of a pandemic. Here`s Morgan Radford.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MORGAN RADFORD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Lupe Calderon lost her mother to COVID in December, just weeks after her father`s death.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s two of the most people that I love the most.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Now, she`s turning those memories into an ofrenda or offering, a tradition of Dia de los Muertos or Day of the Dead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I want to honor them for everything that they`ve done for us. It`s a day for me to commemorate them and show them the love that we have for them.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: The holiday began in Mexico nearly 3000 years ago. Those who celebrate believe on midnight of October 31st the souls of their loved ones come back to visit. And when they do, ofrendas make them feel at home. How tall is this?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: 15 feet.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: That`s why Isabella Randes made hers bigger than ever.\par \par \uc2 Why was it especially important for you to make this this year?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Friends, family dying of this virus. I just wanted to lift up the spirits and just remember that they will never be forgotten.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Here in the National Museum of Mexican art, this ofrenda honors Latinos who died of COVID.\par \par \uc2 Does this day have a different significance this year in light of COVID?\par \par \uc2 CESAREO MORENO, CHIEF CURATOR AND VISUAL ARTS DIRECTOR, NATIONAL MUSEUM OF MEXICAN ART: It`s more important than ever. We`ve not been able to mourn as a community. This year, the Day of the Dead really allows for that to happen.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I put a candle for them. I put their favorite food. It`s though as if they were alive to me.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Alive through memory and tradition. Morgan Radford, NBC News, Chicago.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Up next, I hit the road with a marathon man making history and inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, just over a week from now thousands of runners will hit the pavement for the 50th New York City Marathon including one man who was there from the very start returning now with a different motivation that`s inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voice-over): Larry Trachtenberg has been down this road before, one of the original 55 runners who completed the first New York City Marathon which in 1970 was an understated event confined to Manhattan Central Park.\par \par \uc2 What`s it like to be in Central Park again?\par \par \uc2 LARRY TRACHTENBERG, RUNNER: This is amazing. It`s great.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: There was no fanfare back then, no roars from crowds lining the streets, and no buildup.\par \par \uc2 TRACHTENBERG: Little blurb in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  saying that there was going to be a marathon in Central Park.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A teenager then, now the 67-year-old Trachtenberg is back returning to the starting line for the marathon`s 50th anniversary.\par \par \uc2 What do you think we when you watch the New York City Marathon now?\par \par \uc2 TRACHTENBERG: All these years, you know, that I`ve been in Oregon, you know, watching it on TV, you know, it`s like this hang in me. You know, God, you know, I got to get back there sometime to do this thing, you know. It make me homesick every time I watch the marathon.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: So, what made you decide to come back this year to New York City?\par \par \uc2 TRACHTENBERG: So, you know, I kind of had like this -- always had this pipe dream, you know, kind of like that I would return.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: For Lary, November 7 marks more than just his story of triumph. He represents the resilience of all the runners regaining their stride after a canceled race last year. Just one of the many things that pandemic had taken from the city.\par \par \uc2 TRACHTENBERG: The city is going to be open again. It`s going to be a celebration. And I said, you know, I want to be part of that celebration. It`s part is my history, you know, like why shouldn`t I be there?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A finish line that once felled so far, now the start of a comeback.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): And I`ll be doing a little running myself next week mostly racing for planes as we take the broadcast across America to Austin, Texas, St. Louis, Washington D.C., Nashville, and Phoenix, where I`ll be reporting on top issues affecting you from crime to jobs, housing to education.\par \par \uc2 And that`s NIGHTLY NEWS for tonight. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020211030ehat00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Kristen Dahlgren Shares An Encouraging Step In Her Own Breast Cancer Journey\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Savannah Guthrie, Jo Kristen Dahlgren \par \uc2 5,859 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: We`re back with Pink Power and a story for breast cancer awareness month that`s very close to our hearts, the journey of our friend and our colleague Kristen Dahlgren.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: Yes. It`s been almost two years since Kristen first shared her diagnosis right here on the show. And she`s back this morning with an update and some inspiration. And just look at you, that`s our inspiration.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN DAHLGREN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: You know what, it is so good to be back here with all of you, what a two years it`s been. I finished my treatment the first month of the pandemic, since then I`ve had no evidence of disease. But I have to say the time since treatment ended has been the hardest for me. I`ve had complications with reconstruction and multiple surgeries. Emotionally, I`ve struggled.\par \par \uc2 But then, I discovered a foundation for women just like me. I headed to Utah with them to shoot this piece. And in the process, I started rewriting my own story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voice-over): Sunrise near Zion National Park, a moment of peace, something I haven`t had much of the past two years. My cancer may be gone but can still feel like a constant companion.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A lot of us, you know, are told we`re done. Now you should go back to your normal life and kind of stop talking about it and we`ve been forever altered. There`s no going back.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voice-over): So going forward has led me here with a group of women who knows surviving breast cancer is just the first step. This year, more than 300,000 women are expected to be diagnosed with breast cancer. Like us, one in five will be under the age of 49.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN LICHOTA, FOUNDER, IRISE ABOVE BREAST CANCER: Our needs are just completely different. There`s unique challenges that are associated with breast cancer at a young age.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voice-over): Gillian Lichota founded \uc2 iRise\uc2  Above Breast Cancer, a nonprofit to meet those needs. For women who may be mid career, active, oftentimes mothers.\par \par \uc2 LICHOTA: The day that I found out that I was pregnant, I also found out that I had breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voice-over): While carrying her baby, Gillian underwent surgery and chemotherapy. She had a healthy son and a lot to live for.\par \par \uc2 LICHOTA: I really set my intention on wanting to summit Mount Kilimanjaro.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voice-over): She did it and set her sights on helping others, in a group that combines the benefits of fitness, mindfulness, camaraderie and adventure.\par \par \uc2 [08:40:05]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How`s that feel, Gillian?\par \par \uc2 LICHOTA: Fresh.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: I think for a lot of us, you think that part of your life is over when you get a breast cancer diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 LICHOTA: I think a lot of women mourn what they thought their life was going to be, but I think it`s important that women realize that this is an opportunity to be transformed.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voice-over): On this day, transformation includes stepping off a cliff.\par \par \uc2 And I mentioned I`m petrified of heights.\par \par \uc2 But with my new sisters looking on.\par \par \uc2 This is really hard\par \par \uc2 Patiently\par \par \uc2 This is crazy.\par \par \uc2 I start to believe in myself.\par \par \uc2 I did it.\par \par \uc2 Surrounded by nature, and some of the most inspiring women I have ever met. Kate Lowry completed chemo three days after our climb.\par \par \uc2 After this whole journey, this is just the great pinnacle.\par \par \uc2 Deborah Hutchinson was eight months pregnant when she was diagnosed.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH HUTCHINSON, DIAGNOSED WITH BREAST CANCER: There`s no boundaries to what you can or cannot do. Breast Cancer doesn`t limit us.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voice-over): Christy Mangan is raising two sons and ran the Boston Marathon after cancer.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTY MANGAN, BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR: I feel very fortunate to be nine years past my last treatment and cancer free. But that fear still remains. It is something that I think about every day.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voice-over): Yes, there is also very real talk about the challenges we still face, physically and emotionally. Gillian`s cancer returned, stage four metastatic.\par \par \uc2 LICHOTA: That heavy weight over my head.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voice-over): Sorry. I cried the first time I talked to you because I`m so petrified of it coming back. And yet, I see you living this amazing life and doing so much. How do you do it?\par \par \uc2 LICHOTA: I just don`t want cancer to take anything more of me. And I`m here now, I plan to celebrate my 100 birthday, with my husband and my children.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voice-over): And hopefully all of us, bonded by a devastating diagnosis, but learning to live with it. For me, every step is easier now that I know, I`m not alone.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED SPEAKERS (in unison): \uc2 iRise\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh my god.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voice-over): It was transformative. I got back and my husband said you`re you again. It really just changed my outlook on everything.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You`re you again, that`s what he said?\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Yes. I mean, I`ve been struggling. And there was a lot that I needed to deal with. And just to turn my mindset into positivity, and see these incredible women.\par \par \uc2 DALY: That`s the power of community, right? You go away, go to Utah.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Right.\par \par \uc2 DALY: You immerse yourself with a sisterhood. All the feelings that I`m alone, when you`re here go away, you start to rebuild, so powerful.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: What`s validating too it`s not you, you know, just because the treatments over people might think, oh, it was just a bounce back. And they`re saying, no, this is a process. It`s a journey, you`re on that journey.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: There`s no roadmap for this. And, you know, you go through treatment, and then you kind of feel like what`s next? And these women helped me see what can be next.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. I think a lot of women do want to learn more. When I saw that I was like, where is that place? So if you`re interested, how can people learn more about this spot? How can they get involved?\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Right. So we`re going to have more on today.com. People can get more information. There are webinars and fitness plans through \uc2 iRise\uc2  Above as well as these trips with which they do in partnership with a group called Adventure Women. And so, I encourage everyone to check.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Kristen, my favorite part was when you said I did it.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes, you conquer the fear.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Yes, it`s really hard. I`m not going to say, you know, breast cancer wasn`t hard but (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Well, you did great. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Thanks for sharing that.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: That was beautiful. Thanks, Kristen.\par \par \uc2 DALY: All right. We`ve got a lot more ahead including the return of some fan favorite Steals and Deals. These are big bargains, but they`re not going to last long. But first, this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 [08:44:10]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s a fan favorite edition of Steals and Deals, inhale deeply.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Al