cial edition of Today Food.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Yes, a good one. We love supporting local restaurants and our sponsor, Citi, is helping us do just that. When you spend $5 or more dining out with your enrolled Citi credit card, $1 will be donated to a great program No Kid Hungry until they reach $1 million through December 1st. It`s a great way to give local businesses a boost while also helping feed kids across the country.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes. And we asked one of our favorite local chefs who works closely with No Kid Hungry to join us this morning, chef Dan Kluger. He`s the chef and owner of three great New York City restaurants including the recently opened Penny Bridge in Long Island City which is just right across the river here.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Super close. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DAN KLUGER, CHEF: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, I know No Kid Hungry is super important to you.\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: This is a great initiative.\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: I mean getting kids food, that is important.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, let`s -- you`re making steak this morning. It makes Carson so happy.\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: You might help me.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Chef, we`ve talked a lot about the sauce. You`re going to make a sauce.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: We`re going to make the barbecue sauce. She`s going to help as basic ketchup, Worcestershire, molasses, Tabasco, mustard and smoked paprika.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Isn`t it like a stigma of putting a sauce like to the A1 on a steak, sort of -- it`s not great because we have a good piece of meat.\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DALY: That`s the old thinking.\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: It is. And I think, you know, the menu at Penny Bridge is really inspired by classics and comfort food, and things are important to my childhood. And some things are like best in class what they are, and then some are a variation. And this was like my father made meatloaf when I was young after he had a heart attack, and he put spinach in there to make it healthy. But then covered in bacon and sauce --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, I love that. You make your own sauce, no guilt.\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 DALY: So we`re going to do the --\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: Filet mignon, season heavy. The two things I think a lot of home cooks don`t do is they don`t season well. And then, when they put in, they start moving around.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Right.\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: Just let it sit, let it develop that crust.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Look, I`m a natural.\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: And so, we had these cooking for a little, we made the sauce. And then, what you want to do is --\par \par \uc2 DALY: Are you cooking this whole meat in the skillet right here?\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: Yes. But we`re going to put it in a broiler so basically --\par \par \uc2 DALY: Just to finish it off because it`s so thick?\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: We want to glaze this up. We want to create a crust of the sauce. So that`s like the component here. There`s little bit of bitterness, sweet sour crunch that you`re going to get from the sauce.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I made the sauce, chef.\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 [08:55:00]\par \par \uc2 DALY: So this is going to searing, saucing process to get the crust. And you bake it off for how long?\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: Then you put it in the boiler, I would say probably eight minutes.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Or you just check it and then you hit the number, five degrees short, it`ll rise a little bit.\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: I go to about 125 to 130. That will take me to my mid rare medium.\par \par \uc2 DALY: After it sits for 10 minutes.\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: Exactly. Let it rest. Then we have spinach. We`re using some lard on, some bacon that we chopped up, render that out. And then we`re basically just going to throw all the spinach in. We want to start wilting that down.\par \par \uc2 DALY: How was it, guys?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yummy.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: So good? Turn these over or anything?\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 DALY: You taste the sauce?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Oh yes. And, Carson, I`m like, you know, I`m a purist. No sauce. This sauce is incredible.\par \par \uc2 DALY: It makes sense. It helps the crust too.\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: So then, we`ve wilted the spinach down. And then one of the things that I like to do is add a lot of herbs. So I`m adding mint and dill too in the spinach. And so, this is going to become -- the herbs are really part of our vegetable.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You got a fork over there?\par \par \uc2 DALY: No one cares about the vegetable.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: The spinach is good.\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: He likes the spinach.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Yes, that`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 KLUGER: And then, we finish it with some chips to give some texture and a little more flavor.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, I just need a fork.\par \par \uc2 DALY: What`s your cut, Tom? I have the rib-eye.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Rib-eye.\par \par \uc2 DALY: New York strip.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Bone and strip.\par \par \uc2 DALY: I don`t do the filet enough, though. These are my habit and the --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Best of both worlds.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s so good.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Chef, thank you so much. Today.com/food get the recipe.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Could you just read it because I`m chewing.\par \par \uc2 DALY: You got it. We`re back (inaudible) Today but first your local news.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTA DELCAMP, NBC10 BOSTON ANCHOR: Good morning, everybody. It`s four minutes before the hour. Take a look at some of the damage here from this nor`easter blowing its way through the area. You could see this plane apparently got blown off the runway in New Bedford. There are not reports of any injuries at this point. Certainly we`ll keep you posted. We don`t know if anyone is actually inside at the time but what a situation out there. We have 480,000 people still in the dark out there. Let`s get right over to Matt Noyes with the latest on your forecast right now.\par \par \uc2 NOYES: Yes. Thanks very much, Christa. So, you know, I mean, it makes sense. We hit winds at 97 miles an hour, Provincetown. Edgartown hitting 94, Scituate at about 87 miles an hour and, you know, the wind was ferocious and cape in as well. Gust now, not even being reported in Southeast Mass because all the airports are without power, the farther north and west you go, that`s where you had winds that weren`t as strong, the damage not nearly as bad.\par \par \uc2 The gusts by 12:15 are going to be still running 50 or better in the Cape and Southeast Mass but consider this. We`ve already had those really epic gusts so that means a lot of the damage has been done. Still, there may be some scattered new damage here later on in the day. OK, let`s get it over to Olessa.\par \par \uc2 OLESSA STEPANOVA, NBC10 BOSTON TRAFFIC REPORTER: Yes, Matt, still very busy out there, still watching that closure because of flooding on Memorial Drive. That is closed near the Longfellow Bridge as they come crash is gone inside the O`Neill Tunnel. So things are starting to ease out still not great here in Somerville. And then, I`m still watching that down utility pole on the expressway blocking the rin to Granite Ave, as well as some fallen trees on Route 3 here by Derby Street.\par \par \uc2 [09:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza, this is the "3rd Hour of Today."\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC`S TODAY WEATHER ANCHOR: Good morning and welcome to the "3rd Hour of Today" for a Wednesday, October 27th. If Craig was here, he would say, Hump Day.\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES, NBC NEWS 3RD HOUR OF TODAY, CO-HOST: Hump Day. I can`t even do it.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JONES: And look who is here.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: We got Jill Martin.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yay.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Our super sister here.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes. We name people who come. You`re a super sister to the show.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JILL MARTIN, LIFESTYLE AND COMMERCE CONTRIBUTOR: I just wants to say I have allergies, I`m not sick. And I feel like now, everybody, if something happens and they`re like, oh, are you OK? I mean, don`t you feel like that all the time now?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Especially adult-onset allergies.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JONES: But it`s a thing this season.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Allergies. Just saying.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That`s right. And all this week, we`ve been bringing you a refresh in adulting. And today, is all about home buying. Stephanie Ruhle is going to tell us what to do before we start the process, some common mistakes to avoid as well. And the biggest hurdle for a lot of folks. You`re going to want to hear what she says about the 20 percent down payment.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s a big deal.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s a big chunk of money.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. And then, Jill, you have a great "She Made It." It`s going to, I think, resonate with a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Al Roker is really going to be interested in this one.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You`re going to love it.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes. And do you remember what it was like when you got your ears pierced, Al?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: No.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Well, for a lot of women, it`s not exactly --\par \par \uc2 JONES: We were in a mall.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Right. It`s not exactly a fun experience. But there is one entrepreneur who is trying to change that, not only celebrating it, but also making sure it`s safe. We`ll show you how she`s doing it. It`s an incredible story.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s a great idea. I`m going to do it, I saw this story, to make it a moment. Like for me, I had to earn getting my ears pierced. I was 12. I had to get all A`s first.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes. I did as well. And then you make it a beautiful, it`s something that`s symbolic and you`re family -- what?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Oh, I`m sorry. Were you talking?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Who knows. Maybe Nick will come home with an ear pierced and you`ll say, oh. Anyway, all right. And this is becoming a trend. Every time Jill is here, she brings is something to eat. Oh, wait, I saw you guys and didn`t even notice it.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: OK. So, I know how much al loves pumpkins. But this is from Bagel Nook, which, you know, I love a small business.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: This is from a small business. Please start eating. This is from a small business --\par \par \uc2 JONES: Wait. Is this a bagel?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: It is a bagel. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh, look at this.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Look at that. That`s very creative.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: And now, it`s not pumpkin taste, that one. But I would like you to just try a few of these. It`s a French toast bagel, an apple cider bagel, a tie dye bagel, but this is a regular bagel with a green licorice stem --\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s really cute.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: -- and with regular cream cheese.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK. Is this a local place?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: So -- then it`s not a pumpkin taste. It`s in Freehold, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: The father/son came up with this idea. It`s David and Alex Berkowitz. And they try to outdo each other. They have Oreo stuffed bagels. And I thought this would be fun for fall. Look at these tie dye