 at the pediatrician or wherever they get these shots to actually start getting them?\par \par \uc2 DR. PATEL: Yes, Al, we could see shots in arms as early as late next week because the CDC`s advisory committee meets Tuesday, Wednesday, sends that recommendation, as you mentioned, to the CDC director. And \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  is ready and waiting with different colored vials and smaller vials that are ready for pediatric sizes out to roll out to practices like mine and ones near you, as well.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Can I tell you a hot topic in my parent`s circles? I can show you the text messages as we speak. It`s extremely rare, but a lot of us, and I`m included here, are worried about myocarditis. It`s still a concern for parents out there. It even came up, in fact, during yesterday`s advisory meeting. Can you talk to us very quickly about that? What should people be watching out for, and does this type of inflammation affect this age range as much as it does for kids who are a bit older?\par \par \uc2 DR. PATEL: Yes. So, Sheinelle, a couple of points of good news. In general, even before COVID, this five-to-11-age group, you just do not see cases of myocarditis in the same degree that you would see in older children. Now, of course, that`s not cold comfort to parents who are worried about it. But it is reassuring that we generally do not see this in the five-to-11-age group.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:00]\par \par \uc2 The CDC and the \uc2 FDA\uc2  have also collected data. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  went a step further and modelled data to look at kind of "worst-case scenarios." And about one in 10,000 children who receive the vaccine, in a worst-case scenario, might have this inflammation of the heart. But here is the good news.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 DR. PATEL: the CDC`s data so far supports that all of these cases -- very rare, all of these cases in real world evidence have actually resolved completely without hospitalization or anything that`s really severe.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK. So, the kid doesn`t have any problems after that?\par \par \uc2 DR. PATEL: They can have symptoms. They can last several months. We still recommend a cardiologist to look at them and restricting their activity because we want to make sure they`re as safe as possible. But key points, very rare. Number two, all pediatricians and physicians are heightened for their awareness after the shot. That`s generally after the second dose. But, of course, we`re going on be looking for anything concerning.\par \par \uc2 And here is where it kicks in. A mother`s and father`s instinct reigns supreme. If something seems off with your kid, come in so we that can make that evaluation and get them to a specialist if we need to quickly.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: OK. So, you just said second dose. Is one dose enough for some children? And also, another main take away, where can parents go, any location to get the shot?\par \par \uc2 DR. PATEL: Yes, great question, Jill. So, number one, it`s two doses that have been recommended by the FDA`s advisory committee separated by three weeks of the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine. But you`re highlighting something that I think is a good conversation. About 42 percent of children age five to 11 have had the coronavirus according to the CDC. That`s a lot of kids.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DR. PATEL: So, many parents, especially if you know your child has been infected, could have a conversation that says, would one shot be enough? Now, I would still recommend two, but I always recommend open conversations asking the question and, of course, tailoring it to your children`s individual needs. That`s the key here. Making sure that you have your child`s best needs in mind. Overall, my kids are going to get vaccinated. I think the benefits outweigh the risks and I felt very confident looking at a data that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  reviewed.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: All right. That`s great news. Really reassuring. Dr. Kavita Patel, thanks so much and thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 DR. PATEL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s helpful information this morning.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Turning now to the fatal shooting on the set of Alec Baldwin`s new movie, "Rust." Today, we expect to learn much more about what happened when investigators hold their first news conference in a few hours. And the district attorney charge of the case told "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " that criminal charges have not been ruled out and that "everything is on the table."\par \par \uc2 ROKER: In the meantime, this photo posted by a crew member to social media shows what the scene was like moments before the shooting. Cinematographer Halyna Hutchins is seen standing in front of the Baldwin next to the camera. Hutchins dying when police say Baldwin pulled the trigger of a prop gun that he was told was supposed to be safe.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Just heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: It`s so heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: And another story we`re watching closely, Queen Elizabeth`s health. We saw if 95-year-old monarch at a virtual engagement yesterday. But then hours later, Buckingham Palace announced the queen will not attend a climate even next week in Scotland. Other members of the royal family will be there. In a statement, the queen says, she`s disappointed she won`t get to meet with world leaders. Last week, as we know, she canceled another trip to Northern Ireland and spent a night in the hospital after doctors told her she should rest.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Again, she`s 95.\par \par \uc2 JONES: She`s 95.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: I know.\par \par \uc2 JONES: She just has to --\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: She doesn`t want to rest.\par \par \uc2 JONES: She doesn`t want to rest. She said so in her schedule, as a lot of us should.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Quite frankly. All right. Meantime, a member of the U.S. soccer royalty in the news this morning. Carli Lloyd is hanging up her cleats after a historic career. She played her final match last night for team USA in St. Paul, Minnesota. She`s been on two World Cup winning teams. She has two gold medals.\par \par \uc2 But this is what grabbed our attention. This special moment. She was subbed out of the game and she took a moment to acknowledge her biggest supporter. The name on the back of her jersey there, it says Hollins, that`s her husband, Brian Hollins. As you can imagine, for a spouse of somebody so high-profile, he`s been her biggest cheerleader, sometimes often behind the scenes. So, she took that jersey off with his name on the back. She says, you know what? It`s a tribute to the next chapter in their lives together.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: That`s really beautiful.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That`s really nice.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Isn`t that great?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: So sweet.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s a good one. That resonates with a lot of us\par \par \uc2 All right. Up next, we`re going to show you how to adult as an adult.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Get some bagels.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Get some. This cinnamon French toast bagel situation is a game changer.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Here`s how we adult.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. We`re going to talk about buying a home from down payments to common mistakes. Our friend, Stephanie Ruhle, is helping open the door for any of you in search of your dream home.\par \par \uc2 And then later, taking some of the pain out of a rite of passage. Jill will introduce us to the woman who is trying to change the ear-piercing experience in our next installment of "She Made it." We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020211028ehar000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Rising Above Breast Cancer\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kristen Dahlgren \par \uc2 489 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:25:00] LESTER HOLT: Finally, tonight, this is breast cancer awareness month. One in five women diagnosed will be under age 49, like our own Kristen Dahlgren, and tonight shares a personal story and the adventure she joined to inspire others.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN DAHLGREN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Sometimes, surviving breast cancer could feel like just the first step, which is what led me here to the wilds of Utah after two years of dealing with breast cancer and aftermath.\par \par \uc2 KRISTI MANGAN, BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR: A lot of us are told we`re done now. You should go back to your normal life, and we`ve been forever altered.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Gillian Lichota founded I Rise Above Breast Cancer after she was diagnosed at 35.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN LICHOTA, FOUNDER, I RISE ABOVE BREAST CANCER: The day that I found out that I was pregnant, I also found out that I had breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: She couldn`t find any resources for younger women dealing with cancer.\par \par \uc2 LICHOTA: You know, create the world that you want to see, so I did.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: I rise above focuses on fitness, mindfulness, camaraderie, and adventure.\par \par \uc2 LICHOTA: And so, I mentioned I`m petrified of heights.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Pushing them to do things they can`t.\par \par \uc2 It is really hard.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH HUTCHISON, BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR: There`s no boundaries to what you can or cannot do.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Laurie finished chemo three days after this climb.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I was never doing this before.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Really?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No. So, it`s crazy that it takes something like this to, you know, push yourself even further.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Of course, along with the summits, there are valleys. Gillian`s cancer returned, stage four metastatic.\par \par \uc2 I`m sorry. I`m so petrified of it coming back. And yet, I see you living this amazing life and doing so much. How do you do it?\par \par \uc2 LICHOTA: I just don`t want cancer to take anything more of me.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Today, she has no evidence of disease and for first the term breast cancer thriver inspiring others to challenge expectation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, God, I did it.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: And rise above.\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER SURVIVORS: I rise!\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Kristen Dahlgren, NBC News Oroville, Utah.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And we`re grateful to Kristen for sharing her story. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Wednesday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020211028ehar0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 An Exclusive Look At What`s Being Done To Improve The Safety Of Military Vehicles\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Savannah Guthrie, Tom Llamas, Vicky Nguyen \par \uc2 3,742 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:40:22] HODA KOTB: Back now, 7:40 with In-Depth Today. This morning rare and exclusive access to a crash test involving military Humvee.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: Yes, this is a surprising one. Some say the vehicles lack safety features that could prevent injuries and even deaths.\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS: Yes. Our good friend NBC investigative and consumer correspondent Vicky Nguyen is here now with more on that. Vicky, good morning.\par \par \uc2 VICKY NGUYEN: Hey, good morning to all of you. You are about to see what happens inside a military Humvee when it rolls over. A government report released earlier this year found Humvees, the most common military vehicle, are involved in more Army and Marine Corps accidents than any other tactical vehicle. What`s more, many of these crashes happen in noncombat situations. This morning, see why advocates are calling for changes to help save the lives of service members. A warning, some of this footage may be difficult to watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): The impact is jarring. You`re watching a crash simulation involving a military Humvee, 10,000 pounds of metal traveling at just 25 miles an hour, rolling over twice after hitting a ramp, a sobering example of what can happen in combat and during routine training.\par \par \uc2 ABBY KUBIE, WIDOW OF Staff SGT. SERGEANT WESLEY KUBIE: We knew it was bad but nobody thought that it wasn`t going to make it.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): Abby Kubie is the widow of Staff Sergeant Wesley Kubie. In June her husband was on a training mission at the Smoky Hill Air National Guard range. He died after the Humvee he was riding in rolled over. Abby is now raising their three children,\par \par \uc2 KUBIE: I didn`t actually get to be with him for a second time.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): The investigation into what caused the accident is still ongoing, but Kubie`s death is not isolated. According to the \uc2 Government Accountability Office\uc2 , there were more than 3,700 tactical vehicle accidents from 2010 to 2019 in noncombat situations, almost a third involved Humvees, at least 123 service members were killed contributing to what Congress calls a trend of non-war related deaths in the military.\par \par \uc2 The report also found that nearly 25 percent of the accidents involved a rollover. Military experts say the armor added to Humvees makes them prone to rollovers. Two years ago, the army contracted advanced safety supplier, IMMI, to research and test safety solutions for the Humvee. NBC News was invited to IMMI`s crash test demonstration for look at how additional safety measures could help service members survive.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): I`m here in Indiana and joining me now is Nick Awabdy. He`s the lead engineer here at IMMI. Nick, we`re standing in front of this Humvee. It`s specially equipped, what do you expect to see today?\par \par \uc2 NICK AWABDY, LEAD ENGINEER, IMMI: So we`re going to induce a rollover which simulates a real world scenario and we`ll see how our technology will help a service member and a crash over conventional three point seatbelt.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): Engineers install sensors and rig the Humvee with 30 cameras to capture every angle inside and outside the vehicle. Inside the Humvee, both test dummies are belted. The passenger in the back is wearing a standard seat belt and the driver is wearing IMMI`s embrace technology, a four point seatbelt system that deploys an airbag and pulls the driver down to create space between their head and the ceiling.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Watching vehicle in three, two, one.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): Traveling at 25 miles an hour.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): OK, here it comes.\par \par \uc2 (voice-over): The Humvee it`s a ramp designed to simulate what could happen in a real life rollover. The Humvee flips twice almost jumping the barricade. And on the inside chaos, watch as this rider wearing just a standard seat belt is thrown from side to side. In slow motion, you can see the riders had hit the ceiling with every roll. But not the driver, the airbag and the four point seatbelt protect the drivers head and neck.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Nick that was incredible to see in person. We saw what happened on the outside. What happened on the inside?\par \par \uc2 AWABDY: Yes, so everything worked just as planned.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: OK.\par \par \uc2 AWABDY: And then the entire seat was pulled to the floor, opening up survivable space for the service member, for the service member.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: And that protects their head.\par \par \uc2 AWABDY: And that protects the head and neck from coming in contact with the roof.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): In addition to IMMI`s technology, the Army is exploring other safety solutions, including better driver training and the use of simulators to build driver experience. But for families who`ve lost service members, the changes are long overdue.\par \par \uc2 KUBIE: They never really realized that was a problem. But now that I`m, I guess you could say kind of living a nightmare that is the problem. And it is frustrating.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: So sad for that family. That demonstration was just troubling just watching that. So this is new to us, but it`s not necessarily new to the military.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: No, it`s not, Tom. This has been a known problem since at least 2014 when a military report found blunt force trauma in Humvee crashes to be especially fatal. Now some have been equipped with antilock brakes and stability control system by Ricardo defense.\par \par \uc2 [07:45:10]\par \par \uc2 But when it comes to the technology that you`re looking at right there, IMMI says installing that embrace system will cost between 4 to $5,000 per vehicle Humvee maker \uc2 AM General\uc2  they were also at that demonstration they say they do support IMMI`s research to make their Humvees safer.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right, Vicky, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, it is 7:45. Let`s check in with Mr. Roker again a busy day --\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: That`s right, we`re going to be looking at some severe weather later today. Part of that weather Bomb we were talking about out west, this weekend it`s the strongest storm ever that was recorded to have hit the West Coast, this Bomb cyclone, the pressure drop, 46 millibars in 24 hours, that`s almost double what it would normally take to make for a Bomb cyclone.\par \par \uc2 Now we`re going to be seeing more of these because of climate change. Warmer waters make these Bomb cyclones more common, larger clash between warm water and colder air masses as they move on shore. So we`re going to be watching that. Now, the good news is the heavy rain has ended on the West Coast snow through the Rockies but today later today into the overnight period, that same system, this is going to cause severe weather for 12 million people, wind gusts, very large hail two inches in diameter, and several tornadoes possible.\par \par \uc2 We move on into Wednesday and look at this, 13 million people along the Gulf Coast up for severe weather from Bay City all the way to Mobile Alabama for strong wind gusts, few tornadoes and isolated activity going on. We`re looking at heavy rain from the gulf coast all the way into the mid plains with anywhere from one to three inches of rain as the system moves to the east. That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 MATT NOYES, NBC10 BOSTON METEOROLOGIST: You know I`ve been showing you a live radar to give you the updates. We`ve been in and out of the downpours and there`ll still be a few of them from time to time. But in general, it`s not a lot of action going on with the rain here as we get through the midday. It`s once we get to the middle and late afternoon that we get increasing rain and wind that it`s pouring, it`s gusty, the damaging wind sets in about 8 o`clock this evening, last few until tomorrow morning around this time. It only slowly winds down over the course of the day tomorrow. Thursday is better and another storm Saturday I`m still hopeful we`ll be gone by trick or treating Sunday evening.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And that is your latest whether, guys?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Al, thank you. Still to come this morning, potential hope on the horizon when it comes to the fight against breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, this morning, a first look at the experimental vaccine being tested to tackle the most aggressive and lethal form of the disease. But first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:51:50]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We`re back. We say good morning to Carson.