ast, all weather problems of their own.\par \par \uc2 Al`s got the very latest on all of this.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Al, busy morning.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS WEATHER ANCHOR: Yes, it is, guys. We have a flood watch right now, flood warnings. Thirty-nine million people in the Northeast and New England. The rain now really getting itself together as this low pressure system develops. Rain and flooding widespread stream flooding. Flooding could be made worst by those storm drains clogged because of the downed leaves.\par \par \uc2 We`re talking anywhere from two to three inches of rain. But locally, we could see upwards of 5 inches of rain, as well. The winds will be a big problem, too. Knocking down power lines and trees, strongest winds will be along the New England Coast, and those power outages stretching from Augusta, Maine, all the way down to Point Pleasant, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Later today been on into the overnight, that storm system, that big bomb that came into the Northwest and the California coast, well, it is now going to be in the Mid-Plains. Twelve million people at risk for severe weather, wind gusts and large hail and tornados possible. And then by Wednesday, that moves into Houston, Texas, all the way to Mobile, Alabama, for 13 million people, wind gusts of 60 miles per hour or higher. Few tornados and isolated hail, as well.\par \par \uc2 So we`re going to be very busy over the next 72 hours -- guys.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. Al, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And we could be on the verge of a break through in the battle against COVID-19. Experts at the \uc2 FDA\uc2  are about to decide if they will recommend the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine for young children.\par \par \uc2 [08:05:03]\par \par \uc2 And Moderna is about to seek approval, as well.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Sam Brock has a closer look at the meetings today.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Sam. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Savannah. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Here, we`re at Children`s Hospital in New Orleans which has been conducting trials for kids with a Moderna vaccine. Nearby Ochsner Health has been conducting the panels with \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , as you mentioned. That is what is up before an advisory panel later today, and the early results so far, Savannah, have been excellent for that two-dose vaccine. About a third of what adults were getting is almost 91 percent effective, according to the data, in preventing symptomatic COVID infection for kids 5 to 11 years old.\par \par \uc2 Savannah, the reason this is so critical right now, just last week alone, there were 118,000 kids across the country that had the COVID-19 infection. That is down from a peak of about 250,000 in September. So that`s been cut. Great news there, but still a staggering number.\par \par \uc2 And it comes at a time when the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2  most recent study shows roughly 32 percent of parents said they would wait and see, 24 percent said definitely not. Physicians unequivocally say getting your child vaccinated is the best way to prevent a surge. It is entirely possible that the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine could be available for kids by the first week of November.\par \par \uc2 That`s the very latest, Savannah, from New Orleans. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: That`s next week, Sam. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right. Following the successful launch of its space tourism business, Jeff Bezos`s Blue Origin company is teaming up with \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and others to build a space station. They plan to call it Orbital Reef.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it`s still on the drawing board, but the company says it could be in orbit by the end of a decade. It would resemble the International Space Station, which is now 20 years old. Blue Origin says its own space outpost would generate new products and discoveries and even new forms of entertainment.\par \par \uc2 Just think, guys, our kids may grow up to go on vacation in space, hit that space station and hit the all you can eat buffet right there up in cosmos.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: What happens in the space station stays at the space station.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: There you go, that too.\par \par \uc2 GUTHIRIE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get our boost.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I got you covered. Here we go, a man who has been married to his wife for 18 years also helped raise her three children who are now young adults. So one day he decided to surprise them all with special presents for them to open. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO CLIP PLAYS)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And all the paperwork right there to start that adoption process. Of course, the kids teared up. Big hugs to dad.\par \par \uc2 Sweet moment.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Look like dad was crying, too.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Coming up next, a TODAY exclusive. Exciting news in the fight against breast cancer, an experimental vaccine a decade in the making now being tested. We`re going to have exclusive details right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020211027ehaq0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 The Today Show for October 26, 2021, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Hoda Kotb, Tom Llamas, Al Roker, Carson Daly,  Matt Noyes, Olessa Stepanova, Sam Brock, Anne Thompson, Chuck Bell, Jummy  Olabanji \par \uc2 8,633 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Conten