re I go. I love that you kept one of your favorite books that a teacher gave you. I think from when you were like even in kindergarten or first grade. Why? Why did you keep that? And what book was it?\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: So, it was my kindergarten teacher Mrs. Enterante who is watching, probably is. We`re still close. It was the book "Green Eggs and Ham."\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh.\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: "Green Eggs and Ham." And we read it in school. And you know the story is about trying new things, things that you`ve never done before. And I remember she came -- like the next day after we finished reading that book at snack time, we had for lunch green eggs and ham.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Oh. I love her.\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: And nobody wanted to try them at first because they were green.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 And she keeps saying, try them. Try them. And then we took a bite and we tried it and it`s like they taste just like green eggs and ham.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Oh, I love --\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: And it just stayed with me to try new things.\par \par \uc2 [10:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I love that teacher.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: By the way, I love that you`re still close with your teacher, I love that.\par \par \uc2 All right. Well, since this is Netflix`s first "Book Club," we thought we`d play a little game. It`s called "First for Everything." So, we`re going to ask you questions and you`re going to tell us your first.\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: OK.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 What was your very first job?\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: Waiting tables at Friendly`s restaurant.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, I remember Friendly`s.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I loved Friendly`s.\par \par \uc2 OK.\par \par \uc2 Who was your first kiss?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh.\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: My friend, Barron.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Barron.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: He`s still your friend?\par \par \uc2 He`s still your friend?\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Is he there?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: No. He`s not.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right.\par \par \uc2 First album you ever bought?\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: First album I ever bought. Well, I don`t know the first album I ever bought but the first CD I ever had that was mine was Boyz II Men.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 "End of the Road?"\par \par \uc2 Was it "End of the Road?"\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: No, it was "Water Runs Dry." You know that?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh.\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: The blue one with the Roman numeral II.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, cool.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: OK.\par \par \uc2 Who is the first person you call when you just want to laugh?\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: My sister Chichi.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I love that.\par \par \uc2 Who was your first celebrity crush?\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: Jonathan Taylor Thomas.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 I think we have the same age. How old are you? We have all the same things.\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: 40. I just turned 40 this year.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I`m turning 40 in November.\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: I know.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes. I`m like, hold on. Did you read "The Baby-Sitters Club," too? Talking about books. Right?\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: Of course. Yes, yes. Absolutely. "Sunset Island."\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 OK.\par \par \uc2 What was the first thought that popped into your head this morning?\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: What am I going to wear for Jenna and Hoda?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh. Well, we adore you. Good luck with your show, and good luck -- good luck with the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  show and your acting, and when -- when you`re back in New York, and I know here you`re now, come by and see us in studio.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We want to see you here. OK?\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: Would love to.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And we can`t wait to see "Clyde`s." We`re going to come watch you on Broadway.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh my gosh. You`re going to be amazing.\par \par \uc2 All right. If you want --\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: Please, yes. Would love to have you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Cool.\par \par \uc2 If you want to be part of Netflix`s "Book Club," read "Passing" by Nella Larsen. You can check it out TODAY.com/shop and watch the movie adaptation on \uc2 Netflix\uc2 . November 10th.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Brilliant.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And you can Uzo in "Clyde`s" on Broadway right here in New York City. Previews begin November the 3rd.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Uzo.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Thank you, Uzo.\par \par \uc2 ADUBA: Thank you, ladies.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Coming up next, Hoda sits down with Grammy winner Sheryl Crow.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yeah. She opens up about her path to motherhood and how her decided to become a mom impacted one of her past relationships. Coming up after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:30:40]\par \par \uc2 HAGER: It`s Monday which means a new episode of Hoda`s podcast "Making Space" drops today. And we`ve been so looking forward to this one. Got to sit down with Sheryl Crow.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, my gosh, Jenna. I had such a great time talking to her. We all know Sheryl Crow is the singer, but on a personal level Sheryl is pretty private. But she did open up to us about a lot of things, especially about becoming a mom in her 40s and what her own mother said to her that changed her life.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYS "ALL I WANNA DO" BY SHERYL CROW MUSIC VIDEO)\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voice-over): Sheryl Crow has built her career at the intersection of rock, pop and country with nine Grammys too boot, but this recording icon is so much more than music and lyrics.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW, SINGER: If I never got to sing another song, I would still feel the joy that I feel right now, and that`s a good place to be.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voice-over): But like in most of our lives the path to happiness hasn`t always been a straight line.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYS "EVERY DAY IS A WINDING ROAD" BY SHERYL CROW MUSIC VIDEO)\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): I feel like our lives have these weird parallel paths and the more I learn about you, the more I feel it.\par \par \uc2 CROW: Oh, I love that.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): You were here at the "TODAY" Show downstairs in the dressing room with your boys when they were little.\par \par \uc2 CROW: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): And I went down there. And I remember thinking watching you with your kids, I want something like that one day.\par \par \uc2 CROW: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And I`m telling you. You don`t get the wrong kids. It just doesn`t happen that way. And my kids so clearly not only picked me but picked each other and, man, what a cool honor.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): An honor Sheryl and I were gifted with later in life, both by way of adoption. I never spoke out loud the fact that I wanted children because I thought I had missed my window. One day, I was walking with a girlfriend down the street and she said, well, me and you, we didn`t want to have kids you know. So, it worked out great. And I said, well, I actually did. I just remember saying it out loud and how weird that was to speak it and then to say it again. And it kind of made it real.\par \par \uc2 CROW: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Did you always want kids?\par \par \uc2 CROW: I just never didn`t think I would have kids. I was thinking of it in the context of family. It wasn`t like I saw myself being pregnant, having a baby inside of me. It wasn`t so much that. It was much more about the wonder of getting to raise a person.\par \par \uc2 I remember my last relationship crumbling largely because that is what I wanted. I didn`t want to be somebody`s girlfriend and raise their kids.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 CROW: I didn`t want to be a stepmom and not be made a wife and a mom, you know. It`s sort of like being in a relationship with someone but being also the babysitter but not getting to be the real parent. And yeah, that did crumble that relationship and also had breast cancer, I went through that. And it was my mom who said why don`t you just get a surrogate and get some sperm, my head is like exploding like what did she just say? Like just have my own babies, what? And that`s kind of what gave me the - you know, the life raft to not limit myself to that oh, you`ve got to be married. You`ve got to be stable. Then you have kids. My story didn`t lay out like that.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): You know what`s interesting. You just -- you said something in the middle of that story that struck me, too, because I was diagnosed with breast cancer at the same time my relationship was falling apart. It was kind of strange because I felt like I had two pains going on at the same time, and I couldn`t go down the rabbit hole on one. Like I was so angry at him. And then I had to go in for my test and they were like you need a mastectomy. And I was so mad at the doctors. And why me. And I ate apples. And ran in Central Park. And what are you talking about? I was so mad. And I kind of liken it to like having two kids and one kid is coloring on the wall and the other kid is spilling flour all over the floor. And I wondered in a weird way. Did that help me in some strange way? I couldn`t - - I couldn`t get so depressed on one thing because I had two things to worry about.\par \par \uc2 CROW: Yes. I - you know, there were a lot of facets to it, but it was a relationship that -- that I seemed to keep going back to. And I think when I got diagnosed with breast cancer that was like oh, wait a minute. I think we have to really look at this and you have to put yourself first.\par \par \uc2 [10:35:00]\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Did you ever -- I never, and this is probably not healthy, this is me. I never really had a follow-up conversation after it was over. That was the end. And that`s how I played it.\par \par \uc2 CROW: Man, I`ll tell you what. I`m blessed to have a wise mom. She said no matter what you think you`re going to get from that conversation, you`re never going to get what you think. And I was not ever going to get the kind of answer I thought I was going to get.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Yeah.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voice-over): These days, Sheryl, who by the way is just shy of 60, is back on the road. She`s enjoying her life just the way it is. But making space for the possibility of more. Do you have room for another love in your life, do you think?\par \par \uc2 CROW: I do.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Do you already have that?\par \par \uc2 CROW: I don`t.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): OK. I wasn`t sure.\par \par \uc2 CROW: I don`t. I mean, I don`t want to say sadly, and I would love to be loved and to love, but my first loves are my boys and I`m open. I`ll just say that. I`m open.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Come on.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I love her.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: She is open. She`s open to everything, by the way. And, you know, she`s one of those people who sings with all the greats, Stevie Nicks and everybody. Everybody.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: She`s like a musician`s musician, a beautiful songwriter. And she`s got this incredible life.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I love listening to y`all`s conversation.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It`s cool.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: It was like you were just on the telephone.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It was fun.\par \par \uc2 You can hear more of our conversation on my podcast "Making Space." It`s on Apple podcast, or Spotify or wherever you get your podcast, or you can just scan the QR code and they`ll take you where you need to go.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: That`s so awesome. We`re going "Unscripted," after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:40:00]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It`s time for "Unscripted."\par \par \uc2 So, there was a little picture that kind of caught our atte