n`t think you need it.\par \par \uc2 Do you guys have a wedding date yet?\par \par \uc2 TEN: Not yet.\par \par \uc2 VAN TASS: Not yet.\par \par \uc2 TEN: We -\par \par \uc2 VAN TASS: First, Vegas, then wedding.\par \par \uc2 TEN: So, now -\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Vegas then wedding.\par \par \uc2 TEN: We`ll be in Vegas for one year performing with "America`s Got Talent." Then maybe, you`ll never know what happens in Vegas, stays on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Congratulations, guys.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Oh, my gosh, y`all are so talented.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Get tickets to see The Clairvoyants in person as part of "AGT`s Las Vegas Live" at the Luxor Hotel and Casino. That`s in Vegas.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And we`ll be back, after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:56:15]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Coming up tomorrow, guys, Chrissy Teigen, she will be here. And she`s got a lot to talk about.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yeah. Plus, a performance by country superstars Lady A.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And we are going to surprise someone very special, because we are going to flip their fall. It`s one of our favorites.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Our favorite thing.\par \par \uc2 And do not forgot to check out Hoda`s podcast "Making Space." Download her latest episode with Sheryl Crow and you could also check out our podcast "TODAY with Hoda & Jenna."\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And guess what, Al got one too. Just in time for the holidays for cooking. All you need to do is follow "Cooking Up A Storm with Al Roker." He got recipes. He got everything you need especially with Thanksgiving coming.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: We can`t wait to listen to that. And we`re going to see y`all tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Can`t wait. Bye!\par \par \uc2 HAGER: It will be Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220406ehap002jp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Supreme Court Taking Up Challenges To The Texas Abortion Law But Leaving It In Place\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Pete Williams \par \uc2 299 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: New tonight, the Supreme Court refusing once again to block that Texas law that bans most abortions in the state. Pete Williams is here. And Pete, the court agreed, however, to take up challenges to the law with unusual speed.\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Lester. The court agreed to take up to challenges to the law on November 1st, but it delayed acting on the Biden administration`s request to put the law on hold while these cases play out.\par \par \uc2 One of them is a challenge brought by abortion providers in Texas who say the state can`t pass a law violating the rights of access to abortion, but then hand off enforcement to private lawsuits. And then the court will decide whether the Biden Justice Department has the legal authority to file its own lawsuit. Texas says the federal government can`t sue the state this way.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  said the court should have blocked the law for now. She said women seeking abortion are entitled to immediate relief. So, as a practical matter, this means the Texas law is likely to remain in effect for at least several more months, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Pete Williams in the Supreme Court, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020211024eham0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A New Drive By The Biden Administration To Block That Controversial Law In Texas That Bans Most Abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Catie Beck \par \uc2 563 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: A new drive by the Biden Administration to block that controversial law in Texas that bans most abortions. The Justice Department urging a federal appeals court to suspend the law once again. Our Catie Beck is in Texas with more tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: In Texas tonight, a new firestorm of legal chaos over the state`s controversial abortion law.\par \par \uc2 AMY HAGSTROM MILLER\uc2 , FOUNDER, PRESIDENT AND CEO, WHOLE WOMAN`S HEALTH: There`s just a remarkable uncertainty, confusion with patients about what the requirements are of the law and whether or not abortion is still legal.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Last week, a lower court in Texas suspended the law declaring it unconstitutional. Then, 48 hours later an appeals court reinstated it.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): The bill that I`m about to sign that ensures that the life of every unborn child who has a heartbeat will be saved from the ravages of abortion.\par \par \uc2 BECK: The most restrictive abortion law in the country took effect September 1st, banning abortions after a fetal heartbeat is detected around six weeks. But it does not criminalize abortion. Instead, it allows citizens to be enforcers who can file suit against anyone who helped obtain an abortion past the deadline for up to $10,000. Anti-abortion advocates encouraged to see the law once again in effect.\par \par \uc2 KIM SCHWARTZ, MEDIA AND COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR, TEXAS RIGHT TO LIVE: The fact that it has saved about a hundred lives a day for over a month, meaning over 3000 lives saved is just an incredible victory.\par \par \uc2 BECK: But overnight, the Justice Department asking an appeals court to suspend the law again, calling it state-sanctioned sabotage of the constitution. Texas Democratic State Representative Donna Howard says the confusing climate is by design to make getting an abortion virtually impossible.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:18]\par \par \uc2 DONNA HOWARD, DEMOCRATIC TEXAS STATE REPRESENTATIVE: If you`re not paying attention right now to what`s going in Texas. You need to be. Because what`s happening here is absolutely what`s going to flow across the rest of our nation.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Texas has until Thursday to respond to the Justice Department. This case could ultimately be decided at the Supreme Court. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Catie Beck, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In just 60 seconds, details about the downfall of Las Vegas Raiders head coach Jon Gruden. And after days of chaos at \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2 , new warning signs about holiday travel.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Harrowing new images of that deadly plane crash in a suburb of San Diego. Surveillance video shows the plane going down before it struck two homes and several vehicles. Two people were killed including a UPS driver and the pilot who was a doctor from Arizona. People in the neighborhood helped to rescue one couple from their burning home.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020211013ehac0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Texas Abortion Law Temporarily Halted By A Federal Judge\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Morgan Chesky \par \uc2 551 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: And now to the legal back and forth over that controversial abortion law in the state of Texas. It was temporarily halted by a federal judge. But now it has been reinstated as the federal appeals court considers it. The twists are leaving many patients and doctors confused and concerned. NBC`s Morgan Chesky is in Dallas this morning with the details. Morgan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Savannah, good morning to you. That law once again back into effect. It means about a half dozen clinics here in Texas that had temporarily resumed services have yet again stopped. In the meantime, the Justice Department now has until tomorrow to respond to a federal appeals court to a law it`s calling unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [07:10:13]\par \par \uc2 CHESKY (voice-over): After a brief pause, this morning a highly restrictive abortion measure is once again the law of the land in Texas. A federal appeals court reinstating that measure late Friday temporarily halted just two days prior after the Justice Department sue Texas, arguing the state`s law violates the Constitution. The DOJ now has until tomorrow to respond.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA PARMA, TEXAS RIGHT TO LIFE SENIOR LEGISLATIVE ASSOCIATE: We`ll have to wait and see what the full appellate court decides. But we are optimistic that this victory signals that they may be favorable in that ruling.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY (voice-over): That law, S.B. 8, bans most abortions as early as six weeks before many women know they`re pregnant. It provides no exceptions for rape or incest, and allows citizens to sue anyone involved with providing abortion services after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 AMY HAGSTROM MILLER\uc2 , WHOLE WOMAN`S HEALTH CEO: It is meant to intimidate, to threaten, to make us afraid of providing the abortion care that people need.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY (voice-over): Since taking effect last month, clinics in neighboring states have seen an uptick in women traveling from Texas for abortion services, the law also prompting nationwide protests with margins from Austin to Washington.\par \par \uc2 And already in Florida, a bill that mirrors the Texas law has been introduced with the GOP lawmakers in other states considering similar measures, the ultimate fate of the Texas law likely lies with the nation`s highest court, which previously refused to block the law from taking effect without ruling on its constitutionality.\par \par \uc2 REP. SHEILA JACKSON LEE\uc2  (D-TX): But we are suffering in Texas and the Supreme Court must do its job now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: And later this year, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  will take on a case that will be a direct challenge the landmark decision of Roe v. Wade when it hears your arguments in December over a Mississippi law banning most abortions at 15 weeks. Savannah?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Morgan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020211013ehab0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Federal Judge Blocks That Near Total Ban On Abortions In The State Of Texas\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Kelly O`Donnell, Hoda Kotb \par \uc2 1,159 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:00:10]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning. Breaking overnight, a federal judge blocks that near total ban on abortions in the state of Texas. The ruling of victory for the Biden administration, as it challenges the controversial law. We`ll have the latest on that ruling and the potential impact in other states around the nation.\par \par \uc2 Terror in the Classroom, disturbing images from inside the Texas high school where a student opened fire. Four people hurt. The 18-year-old suspect now in custody, as police investigate this video of a classroom fight just moments before the shooting.\par \par \uc2 Turning a Corner? A dramatic decline in COVID cases especially in the hard hit south, several states down more than 70 percent. Health officials voicing hope but reminding Americans it is not time to let the guard down.\par \par \uc2 Submerged, heavy rains soaked the South, triggering a flood emergency and rescues in Alabama and Al says the rain is not over, his full forecast straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 Those stories, plus, Halloween Scare, we`ll take you inside the shortages of everything from costumes to pumpkins this year. What you need to buy now before it`s too late.\par \par \uc2 And LA Story!\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And now here`s Taylor with a chance to be a hero.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The Dodgers beat the Cardinals in dramatic fashion. Thanks to this game winning blast.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Swing and a drive, deep left field, this is winning back will come off.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The reigning champions advancing in the postseason with dreams of defending their world series crown today Thursday, October 7th, 2021.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From NBC News, this is today with Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Hi guys. Welcome to Today. I`m so happy that you are joining us on this Thursday morning. A really, really busy morning, including some positive signs in the fight against COVID.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Doesn`t that feel good to say.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It sure does.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Take a look at this. You can see the decline in cases since the summer surge and it`s leading many to say the U.S., yes, is finally turning a corner. We`re going to talk about that and more with Dr. Ashish Jha, who`s here with us live in studio today.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And a little later has the mystery behind the cause of that devastating oil spill in California now been solved. We will have the very latest. Also, the first images released of that ruptured pipeline as Miguel Almaguer joins us live from the waters off the Southern California coast.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: But we begin with that breaking news overnight, a federal judge blocking that controversial law in Texas that bans most abortions.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It`s a temporary measure in the battle that`s reverberating from coast to coast. So where does it go from here? NBC`s Kelly O`Donnell is at the White House for us. Hey, Kelly, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Hoda. The Biden administration breathing a sigh of relief, its legal action against the Texas abortion law has won this temporary relief that holds enforcement of the state`s sweeping abortion restrictions. The White House call this an important first step to protect what it argues are women`s constitutional rights. The White House also noting that the fight has only begun.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL (voice-over): This morning, the toughest new abortion restrictions in the nation now on hold, a consequential but temporary development in the deeply personal and very public battle over abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge in Texas ruling late Wednesday, the state`s near total ban on abortions is unconstitutional after the Biden administration sued to stop the new law. The judge called the Texas restrictions and offensive deprivation of such an important right. The Texas law which has been in effect since September 1st bans abortions once a fetal heartbeat is detected as early as the sixth week of pregnancy before many women even know they`re expecting. The measure notably makes no exceptions for rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 During arguments between the Justice Department and the Texas Attorney General`s Office, the judge pressed Texas lawyers on the laws very unusual provisions, which allow private citizens to sue abortion providers and even those who assist women getting to clinics or paying for procedures.\par \par \uc2 In a 113 page ruling, the federal judge expressed concern that other states could enact similar legislation. The Texas law became a catalyst in the larger debate with anti-abortion advocates energized, pledging to use the Texas example in other states while those supporting abortion rights demand a federal response to protect women`s rights.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:07]\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, women marched in cities nationwide to urge the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  to protect existing abortion rights. The \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  list and anti-abortion group responded to Wednesday`s ruling saying, now an unelected judge has interfered with the clearly expressed will of Texans.\par \par \uc2 In Washington, the Supreme Court has already refused to take up the Texas law. But more challenges are expected. And this term, the High Court will look at another law on restrictive abortions in Mississippi, all set against the question of whether the decades old landmark case of Roe v. Wade will stand.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK. So Kelly with this overnight ruling in Texas, what -- how does that affect abortion providers there now?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, there are real practical questions. One of the state`s largest providers has already said clinics are making plans to resume abortion services for patients up to eight weeks -- 18 weeks into their pregnancy. Now that comes as neighboring states around Texas report seeing an uptick in patients crossing state lines as a result of that law that banned abortions at six weeks.\par \par \uc2 But other providers are reluctant and they`re reluctant to resume their services because they know that the law was written in such a way that they could be retro actively sued for any abortions performed while this law is blocked. And of course we expect additional legal challenges to this new ruling. Hoda?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Kelly O`Donnell for us at the White House. Kelly, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220406eha7002gx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Raising Awareness Of A Lesser-Known Breast Cancer Risk\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Anne Thompson \par \uc2 429 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:25:00] LESTER HOLT: Finally, this is Breast Cancer Awareness Month. And tonight, one woman`s mission to make people aware of a lesser-known variant that can be deadly. Anne Thompson on how she`s inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CHIEF ENVIRONMENTAL AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: Knowledge is power. That`s freelance journalist Susan Berger`s mantra even more so since Berger learned she and two of her three daughters have a little-known genetic mutation that increases their risk for breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN BERGER, FREELANCE JOURNALIST: The more I did my research, I realized that it`s a gift.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: So, are you on a one-woman crusade to tell other breast cancer survivors that you should be tested for PALB2?\par \par \uc2 BERGER: I am.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: PALB2 is often called the third breast cancer gene. The less than one percent of breast cancer patients have the mutation. Its impact can be devastating, increasing the risk for breast cancer by 58 percent with a family history, 33 percent with no history, and upping the risk for ovarian and pancreatic cancers.\par \par \uc2 BERGER: My reporter instincts kicked in and I go, this is a story. I have to write about this.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Berger`s report in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  this summer struck a nerve with other breast cancer survivors like herself.\par \par \uc2 Why can women know about this gene?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISA PORT, CHIEF OF BREAST SURGERY, \uc2 MOUNT SINAI MEDICAL CENTER\uc2 : There was a lack, I would say, of general knowledge or general public knowledge about this gene is because it is much less common.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Dr. Elisa Port says patient screens before 2014 should be tested for PALB2. Those who have it can be monitored or undergo preventive surgeries. Action powered by knowledge.\par \par \uc2 BERGER: To turn it into something positive is -- it`s pretty satisfying.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: And save lives in doing so. Ann Thompson, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Thursday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020211008eha7000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Texas` Abortion Ban Temporarily Blocked By A Federal Judge\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Hallie Jackson \par \uc2 481 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: That controversial new law banning most abortions in Texas is blocked tonight, but the battle is far from over. After the ruling by a federal judge, the state says it will appeal. Hallie Jackson has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, NBC NEWS SENIOR WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): In Texas, tonight, some providers restarting abortion services for people more than six weeks pregnant. That`s after a federal judge press pause on the country`s most restrictive abortion law there which prohibits the procedure after a fetal heartbeat is detected, usually, before most women even know they`re pregnant.\par \par \uc2 The divisive law met with widespread protests after it went into effect last month with the judge now saying, since that moment, "women have been unlawfully prevented from exercising control over their own lives in ways that are protected by the Constitution." But with the Texas Attorney General pledging to appeal, abortion providers could still get sued if the injunction is lifted and the band goes back into effect.\par \par \uc2 AMY HAGSTROM MILLER, FOUNDER AND CEO, WHOLE WOMAN`S HEALTH: It`s scary because we know that the retroactive piece is still there. Although it`s like completely unjust and it`s cruel. And the majority of people in this country and in the world who have been watching what`s happening in Texas think this is absurd.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: The law is so controversial partly because of how it`s written. It gives $10,000 to private citizens who successfully sue anyone who helps with an abortion, even someone who say drives a patient to the clinic. One anti-abortion group which supports the Texas ban slammed the ruling as an unelected judge interfering with the clearly expressed will of Texans, hoping for victory on appeal and pledging the fight will go on.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER, PRESIDENT, \uc2 SUSAN B. ANTHONY\uc2  LIST: This is the beginning, a beautiful beginning of being able to pass laws all over the country that protect babies and serve their mothers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON (on camera): Lawmakers in multiple states are looking at the Texas model for their own new abortion laws. But whatever happens with those could be eclipsed if the Supreme Court decides to overturn the landmark abortion rights decision Roe v Wade in a case from Mississippi in front of them this term. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Hallie Jackson in our Washington newsroom, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020211008eha70005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE NIGHTLY NEWS for October 7, 2021, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Stephanie Gosk, Hallie Jackson, Miguel Almaguer,  Peter Alexander, Sam Brock, Gabe Gutierrez, Morgan Radford, Anne Thompson \par \uc2 3,970 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, the major step forward in the race to vaccinate younger children against COVID.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voice-over): \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  officially asking the \uc2 FDA\uc2  for emergency use authorization for children five to 11, saying a smaller dose is safe and effective. How soon could the \uc2 FDA\uc2  give the green light? Dr. Jha is here with what parents need to know.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, Texas` abortion ban temporarily blocked by a federal judge. The immediate impact on women in the state and what`s next in the battle.\par \par \uc2 The first images of the damage pipeline blamed for the California oil spill disaster. Could it have been caused by a 30-ton anchor? And new tonight, the major class-action lawsuits just announced.\par \par \uc2 Deadly flash floods and high water rescues in the southeast where the severe weather threat is moving tonight.\par \par \uc2 The Senate announcing a deal to raise the debt limit but for how long?\par \par \uc2 The sweeping new report detailing then-President Trump`s attempts to overturn the 2020 election. What it reveals?\par \par \uc2 The suspect in a Texas high school shooting out of jail. New video of a fight breaking out moments before shots were fired. And tonight, the allegations of bullying from the suspect`s family.\par \par \uc2 And 18 former \uc2 NBA\uc2  players charged on the multi-million dollar scam they`re accused of running on the league.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening. And we may be moving rapidly tonight towards emergency approval of COVID vaccines for children ages five to 11. \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  today submitting its application for emergency use authorization for kid-size doses of its vaccine, potentially extending COVID protection to nearly 30 million children.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  promising to act quickly. An Advisory Committee now set to meet and make its recommendation on October 26, less than three weeks from now. Regulators to review Pfizer`s plan to administer smaller doses of the vaccine to children as we get new snapshots today of just how much the pandemic is impacting kids.\par \par \uc2 The pediatric shots if approved could help narrow the vaccination gap. Right now, 56 percent of Americans have been fully vaccinated. Stephanie Gosk has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Just in time for holiday travel. That`s when nearly 30 million children between the ages of 5 and 11 could become eligible for the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine. Today, the company officially asking the \uc2 FDA\uc2  for emergency use authorization.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think that is amazing. I have been waiting for that for a long, long, long time.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: According to \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , a dose 1/3 the size of that given to adults is both safe and effective in young children.\par \par \uc2 DR. YVONNE MALDONADO\uc2 , INFECTIOUS DISEASE PHYSICIAN, STAFFORD UNIVERSITY: I do think it will make a big difference in reducing sick days, missing school, potential for hospitalizations, and other complications.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: The vaccine not only protects from the disease, it protects from disruptions in life, doesn`t it?\par \par \uc2 MALDONADO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  advisory committee is expected to meet October 26 to make a decision. Convincing some parents may be a challenge. Only 30 percent say they would get their children vaccinated immediately.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s something that I would definitely like to see a lot more information.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: The \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  announcement comes on a day when two new studies expose the wide-ranging impact the pandemic has had on the nation`s children. A report in the medical journal JAMA found substantial and persistent declines in pediatric vaccinations for other illnesses, including measles.\par \par \uc2 The biggest drop took place when COVID first hit, but numbers are rebounding slowly because of staffing shortages and health center closures. Another study done by the CDC reveals that more than 120,000 children have lost a primary caregiver from COVID. A disproportionate number, 65 percent are racial or ethnic minorities.\par \par \uc2 DR. SUSAN HILLIS, SENIOR ADVISER, CDC: We do not yet have a coordinated response to address the needs of those children. And the time is now to do that.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Among the risks they face, dropping out of school early, violent behavior, and depression.\par \par \uc2 HILLIS: This problem of being without your parent or caregiver is not something that young people or children recover from.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: A reminder that ensuring protection from the virus is a priority. But rebounding from the damage done by the pandemic could be a lot harder.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: So. Stephanie, once this vaccine is authorized for children, how long before we see people getting shots in arms?\par \par \uc2 GOSK (on camera): Well, Lester, you may remember, when the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine was authorized for adults, it took just three days. The company tells us that they`re ready to ship right away. They are just waiting for the word. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Stephanie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll talk more about this now. I was joined a short while ago by \uc2 Dr. Ashish Jha\uc2 , Dean of Brown University School of Public Health. And I began by asking him if we should lower our expectations about how many children may receive these vaccinations.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:02]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHISH JHA\uc2 , DEAN, BROWN UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF PUBLIC HEALTH: 70 million adults have not yet gotten vaccinated. I do expect that some chunk of kids will not get vaccinated immediately as well. But I`m hoping a majority will. And of course, I think all kids should. We`re going to have to kind of manage our expectations on this.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We are seeing mandates begin to take hold in a lot of industries, a lot of places. Do they appear to be working? And in your opinion, are they going far enough?\par \par \uc2 JHA: They are working. They are working. There`s been a very noisy minority who`s protesting. But when you look at \uc2 United Airlines\uc2 , you look at the hospitals, 98, 99 percent of people are getting vaccinated. I think we`re just going to see this play out across the country. And I think they are far enough.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Let me ask you about communications and how well the health industry is doing and educating all of us. You know, at the outset, when the -- when the vaccines came about, nobody was talking about breakthrough infections. Yes, we heard there were 90 some odd percent effective. But now we`re seeing these breakthrough infections a lot. And it`s a bit rattling and unsettling.\par \par \uc2 JHA: I think all of us could have done a better job explaining to people that even if it`s 90, 95 percent effective, that means there are going to be breakthroughs. And over time, there is going to be some waning. And I think none of us knew at the Delta variant, which is, I think, a major reason we`re seeing a lot of breakthroughs. So, we should have done a better job communicating that that was a real risk.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And speaking of communications, the booster shots for a select number of Americans in different -- in different demographics, do you see that being extended to all vaccinated people?\par \par \uc2 JHA: I do, eventually. I expect over the next couple of months, we`re going to see more data for relatively young, healthy people. My expectation is all Americans will end up needing to get a booster.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`re coming down from Delta right now. What is the next wave look like? Are they going to become increasingly small?\par \par \uc2 JHA: Yes. I think you`re going to see little blips that look much better. They`re going to vary a lot based on underlying vaccination rates in New England, high vaccination rates. I expect a little blip over the winter. Midwest, Great Plains, still a lot of people not vaccinated. The surges will get smaller. We`re going to really learn to manage this virus.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: \uc2 Dr. Ashish Jha\uc2  on a conversation just a short time ago.\par \par \uc2 That controversial new law banning most abortions in Texas is blocked tonight, but the battle is far from over. After the ruling by a federal judge, the state says it will appeal. Hallie Jackson has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, NBC NEWS SENIOR WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): In Texas, tonight, some providers restarting abortion services for people more than six weeks pregnant. That`s after a federal judge press pause on the country`s most restrictive abortion law there which prohibits the procedure after a fetal heartbeat is detected, usually, before most women even know they`re pregnant.\par \par \uc2 The divisive law met with widespread protests after it went into effect last month with the judge now saying, since that moment, "women have been unlawfully prevented from exercising control over their own lives in ways that are protected by the Constitution." But with the Texas Attorney General pledging to appeal, abortion providers could still get sued if the injunction is lifted and the band goes back into effect.\par \par \uc2 AMY HAGSTROM MILLER, FOUNDER AND CEO, WHOLE WOMAN`S HEALTH: It`s scary because we know that the retroactive piece is still there. Although it`s like completely unjust and it`s cruel. And the majority of people in this country and in the world who have been watching what`s happening in Texas think this is absurd.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: The law is so controversial partly because of how it`s written. It gives $10,000 to private citizens who successfully sue anyone who helps with an abortion, even someone who say drives a patient to the clinic. One anti-abortion group which supports the Texas ban slammed the ruling as an unelected judge interfering with the clearly expressed will of Texans, hoping for victory on appeal and pledging the fight will go on.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER, PRESIDENT, \uc2 SUSAN B. ANTHONY\uc2  LIST: This is the beginning, a beautiful beginning of being able to pass laws all over the country that protect babies and serve their mothers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON (on camera): Lawmakers in multiple states are looking at the Texas model for their own new abortion laws. But whatever happens with those could be eclipsed if the Supreme Court decides to overturn the landmark abortion rights decision Roe v Wade in a case from Mississippi in front of them this term. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Hallie Jackson in our Washington newsroom, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Southern California, the first class-action lawsuit against the owners of that broken oil pipeline by businesses that say they were harmed by the massive spill. This comes as we get a first look at the damage pipeline itself. Miguel Almaguer is there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): The first images of the splintered pipeline captured 80 feet underwater reveal the more than foot-long gash were oil gushed into the Pacific. Authorities now say they`re inspecting the 41-year-old pipe which runs from this oil rig to shore for signs of corrosion and pressure problems. But for now, many believe the pipe was likely hit by a 30-ton anchor like this one.\par \par \uc2 STEVE BROWN, PROFESSOR, CALIFORNIA STATE UNIVERSITY MARITIME ACADEMY: The fact that the pipeline appears to have been dragged leads me to conclude that it probably was caught on the ship`s anchor.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Reviewing satellite imagery in the hours before and after the spill, the Coast Guard will likely be able to pinpoint which cargo ships were anchored in the area, a jam shipping lane dotted with a maze of drilling platforms.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:11]\par \par \uc2 JOHN AMOS, FOUNDER AND PRESIDENT, SKYTRUTH: As long as you`re shipping and offshore oil development operating in close proximity, this is the kind of accident and spill that you can expect to have happen.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: With so much focus on the investigation, the true disaster is unfolding out here at sea. These booms can only hold back so much oil, which is quickly moving through this fragile ecosystem.\par \par \uc2 With more wildlife in pristine miles of beaches stain from this massive spill, tonight the search for answers and the probe to hold someone responsible. Miguel Almaguer, NBC News Huntington Beach, California.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In Alabama tonight, at least four people are dead after a flash flooding event caused by more than a foot of rain in some places. They were in cars swept away. More than 100 people were rescued from homes and cars. The threat continues in the southeast with nine million under flash flood watches.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the Senate announcing a deal to avert a financial meltdown. Democrats and Republicans are set to raise the debt ceiling which allows the government to keep borrowing money. It`s a temporary fix until early December when they`ll have to negotiate again.\par \par \uc2 And a new report just down from the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  is detailing the extremes then-President Trump allegedly went to overturn the 2020 election. Peter Alexander is at the White House. Peter, it`s the first time we`re hearing some of these accounts.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Lester, that`s right. This new report says that former President Trump directly asked the Justice Department officials nine times to investigate his baseless claims of mass voter fraud.\par \par \uc2 One key moment, just days before the Capitol Riot, Mr. Trump in the Oval Office pressured the Acting Attorney General \uc2 Jeffrey Rosen\uc2  and others and discussed replacing Rosen with someone who had promised to pursue those election lies.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump, the report says, only back down after Rosen, his deputy, even the top White House lawyer all threatened mass resignations. A House committee also wants to learn more but the former President`s allies, Lester, say they will not cooperate.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Peter, thank you. In just 60 seconds, did bullying play a role in that high school shooting in Texas? What the suspect`s family is saying? And 18 former \uc2 NBA\uc2  players indicted in an alleged scheme worth millions.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Breaking news tonight in Texas where that 18-year-old student accused of shooting three people in his high school has now been released on bond. This comes as police confirm the identity of the two teens seen in a video fighting before the shooting. Sam Brock has details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Tonight, Timothy Simpkins is out on bond a day after police say he perpetrated a school shooting that left a 15-year-old in critical condition.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you have anything to say?\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Among the many tragedies unfolding, is the fear students are now carrying to class.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN KOLBYE, ASSISTANT CHIEF, ARLINGTON POLICE DEPARTMENT: And there is no reason that a student should bring a firearm into a school or feel threatened in that way that they feel like they got to have a weapon.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Arlington P.D. investigating several disturbing videos.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What are you saying?\par \par \uc2 BROCK: The student in the white shirt being beaten as the alleged shooter and the aggressor the victim in the ICU. Simpkins` family says they reported bullying to the school.\par \par \uc2 CAROL HARRISON-LAFAYETTE, FAMILY MEMBER OF SIMPKINS: The decision that he made taking the gun, we`re not justifying that. That was not right. But he was trying to protect himself.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Do you believe that bullying played a role or could have.\par \par \uc2 KOLBYE: I do understand the family`s concerns. And to the family, I want to assure them that we are going to do everything we can to look into why that happened.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Today, police made another arrest for a young student threatening violence against a different Arlington Area School on social media. Parents and Police calling for sweeping change in safety and culture. Sam Brock, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [19:15:06]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Next, 18 former \uc2 NBA\uc2  players are facing federal charges accused of a multi-million dollar fraud scheme. Here`s Gabe Gutierrez.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): The 18 \uc2 NBA\uc2  veterans are accused of ripping off the league`s health benefits plan by filing fake claims for medical procedures that never happened.\par \par \uc2 AUDREY STRAUSS\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY, SDNY: The defendant`s playbook involved fraud and deception.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Prosecutors allege ex-NBA player Terrence Williams orchestrated the scheme recruiting other players by offering fake invoices for chiropractic treatments and dental care. According to the indictment, they submitted nearly $4 million worth of claims. The health plan paid out $2.5 million. Williams allegedly got $230,000 in kickbacks from the other players.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL DRISCOLL, ASSISTANT DIRECTOR IN CHARGE, FBI NEW YORK FIELD OFFICE: This industry loses tens of billions of dollars a year to fraud. These costs are then passed on to businesses and customers.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Prosecutors say the scheme unfolded from 2017 through 2020. Ex- power forward Gregory Smith is accused of seeking reimbursement for root canals in Beverly Hills when he was actually playing basketball in Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 Among the others charge, Glen Davis, Shannon Brown, and six-time \uc2 NBA\uc2  All- Defensive Team member Tony Allen along with his wife. Late today, Sebastian Telfair pleaded not guilty walking on a federal court with an ankle monitor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (on camera): The other former players have not yet commented. The \uc2 NBA\uc2  today called the allegations disheartening and promised to cooperate fully with the U.S. Attorney`s office here. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Gabe, thank you. Up next tonight, the crisis at the border and the COVID threat.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: There is another crisis across from the border. Thousands of migrants in the camp just south of the Texas border in Mexico where they face not only deportation and starvation but also the threat of COVID. Morgan Radford is in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MORGAN RADFORD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): For Manuel and his young son, this is home. Fleeing from El Salvador, he`s been here for more than 45 days. He`s one of nearly 2000 people living in this encampment in Reynosa, Mexico hoping to get across the Texas border just steps away.\par \par \uc2 You`re not here because you want to be here. It`s because you were threatened.\par \par \uc2 Manuel asked us not to use his full name or show his face for fear of violence from the cartels. Which aid workers like Felicia Rangel-Samponaro say have fought over this city for years as a trafficking route.\par \par \uc2 People are afraid to leave this encampment because they`re afraid to get kidnapped.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, they will get kidnapped. One person is worth between $2000 to $6,000. So, if you leave this encampment, yes, you will be kidnapped.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: And when you say the different gangs are watching and monitoring this area --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: I mean, are they watching now?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: There are nearly 2000 people inside this encampment. You can see them lining up for basic necessities like food and water. But now, they say, the real threat is COVID.\par \par \uc2 Do you have -- with so many people here in this encampment, are you afraid of COVID?\par \par \uc2 We`re always afraid of it. We can`t really maintain distance. But you`re always living with that fear.\par \par \uc2 A fear that many here say could soon get worse, since basic sanitation, like washing your hands or showering, is hard to come by.\par \par \uc2 So, to shower, you have to pay 10 pesos just to shower.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (text): We are exposed. We are exposed.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Aid workers like Felicia say, they`ve seen a surge in COVID cases in recent weeks with up to 150 people per day testing positive.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: At one point, in each family, there`s at least one person who had COVID. The kids have COVID, the moms had COVID, pregnant women had COVID. Everyone had it.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: It`s a problem that traces back to a policy called Title 42, a section of us health law first enforced by the Trump administration and still in place under President Biden. It essentially shuts down the Mexican border to many asylum seekers, leaving them to wait and face a shortage of COVID tests while they do.\par \par \uc2 What`s the worst-case scenario if you run out of COVID test and simply cannot bring enough here?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The worst-case scenario has already been happening. Ambulances have been called multiple times to this Plaza because people can`t breathe.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: So, to be clear, most of the people in this encampment do not have asylum or legal cases pending. Most are here just waiting for the border to open.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes. So, this is hope you`re looking at.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: This is what hope looks like right now?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is what hope looks like right now.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Hope to one day find a home better than this.\par \par \uc2 And you don`t have any place to go? This is the last chance?\par \par \uc2 Morgan Radford, NBC News Reynosa, Mexico\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And next for us tonight, raising awareness of a lesser-known breast cancer risk. Her mission inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, this is Breast Cancer Awareness Month. And tonight, one woman`s mission to make people aware of a lesser-known variant that can be deadly. Anne Thompson on how she`s inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CHIEF ENVIRONMENTAL AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: Knowledge is power. That`s freelance journalist Susan Berger`s mantra even more so since Burger learned she and two of her three daughters have a little-known genetic mutation that increases their risk for breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN BERGER, FREELANCE JOURNALIST: The more I did my research, I realized that it`s a gift.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: So, are you on a one-woman crusade to tell other breast cancer survivors that you should be tested for PALB2?\par \par \uc2 BERGER: I am.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: PALB2 is often called the third breast cancer gene. The less than one percent of breast cancer patients have the mutation. Its impact can be devastating, increasing the risk for breast cancer by 58 percent with a family history, 33 percent with no history, and upping the risk for ovarian and pancreatic cancers.\par \par \uc2 BERGER: My reporter instincts kicked in and I go, this is a story. I have to write about this.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Berger`s report in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  this summer struck a nerve with other breast cancer survivors like herself.\par \par \uc2 Why can women know about this gene?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISA PORT, CHIEF OF BREAST SURGERY, \uc2 MOUNT SINAI MEDICAL CENTER\uc2 : There was a lack, I would say, of general knowledge or general public knowledge about this gene is because it is much less common.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Dr. Elisa Port says patient screens before 2014 should be tested for PALB2. Those who have it can be monitored or undergo preventive surgeries. Action powered by knowledge.\par \par \uc2 BERGER: To turn it into something positive is -- it`s pretty satisfying.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: And save lives in doing so. Ann Thompson, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Thursday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020211008eha700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 From Abortion Rights To Gun Control, High Stakes As The Supreme Court Begins Its New Term\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Pete Williams \par \uc2 372 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: On his first Monday in October, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  started its new term, one that will be dominated by abortion, gun rights, and religion. And it began with one absence on the bench. Here`s Pete Williams.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): In a dramatic show of what`s at stake, demonstrators took to the streets nationwide over the weekend, most of them urging the Supreme Court and Congress to protect existing abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 CAROL FREEMAN, ABORTION RIGHTS DEMONSTRATOR: And that it`s not anybody else`s business what we do with our bodies and our reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The court will take up a Mississippi law that would ban virtually all abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy. The state says the landmark Roe v Wade decision should be overruled as out of date. And the new Texas law shutting down abortions after just six weeks is bound to the back too.\par \par \uc2 The court will also decide whether the Second Amendment provides a right to carry a gun outside the house, and whether states can block giving public tuition money to schools offering religious education.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: God save the United States and this honorable court.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The justices were back in their own courtroom today for the first time since the pandemic forced them to work from home 18 months ago. But seat was empty. Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  was absent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS (on camera): He`s listening to this week`s cases remotely after testing positive for COVID four days ago. He`ll likely be back next week. All the justices are fully vaccinated. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Pete Williams, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020211005eha4000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Who Has The Upper Hand When We Go Inside The Real Estate Market\b0\par\par\uc2 Craig Melvin, Sheinelle Jones \par \uc2 9,278 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [09:15:00] CRAIG MELVIN: And welcome back. Today in our series "The Great Divide," we`re talking about the state of real estate.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER: Housing prices are rising in a 2020 report from RenoFI said that the average cost of a house could be $382,000 by 2030. So, what do you do if you`re looking to make the right move now?\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES: Well, the real estate expert -- I`m like listening to what you`re saying. I have so many questions. Stefani Berkin, president of R New York, one of New York`s largest real estate companies is here with us this morning with what you need to know.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Sorry you had to see that, Stefani.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You know what, honestly, I turned into a viewer.\par \par \uc2 STEFANI BERKIN, REAL ESTATE EXPERT & PRESIDENT OF R NEW YORK: I loved it. I love it. Good morning. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Thank you for coming. Here`s the truth.\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: So, full disclosure, during COVID, there were a lot of us, a lot of -- who wanted to get out of the city, right? So, you have all these people who were buying outside of the city. For people -- I have friends who are in St. Louis, Kansas City who have always wanted to live, let`s say, in Seattle. People were all over the place.\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: And so, we had heard that there was this big housing boom. But then, now we`ve heard there`s a bubble.\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: So, what is the truth, especially with COVID, for people who are sitting at home and want a chance?\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: So, it`s a great question, Sheinelle. And that`s exactly what -- she`s right, everyone is asking the same question. So, during COVID, we were in a really strong buyer`s market. Buyers had the upper hand. It has completely shifted.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK. So, I`m not crazy?\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: No, you are spot on. No, you are spot on.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: In this and in life, yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: Yes. I`ll back up.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Well, let`s not get crazy. On this, yes.\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: So, we are actually in a really, really strong seller`s market. Because demand right now is outpacing inventory. There are more home buyers, more people looking to scoop up the inventory and the inventory doesn`t really exist. So, we need to equip our buyers, our home buyers and our renters with sort of tips and tricks as to how to secure the deal in this kind of environment.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh, I like this.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: So, do they -- do home buyers and renters need to make certain sacrifices, if you will, to get what they`re looking for?\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: Yes, sacrifices, compromises, maybe just temporarily. But I like to tell people, you want to think about your need list. Don`t think so much about your want list. Think about the non-negotiables.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So, make a need list?\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: Make a need list with absolutely non-negotiables. And expand your search from a neighborhood perspective. Commuting is not as relevant anymore because so many people are working from home and working remotely. So, look into different neighborhoods. They could be more affordable now when they`re up and coming. You could make a really nice profit. And I also tell people, see everything. The more you see, the more you know and convertibility. It`s the biggest thing.\par \par \uc2 JONES: What do you mean?\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: So, when someone is looking for a two-bedroom home, they may search and there would be very little inventory. Think about searching for a one-bedroom home, a large one-bedroom, and see maybe something has an L- shaped living dining, you cut the dining, there you have your second bedroom.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Boom. Or you add room.\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: Or you add a room.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So, obviously, you need to do to get completely prepare when you start this process.\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: What does that look like?\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: How do you go completely prepared for your search?\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: Yes. So, that`s a perfect again. And actually, that, Craig, is the difference between winning a deal and losing a deal, is being prepared, because time kills deals. So, I tell people, before you ever see anything, have a packet ready of pay stubs, bank statements, tax returns, reference letters, employment, personal, business, and have your real estate broker send you samples of those letters.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh, that`s good.\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: Because that will have the sort of bulk of what you need in there and then, people can just personalize it. It could turn you around. You identify the property, you get it.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK. I was looking at this. According to a report from \uc2 Apartment List\uc2 , the national median rent has increased by 16.4 percent since January. Can renters expect those prices to continue to rise? Because reality is there are a lot of people watching this morning who rent.\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: Yes. So, renting is really smart right now because people are thinking more about the temporary than the permanent. So, they want to get their feet wet in these more densely populated areas. See if it works. And you`re not as committed financially. It`s 12 months and you can be out. So, yes, rental prices will go up, but don`t ever be afraid to negotiate. Negotiate, negotiate, negotiate. They could always bring it right back down.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Stefani, you know your stuff. Thank you for coming in.\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: Thank you. I love it.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: She`s an expert.\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: Thanks for having me. Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: You`re not crazy.\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: Make it easy.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BERKIN: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, a musical addition of "Motivational Monday." How the tuba was instrumental in changing one man`s life forever.\par \par \uc2 And then a little bit later, Actor Bill Pullman, he`s going to join us live to fill us in on the return of that creepy series of his and the iconic role that`s turning 25. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:20:00]\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: This morning in "Motivational Monday," an accomplished musician whose journey nothing short of incredible. His name is Richard Antoine White. He`s a professional tuba player. He`s also the author of "I`m Possible." He told me how the instrument helped him go from the streets of Baltimore to the symphony stage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ANTOINE WHITE, "I`M POSSIBLE" AUTHOR, TUBA PLAYER: Going to do a little beat boxing in the tuba.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN (voiceover): The sounds and rhythms coming from his tuba are truly unique. But, then again, so is the guy playing it.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN (on camera): What? What? Do you get strange looks even now? I mean, you are 6`5", 300-pound black dude with corn rows hauling around a tuba.\par \par \uc2 WHITE: You have no idea. People still ask me, have you ever been to a concert? I was like, well, I dabble a little bit, you know, sometimes. I don`t tell them that I`m a (INAUDIBLE). And then, they see me on stage and go, what?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN (voiceover): This is Richard Antoine White, and as the principal tubist for the New Mexico Philharmonic in Santa Fe Symphony, he`s right at home on stage. A far cry from his childhood in Baltimore where was often the street.\par \par \uc2 WHITE: This water fountain has got to be just as old as me because it`s where I would drink and bathe if I needed to. It was my bathroom, man.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN (on camera): Growing up on the streets, I mean, mom was in and out of the life. She was an alcoholic. What were those days like?\par \par \uc2 [09:25:00]\par \par \uc2 WHITE: Every day was literally about finding my mom or finding something to eat. I would find food in trash cans. And because I didn`t know if I would eat again, I would chew it and then just store it under my tongue. So, later on, if I got really hungry, I would take that food that I had stored under my tongue and eat it later.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN (voiceover): Richard eventually found two lifelines. His adoptive grandparents who took him in and joining the school band, which revealed a natural ear for music.\par \par \uc2 WHITE: It was exciting to me. And when I first started playing, I would play the "Cosby Show," "Sanford and Son" theme shows and beat box. No one else could do that on their instruments.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN (on camera): But you couldn`t read music, right? You couldn`t count the beat. How did you pick it up?\par \par \uc2 WHITE: I started playing tuba on something called a cassette tape. It would say, this is B flat. Play B flat. Pause the tape. When you mastered B flat, move on to C. Doo, pause.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: You learned from a cassette tape?\par \par \uc2 WHITE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN (voiceover): He became good enough that he decided to audition to get into the prestigious Baltimore Schools for the Arts. There was just one problem.\par \par \uc2 WHITE: I showed up a day late. The director had left something there. He just happened to be here. He said, what are you doing here? Auditions were yesterday. And I don`t know how but I boldly looked him right in the eyes and said, but I`m here now. And he said, the audacity or the determination was so fierce that he had to hear me.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN (voiceover): Richard got in. And from there, he went on to Peabody Conservatory of Music, then \uc2 Indiana University\uc2 , where in 2012, he became the first black man in America to get a doctorate of music in tuba. He credits his many mentors along the way.\par \par \uc2 WHITE: To all the teachers out there, I really want you to know that I am what happens when you don`t give up.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN (voiceover): He is now a tenured professor himself at the \uc2 University of New Mexico\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WHITE: My primary responsibility is to inspire hope, to show every little kid, whether they want to play tuba or trump, regardless of their advantages or disadvantages, that they can do it.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN (voiceover): And much like a symphony, Richard says the key to life is everyone playing their part together.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN (on camera): What do you want people to take away from your story?\par \par \uc2 WHITE: Be kind. Recognize and acknowledge that it takes a village. We were put on this planet to work and live together. Let`s offer the world the best version of ourselves. Cumulatively, that`s got to equal something magnificent. And I hope we can all help each other to achieve that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That was fantastic.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Dr. White, is -- you talk about a spirit animal, he is quite the motivation. And if he hasn`t achieved enough, by the way, Richard also set the goal to be the first musician to perform on all seven continents, even Antarctica.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s great/\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: His story is featured in an anthology series call "Hi, I Am." It`s over on the Magnolia network. And his new book, "I`m Possible," that book is out tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I love that. "I`m possible."\par \par \uc2 ROKER: I can`t wait to read that.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: "I`m possible."\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s great story.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thank you, Dr. White.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Terrific. Wow.\par \par \uc2 Well, coming up next, the one, the only Bill Pullman live here in our studio. We`re actually going to talk to him. No more teasers. He`s actually going to come here and talk about the return of his chilling series and his love of cider?\par \par \uc2 And later, some great products for your home wardrobe and more. Best part, they all help fight breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Love it.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: As the "3rd Hour of Today" continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:30:00]\par \par \uc2 JONES: This morning, we are catching up with an actor who can do it all, Bill Pullman. He`s played iconic roles like Lone Starr in the hilarious "Spaceballs."\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I forgot about that.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Right? And who could forget his inspiring turn as President Whitmore in the epic film "Independence Day."\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Well, now, Bill is back and he`s back in the psychological thriller "The Sinner" as Detective Harry Ambrose. It`s a role which earned him a Screen Actors Guild nomination.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Now, even though Harry retired last season, trouble seems to follow him wherever he goes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL PULLMAN, ACTOR: It was about an hour or ago or so that I was walking in town and I saw your granddaughter ahead of me. And it seemed like she was uneasy. Maybe she was trying to get away from somebody. And she took the path up here, tried to catch up to her. That`s when I saw her out on the bluff. And she went straight out on to the edge. All of a sudden, she jumped.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Bill joins us now. Good morning, Bill. It`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 PULLMAN: Yes. It`s good to see a little clip there. Just finished shooting it. So --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: So, have you seen the show?\par \par \uc2 PULLMAN: No, I haven`t.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: No?\par \par \uc2 PULLMAN: I have kind of always had an agreement with the showrunner to watch it like everybody else and when it comes out on USA. It`s once a week for eight weeks. And so, I then call him the next day, amazing how you did this, amazing how you did that.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Wow. So, this will be your fourth season. It`s the longest you`ve played one character. It must be really fulfilling to be able to build somebody like this over a period of time.\par \par \uc2 PULLMAN: Yes. You know, I avoided it for a long time. I didn`t think I was the actor for doing recurring roles. What if you dry up? Do you start hating your work? And just watching you guys getting ready for this segment, I thought, they love their work.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We do.\par \par \uc2 JONES: We do.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We have each other.\par \par \uc2 PULLMAN: And that`s what I feel 