key recipe ever.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Really?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Which, hopefully, going to get Sohla here to do.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: But in the meantime, you`re doing -- which is a version of this, of your -- what you did with me with turkey, you`re doing a crispy roast trick chicken within a dry brine.\par \par \uc2 SOHLA EL-WAYLLY, "OFF-SCRIPT WITH SOHLA" CHEF AND HOST: Yes. It`s just going to be a mini version of the turkey we made together. So, I spatchcocked by turkey and then I covered it in a dry brine. Overnight is best. But if you are afraid of spatchcocking your turkey --\par \par \uc2 JONES: Especially if you don`t know what it is.\par \par \uc2 EL-WAYLLY: -- you could also just use thighs. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EL-WAYLLY: All I did here was I used some scissors to cut out the backbone and then you can press your chicken flat so it cooks a little bit more evenly. But if you don`t want to do that --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And what`s with the dry brine, Sohla?\par \par \uc2 EL-WAYLLY: Yes. The dry brine is a mix of salt, sugar, pepper, you can add some spices here if you like. And if the spatchcocking seems intimidating, you can also just the same thing on some thighs, some legs, some breasts, whatever part you like.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 EL-WAYLLY: And we`re going to let it sit in the fridge uncovered. A couple of hours is good, but overnight is best. And the best thing you can do for yourself on Monday is make a couple of chickens the day before and then you`re going to have chicken in your fridge to have a lot of fun with, which we`re going to do right now.\par \par \uc2 So, with our leftover chicken, after you roast the chicken, you always have a little bit of meat left, but it`s not enough for a full meal. So, we`re going to stretch it into a tamale pie. And so, in my skillet, I`ve already got a couple of table spoons of oil and one onion that`s been cooking. Now, it`s like nicely frizzled and brown. And we`re going to jazz this up.\par \par \uc2 Here I`ve got three cloves of garlic that`s been chopped.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 EL-WAYLLY: Pop that in there.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Tamale pie. OK.\par \par \uc2 EL-WAYLLY: We`re going to add 1.5 teaspoons of ground cumin.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 EL-WAYLLY: And I`m going to spice it up. I`ve got a couple of chopped chipotles. But, you know, get as crazy as you like. You want three in there, you want one in there, you decide.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Throw jalapeno in.\par \par \uc2 EL-WAYLLY: Jalapeno, yes. A little chili powder. A little bit of heat is nice. So, I`m going on let this saute for a few seconds just to warm up that spice. And now, we`re going to add a can of -- I`m using black beans, but any kind of bean, and we`re going to use all of the liquid. It`s going to be like our sauce.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh, you don`t drain it. OK.\par \par \uc2 EL-WAYLLY: I don`t drain it because it`s nice and starchy and it`s going to kind of make a little gravy for us.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: So, it thickens up?\par \par \uc2 EL-WAYLLY: It`s going to thicken up really nice. And I`ve got one cup of frozen corn.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK. You know what, it`s interesting, after Al endorsement, I want to try that chicken. So, I can only imagine once you use it the next time, since this is "Make Ahead Monday," it probably gets even better, doesn`t it?\par \par \uc2 EL-WAYLLY: It just keeps getting better.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EL-WAYLLY: Yes, definitely. So, I use frozen corn, but use whatever veggie you feel like using. A little bit of water. And then, we`re going to add our picked (ph) chicken and season this up with a little bit of salt and pepper. And that is our filling, boom.\par \par \uc2 JONES: If I was over your shoulder, I would be picking out of your pot there.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: OK. So, now, how -- now, for the pie part, how do you get the batter going?\par \par \uc2 EL-WAYLLY: So, for the pie part. We`re going to use a little bit of masa harina. So, this is the stuff that you use to make tamales but dry. So, you can make it like instantly. And to this, I`m going the add some kosher salt, baking powder and we`re going to whisk that up. And then, we`re just going to add a little bit of melted butter and water. And this is going to make a really nice batter that we`re going to put on top of our tamale pie.\par \par \uc2 What`s great is it`s going to take just like a tamale, but you don`t have to fold all those individual little pouches, which can take so long. Now, I`m going to -- to make this even better, it already looks good, but we`re going to top it with cheese because --\par \par \uc2 JONES: Of course.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Why not.\par \par \uc2 EL-WAYLLY: Of course. You must.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Man, I wish I could try that. That looks so good.\par \par \uc2 EL-WAYLLY: And then, I`m going add our batter right on top. And then, this is going to get covered and baked. And it`s just -- you can do this with any kind of filling. If you don`t like corn, you can try it with kale or spinach. You could try -- this is a great place to use up all of your leftovers. It`s just a casserole. So, maybe --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And, Sohla, how hot an oven and for how long?\par \par \uc2 [09:55:00]\par \par \uc2 EL-WAYLLY: So, I`m going to cover this and it`s going to bake at 400 degrees for 30 minutes. And I`ve got one that we already made right over here. And now, we`re going to have a lot of fun. So --\par \par \uc2 JONES: I want to see what it looks like. This is a true "Make Ahead Monday."\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: The two distinct --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Oh, look at that.\par \par \uc2 JONES: -- yummy -- yes -- dishes here.\par \par \uc2 EL-WAYLLY: So, it`s not like the most beautiful thing, but I`m going to serve it alongside some shredded lettuce, some radi