s see.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 Is this a love scene from "Rocky"? I love that.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I don`t know. But it`s probably just the only thing clear for me.\par \par \uc2 And the best part is that this is -- if you turn on any sort of show this morning, this is what we`re showing.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh yeah.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Really it was a one-second hug.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All I have seen was slo-mo. I`m like, boy, they really like they hugged it out.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: No. They really didn`t.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: That was a deep and touching moment. No.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: But I`m glad he was still wearing his signature sweats because everything is right in the world.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It is. It is.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Now, I have to ask you. Have you ever run into like an old boyfriend or something?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: An old boyfriend and had that moment?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: No.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Had any moment?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: No.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Not even a second of a moment? Not even --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: No. It`s more like -- no, I haven`t. I mean, I have - I did run actually into my ex-husband once.\par \par \uc2 Yeah, that was super awkward. It was fine.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Where were you?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I was on a shoot in Paris of all things. I know. And yeah, it was super weird. But then I was like, I literally, I was like, well, good to see you. I`ve got to go. Because I did. I had to do the show. But it was totally nice and normal. And it`s like no acrimony or anything.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Did you look cute?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I don`t know how cute I looked. No, I don`t think I did.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Are you sure? Because you know you always want to look your best.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I know. I don`t think I did. Thanks for bringing it up.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Oh, gosh.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I don`t know. What about you? Have you ever run into -- I was about to name names, but I`m not going to.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: There are a few that come to mind. They are like famous.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: No, don`t.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I`m not going to.\par \par \uc2 OK. I know.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: They`re famous because you can tell that one time my dad said something to you about it, right?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yeah. Your dad remembers them.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yeah. He does. I did once see an old boyfriend in the airport.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You didn`t say hi?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I didn`t say hi.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yeah, yeah.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And then I regretted it. But I just - I - my hair was wet. You know, it was a Sunday.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: No, you don`t want to.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: No.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: All right. Coming up - that`s it.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: That`s it. That`s it. That`s all.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Coming up. Hoda`s conversation with two longtime friends. Oprah and Maria Shriver.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And what they say they cherish most about each other. It might surprise you. After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:14:05]\par \par \uc2 HAGER: The reviews are in. And there`s no surprise. Listeners of Hoda`s podcast "Making Space" are loving it and saying how much they`ve already learned. And today`s episode does not disappoint. Hoda had a candid conversation with Oprah Winfrey and Maria Shriver.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Can you imagine?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: The three of them.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: The three of them\par \par \uc2 HAGER: In a podcast booth?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yeah. And you know I actually didn`t know this. Oprah and Maria go way back. More than four decades. They`ve been friends for such a long time. They`ve seen highs and lows in each other`s lives including love and lost.\par \par \uc2 So, Hoda had the idea to sit down with both of them and talk about friendship and why their bond remains so fulfilling.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARIA SHRIVER, JOURNALIST: I just want to say that we both love you.\par \par \uc2 Yeah, because we have never done this before.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): By the way, I am so honored.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER: We`ve never done this. And neither one of us knew what we were doing or why we were doing this.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY, HOST: I just said I had to do it because Hoda asked.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Here`s my favorite thing right now. I feel like there`s a lot of friendship in this room.\\\par \par \uc2 [10:15:00]\par \par \uc2 And I`m looking at Maria. I`m looking at Oprah. And I`m just thinking about, like how would you -- Maria, how would you describe your friendship with Oprah?\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER: Oh, my God. Long. Simple. Low maintenance. Trustful. Loyal. Constant. Consistent. Honest. Tried and true.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Wow.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER: Want me to keep going?\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): I love that. Oprah, how about you? How do you describe your friendship?\par \par \uc2 WINFREY: I would say that there is a spiritual connection. I would say the reason that I first met her in the bathroom at WJZ-TV early in the morning and we started a conversation. And I forever think that was like a divine moment that happened because she was one of my true, grounded friendships that carried me through my entire career. There`s Gayle, there`s Maria, there`s Bob. And that`s about it.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voice-over): For Maria who was raised in a stoic family, it`s Oprah she turns to for comfort.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER: When I have gone to visit Oprah, when I went, she would, you know, as this sounds so kind of silly, but she like would say like, oh, here`s a cup of coffee I made for you.\par \par \uc2 WINFREY: Brought the coffee and paper to your door.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER: And I was going to -- I`m going to cry. It was so moving to me because I didn`t -- that`s not how I grew up. You know, nobody brought a cup of coffee or a cup of water to me. And she, in a funny way, even though I had a very close relationship with my mother. I wasn`t nurtured, mothered in that way, right? And she wasn`t mothered in her own way, but I think in a way we have mothered each other.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voice-over): And then there are the big moments in life. Like the loss of a parent.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER: If you had told me that at the age of 52, I would finally get up the nerve to crawl into bed with my mother, hold her and tell her that I love her, I would have said, you are nuts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): For some reason I came upon your mom`s funeral. And I watched your eulogy. And it moved me to my core. And then I looked into the - into the congregation and there was Oprah. And I thought, right there, right next to her.\par \par \uc2 How did you support Maria during that time?\par \par \uc2 WINFREY: (AUDIO GAP)\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER: Like you know had somebody who`s gone through the birth of all your children with you, right?\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER: Your marriage, your divorce, the death of your parents, the death of yourself. The rebirth of yourself. The evolution of herself. The change of herself. You just step back and you`re like, damn, like how blessed am I to have that for that length of time? The duration of that time. And all the laughs and tears and joys and not have lost our way.\par \par \uc2 WINFREY: The thing that grounds me in my friendship, relationship and cherishing Maria is that she really sees me. And she tries to do that with everybody. I know you feel that in your relationship with her. I know her children feel that. When you`re in her presence you feel like you`re the most important person. That`s one of the reasons why I cherish you so much.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER: When I close my eyes, it`s the little moments. It`s you, having dinner with me. It`s her bringing the coffee. It`s having a laugh. It`s, you know, I think that`s -- I think we`re all pushed to have these big, big moments, but I think it`s the little moments that people don`t -- aren`t pushed to have that really are the transforming moments.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: That`s so cool.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: How profound was that conversation? I have to tell you. I was a little jealous.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, I thought it was interesting because you`ve been telling me for a while now that you and Oprah are best friends. But when she listed her best friends in her life, there was Maria, Gayle, Bob somebody.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: No, instead of Bob, she meant Jenna. Bob and Jenna are --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Sometimes we call you Bob. Don`t worry.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yeah. Sometimes you call me Bob. But how beautiful was that conversation? I think anybody that has a good friend can relate with it.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s such a good idea like for Hoda to convene those two women to get this conversation going.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And there`s more of it.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: There`s a lot more of it.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: There`s more of it.\par \par \uc2 So, you just have to -- for more of the conversation, you scan this QR code right there or search for "Making Space with Hoda Kotb" on Apple podcast, Spotify or wherever you get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 [10:20:00]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s rising up the charts.\par \par \uc2 Jenna, you`ve been busy too.\par \par \uc2 Oprah over here. Jerry Seinfeld for you.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I sure did.\par \par \uc2 He`s back, baby.\par \par \uc2 He`s got some exciting news for Seinfeld fans. Plus, he reveals his secret love.\par \par \uc2 That`s a tease. Right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020211005eha40005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE NIGHTLY NEWS for October 4, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Hallie Jackson, Miguel Almaguer, Stephanie O`Donnell,  Kelly O`Donnell, Petre Williams, Andrea Mitchell, Stephanie Ruhle, Antonia  Hylton \par \uc2 3,920 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Tonight, the massive outage. \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , and WhatsApp crippled here in the U.S. and around the world.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voice-over): The outage, one of the longest in Facebook`s history. For hours, hundreds of millions of users unable to connect. The mystery, what caused the meltdown. It comes on the eve of what should it be scathing testimony from a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  whistleblower. What she`s accusing the tech giant of.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight the urgent race to contain the oil spill disaster on the California coast. The famed beaches shutdown covered in black, birds coated in oil. The race to save them and what`s now being hired as the cause.\par \par \uc2 The CDC abruptly removing its latest COVID guidance for holiday gatherings and travel after comments by \uc2 Dr. Anthony Fauci\uc2  sparked confusion. What he`s now saying.\par \par \uc2 The shooting on an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  train. Video appearing to show a man firing from the doorway. A DEA agent killed. What we`re learning.\par \par \uc2 The debt limit showdown. President Biden accusing the GOP of playing Russian roulette. And the other fight over his spending bills. Senator Kyrsten Sinema, one of the moderate holdouts, followed into the bathroom by activists. Her new response.\par \par \uc2 And Bubba Wallace, the moment today he drove into NASCAR history.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening. \uc2 Facebook\uc2  tonight caught in cascading crises. The sprawling social network including \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and WhatsApp suffering a worldwide outage some six hours long. Users met with frustration as they tried to refresh their feeds.\par \par \uc2 The outage fueling wild speculation coming just a day after a company whistleblower step from the shadows with explosive claims that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  put profits over safety. The company`s stock tumbling today, its worst sell off in a year, as engineers race to unravel the mystery of today`s crippling outage. Here`s Hallie Jackson.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, NBC NEWS SENIOR WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Massive and mysterious a global outage taking down \uc2 Facebook\uc2  around the world for hours along with \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and WhatsApp which \uc2 Facebook\uc2  owns. One \uc2 Facebook\uc2  employee telling NBC News everyone is just sort of standing around with the outage affecting pretty much everything.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN COLLIER, NBC NEWS REPORTER: But this is a misconfiguration, a some sort of technical glitch that is basically unheard of. The scope of it is just utterly remarkable.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: \uc2 Facebook\uc2  has not said how many people have been affected in the outage which began just before noon eastern time, but its platforms altogether have billions of users worldwide. A spokesperson late tonight apologizing to those affected, adding we know billions of people in businesses around the world depend on our products and services to stay connected. We appreciate your patience as we come back online.\par \par \uc2 Facebook\uc2  now scrambling to manage this problem and a separate PR crisis after a whistleblower revealed herself to level this explosive allegation on 60 Minutes.\par \par \uc2 FRANCES HAUGEN, FACEBOOK WHISTLEBLOWER: \uc2 Facebook\uc2  over and over again has shown it uses profit over safety.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Frances Haugen left \uc2 Facebook\uc2  in May, taking 10s of 1000s of documents that she says show Facebook`s dangerous disregard for safety, pointing to how they turned off internal settings meant to fight election misinformation before the January 6 Capital riot.\par \par \uc2 HAUGEN: As soon as the election was over, they turn them back off, or they change the settings back to what they were before to prioritize growth over safety. And that really feels like a betrayal of democracy to me.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: \uc2 Facebook\uc2  acknowledges it rolled back some emergency measures after the 2020 election but kept some in place. Its head of global safety talking exclusively with NBC News.\par \par \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , she says values profits over safety. Is she right?\par \par \uc2 ANTIGONE DAVIS, GLOBAL HEAD OF SAFETY, \uc2 FACEBOOK\uc2 : That is absolutely contrary to my experience. It`s also contrary to the facts. I mean since 2016, we`ve spent $13 billion on safety and security. We have 40,000 people working on safety and security\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Haugen will testify against \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and a Senate hearing tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER, CO-FOUNDER, RECODE: There`s so many different laws that needs to be passed. And I`m not even blaming \uc2 Facebook\uc2  because regulators have sat on their hands and done nothing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Hallie, circling back to this outage now. Put it into context for us.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON (on camera): Lester, it seems to be one of the longest ones \uc2 Facebook\uc2  has. Had back in 2008, it went down for about a day. But remember at the time, the company only had 80 million users and that was before \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and WhatsApp even existed. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Hallie Jackson tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Southern California, an unfolding environmental disaster tonight. 126,000 gallons of crude oil leaking from an offshore pipeline. Beaches and harbors are closed along a 25 mile stretch of Orange County oceanfront. Miguel Almaguer is there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): This still- unfolding disaster has tonight left the waters of Southern California coated in oil and some of its most pristine beaches covered in crude. Categorized as a major spill, up to 126,000 gallons of oil could have poured into coastal waters, but still no exact source.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:07]\par \par \uc2 MARTYN WILLSHER\uc2 , PRESIDENT AND CEO, \uc2 AMPLIFY ENERGY CORP\uc2 : We have examined more than 8000 feet of pipe. And we have isolated one specific area of significant interest.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: With countless birds, fish, and protected wetlands under imminent threat, the massive spill was first acknowledged Saturday, but some of the areas smelled fuel Friday.\par \par \uc2 TK BREMER, FROGHOUSE SURF SHOP: Someone had opened that front door up, and man, a whiff of oil came through that door.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Today, oil slick animals are slowly being rescued. But it could be weeks, even months before the impact to wildlife is known.\par \par \uc2 KATRINA FOLEY, ORANGE COUNTY SUPERVISOR: You have to pour our resources in to making sure that we mitigate the damage.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: With 25 miles of Southern California`s iconic coastline close to fishing, today, tarballs washed along shore in Huntington and Newport beaches. It`s believed the leak occurred in a pipeline connecting with drill platform to the shore. Divers are looking for the source of the rapture about four miles off coast under more than 80 feet of water.\par \par \uc2 With booms deployed to capture oil, Beta Offshore which runs the platform, model the worst-case scenario spill back in 2012. It mirrored a similar disaster to the very one unfolding today, highlighting the danger of offshore drilling.\par \par \uc2 MIYOKO SAKASHITA\uc2 , DIRECTOR AND SENIOR COUNSEL, CENTER FOR BIOLOGICAL DIVERSITY OCEANS: There are about 23 offshore drilling platforms off of California`s coast, and most of them were built in the 60s, 70s, and 80s. They`re all incredibly old and have outlived their intended lifespan. They`re really a ticking time bomb for accidents like this to happen.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tonight, the potentially deadly impact of a devastating spill that may soon bring even more oil washing ashore.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Miguel, I know we`re watching the initial response here. But is there any more information on a potential cause of a leak?\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (on camera): Well, Lester, still no definitive cause. Officials say they`re looking at the possibility that an anchor may have struck the pipeline. Meantime, here at the scene, you can see cleanup crews have been working here over the last 24 hours. They say this cleanup will continue for days, possibly even weeks. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Miguel Almaguer in Southern California, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn to the COVID situation now and new confusion over a CDC warning about safety at holiday gatherings. Officials now backtracking and the CDC taking down that warning. Our Stephanie Goss has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: With summer officially over, so may be the summer surge. COVID cases are down more than 30 percent in the last two weeks. In some hard-hit southern states like Florida and Louisiana, the numbers are falling even more dramatically, which is why Dr. Anthony Fauci`s hesitation this weekend when asked about holiday gatherings to created confusion.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER TO THE PRESIDENT: It`s just too soon to tell. We just got to concentrating on continuing to get those numbers down.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Social media buzzed. People accusing Dr. Fauci of being a real-life Grinch. Meanwhile, the CDC had this holiday guidance on its Web site, including an image of a virtual Thanksgiving. Today, the messaging changed. Dr. Fauci says his comments were misinterpreted.\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: I will be spending Christmas with my family. I encourage people, particularly the vaccinated people who are protected to have a good normal Christmas with your family.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: And the CDC has removed holiday guidance altogether from its Web site saying it is working on an update.\par \par \uc2 Does the government run the risk of erring so far on the side of being conservative that people just flat out get alienated from the message?\par \par \uc2 KAVITA PATEL\uc2 , NBC NEWS MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, we have already run the risk. I think the American public, including many health officials, are confused. We also need to give people confidence. I would tell you, the majority of situations almost all of them to a person are incredibly safe if you`re vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: It is that certainty that led New York City to issue a vaccine mandate in schools which went into effect today. The city says five percent of its permanent staff remains unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 MEISHA PORTER, CHANCELLOR, NEW YORK CITY DEPARTMENT OF EDUCATION: Get your first dose today. You are more than welcome to come back to work. Our students need you. Our city needs you.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: But no vaccine means no job. One message not open to misinterpretation. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: There were some frightening moments on an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  train in Arizona today. Surveillance video shows what appears to be a gunman firing from the door of this train. Authorities say a DEA agent was shot and killed and two other officers wounded on the Sunset Limited in Tucson. When officers detained a suspect on the train, a second shooting -- suspect began shooting. The gunman was later found dead on the train. \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  says none of the passengers or crew members were hurt.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, yet another looming showdown in Washington, this went over how much the government can borrow to pay its bills. Plus, a stunning moment posted online. Activists confronting a senator even after she went into a bathroom stall. Kelly O`Donnell is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:01]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Tonight, President Biden warns that economic calamity could strike within two weeks if Congress does not raise the debt ceiling which allows the government to borrow money and pay past debts and meet current obligations like social security and military pay. Blaming Republicans who refuse to help Democrats to pass a debt limit increase.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The need to stop playing Russian roulette with the U.S. economy. If you don`t want to help save the country, get out of the way.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Republican Leader Mitch McConnell says Democrats must act alone.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2  (R-KY): This unified Democratic government is having trouble governing.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The President still struggling to convince Democrats to support his multi-trillion-dollar spending plans. Progressives refused to pass a bipartisan infrastructure bill until a much larger climate and social policy bill is ready.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Look, I need 50 votes in the Senate. I have 48.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: One of those moderate senators still not on board, Arizona`s Kyrsten Sinema, who was followed this weekend by progressive activists, from a classroom where she teachers, into a restroom where they continue to confront her and record her even if she went into a bathroom stall.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The Build Back Better plan needs -- has the solutions that we need.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Sinema responded that those protesters violated school security and students privacy, writing, "This is wholly inappropriate." Asked about protesters conduct, the president said --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I don`t think they`re appropriate tactics. It happens to everybody.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Today, the President held a video conference with House Progressives trying to convince that influential group to compromise on a lower total price tag to get it done. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kelly, thank you. In just 60 seconds, from abortion rights to gun control, high stakes as the Supreme Court begins its new term. And world leaders hiding millions, some stunning new allegations about the secret financial lives of the powerful.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: On his first Monday in October, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  started its new term, one that will be dominated by abortion, gun rights, and religion. And it began with one absence on the bench. Here`s Pete Williams.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): In a dramatic show of what`s at stake, demonstrators took to the streets nationwide over the weekend, most of them urging the Supreme Court and Congress to protect existing abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 CAROL FREEMAN, ABORTION RIGHTS DEMONSTRATOR: And that it`s not anybody else`s business what we do with our bodies and our reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The court will take up a Mississippi law that would ban virtually all abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy. The state says the landmark Roe v Wade decision should be overruled as out of date. And the new Texas law shutting down abortions after just six weeks is bound to the back too.\par \par \uc2 The court will also decide whether the Second Amendment provides a right to carry a gun outside the house, and whether states can block giving public tuition money to schools offering religious education.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: God save the United States and this honorable court.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The justices were back in their own courtroom today for the first time since the pandemic forced them to work from home 18 months ago. But seat was empty. Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  was absent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS (on camera): He`s listening to this week`s cases remotely after testing positive for COVID four days ago. He`ll likely be back next week. All the justices are fully vaccinated. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Pete Williams, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, to the explosive report from 150 news organizations based on millions of documents claiming world leaders including \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  are hiding their wealth in offshore accounts. Andrea Mitchell is tracking this for us tonight from Paris.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): In a leak of nearly 12 million confidential documents called the Pandora Papers, analyzed by 150 news organizations including the Washington Post, heads of state and other major figures have deposited vast assets offshore to hide billions from tax authorities. NBC News has not been able to verify the documents.\par \par \uc2 Today the president responding.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:02]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You said that we could fight against corruption in the core of your national security policy. So, what is your reaction?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I`m looking to that right now?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Why should people care about this trove of 12 million documents?\par \par \uc2 GREG MILLER, INVESTIGATIVE FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT, THE WASHINGTON POST: It means that the wealthy elite and those who can take advantage of this are paying less in taxes if they`re paying any taxes at all.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: According to documents reviewed by The Post, an alleged former mistress of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  became the owner of a luxury apartment in Monaco, financed by Putin`s inner circle of oligarchs and loyal banks. The Kremlin tonight brushing off the claims.\par \par \uc2 JUAN ZARATE, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST: So, the big concern for Americans should be, do we know who owns assets in the United States and what are they doing with that control?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: The paper is also revealing Jordan`s King Abdullah spent more than $106 million on luxury properties in Malibu, California, Washington D.C., and other countries. The purchases are legal but embarrassing given Jordan`s economic crisis. A Jordanian official tells NBC News the king is spending his own money, not public funds, or the millions his country received in \uc2 USAID\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I asked Secretary of State Blinken about it tonight as he arrived in Paris.\par \par \uc2 Do you have any reaction to the Pandora Papers, sir?\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Welcome to Paris. Nice to see you here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL (on camera): And it`s not only world leaders. Elton John, Ringo Starr, and Shakira are also named in the documents. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Andrew Mitchell in Paris this evening, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, inaction on the President`s agenda and the potential ripple effect for millions of families. What you should know.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: History has just been made in NASCAR. Bubba Wallace is now the first Black driver to win an elite NASCAR Cup Series race since Wendell Scott nearly 60 years ago. Wallace had just driven through a crash at the Talladega Speedway and was in the lead when rain stopped the race giving him the victory.\par \par \uc2 And more now in the showdown in Washington over President Biden`s spending plans hanging in the balance, the expanded child tax credit that millions of families have benefited from this year. Stephanie Ruhle on the fight to extend it in our series The Great Divide.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): For Jennifer Dickman in North Carolina, it`s a life-changing $500 a month.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Child Tax Credit money has really helped us with our medical expenses. I have two special needs kids.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The expanded payments that began in July as part of the American rescue plan impacted tens of millions of families immediately.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If my kids need this to school, I got it. If -- and he gets to take them to here and there, I got it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That went to an air conditioner which was so helpful. I was worried about my son becoming dehydrated.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I bought some school supplies. I bought (INAUDIBLE) and wipes for my daughters.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But the payments expire at the end of this year unless Congress passes some version of the human infrastructure bill that is central to President Biden`s economic agenda. Jhumpa Bhattacharya at the left-leaning research center Insight says it`s needed more than ever.\par \par \uc2 JHUMPA BHATTACHARYA, VICE PRESIDENT OF PROGRAMS AND STRATEGY, INSIGHT: What it will do is that it will help families who have -- were already struggling economically before the pandemic. It will help them plug some holes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: At a time when the pandemic has exacerbated financial hardship for millions. But the 10 richest Americans are worth more than a combined $1 trillion. President Biden wants a massive increase of the social safety net.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are negotiating over plans to extend the child tax credit, create free universal preschool, subsidized childcare costs for young kids, and expand Medicare to cover dental, hearing, and vision.\par \par \uc2 BHATTACHARYA: I think what this bill is doing is it`s showing that the government doesn`t just care about the wealthy, it cares about the average American.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Democrats say the money for these programs would come in part from raising income and capital gains taxes on the wealthy and increasing taxes on corporations. Congressional Republicans stand opposed.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2  (R-KY): Wasting trillions and trillions of dollars on socialism would be a bad idea any day. But it`s a uniquely bad idea at a time when American families are already being hammered by inflation and soaring costs.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But some parents who have seen the benefits of the child tax credit already can`t imagine a world without it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The child tax credit puts food on the table. And that`s just an incredible thing. And how can we take that away?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Stephanie Ruhle, NBC News New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Up next, we`ll tell you about one dad`s coast-to-coast mission to push for justice.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: It began in July. One man running and biking from Washington State to Washington D.C., all to call attention to some Americans who often feel forgotten. Here`s Antonia Hylton.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Duane Garvais Lawrence is on a mission in search of justice for missing and murdered indigenous women.\par \par \uc2 DUANE GARVAIS LAWRENCE, FORMER TRIBAL LAW ENFORCEMENT OFFICER: The percentage of women, you know, missing was unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Biking and running across the country finally making it to Washington D.C. last week to meet with some of the nation`s lawmakers, to discuss dedicating more resources to finding missing native women. More than four out of five Indigenous women experienced violence in their lifetimes and are murdered at rates more than 10 times the national average.\par \par \uc2 Is this kind of journey what it takes to get attention on your cases?\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: If that`s what it takes, then that`s what we`re doing.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Duane, a father of five, met with families of the missing along the way. Paula Castro`s 14-year-old daughter Henny went missing from the Northern Cheyenne reservation in 2018 after playing basketball with friends. How do you feel law enforcement handled your case?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I don`t think they did. That`s how I feel. We would keep calling them, keep calling them, we filled out missing person report there. I come to find out that it was just sitting on a desk.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: The family organized their own volunteer search party. Two weeks later, Henny`s body was found with injuries to her face. Still, her death was ruled accidental. The lack of attention has left Henny`s family heartbroken.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Like, she wasn`t important enough. That`s how it made me feel.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: In D.C., Duane`s wife and daughters traveled to join him.\par \par \uc2 What needs to change now?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We need more detectives. We need people to come up with programs and plans.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: On Friday, Duane concluded his cross-country journey by running three miles around the National Mall, celebrating with drummers, dancers, and tribal leaders. For his family, this moment represents a turning point.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: I believe that. I can feel it. I really can.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: A prayer that missing and murdered indigenous people`s stories no longer go ignored. Antonia Hylton, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And that`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Monday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020211005eha400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Starts One Of Its Most Consequential Terms\b0\par\par\uc2 Kristen Welker, Pete Williams \par \uc2 517 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER: The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  begins its new fall term tomorrow and it is shaping up to be a blockbuster on some of our country`s most divisive issues, including the future of abortion, gun rights and religion.\par \par \uc2 Justice Correspondent Pete Williams has a preview.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (voice over): It`s likely to be the most important term for abortion rights in at least three decades. The court will take up a Mississippi law that would ban virtually all abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 JOEY FILLINGANE (R) MISSISSIPPI STATE SENATOR: After this time the unborn child can feel pain, we ought to offer an extra level of protection from the abortion.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But the Supreme Court has consistently ruled beginning with Roe v. Wade that states cannot ban abortion before around 24 weeks. Mississippi urges the court to take a bold step and overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 NANCY NORTHUP\uc2 , CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS CEO: If Roe is reversed, probably almost half the states in the United States would strictly limit abortion and perhaps ban it all together.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And the new Texas law shutting down abortions after just six weeks is bound to be back too. The Supreme Court allowed it to take effect a month ago.\par \par \uc2 The justices will decide whether local laws that require showing a special need to get a permit to carry a concealed weapon violate the constitutional right to keep and bear arms.\par \par \uc2 TOM KING, NEW YORK RIFLE & PISTOL ASSOCIATION PRESIDENT: This is contrary to the Second Amendment. I mean, does your right to self-protection stop when you leave your home?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: A case from Maine invites the court to rule on another contentious issue, does using public money to send children to schools offering religious education violate the separation of church and state?\par \par \uc2 And the court might take up the affirmative action controversy over considering an applicant`s race as a factor in college admissions.\par \par \uc2 TOM GOLDSTEIN, SCOTUSBLOG CO-FOUNDER AND PUBLISHER: I think we`re going to look back at this as the year in which the conservatives really did fully take over the Supreme Court and American constitutional law, where they got what they were really looking for on the big, hot button issues that affect all of Americans` lives.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS (on camera): The end of the term will bring more speculation about whether Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  who turns 84 in August intends to retire while Democrats hold both the White House and the Senate. Kristen?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Pete Williams outside of the high court. Pete, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020211004eha30005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A New Round Of Women`s Marches Across The Country\b0\par\par\uc2 Jose Diaz-Balart, Emilie Ikeda \par \uc2 437 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART: Today, tens of thousands turned out at women`s marches across the country to protest strict new abortion laws. Emilie Ikeda has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Coast to coast, an army of women --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We believe in women.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: -- defending reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I believe that we will win.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Hundreds of gatherings under way in communities across the country.\par \par \uc2 Why was it important to you to be part of this effort today?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think not all voices are heard, and the voices of women are oppressed.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: A women`s march on this scale, the largest since 2017, the day after President Trump was inaugurated.\par \par \uc2 Texas` new restrictive abortion law powering the steps of hundreds here in L.A. and thousands across the country.\par \par \uc2 Now in effect for a month, it bans abortions once a fetal heartbeat is detected, as early as the sixth week of pregnancy, before many women even know they`re pregnant. And it makes no exceptions for rape or incest. Americans divided on the issue. In D.C., a smaller gathering outside of the Supreme Court today, anti-abortion rights advocates praying for the unborn.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Today we come before you almighty father --\par \par \uc2 [18:35:02]\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Congressman Kat Cammack defending Texas` law on Capitol Hill this week.\par \par \uc2 REP. KAT CAMMACK (R-FL): I would not be here had it not been for the very brave choice that my mother made 33 years ago.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: But opponents say that was her mom`s personal decision, and all women deserve the choice.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No one, specifically the government, has a right to step in and say, hey, you know, we`re going to take that away from you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Emilie now joins us from Los Angeles. Emilie, that Texas law is now in front of a federal judge.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (on camera): That`s right. A federal judge will weigh in on Texas` new abortion law, though it`s not clear when. Jose?\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Emilie Ikeda in Los Angeles. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020211003eha20002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Kristen Welker Talks About Motherhood And Returning To Work\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Savannah Guthrie, Kristen Welker \par \uc2 3,609 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [08:12:06] HODA KOTB: We are back. It`s a special day around here.\par \par \uc2 Kristen Welker, our Weekend TODAY co-anchor, and chief White House correspondent and great friend is ready to come back to work after her maternity leave.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: Yeah, Kristen and her husband John have spent a few months bonding with their baby girl Margot Lane Welker Hughes. We`re going to chat --\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Cutie.\par \par \uc2 But, first, their inspiring journey to parenthood.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DALY: We`re thrilled to announce that Margot Lane Welker Hughes was born Saturday morning via surrogate.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It was the news we`ve all been waiting for.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: The moment I looked at her, we were so deeply in love and she looked back at me and it was just the most incredible moment of my life.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s been nearly four months of sleepless nights, too many diapers to count and endless joy, moments Kristen and her husband John Hughes do not take for granted.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: John and I have struggled with infertility for years and we`re so excited to be able to announce that we, with the help of a surrogate, are expecting a baby girl.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh! Kristen. Wow.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Back in April here on TODAY, Kristen and John shared their excitement and also the heartache they faced on their path to parenthood.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: After two years of trying to carry my own baby and after having consulted with four different doctors, they all agreed that I wasn`t going to be able to carry a child. It was probably the lowest and realization I`ve ever had.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: They considered a number of options and ultimately decided to move forward with the help of a surrogate.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HUGHES, WELKER`S HUSBAND: Our surrogate has been amazing. For her this was about giving something to us that she felt a deep connection to.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Last fall, Kristen and John learned that their baby girl was on her way.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Sweet little girl.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Since her birth in June, baby Margot has spent a lot of quality time with her parents sharing new adventures, many milestones and lots of love and laughter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We can`t take it, Kristen, John and, most of all, Baby Margot. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Hey, guys.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Good morning! It`s so great to see you guys. Thank you for having us. We`re excited to be here.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We just want to say ah!\par \par \uc2 How are you doing, guys?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: We are doing great. We`re doing so great. The last few months have really been I think the best of our whole lives and we are just so in love with Margot. She is the love of our lives. Every day is a new adventure in watching the world through her eyes is the greatest gift and joy and blessing.\par \par \uc2 [08:15:03]\par \par \uc2 So we`re just doing so great.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You`ve imagined motherhood for a long time and dreamt about it and now it`s here. How do your dreams compare with what it`s really like?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: You know, Hoda, the funny thing is, I spent so many years thinking about, dreaming about motherhood, struggling, fighting for this moment, and motherhood was even more amazing and is even more amazing than I could have ever imagined.\par \par \uc2 I think one of the things that just blows my mind every day is that I literally miss her when I`m not in the same room with her. And so that applies to those late nights and when I do finally get her to sleep and everything is peaceful, I go in just to take a peek at her and just to visit with her a little bit longer. John says don`t wake her up. Whatever you do, don`t wake her up.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Please.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But it`s the most amazing, wonderful experience.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Hey, John, first time dad. What`s it been like -- what`s it been like for you so far?\par \par \uc2 HUGHES: Well, it`s been really wonderful experience. You know it`s completely changed everything. All the routines are out the window, but at the center of our lives now we have this bubbly, talkative, you know, really spunky little bundle of joy so we couldn`t be happier. She`s been a true blessing.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: She really has been. Every time John walks into the room she lights up and starts giggling. They already have that sweet little daddy/daughter relationship.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS WEATHER ANCHOR: Girl dad.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Watching John become a dad is wonderful.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I was going to say, sorry to cut in, I was thinking about your mom, Kristen. This is the first grandchild on both sides of the family. So how has that been?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Oh, Savannah, it has been the most beautiful part of this experience to watch our parents become grandparents and Margot is literally showered with love and joy and just gifts all of the time. I think that what`s so beautiful about it is that this is their dream too and she is a product of them and so getting to share this special joy with them, all of the joy has been one of the most special parts of this entire experience.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I think what`s another amazing part is you were in the delivery room. You were there the moment she came into this world. You held her in that moment. What was that like for you?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, you know what`s interesting is in the weeks leading up to her birth, and, of course, I had been so excited for so long, I started to feel really nervous because I hadn`t carried her and I had this self-doubt that maybe I was missing a crucial piece of information or experience that I would need to be a good mom to her. And I just didn`t want to let her down ever at any moment ever in her life. So I was worried. Then we got to the hospital and the doctor and the midwife said you can help catch her when she comes out.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And I was nervous for that too. But I was able to help catch her and to be literally like the first set of hands to welcome her into this world was the most incredible experience I`ve ever had and we were bonded from that moment forever. And all of that self-doubt went away.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Time is up.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: It`s really a blessing. It is a blessing.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I think the baby is ready.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: I think Margot has decided we`re done.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Margot not the only one crying. I`m crying too.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Kristen and John, I want to say notice at the beginning of the piece. we made the announcement at the top of "POPStart" about the beautiful birth. So, as milestones are occurring, I`ll give you the "POPStart" hotline, just give me a call and we`ll make sure to audience updated.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Oh, Carson, we will. And we love that you made that announcement. You did it with so much love. You guys have just been so loving and supportive the entire time and we`re so, so grateful.\par \par \uc2 And I should say we`re so grateful to our surrogate too. I think that in that delivery room, John and I were just filled with so much gratitude. We have a new sense of what that word means and at one moment during the delivery I collapsed on to my surrogate and hugged her, thanked her.\par \par \uc2 We continue to thank her all of the time. I can`t wait until the day when I can share with Margot her incredible journey to this earth and when she can meet her surrogate and actually know all of the love and strength and community it took to bring her here.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Kristen Welker your blow me away.\par \par \uc2 [08:20:00]\par \par \uc2 Margot, you have the perfect role model for life, my little friend. John, thank you so much. We`re just delighted for you. Really delighted for us because we get to see you tomorrow on Weekend TODAY and we really missed you.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Welcome back, welcome back.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I can`t wait. Missed you guys too. Love you. So excited for tomorrow and to be reunited with Peter. It will be so fun.\par \par \uc2 DALY: See you in the morning.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Bye, guys.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: You know, she`s gotten accustomed to getting up early.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yeah, that`s right. She`s ready.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: How about another check of the weather?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: All right. Let`s look ahead, show you what we`ve got for today.\par \par \uc2 We are watching a little bit of wet weather coming up out of the gulf pushing in part of a front from Minneapolis to Houston. Temperatures are going to be fairly calm today. 90s in parts of Florida, the southwest, otherwise we`ve got conditions to fall like conditions into northeast New England. Another warm day around the great lakes. Abundant sunshine around the southwest. Sunshine through Texas. Eastern third of the country spectacular.\par \par \uc2 That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK BELL, STORM TEAM4 METEOROLOGIST: Good morning. I`m Storm Team 4 meteorologist Chuck Bell. A little chill in the air this morning but temperatures will rise quickly currency of a mostly sunny sky that we get to enjoy all afternoon. Today`s high 72, that`s two degrees cooler than average but the cool air won`t hang around for long. Back to 78 degrees coming up on Saturday afternoon and 84 on Sunday. Although Sunday will turn cloudy and rain chances return on Monday and hang around for a big part of next week. So make the most of the sunshine while we have it.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That`s your latest weather. Nothing better than a Friday edition of -- POPStart!\par \par \uc2 DALY: Go ahead. Okay. We`re just hearing word now that beautiful baby Margot Lane Welker Hughes has just pooped. Thank you very much. That`s your update. She made a duty.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Now it`s your duty to continue.\par \par \uc2 DALY: And John is on it. There you go.\par \par \uc2 DALY: First up, Super Bowl LVI, sure football season may have started, sure, the Raiders are 3-0 but we`re looking ahead to the Super Bowl in Los Angeles the halftime show awesome in February. The big game is here on NBC and we know the amazing halftime lineup of performers.\par \par \uc2 They are Snoop Dogg, Mary J. Blige, Dr. Dre, Eminem and Kendrick Lamar. They were all be headlining the Pepsi Super Bowl halftime show in SoFi Stadium in Los Angeles. Snoop and Dre sharing why they decided to say yes to the show.\par \par \uc2 (B EGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. DRE, RAPPER: One of the main reasons I decided to say I`m going to do the show the \uc2 NFL\uc2  and Pepsi decided to donate money to the schools we`re trying to build. Starting here in Los Angeles and we`re going to be able to build ten high schools across the United States, only in urban areas.\par \par \uc2 I just want to give back to kids that resemble me and didn`t have the opportunity as I didn`t have.\par \par \uc2 SNOOP DOGG, RAPPER: They`re also building things that`s affordable in the community for people to stay here. They really do what they --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Local guys, right, Snoop and Dre and Kendrick from L.A., Mary J. from the Bronx and Eminem from Detroit. But that`s going to be a real local feel. A good show.\par \par \uc2 Lot of bleeping. Lot of bleeping. I don`t know how many lyrics we`re going to get out.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DALY: Going to be on the but than night.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: I think they know their audience.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You know all the words.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Collectively, these five artists have 45 Grammys all together and the Super Bowl halftime show will be the first time they perform together on stage. It`s going to be great.\par \par \uc2 All right. Next up on POPStart, Mick Jagger. As the Rolling Stones continue their cross-country no filter tour they`re taking time to enjoy the local hot spots of cities they`re playing in. Mick posting this photo on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  writing out and about last night in charlotte. Probably thinking he`s there at this bar surrounded by security guards and the guards keeping back fans that want to take selfies. But no. Looks like no one noticed that Mick Jagger the legend was standing there. Fellow bar patrons oblivious to the rolling stones front man enjoying a beer at the counter.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, fans online couldn`t believe it. A couple of comments, imagine running into Mick Jagger at a dive bar. Looks like you`re just waiting on a friend.\par \par \uc2 So the moral of the story is that the Stones are playing in a town near you, keep your eyes peeled at your local dives. They could be there.\par \par \uc2 Next up, Harry Styles out on the road for the love on tour concert series. Recent stop in Nashville spotted and shared a little love to an expecting mom-to-be by announcing the baby`s gender from the stage. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGI VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRY STYLES, MUSICIAN: Little baby girl. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DALY: Wow.\par \par \uc2 Kind of hard to notice they lit up the arena in pink to celebrate the baby girl. Apparently harry spotted a sign in the crowd saying I`m having a baby make it your business, so we decided to help out and made it his business and made the announcement.\par \par \uc2 Finally, Colin Jost. The "SNL" star stopped by Seth Meyers show last night and shared some of the interesting reactions that he`s received since announcing he and his wife Scarlet Johansson named their son Cosmo, particularly how the name seemed to have confused his mom.\par \par \uc2 [08:25:08]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SETH MEYERS, COMEDIAN: It is a unique name. How did your family react?\par \par \uc2 COLIN JOST, SNL STAR: My family is very supportive always. My mom I would say was slightly thrown by it, and didn`t quite understand it. I don`t know if she thought it was kind of like a hippie thing. She would call us and say, you know, like after three or four days, like, Cosmo? She`s like -- and now is it final? Like did you submit the birth certificate. We`re like oh, yeah, we did that at the hospital.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: That`s mom speak for I don`t like it.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: A Seinfeld reference.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That`s Cosmo Cramer.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Right.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Coming up, guys, we got some more breaking news?\par \par \uc2 DALY: Baby Margot is clean now.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Okay, excellent.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well done, John.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Get some sleep.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We are painting the town pink and it goes beyond our plaza for an incredible cause -- first day of Breast Cancer Awareness Month.\par \par \uc2 But, first, your local news.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is a News4 Today Newsbreak.\par \par \uc2 EUN YANG, NEWS4 TODAY ANCHOR: Eight-twenty-six is your time on this Friday, October 1st, 2021. Good morning to you. I`m Eun Yang.\par \par \uc2 Right now, police are searching for a man wanted in three murders. Jeffrey Burham is accused of killing an 83-year-old woman in Cumberland, Maryland, Wednesday night and police say he shot his brother and another family member in Ellicott City. Police say he could now be driving a stolen red Corvette with a black top.\par \par \uc2 Well, anyone working for the city including health care workers need to be at least partially vaccinated against COVID-19 by today. If not they risk losing their job and medical license. The D.C. hospital association says it will start taking action today against employees who are not vaccinated. We also expect to learn how many licensed D.C. health care workers are not in compliance with the vaccine requirement.\par \par \uc2 We`ll take a break now and we`ll check your forecast next. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BELL: Finally, Friday, and the sun is shining. Everything is coming up roses for you on your forecast today.\par \par \uc2 Seventy-two for a high temperature this afternoon. If you need it warmer than that don`t worry, 78 tomorrow. Again with plenty of sunshine on your Saturday. Clouds come back on Sunday but it will be a warm one with Sunday`s high up to 84.\par \par \uc2 YANG: All right. Chuck, thank you. Get the latest news and weather any time in the NBC Washington app. Have a great day.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:30:23]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Welcome back, guys. It`s 8:30. It`s a beautiful Friday morning, very first day of October. That means, guys, it is the start of Breast Cancer Awareness Month. We have an awesome half hour ahead, and it`s going to get even better because Jenna Bush Hager is here.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Guys, not only are we going to paint this closet pink, we`re doing it in cities all over the country with the help of our NBC stations. We`ve got WCNC in Charlotte.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: WKYC in Cleveland, NBC4 Washington.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JENNA BUSH HAGER, NBC NEWS HOST: OK. They`ve been sharing stories of their heroes in their areas, making a difference in the fight against breast cancer. And coming up, we`re going to introduce you to all of them.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: That`s going to be fine.\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Looking forward to meeting them. Also ahead, we`re going to check in with Sheinelle. She is up at Gillette Stadium where fans of the Patriots are getting ready for Tom Brady`s return. Also a world class chef is about the layout a very fitting menu for you to enjoy if you