d. He doesn`t have Buccaneers. He`s got a big head.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh god.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Stop, stop.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: What?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Save us. Save us.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Just ahead, guys, we`re going to kick off the pink power and Breast Cancer Awareness Month with a celebration of people touched by the disease who turned their pain into purpose.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We are honored and privileged to introduce you to some local heroes from all across the country. And we`re going to paint their towns pink too, but first, this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020211003eha10005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The CDC`s New Health Advisory Urging Pregnant Women To Get Vaccinated\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Gabe Gutierrez \par \uc2 614 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: As the COVID vaccination rate creeps upward, about 56 percent now fully vaccinated, the CDC is out tonight with its strongest warning yet about pregnancy and COVID. It significantly raises the risk of death for the unvaccinated. Here`s Gabe Gutierrez.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:11]\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Tonight, a new urgent CDC health alert for pregnant women urging them to get a COVID vaccine. New data show that symptomatic pregnant women have more than a twofold increased risk of requiring ICU admission and a 70 percent increased risk of death.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER TO THE PRESIDENT: The impact of the COVID-19 disease on pregnancy can be profound in a negative way. On the woman who`s pregnant herself is at high risk for a severe outcome for COVID for her. The negative impact on the fetus is also very clear right now.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Only around 1 in 3 pregnant women nationwide are vaccinated right now. Across the country, public health officials are stepping up their push and in some cases requirements to force people to get the shot. Mark Bishofsky is a respiratory therapist in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 MARK BISHOFSKY, RESPIRATORY THERAPIST: We had signs outside of our hospital I was a hero. Right now I feel expendable.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: He`s now of more of more than 180 health care workers in the state suing their employers to stop vaccine mandates.\par \par \uc2 GREGORY ERICKSON, ATTORNEY FOR MARK BISHOFSKY: To me, it`s really irresponsible to put this mandate in place without accommodations for the people that have already had COVID and for the folks that have religious exemptions.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Today, \uc2 YouTube\uc2  said it was banning all anti-vaccine misinformation from its platform, reigniting a heated debate over free speech, censorship, and public safety as more companies hit vaccination deadlines.\par \par \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  says it`s firing nearly 600 employees for refusing to get vaccinated. The pilot`s union is furious.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL KLEMM, DISTRICT 141 PRESIDENT, INTERNATIONAL ASSOCIATION OF MACHINIST AND AEROSPACE WORKERS: It is just a matter of voice. I`m vaccinated. I believe in the vaccine. But I also believe in people`s opportunity to decide whether to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: United`s CEO says travel won`t be impacted.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT KIRBY\uc2 , CEO, UNITED AIRLINES: It`s such a small percentage and we planned and prepared for this.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: But in Upstate New York where a vaccine mandate for health work workers took effect this week, some doctors are already sounding the alarm about a staffing shortage.\par \par \uc2 DR. DENNIS MCKENNA, PRESIDENT AND CEO, ALBANY MEDICAL CENTER: It is absolutely a staffing crisis. There is no question about it. It`s real.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: But, Gabe, it is worth noting here the number of health care workers vaccinated in New York has gone up sharply in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (on camera): Yes, Lester. The governor says more than 92 percent of health care workers in the state have gotten at least one dose, proof she argued, that the mandate is working. But critics say thousands of employees are now out of a job at the worst time. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Gabe Gutierrez, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020210930eh9t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Democratic Progressives, Moderates Battle Over Spending, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Eddie Glaude, Jr. \par \uc2 9,682 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 September 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [10:30:10]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST (voice-over): This Sunday, the Biden agenda at risk.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS\uc2  (I-VT): We`ve got 50 people. We can`t lose one.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Progressive Democrats holding firm on spending bills.\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL\uc2  (D-WA), CHAIR, PROGRESSIVE CAUCUS: We`re not leaving one part of the agenda behind and giving up on the idea of child care, paid leave, community college, health care.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Moderate Democrats hoping to trim the cost.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM KAINE (D-VA): I understand people are concerned about the top line dollar amount.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And Republicans vowing to stand in the way.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN BARRASSO\uc2  (R-WY): This is government-run socialism. Every one of us is opposed to it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Will Democrats reach an agreement or fail and risk sinking the president`s agenda? My guest, Democratic Senator \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  of New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the border crisis.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`ve seen desperate looks of the people just trying to get processed. I mean, it`s a completely dire situation.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That encampment of migrant Haitians finally clear.\par \par \uc2 SEC. ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, DEPARTMENT OF HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: There are no longer any migrants in the camp underneath the Del Rio International Bridge.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But questions remain about where the migrants were sent, their treatment and how to secure the border. I`ll talk to Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas. Plus, the booster rollout.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROCHELLE WALENSKY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, U.S. CENTERS FOR DISEASE CONTROL AND PREVENTION: I must do what I can to preserve the health of across our nation.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Rochelle Walensky\uc2  overrules her own CDC advisory panel and recommends \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  boosters for workers at risk.\par \par \uc2 And new revelations about what President Trump was doing on January 6th and the efforts being made at the Trump White House at the time to overturn the results of the election. I`ll talk to the authors of the new book "Peril," Bob Woodward and Robert Costa.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joining me for insight and analysis are Amy Walter, editor in chief and publisher of "The Cook Political Report," Eddie Glaude Jr. of \uc2 Princeton University\uc2 , NBC News Capitol Hill correspondent Leigh Ann Caldwell and \uc2 Meghan McCain\uc2 , columnist for "TheDailyMail.com."\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s MEET THE PRESS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is MEET THE PRESS WITH CHUCK TODD.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Good Sunday morning. When Democrats won control of the White House, held the House and narrowly grabbed the Senate in that Georgia upset, they embraced some very large goals that carried some very large risks. President Biden proposed a vast and ambitious progressive agenda despite those narrow majorities in Congress, much of it packed into two bills, an infrastructure bill and a $3.5 trillion domestic spending bill.\par \par \uc2 But even as the president faces a series of challenges that have hurt his own poll numbers, Democrats are fighting amongst themselves. Progressives and moderates are arguing over the size and scope of the $3.5 trillion spending bill with each side threatening to sink it without compromises from the other.\par \par \uc2 John Podesta\uc2 , who worked for presidents Clinton and \uc2 Obama\uc2 , warned Democrats, if they failed to reach agreement -- quote -- "It would signal a complete and utter failure of our democratic duty and a reckless abdication of our responsibility." In order words, for Democrats, on this one, failure is not an option.\par \par \uc2 At stake, simply the agenda of the president, of their party who desperately needs a win. But Democrats have little margin for error in the House and no margin for error in the Senate and no clear path yet for making good on the president`s promises.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is a process, and it`s going to be up and down. That`s why I don`t look at the polls.\par \par \uc2 TODD (voice-over): President Biden who ran on bringing competency back to Washington is facing a string of setbacks, a chaotic withdrawal from Afghanistan, a migrant crisis at the border, the ongoing COVID pandemic and now a brewing fight on Capitol Hill between progressive and moderate Democrats over his domestic agenda. The president`s job approval rating is now under water in most polls.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Remember, I said it`s going to take a year deliver everything I`m looking at here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: On the border, the camp in Del Rio, Texas, that was once packed with 15,000 migrants, mostly Haitians, is now gone.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: There are no longer any migrants in the camp.\par \par \uc2 TODD: On Thursday, the U.S. special envoy to Haiti resigned in protest over the administration`s inhumane, counterproductive decision to deport thousands of Haitian migrants back to Haiti. And Democrats expressed horror over the images of agents on horseback chasing migrants and swinging leather reins.\par \par \uc2 REP. JOYCE BEATTY (D-OH): The nation has witnessed, over the last 48 hours, some of the most horrific things that we`ve seen.\par \par \uc2 REP. AYANNA PRESSLEY\uc2  (D-MA): This is abhorrent.\par \par \uc2 REP. ANDY LEVIN\uc2  (D-MI): It is outrageous.\par \par \uc2 REP. MAXINE WATERS\uc2  (D-CA): I`m pissed.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Of course I take responsibility. I`m president. To see people treated like they did, horses nearly running them over and people being strapped. It`s outrageous. I promise you, those people will pay.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The president also faces the threat of a shutdown. Republicans are refusing to help keep the government open and solvent threatening legislative sabotage as a political tactic.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2  (R-KY), MINORITY LEADER: The debt ceiling will be raised as it always should be, but it will be raised by the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And Mr. Biden is attempting to negotiate a political truce between progressive and centrist Democrats at odds over the size of his domestic spending package and the timing of a vote on a bipartisan infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 [10:35:09]\par \par \uc2 House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is promising a vote this week.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: Have a little patience. Follow it. See it unfold. It`s very interesting. We`re very encouraged.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But progressives are threatening to withhold their support until they have reassurances on the size of the proposed $3.5 trillion domestic spending package.\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: The reality is the bill coming up and not having the votes is not something that the Speaker will be willing to do and we don`t have the votes for it.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): It`s still a shame, truly is a shame that they`re using that as a hostage towards this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Former Obama White House chief of staff \uc2 John Podesta\uc2  is calling on progressives to pare back their ambitions writing, "The political reality is clear. We will not secure the full $3.5 trillion investment." And Podesta is warning centrists, "You are either getting both bills or neither and the prospect of neither is unconscionable."\par \par \uc2 JOHN PODESTA\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: It`s politically catastrophic if we don`t move forward with these bills at this time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And joining me now is the Democrat senator from New Jersey, \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2 . Senator Booker, welcome back to MEET THE PRESS.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CORY BOOKER\uc2  (D-NJ): It`s really great to be back on.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s good to have you in person.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`re getting there. \uc2 John Podesta\uc2  earlier this week wrote a memo that said -- you know, it`s basically sending a memo to both sides of this argument, moderates and progressives like, look, you got to have to come together.\par \par \uc2 And to progressives he said, you`re going to have accept the reality that 3.5 trillion is going to -- get pared back.\par \par \uc2 Do you concur?\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Well, first of all, I don`t like how this has been characterized as progressives versus moderates. This is Joe Biden`s bill. And as he said to me in the Oval Office and a handful of others he goes, I`ve never really been a progressive. This is about seizing what is a once in a generation opportunity, like those who built the canals and the railroads, like those who built the incredible Eisenhower Highway Act. If we miss this opportunity every dollar that we shrink it is a dollar that we`re not investing in our future and it`s unfortunate.\par \par \uc2 So how this plays out, yes, this is Washington. I`m sure there`s going to be some kind of compromise. But to me, the compromise -- repairing our electrical grid or to compromise ending, being the only nation on the planet Earth, of industrial nations that doesn`t have paid family leave, that has its costs. And it`s going to make America lose the boldness of the possibility of jumping forward as a nation.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is there a sense here though that maybe -- you know, there`s an old saying that all of our parents used to say, "Your eyes are bigger than your stomach."\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Were the political eyes bigger than the political reality? Meaning, like, look, you`re only -- you can only do as much as the 50th senator.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: That`s -- that is -- that is 100 percent true. And, I think, a lot of times we`re losing sight of what we`ve already accomplished under this president. I was at the -- a person from the Black media who just said to me, "Oh, well, voting rights and police reform. What has he done for the black community?" I go, "What are you talking about?" I said, "We`ve cut Black poverty -- child poverty 50 percent. We are -- the first president stepping up on Black farmers, maternal mortality, getting lead pipes out of the ground."\par \par \uc2 Biden has racked up one of the greatest agendas of accomplishments. It`s unfortunate people don`t see that. And if we do a $3 trillion bill, a $2.5 trillion, I`m going to push for as big and bold as we can. But it will be a historic investment in America.\par \par \uc2 Unlike President Trump who racked up $8 trillion worth of debt. And where did it go? Mostly to the wealthiest in our nation.\par \par \uc2 This president, with the child tax credit, has given the biggest middle class tax cut in our country in generations. So I`m proud of what Biden is doing. And as we sat in the Oval Office we passed -- moderates were coming out, progressives were going in and he is really doing shuttle diplomacy trying to land this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, and I guess I get the question is, what`s with the -- it looks like distrust from my perspective, in that you wrote this letter along with 10 other senators it`s to reinforce this idea, "Hey, don`t bring up the bipartisan infrastructure bill in the House until the larger reconciliation bill is done."\par \par \uc2 Why can`t you trust centrists or moderates or however we want to classify the folks that they`ll be there? Why not pass this on Monday, put some points on the score board for President Biden which right now helps the whole party and trust that the moderates will be there for this?\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: I don`t think it`s a matter of trust. I think it`s a matter of I`ve been around here -- this town now for eight years, watching the best of intentions not manifest into something real.\par \par \uc2 We were told that this was -- we see this as one package. The president recent as last week has said, don`t separate this. This is one package that we`re looking at.\par \par \uc2 And so, I just want to make sure -- this is not about a bunch of people who are battling it out in Congress. This is about the American people. And to get half of this done and leave mothers who are looking for affordable child care, Americans who are looking for lower cost prescription drugs, to leave people like that on the wayside -- families that need that child tax credit, to leave those folks on the wayside is unacceptable to me. So I`ve seen the best of intentions. I want a more real guarantee.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, you just don`t believe -- words not enough?\par \par \uc2 [10:40:00]\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: No, again --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, that`s what it comes down to. Basically, saying that Joe Manchin`s word is not enough here.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Look, I have been looked in the eye by people in this town and they`ve meant it sincerely. There`s honor in them saying it. "Hey, Cory, we`re not going to get this in such and such a bill, but we`ll get to it."\par \par \uc2 Well, sometimes that "get to it" turns out to be a year, the next Congress, or what have you. I`m sorry, when you have the leverage, you use the leverage, as long as it`s not about ego or partisanship.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: For me, it`s about the communities in this country. And by the way, Independents, 60 -- 70 percent support what we`re trying to do. Republicans on some -- if you break out the policies that are in these bills --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I want to get to that --\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: -- support it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- breaking out the policies.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m having flashbacks to health care. I heard the same thing. Hey, the individual parts are really popular.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, they are. Should you be dealing with them in an individual piece here? I understand the situation of reconciliation, but you could be debating each part of this bill one at a time here, and you might actually get your message to the people that this isn`t just a money grab.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Yes. I tell you, I was a mayor during the health care debates. And it took, you know, years before suddenly the Affordable Care Act, call it Obamacare, I know a lot people on the right don`t like it. But now the Affordable Care Act polls above water for every group, Republicans, Independents and Democrats.\par \par \uc2 So again, there is movements and moments. This is a moment for America to go big. We didn`t tell FDR, "Hey, break it into constituent parts." We didn`t tell Eisenhower --\par \par \uc2 TODD: But he did do it in different parts though, that`s the difference, is that both LBJ --\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- and FDR did this in pieces.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: And they also --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now, they had bigger majorities.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: I was about to say --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: -- they had much bigger majorities.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. But they did it in pieces.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Yes. This is realpolitik. And the reality is right now, we will have very small opportunities. You know what it looks like for the next Congress to move this big. People elected the president of the United States to do bold, big things that will change America. This is our moment. We should seize it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. But during the presidential primaries, which you were a part of --\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- didn`t the Democratic voters essentially send the message, "Do what`s possible"? They didn`t pick the progressive candidate.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Yes. They picked a moderate guy who has a great track record for getting big things done in the Senate. And that`s why \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was joking with us in the White House. He`s like, this bill is not a progressive wish list. This is an American wish list.\par \par \uc2 I said bipartisanship, when you poll the parts with Republicans, what he is doing is wildly popular with the Republican Americans who want child care and affordable college and affordable daycare. They want to make sure that we`re meeting the challenge of climate change. So again, to characterize this as some -- remember, Bernie Sanders` first wish list was a $6 trillion package. This is significantly less than that.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing is in Washington math, you`ve got to get upset about this. Donald Trump`s tax cut was a $5 trillion bill. People called it a $2 trillion bill because $3 trillion of it was paid for. Paid for in the wrong way of getting rid of our state and local tax deduction. This one is a zero bill. We`re paying for the whole thing. But yet, we`re -- we`re using different math to make it sound bigger and more ornery.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I don`t want to let you go without asking about your take on why police reform didn`t come to fruition. Here`s what Tim Scott said. He said, "Democrats said no because they could not let go of their push to defund our law enforcement." Is that correct?\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: No. Again, why are we descending into partisan name calling or partisan corners? These are two guys that sat down, that worked very hard over nine months, got the FOP to endorse a compromise bill. And I never imagined we would get FOP leadership to come onboard. The \uc2 International Association of Chiefs of Police\uc2  who said, "Yes, our profession`s in trouble." The families were with what we were proposing. It didn`t work. We didn`t --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why do you think Senator Scott`s using this language though? Because you`ve been very diplomatic, he`s not being as diplomatic. Why?\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: You know, I love him. I send him grace all the time. He is a friend of mine. The reality is I`ve gotten big things done in this town in this space with \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . We got big things done to cut --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think McConnell didn`t want him to cut this deal? Do you think there were others above him that were basically, like, "Do the best you can, but"?\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: I promised the families we were going to get something done. I`m still at work. I`m not throwing -- I`m not throwing accusations at either side. I do think this town has got to learn, all of us, that to try to play the singular blame game, I accept responsibility. We didn`t get it done. I haven`t stopped working. We need to lift the frequency of this town, especially on issues like this, that all of America knows -- biggest demonstrations in this country`s history for change. They want change from the federal. And we didn`t do it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s quite notable though that you`re not taking -- going politics here. Senator Scott is. Just saying.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Again, I`ve learned a lot over the last eight years, and that`s not the way to get things done in the long run. And the families deserve -- we`re going to still see videotapes of unarmed African Americans being killed. We`ve had tragedies in this country we`ve all witnessed. We`ve got to start getting things done. And the wonderful thing about it is police leaders understand it as well. We`ve got to get back to the table eventually and get big things done.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2 , it`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 [10:45:01]\par \par \uc2 Thanks for coming in.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TODD: On Friday, Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas announced that all the Haitian migrants who had gathered under a bridge in Del Rio, Texas had been cleared from the area. More than 12,000 were released into the United States and allowed to request asylum. Mayorkas says 8,000 returned to Mexico voluntarily and, as of Friday, 2,000 were expelled to Haiti.\par \par \uc2 Still, the images of white Border Patrol agents appearing to manhandle Haitian migrants drew sharp criticism from Democrats and from President Biden. And Secretary Mayorkas joins me now. Mr. Secretary, welcome back to MEET THE PRESS.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Thanks for having me, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with the decision to send 2,000 Haitian migrants back to Haiti in these current circumstances. In hindsight, was that a mistake?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: No, it wasn`t, Chuck. That is the exercise of a public health imperative. We`re in the midst of a pandemic and the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control\uc2  has a Title 42 authority that we exercise to protect the migrants themselves, to protect the local communities, our personnel and the American public.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic is not behind us. Title 42 is a public health policy, not an immigration policy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But these folks didn`t come from Haiti. They came from some other places in South America. Should they have been sent somewhere else?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: So, we are working with other countries. Chile, Brazil, other countries in South America and the region. These are Haitian nationals. Some of them don`t have documents from the countries from which they just left.\par \par \uc2 So, they are subject to removal. They are subject to Title 42 expulsion and we work with the countries that receive them. In fact, in Haiti specifically, we`ve contributed about $5.5 million to ensure their safe and humane resettlement there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right, but they haven`t -- some of these folks haven`t been there in over five years.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: That is indeed the case. And they`re Haitian nationals, and they`re subject to removal to a country that is able to receive them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you about Title 42. Is that an authority you still want to have?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: That is an authority that the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control\uc2  has determined to still be necessary given where we are in the arc of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to ask you about the behavior of the CBP and Border Patrol. Clearly, a lot of Democrats were upset. The vice president did something that I had never seen before with a Cabinet member. They put out a -- they put out a -- essentially, a readout of a call that she had with you. And it seemed to say that -- the implication was that it was a dressing down of sorts. How did you respond to her about the performance of Border Patrol?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: So, the performance of Border Patrol is the subject of an investigation and the facts that are adduced in that investigation are going to be determinative. But I think the entire American public correctly and necessarily were horrified by what the images suggested. They conjured up such a dark past that we have not entirely put behind us.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Did President Biden go too far when he said, those folks are going to pay? Are they guilty before there`s an investigation?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Oh, absolutely not. The investigation is independent. We know how to conduct an independent investigation. I think the president echoed the sentiments of the public in terms of what those images suggest.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There is some Democrats in this town that think CBP is a rogue agency. What would you say to that? I`m sure you`ve heard that privately.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Oh, I don`t -- I couldn`t disagree more vehemently. I`m intensely and immensely proud of the men and women of the \uc2 U.S. Customs and Border Protection\uc2 . In fact, in Del Rio, Texas, I saw them act heroically. You know, what those images suggest does not reflect who CBP is, who we are as a department, nor who we are as a country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now, the Border Patrol union said that, you know, using horseback, that is -- that is a training that they get. Are they right?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Oh, the horse patrol serves an incredibly important function. Horses can traverse terrain that might not otherwise be covered. In fact, horses are often used to not only assess a situation, but to actually help people in distress. CBP personnel have saved many lives on horseback.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, those images you think are something that -- do you think those images misconstrued their actions? Do you think that it`s going to turn out their actions were actually on the up and up?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: The facts are going to be adduced in the investigation. The investigation will determine the facts. It`s an independent one run by the Office for Professional Responsibility. In fact, I directed that the leadership of that office direct the investigation to ensure its both integrity and quality.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You, I know, are getting a lot of heat from members of Congress on both sides of the aisle. I want to play for you a clip from Congressman \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , who`s a Democrat representing the border down there. Here`s what he said about the current situation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. HENRY CUELLAR\uc2  (D-TX): President \uc2 Obama\uc2  and Secretary Jeh Johnson, they treated the immigrants -- the migrants with respect and dignity, but at the same time they followed the law.\par \par \uc2 [10:50:02]\par \par \uc2 If they`re not supposed to be here, then, with all due respect, you`ve got to send them back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, you were a deputy in that administration, too, so you`ve been at this. What is the -- what is the same and what is different compared to the surges you dealt with at DHS in `14 and `15 versus what we`re seeing today?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: So, this is not new. Remember, in the summer of 2014, we had a great number of unaccompanied children arrive here. And there were people who called for us to do more than we did. And there were people who called for us to do things differently than we did.\par \par \uc2 Getting hit from both sides in the matter of immigration is no surprise. We are in the epicenter of the country`s divide, regrettably. And we do what the law requires, what our humanitarian mission requires.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But do we have enough resources down there to deal with the surge that`s currently happening? Because we thought this was seasonal. It turned out it`s not seasonal this year.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: So, we have an incredible ability to deploy resources to meet the challenges that we face. And we actually have evidence that, quite powerfully, in Del Rio, Texas, in less than a week we addressed a population of 15,000 there and now there are none.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you accept the idea that we have a crisis at the border?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: I am focused on mission, Chuck, and the challenges that we face and our ability to meet those challenges. And I am incredibly impressed with the men and women who do so.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think we have the ability to meet these challenges right now or does the law have to be changed?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Oh, I think --well, I think we meet the challenges, but we are working in a completely broken system. And as to that, there is unanimity in Washington and in the country. I`ve never heard anyone say that the laws in immigration are well-structured.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If we get more Haitian migrants, is the only solution going to be that they`re likely to be deported back to Haiti?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: No, as you mentioned at the very outset, Chuck, there are a number of them that are expelled under the Title 42 authority, the public health authority, and then there are others that are placed in immigration court proceedings and have the ability to make their claims to an immigration judge.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you at all concerned this will serve as a magnet for more migrants to come?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Oh, I don`t think that is the case at all. In fact, 8,000 Haitians returned to Mexico over the past week.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think that`s because we`re sending people back to Haiti?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: I can`t speak for them, but I do think they made that journey, which we admonished them not to make, to not put their lives and their funds in the hands of smugglers who exploit them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Secretary Mayorkas, I appreciate you coming on and sharing the administration`s perspective.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it. When we come back, we`re learning more about what all the president`s men were doing to help \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  during his final days in office to overturn the election and to stay in power. Bob Woodward and Robert Costa of the Washington Post join me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:55:02]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We`ve been learning more about the backstory to the January 6th insurrection and all the events around it post-election. The Biden White House, by the way, is now considering handing over information about what President Trump was doing while the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  was being ransacked that day. The House committee investigating January 6th issued subpoenas to four Trump allies, including his last chief of staff, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , and his on-again, off-again political advisor \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , asking them to hand over their records and appear for depositions.\par \par \uc2 And in Bob Woodward and Robert Costa`s new book "Peril" we learn that a lawyer working for President Trump wrote a memo outlining a way for Vice President Pence to invalidate the results of the election, essentially sending it to the House and hand the presidency back to Mr. Trump. That revelation is one of many in the book "Peril." Bob Woodward and Robert Costa join me now. Gentlemen, --\par \par \uc2 BOB WOODWARD, ASSOCIATE EDITOR, THE WASHINGTON POST: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- welcome back to MEET THE PRESS. Bob, I want to start here with sort of a big picture question, which is --\par \par \uc2 WOODWARD: Sure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- did you guys chronicle an aborted coup?\par \par \uc2 WOODWARD: Well, I mean, there were attempts. And, of course, the core of Trump`s argument is the election was stolen. And what we found in our work is two of the biggest Trump allies, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and Senator Lee from Utah -- I mean this is the core of Trump`s support in the Senate investigated these claims and discovered that there was absolutely nothing there.\par \par \uc2 And what you see Trump and his lawyers doing these kind of legal somersaults that just hold no water with Trump`s allies. And, you know, that you can`t convince \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and Mike Lee that the election was stolen or that there was some fraud means what we found, a weakness in the case. Not just a weakness, but zero evidence.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Robert, there were so many near misses here. I want to look at this excerpt that you guys have between -- a conversation between President Trump and Vice President Pence at the time. He`s trying to get him to work and he says, "If these people say you had the power, wouldn`t you want to do it?" Trump asks of Pence. "I wouldn`t want any one person to have that authority," Pence said. "But wouldn`t it almost be cool to have that power?" Trump asked. "No," Pence said. "Look, I`ve read this and I don`t see a way to do it."\par \par \uc2 It feels as if -- and we weren`t sure of the role \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  played, but was he the lone bulwark here?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, THE WASHINGTON POST: The story of January 6th is not just that day. It`s the days before, all of the movement afoot inside of the Oval Office. On January 4th, John Eastman, the author of the memo you cited here, he was in the Oval with the president, pressuring Vice President Pence. Look at this memo. Try to throw out alternate electors on January 6th. And that meeting, which was the ultimate temptation of power, a president to a vice president.\par \par \uc2 The next day, January 5th, Vice President Pence, who spent four years trying to do the bidding politically for President Trump, he said he didn`t have the constitutional ability to act. This was the brink of a constitutional crisis on the eve of an insurrection.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Had Mike Lee indicated support for this -- for this pathway, do you think the vice president would have changed his mind? How important is that?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Oh, it was critical, as Bob said, for Senator Graham, Senator Lee, and other conservatives, even former Vice President Dan Quayle to say, you can`t do anything here, Vice President Pence, except count the votes.\par \par \uc2 But there are so many historical counterfactuals. What could have happened if Pence went in a different direction and just walked away?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Bob, the roles of \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  here, at times it looked like they were sort of enabling President Trump`s wishes on this election and at times they seemed to want to steer him away from this. Is that the case? How would you characterize their roles?\par \par \uc2 WOODWARD: Well, there`s a lot of ambivalence here, but we quote conversations between Senator Graham and Trump in which \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  said to Trump, "You effed up your presidency," at one point just a couple of months ago. And Trump hung up on him and then called him back a day later and \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  said to him, "I would have hung up, too."\par \par \uc2 And the core conclusion that Graham has is if you lost a close election, face it. Notch it back. And now we have Trump out calling Senator Lee and Senator Graham -- somehow they folded. I mean -- Trump is attacking them because they found the truth. And the truth is this was not a stolen election.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And it really wasn`t even that close.\par \par \uc2 WOODWARD: No, it wasn`t even close. But we felt an obligation to look at that. And that`s how we found these memos that were written.\par \par \uc2 [11:00:02]\par \par \uc2 Giuliani wrote all these memos to \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , saying all kinds of fraudulent votes, kids voted, dead people voted. And then they looked at this and it`s not there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Debunked, debunked, debunked.\par \par \uc2 You`ve covered President Trump since the beginning of, sort of, that staircase, Robert. Does he -- do you think he really believes he won? Or do you think he really believes -- because I feel like the truth sort of showed up in one of the quotes in the book when he said, If I don`t keep fighting, I`ll lose my base.\par \par \uc2 He thinks his political base is because he fights the establishment, not because of any policy he enacts, right?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, THE WASHINGTON POST: I`ve learned, covering President Trump and covering politics, it doesn`t really matter sometimes what people think, it`s what they do, and to assume nothing. In 2015, a lot of people said \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won`t run. He`ll never win, once he announced in 2016. Well, he won and he lost in 2020.\par \par \uc2 He wants power back. Our reporting in the book shows these rallies don`t get a ton of coverage, but he`s out there with this Churchillian type language. We`ll never surrender. We`ll never give in -- whipping up thousands of people across the country. And the most important thing, it`s not just his personality as the story. He`s influencing the Republican Party across the country to now run for office, to be secretaries of state, to be election officials. 2022 to 2024 --\par \par \uc2 TODD: He`s bending the party to his will, isn`t he?\par \par \uc2 COSTA: Certainly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Bob, the final question I have for you, since we noted here, and the puns were necessary, All the President`s Men and Final Days, in some ways you put both books into one in this one. Trumpian versus Nixonian -- define the two.\par \par \uc2 WOODWARD: Well, Trump has -- I mean, it`s what is the presidency about for the average voter out there, and it is evaporating because Trump is staking everything on this stolen election claim.\par \par \uc2 And even we were able to get information about the then CIA Director Gina Haspel, someone who has been very silent, an expert on instability, unstable regimes. And she concluded in November that -- what is this? A right wing coup? That President Trump is like a child, a six-year-old having a temper tantrum.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, does this go beyond Nixonian? To be Trumpian is worse than to be Nixonian?\par \par \uc2 WOODWARD: Well, we were in a dangerous national security moment, which we documented. If things had gone crazy with China or Russia or Iran, we discovered these countries were on alert. And you know, that`s the edge of war and miscommunication, a pretty frightening time about --\par \par \uc2 COSTA: And as Bob and I have talked about, when Nixon gets on the helicopter in `74, he goes away to California and stays there. Not the same dynamic this time with President Trump.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No. There`s a reason, I think, you guys have an aptly named book called Peril. Let`s hope we`re not still at peril. Thank you, both.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, progressive Democrats versus the moderates. And the only thing at stake? Just the presidency of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . The panel is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [11:06:11]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Panel is here: Amy Walter, editor-in-chief and publisher of The Cook Political Report; NBC News Capitol Hill correspondent Leigh Ann Caldwell; Eddie Glaude, Jr. of \uc2 Princeton University\uc2 , and \uc2 Meghan McCain\uc2 , columnist for TheDailyMail.com.\par \par \uc2 It`s good to have a McCain back on "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 MEGHAN MCCAIN\uc2 , COLUMNIST, THEDAILYMAIL.COM: Thank you very much for having me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Nice to have you here.\par \par \uc2 So let`s start with what we`ve learned about what`s going on in Congress. Leigh Ann, this is your turf. I`m sure you caught the number \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  said, which was sometimes, look, whether it`s $3 trillion or $2.5. And I thought -- the number`s moving. What`s going to happen this week?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: The number is moving. And the reason it`s moving is because they know that they can`t get $3.5 trillion. That has been made clear.\par \par \uc2 But there`s problems with the number too. And that is that people like Senator Joe Manchin, who have said they won`t support $3.5 trillion, also won`t give a specific number.\par \par \uc2 And so, Speaker Pelosi is trying to negotiate legislation with the moderates, with the progressives, and with people like Senator Joe Manchin in the Senate who aren`t specific on what they want. So it`s a Rubik`s cube. It`s a Venn diagram. And it`s every sort of, like, crazy mathematical thing that they`re trying to fit together.\par \par \uc2 And it`s extremely complicated. And my sources say they don`t know how it`s going to play out yet.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The Pythagorean theorem in here at all?\par \par \uc2 Amy, it`s remarkable to me that Cory Booker`s, like, yeah, I don`t -- he didn`t say, I don`t trust, but he then described a situation that he says, "I don`t trust."\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, THE COOK POLITICAL REPORT: I know we`re not supposed to do this, but before we came on, we were chatting about this. And we were reminded of the number of times where the House has felt like they were hung out to dry. That they pass something, whether it`s climate, whether it`s back in the `90s, the BTU tax, only to find that the Senate --\par \par \uc2 TODD: This has happened to House Republicans too, right? This is, this is --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Fair.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- the Senate and the House do this to each other no matter who`s in charge.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: That`s fair. That happened with the health care bill, right?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: They took that big vote and then watched the Senate go.\par \par \uc2 To me, where we are though is so much driven by, you know, that phrase that Mike Tyson once had, that, everybody has a plan until you get punched in the mouth, right? And the plan back in the spring was by this point, we would be free from COVID. The economy would be roaring back, and, of course, the momentum that that would bring to this legislative process.\par \par \uc2 Democrats would come together. This would be, you know, not as big of a lift as it is right now. Now, what we have is a president who`s desperate for his party to give him momentum, right? He`s not bringing it to the table.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Meghan, I want you to play a reporter here for us because you may have the best sense of Kyrsten Sinema. I know you have a pretty good personal relationship.\par \par \uc2 What does she, we don`t -- that`s the frustration, Democrats say. They don`t know what Joe Manchin wants. They don`t know what his bottom line is, and they don`t know what her bottom line is. How would you describe it?\par \par \uc2 MCCAIN: Well, I mean, politically in Arizona, I think that`s why people like her so much. Just anecdotally, conservatives in my life really like her because, you know, she`s holding the line for conservatives in a lot of ways.\par \par \uc2 The question I always have is, you know, for people like Joe Manchin, if it`s not him, it`s going to be a Republican. So this distaste and this, you know, outward hostility towards moderates in the Democratic Party surprises me.\par \par \uc2 I also was shocked by seeing Senator Booker talking about sort of openly this distrust between progressives and moderates. How the Democratic Party ultimately threads the needle, I don`t know. But I will say that President Biden ran on being a moderate, as you said. He ran and won with the help of independents, centrists, you know, Trump-wary Republicans. And he is not governing as one.\par \par \uc2 The Build Back Better agenda is the most progressive, modern agenda of all time, up to $5 trillion, and it`s not polling well. So I think I`m just confused as to why they`re doubling down on something that is cratering in the polls right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Eddie, I`m just curious, what do you --\par \par \uc2 MCCAIN: I know you disagree.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, no, no. But should President Biden be leaning harder?\par \par \uc2 [11:10:03]\par \par \uc2 Are you surprised he`s not leaning harder to get this bipartisan? I would think he would need a signing ceremony yesterday.\par \par \uc2 EDDIE GLAUDE, JR., PROFESSOR, PRINCETON UNIVERSITY: Absolutely. President Biden understood that he was in a moment where he needed to be a transformative president. It feels, Chuck, like we`re in a political Groundhog Day, right? We`ve just experienced a pandemic. We`re still in it. Close to 700,000 Americans are dead.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic has revealed, it was like a blue dye into the social and political body of the United States. And that alongside of the threat that Trumpism presented to our democracy, it revealed all sorts of troubles across every aspect of our society. And our response bears a striking resemblance to the very logic that informed how we`ve governed for the last 40 years.\par \par \uc2 So I understand where -- This is a political show. We have to talk about the politics of it all.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`re stuck.\par \par \uc2 GLAUDE: But damn, you know? I mean, it seems to me we`re talking about a figure, a number, $3.5 trillion. America`s in trouble. And what are politicians doing, but haggling over, it seems to me, the elements of an ideology that has revealed itself to be bankrupt. That`s my view.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: And back to Senator Sinema, I have reporting that says that, out of all of this big reconciliation bill, all these different proponents, the thing that is most important to her is climate change, and why that -- so things get scaled back, she wants climate change proposals to be included in it. But that gets back to the Venn diagram because that`s exactly what Senator Joe Manchin in this bill does not like.\par \par \uc2 TODD: By the way, the other talking point is, hey, this is zero cost. Okay. It`s all going to be paid for? And what`s that going to look like?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: To me, this is the other big challenge for Democrats, as you pointed out in your interview with Senator Booker. There`s a lot of discussion about the price tag. There`s not a lot of discussion about the policy and how Democrats allowed this entire debate to be driven by, is it 3.5, is it 2.5, is one-point-whatever, rather than, what is the policy that we`re trying to put forward? What`s the name of this bill? We keep calling it reconciliation. We keep calling it the trillion dollar social package.\par \par \uc2 TODD: At least health care had a name, Affordable Care Act.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: They don`t even -- exactly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Even Obamacare. At least you do "care." It was about health care.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Right, but there`s no -- no, no American knows what`s in this. And when Senator Booker said, well, you take the individual pieces, people like it. Okay. But that`s not what you`re talking about right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ultimately though, until COVID`s resolved, I think --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: I agree.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- the politics for the president is going to be difficult. The COVID confusion doesn`t help things.\par \par \uc2 MCCAIN: At all. I still don`t know if I`m supposed to get a booster or not.\par \par \uc2 The other thing I would piggyback on what you`re saying, Amy, is that Americans may not understand this bill in its entirety, especially if you`re an average American working. But they do understand inflation. They understand what happened in Afghanistan. They understand that their gas prices are going up. They understand that Christmas toys are going to be more expensive for their kids.\par \par \uc2 And that is something that will be a deal breaker for candidates coming up in the midterms. And I believe your Cook Report showed Virginia now officially a toss-up state.\par \par \uc2 TODD: COVID, COVID, COVID. In many ways, the reason why our Christmas presents are going to be difficult to pack? Supply chain.\par \par \uc2 GLAUDE: Supply chain. And, you know, every 43 seconds, an American dies. One in 500, dead from COVID.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`re going to hit 700,000.\par \par \uc2 GLAUDE JR.: Come on. It seems to me that there`s some stuff on the table for us to deal with.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Can`t resolve this now, but I have to take a break.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the fight over abortion is heading to the Supreme Court. How Americans feel about abortion rights and the Court right now, when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [11:16:30]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 It`s Data Download time. This week, the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  announced it would hear arguments on a Mississippi law that would ban abortions after the first 15 weeks of pregnancy, except in the case of medical emergencies.\par \par \uc2 Now, the news was the latest bit of evidence that abortion and the Court are going to remain big political issues in the months ahead. At the same time, the Court is seeing a major shift in voter attitudes about it. In fact, as abortion and the Supreme Court get conflated, you`re going to see the poll numbers change for the Court. In fact, already, you`re starting to see, and I want to show you something here from July to September.\par \par \uc2 As you can see, before abortion -- we knew abortion was going to be front and center in the Court, both Republicans and Democrats had decent approval ratings of this Supreme Court. The right, happy with some of the business decisions, the left, happy with some of the social decisions, on things like same-sex marriage and things like that. But as you can see, as abortion has heated up, all of a sudden, you`re seeing a partisan divide develop when it comes to the Court. So let`s go to the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 Some new polling out this week from our friends over at Fox. Future of Roe v. Wade: Overturn it or let it stand? A whopping 65 percent, highest number they had found. And look at it by party. A majority of Republicans in this Fox poll said, "Let Roe v. Wade stand" and, of course, a large majority of Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Now, that`s the big picture of Roe v. Wade. You start to get in a little bit of the details. An abortion ban after 15 weeks, and you can see the partisan divide grow a bit more. Overall, there`s a plurality in favor of a ban of abortion after 15 weeks, 34 percent oppose -- a large chunk, right, undecided on this issue. And you see the party line differences here on this. So this really is, in some ways, a line of demarcation when it comes to public opinion on abortion.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, all of that means what? To both parties, and especially Democrats, the choice of the next Supreme Court justice as being very important, Democrats now caring more about the next choice than Republicans.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, even after Arizona`s phony election audit found that President Trump lost the state again, Mr. Trump insists it proves he won. So what`s he doing? Calling for more so-called audits.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [11:22:19]\par \par \uc2 TODD: We are back.\par \par \uc2 So the Arizona audit, Meghan, showed that --\par \par \uc2 MCCAIN: He lost by more.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He lost by more.\par \par \uc2 MCCAIN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: President Trump has not taken it very well. Last night in Georgia, he seemed to go after any Republicans that stood in his way. Let me play a little sound.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: Raffensperger, Raffensperger. I`m telling you, I think there`s something wrong with him.\par \par \uc2 And your RINO governor, Brian Kemp, who`s been a complete disaster on election integrity.\par \par \uc2 Governor Ducey was horrible. He was missing in action.\par \par \uc2 We didn`t get any help from \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, Meghan, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2 , Brian Kemp. This conspiracy reaches pretty deep into stopping President Trump in his own mind, doesn`t it?\par \par \uc2 MCCAIN: I don`t even know how to talk about this because it is conspiratorial, you know, insanity from the bowels of the internet. There is a segment of the Republican Party that believes it. I know people that think the election is stolen. It`s the big lie that people are still believing. It`s a huge challenge for the party going forward.\par \par \uc2 But, as \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  recently said, I believe he called President Trump a horse that is out to pasture and should stay there, a thoroughbred that should stay there.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at polling against people like Governor DeSantis, President Trump does not have the stranglehold on the party that he once did. And I think it`s college-educated women. Continue, sorry.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I kind of agree that you`re right, except the party, Eddie, the party leaders publicly act as if Trump still has all of this power.\par \par \uc2 GLAUDE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right? She`s not wrong. There is polling indicating that people -- please go away. But they`re afraid to say it publicly.\par \par \uc2 GLAUDE: Yeah. I mean, in some ways, Governor DeSantis, his popularity has everything to do with he`s a version of Trump in some ways, in particular sorts of ways.\par \par \uc2 But I think you`re absolutely right. The base of the party is locked in lockstep with Trump. And what I heard in Trump`s remarks last night in Georgia was in effect, if we don`t win, somebody stole it. If the results aren`t consistent with the way in which I think they should be, then someone stole it.\par \par \uc2 We heard it from Elder. Seems like it`s going to be a strategy across the Republican Party these days.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Leigh Ann, there`s not a Republican, other than \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  and \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  right now, a House Republican that feels comfortable really going after him publicly, right?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yeah, that`s absolutely right. The base of the party is still -- even though Trump is not the same factor that he was with he was president, the base of the party is still beholden to him. And they are rabid about him.\par \par \uc2 And Republicans, especially in the House, know that. They are afraid of primaries. They are afraid of being on the other end of Trump bullying, because they know that will hurt them even in their gerrymandered districts, where they`re likely to -- they`re going to win a general election. It`s the primaries that matter.\par \par \uc2 [11:25:01]\par \par \uc2 And that`s what they`re worried about.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: And to me, this has been the danger all along for Republicans, is a sense that, we just don`t anger him. Just appease him a little bit. And it`ll go away.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Appease?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: It`ll get better. Don`t worry about it --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Does appeasing work?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: It`s not working.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: I don`t like that it`s being called an audit because that puts an air of legitimacy to it. It is not legitimate.\par \par \uc2 The legitimate forces, the people whose job it is to say, these elections were held fairly have ruled. That should be the end of it. And so I don`t think the point is to say, we want to take every election we lose and say we won, the point is to undermine the integrity of the entire --\par \par \uc2 TODD: All elections?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: -- process. And once that`s gone, I don`t want to be the alarmist, but it is very dangerous and scary.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, that brings me to this Robert Kagan column that everybody`s been buzzing about. This is in The Washington Post, and I`ve been divided. Is it alarmist? Or is it prescient?\par \par \uc2 Let me read a little excerpt.\par \par \uc2 The Republican Party today is a zombie party. Its leaders go through the motions of governing in pursuit of traditional Republican goals. The party`s main, if not sole purpose today though, is as the willing enabler of Trump`s efforts to game the electoral system to ensure his return to power."\par \par \uc2 MCCAIN: I mean --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah. Is it prescient or --\par \par \uc2 MCCAIN: Nothing galvanizes Republicans right now than seeing what happened in Afghanistan, than seeing this giant spending package.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the thing that can unite me with the bigger MAGA person in the world is the Build Back Better progressive agenda. So I do think, while it`s, you know, uncouth in polite society to say that you would be okay having anything to do with President Trump, there are people that will vote for anything except what`s happening right now.\par \par \uc2 And I agree with what you`re always saying. I always consider President Trump like Godzilla under the water. And the sequel could easily come. But there really are people in the party that I think, at the very least, will put up a very good fight against him. And he is still very powerful in a lot of ways. But he is not the kingmaker he once was, as we`ve seen in primaries across the country.\par \par \uc2 GLAUDE: The fascinating part about what you`ve just said, Meghan, is that it`s the feature of the argument that Kagan is making, and that is that there`s a loyalty to national party, a desire to own the libs, to define oneself over and against those folks that you do not want to govern.\par \par \uc2 And so you`re willing to make a certain kind of choice, a kind of Faustian bargain that allowed -- that unleashed this person into our body politic. It`s fascinating. I think it`s a bit alarmist. I disagree with the way in which he tries to disentangle the history of the Republican Party from Trumpism.\par \par \uc2 But he`s onto something, Chuck. And that is, I`m going to echo Amy, this is calling into question the very foundation of our democracy. If the body public do not -- if the people do not trust the process, we can`t exist.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: We have nothing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What are close elections going to look like on November of `22?\par \par \uc2 GLAUDE: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Well, that`s the thing. It`s not about the past election anymore. They`re still talking about the past election, but they`re trying to delegitimize future elections.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think that`s the concern we all have. And it`s sort of, like, boy. I fear -- I used to love a close election. Now I fear the impact of what unpacking that would be.\par \par \uc2 Thank you all.\par \par \uc2 But before we go, I want to let you know that we are kicking off our third season of "Meet the Press Reports". It`s where we deep dive into one subject. You can see new episodes on NBC News NOW on Thursdays at 9: 00 p.m. Eastern and any time on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 So, and by the way, today to get you ready for the new season, you can see all eight episodes of season two from 2:00 to 6:00 p.m. Eastern on NBC News NOW today.\par \par \uc2 That`s all we have for today. So thank you for watching. We`ll be back next week because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020210926eh9q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Companies Take A Stand Against The Restrictive New Abortion Law In Texas As Some Prominent Republicans Speak Out Against The Legislation\b0\par\par\uc2 TOM LLAMAS, HALLIE JACKSON \par \uc2 921 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Now to the latest on that new Texas abortion law that encourages ordinary citizens to help enforce it. Private companies are using their leverage to oppose the law and support those who face punishment.\par \par \uc2 Senior Washington correspondent Hallie Jackson has a closer look.\par \par \uc2 Hallie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, CBS NEWS SENIOR WASHINGTON: Hey, Tom. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 You know, you are now seeing some legal relief funds created by a handful of well known companies to help women and others in Texas all part of the now days-long bash backlash against that state`s restrictive new abortion law.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON (voice-over): Taking a stand this morning against the new Texas abortion law. GoDaddy, a website hosting service, cutting service for the owner of an anti-abortion website that allows people to report anonymous tips on suspected abortions. It comes as \uc2 Lyft\uc2  and Uber announce they will cover legal fees for any driver sued under the new law. And online dating services Bumble and Match both women-led and headquartered in Texas are pledging to support workers and their dependents, including by creating relief funds to access abortion services.\par \par \uc2 [08:05:10]\par \par \uc2 The Texas attorney general defending the law, despite the backlash.\par \par \uc2 KEN PAXTON\uc2 , TEXAS ATTORNEY GENERAL: All Texas is trying to do is protect the unborn. We have a right, I think, to do that.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: The new state law bans abortion after about six weeks and gives a $10,000 incentive to private citizens who successfully sue anyone who provides an abortion or helps a patient get one. Some in the GOP say they`re uncomfortable with that kind of enforcement mechanism.\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: Yeah, I`m pro-life, but what I don`t like to see is this idea of every citizen being able to tattle.\par \par \uc2 GOV. LARRY HOGAN (R), MARYLAND: This bill in Texas seems to be a little bit extreme with this problem of bounties for people that turn in somebody that drove someone to an abortion clinic.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: All of it as more Republicans in red states signal they will considering using Texas as a blueprint to craft their own abortion laws.\par \par \uc2 The White House now gearing up for a fight.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND, WHITE HOUSE SENIOR ADVISOR: And we`re going to do everything we can to try to remedy that situation for people in Texas. It is just a cruel and destructive law on the rights of women.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON (on camera): Keep in mind that while those businesses that we just talked about that you just heard are speaking up, you`ve got a whole lot of other companies choosing not to. It`s a much more limited, much less vocal response than what we saw, for example, on a different controversial Texas bill on restricting voting access -- Tom.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: It`s good point, Hallie. OK, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 JONES: A wild weekend of college football was capped off last night with an overtime thriller in Tallahassee, Florida.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPORTS ANCHOR: Does Doerer have the look of the Irish? He does.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Ninth-ranked Notre Dame with 41-yard field goal in overtime. The Irish topped Florida State 41-38. If this weekend was any indication how the season is going to go, college football fans are in for an exciting fall.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: We had a rough Saturday in our house.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Who you`re rooting for again?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: The Canes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK, yeah.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: The Canes played Alabama. Alabama was ranked number one. They are clearly the best team in the country. It was a tough, tough game.\par \par \uc2 Anyways, a long season.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well, for your fans, I will give you a boost.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: How did you do?\par \par \uc2 JONES: I don`t know. Sorry.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Here is the morning boost. Go for it.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. A lot of teenagers might roll their eyes at their dad, play with a band at a restaurant. But this dad in the striped shirt which you`ll see here ends up stealing the show. Look at\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO CLIP PLAYS)\par \par \uc2 JONES: I love it! Pretty impressive. TikTok user Jessica was so proud of her dad, she is trying to convince him to make his own TikTok videos. But Jessica was not embarrassed by his dad. She thinks he is quite spectacular, which he is.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: To jump in with a mariachi, that takes a lot of guts. But the guy is very, very talented. That is pretty awesome.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Dad was able to meet the moment.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yeah, for sure.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. We have a lot more ahead, including our tribute to a beloved and iconic member of the TODAY family, Willard Scott, a man who brought smiles to faces around the world and was like a second father to Al. He will join us live to celebrate Willard`s life.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, friend.\par \par \uc2 That`s right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020210907eh960008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 President Biden Job Approval Hits New Low; Abortion Politics: Texas Bans Abortions After Six Weeks, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Claire McCaskill, Yamiche Alcindor, Betsy Woodruff  Swan \par \uc2 9,712 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [10:30:11]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST (voice-over): This Sunday, a divided America.\par \par \uc2 STUART MICKELBERG, BUCKS COUNTY, PA PARENT: People are enraged on both sides of the issue, and it`s completely tearing this community apart.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The country split in two on multiple fronts. One battlefield, COVID and masks --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Masks do not stop the spread of this virus. It only stops the spread of friendship and learning.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is all smoke and mirrors. This is tyranny.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- even as the pandemic is again killing 1,500 Americans a day.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I feel like I`m in some weird twilight zone, why we`re still going over and wearing masks and having safe schools for our kids. It`s bananas.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This morning, I`ll talk to two governors trying to bridge that divide. \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , the Republican governor of blue state Maryland, and \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , the Democratic governor of red state Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the newly elevated fight over abortion. Texas enacts the most restrictive abortion law in the country dismaying abortion rights advocates --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: As a matter of reality, Roe versus Wade is a dead letter in Texas today.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- and the Supreme Court lets the law stand for now, cheering abortion opponents.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The child is a child, that this person is a person.\par \par \uc2 TODD: My guest this morning, former Democratic Senator Claire McCaskill of Missouri and former Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Barbara Comstock\uc2  of Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Also, long-term power outages in Louisiana, record rainfall and flooding in the northeast. What Hurricane Ida tells us about the threat of climate change.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joining me for insight and analysis are, Yamiche Alcindor, the moderator of "Washington Week," "Washington Post" contributing columnist, Matt Bai, Betsy Woodruff Swan of "Politico," and Republican strategist Brendan Buck.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s MEET THE PRESS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is MEET THE PRESS WITH CHUCK TODD.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And a good Sunday morning. The U.S. enters this Labor Day weekend suffering from two viruses, COVID and polarization. It is not an exaggeration to say that we are more divided than at any time perhaps since the 1960s and frankly, maybe since the Civil War. As one of our guests today put it, we used to be divided over issues like abortion and guns, we`re used to those divisions, and we still are divided on those issues. But now we can`t even agree about COVID or January 6th.\par \par \uc2 COVID has become an MRI of America`s soul. Who would have imagined that masks, wearing or refusing them, would become such a political statement. This morning we`re going to take a look at how and why we`re so divided primarily through the lens of two issues. One is COVID, where the seven-day average of infections has risen to more than 161,000 driven largely by vaccine hesitancy and refusal. The other is abortion. The Supreme Court letting stand, at least for now, that restrictive Texas law that has left abortion opponents believing Roe could finally be overturned and pro-choice advocates fearing they may be right. With the country increasingly divided by geography, race, age, education, the question we`re asking this morning, do we have the political leadership to meet this treacherous political moment?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MICKELBERG: People are enraged on both sides of the issue, and it`s completely tearing this community apart.\par \par \uc2 TODD (voice-over): The political divide has become deadly with vaccine hesitancy driven by misinformation and the politicizing of the virus leading to the harassment of health care workers, hospitalizations and deaths.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Things are just escalating. The rhetoric, the bullying.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I feel like I`m in some weird twilight zone, why we`re still going over wearing masks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Just 53 percent of Americans are fully vaccinated. Despite the nation`s early start, that rate trails more than 30 other countries. As COVID hospitalizations and deaths rise, red states with the lowest vaccination rates are driving the surge.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JIM JUSTICE (R-WV): You got to help me get the people vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): We are suffering the most at a time when it is the most preventable.\par \par \uc2 TODD: In a recent NBC News poll, just 55 percent of Republicans said they were vaccinated compared to 88 percent of Democrats. Among Republicans who said they support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  over the Republican Party, the number was even lower, 46 percent. Seventy-nine percent of Democrats favor a vaccine requirement. Seventy-five percent of Republicans oppose it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you regret not getting the vaccine?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Absolutely. I 100 percent regret it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What were some of the things you were hearing?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That the vaccine was not a real vaccine, that it was like a tracking chip the government was trying to use on us.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Misinformation has spread rapidly on conservative media --\par \par \uc2 GRANT STINCHFIELD, NEWSMAX HOST: A plan is now under way to use vaccine mandates to take your guns.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: Virtually everything they told us about the COVID vaccine was wrong.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- and on social media.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What`s in the vaccine?\par \par \uc2 [10:35:00]\par \par \uc2 Give me the insert sheet.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH DUKE, \uc2 VANDERBILT UNIVERSITY MEDICAL CENTER\uc2 : We literally have come up with an incredibly safe and efficacious vaccine. People are behind me screaming about how it`s tyranny and it`s communism.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There are also sharp divisions between the two Americans over the results of the election, the January 6th insurrection, the border, guns and abortion. Now likely to produce a national political battle leading up to the next election. The new Texas law which bans abortion after six weeks provides at least a $10,000 reward to any individual who successfully sues someone for performing, aiding or intending to aid in an abortion, including the driver who drops a woman off at a clinic.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s going to be nearly impossible for folks to access the care that they need.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA PARMA, TEXAS RIGHT TO LIFE: Thrilled. We are celebrating this historic day for the pro-life movement.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The Texas law is by far the most restrictive in effect. Fifteen states have attempted to ban abortion before viability but have been stopped by court order. Fifty-four percent of Americans say abortions should always or mostly be legal. Forty-two percent say it should always or mostly be illegal. Still, several states are already considering copycat legislation after the Supreme Court upheld the Texas law.\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. WILTON SIMPSON (R-FL), PRESIDENT OF FLORIDA SENATE: When the Supreme Court goes out and makes a decision like this it clearly is going to send a signal to all the states that are interested in banning abortions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And joining me now are two governors who are living our political divide. Andy Beshear`s is the Democratic governor of a very red Kentucky where the Republican legislature has a veto-proof majority. And \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  is the Republican governor of a very blue Maryland where the Democratic legislature has a veto-proof majority. And in the spirit of bipartisanship the two governors agreed to appear together today. So, I thank you both and welcome to both of you.\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GOV. LARRY HOGAN\uc2  (R-MD): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Governor Beshear, let me start with you. Look, I`m just going to put up the case curve. You know this curve very well with COVID right now. It`s astonishing how fast Delta has surged in the state of Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 You`re trying to deal with it, but you`ve been limited in your ability to do certain things by your Republican legislature. What can you get done now to try to slow this surge in Kentucky?\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Well, our situation is dire. We are setting case records. We have a record number of Kentuckians in the hospital battling COVID, in the ICU battling for their lives. We have a record number of families that are praying for their loved one who is on a ventilator needing that assistance to breathe.\par \par \uc2 A recent Supreme Court case and our legislature limit my ability to do some things like masking. If I had the ability to do it right now, we would have a masking order when you are in public and indoors. We know that`s a proven way to slow the spread of the virus and ultimately help our health care capacity, but that doesn`t mean there aren`t things we can do.\par \par \uc2 So, I sent the National Guard to our hardest-hit hospitals. They do the logistics and free up more clinicians. We`ve taken over the testing for those hospitals so those nurse, again, can be tending to patients. We brought in FEMA who have sent some strike teams. And now we`ve deployed nursing students all over Kentucky again to try to provide more staffable beds.\par \par \uc2 It is a challenge. We are hit very, very hard, but we are going to continue to fight. When you`re at war, you don`t get to cry about what you can or can`t do. You`ve got to do your very best every day because this is a battle of life versus death.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Governor Hogan, look, you lead a state that has one of the highest vaccination rates in the country. But let`s be frank, if two-thirds of your electorate were Republican, do you think you`d be this successful?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, look, I think we`re very proud of the fact that we`re one of the most vaccinated states in the country. And as a result, our metrics are almost completely opposite of what Governor Beshear is dealing with in Kentucky. We -- you know, we have vaccinated 81 percent of all the people that are eligible, everybody 12 and over, 95 percent of our seniors. And, you know, as a result, our hospitalizations are down 70-some percent from their peak. Deaths are down. But we`re still dealing with the unvaccinated folks which account for most of our hospitalizations and deaths.\par \par \uc2 Some of it has to do with the fact that there`s been tremendous disinformation campaigns, and people are believing things that are simply not true. But it`s not just in the red areas where we`re having difficulties. We`re one of the states with the highest percentage of minority populations and in some of our urban areas we have similar challenges.\par \par \uc2 But we`re doing really well. And I`m pleased with the results of where we are. Convincing those last, you know, 19 percent of our people that they`ve got to take action, that`s where we`re focused on.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s talk about this issue of misinformation. We`ve got this issue of the dewormer situation. And I think Kentucky Poison Control has had extra calls about this.\par \par \uc2 [10:40:03]\par \par \uc2 Governor Beshear, are there just certain groups of people that aren`t going to listen to you, and you have to acknowledge that and find other spokes people to try to -- to try to convince the unvaccinated to listen to public health?\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: When it comes to misinformation, I really don`t think this is a red or a blue issue. It is a fact versus fiction or a sometimes sane versus insane issue.\par \par \uc2 My first job in life was mucking stalls at a horse farm. Taking a horse dewormer is crazy under any circumstance. We are well past, I think, all across America the populations that are going to listen to a government official and take the vaccine because of it. We`re probably past even the point where a local official, a pastor or others.\par \par \uc2 Where I think we`re at is where people are going to have to break that Thanksgiving dinner rule. They`re going to have to call or go see that person they love and care about that is unvaccinated, and they`re going to have to put their relationship with that person on the line because they`ve never been at greater risk.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s that type of caring and the person who is willing to do that and to make that sacrifice that will finally get through to those that are not vaccinated. That`s what we`re seeing here in Kentucky. And we need all Americans to do it. Yes, you might lose a friend because of that conversation but that friend might lose their life if they don`t get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Governor Hogan, give some advice to the current COVID task force and the Biden administration. Let`s talk the issue of a third shot or a booster shot. You`re a cancer survivor, you`ve gotten the booster. You`ve got the former president now out there saying he`s not going to take it. And earlier he seemed to chalk up the idea to some sort of profiteering by \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I think it`s clear the Biden administration can`t speak to those folks that need to be spoken to. Who should? And what would be your advice to the COVID task force team on how to talk to the Trump folks?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, I don`t think it`s just about the Trump folks. I mean, I -- you know, I was fairly critical of President Trump when I thought that they were bad on messaging, when I thought they were doing things wrong and not providing the assistance that we needed in the states. And I`ve been the same thing with the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we`re getting some mixed messagings out of the -- out of the administration, out of the CDC, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and the White House. And, you know, we need clear guidance on these booster shots because it undermines, you know, the credibility of it. And so they came out with -- I guess they slipped and pre-leaked an announcement about booster shots with all three vaccines and then had to backtrack and then say you can only use \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 . What about the people that took J&J? What about the people that took Moderna?\par \par \uc2 They haven`t messaged properly about how to take care of these breakthrough infections. Like -- look, it does prevent hospitalizations and death or greatly reduce it but it`s not stopping, you know, people from getting breakthrough infections.\par \par \uc2 So, I think, the messaging was not clear with either administration. And, I think, that`s one of the ways we`ve been successful in our state is a clear, direct messaging that people can believe in and listen to. And that has been a problem all over the map.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Governor Beshear, I`m curious of your thoughts on the Biden COVID task force. And I know you talked about having just a metric that you just -- you know, if you hit the number, mask mandate triggers. If you`re below the certain number of infection, no.\par \par \uc2 Why has it been hard to just create a statistical line here? It`s like we know when a tropical storm becomes a hurricane because you measure the wind speed. Why has that been difficult with COVID?\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Well, it has been difficult, and it shouldn`t be because it`s just basic science. And our health care providers have been wearing masks for decades. Why? To prevent infection in different areas.\par \par \uc2 I think this is a test of our humanity. I believe that that`s what this pandemic is. And remember, a pandemic isn`t an issue. It`s a virus that`s trying to kill as many of us as possible.\par \par \uc2 And so I think it comes down to, are you willing to be selfless and wear that mask, loving your neighbor and protecting them? Or is it more a question of selfish? I have the right to do whatever I want even if it causes harm to others.\par \par \uc2 You know, you can`t walk into a crowded movie theater and yell fire. Your freedom of speech doesn`t extend that far. So let`s remember that liberty is the right to live our lives, not to cause someone else`s death. That should be pretty easy for people of faith, for people of values. And I hope that we can start living that faith and those values to protect one another once again.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me lift up and talk about the larger challenge of polarization and the abortion debate, frankly, I think is only going to exasperate or divide here. Both of you I would -- I could argue and you may disagree with me, but I`m going to play political analyst here. Both of you have benefited when the other party went too far, right?\par \par \uc2 [10:45:01]\par \par \uc2 I could argue that`s why you`re both governor today. Certainly why you may have a second term, Governor Hogan and why Governor Beshear you`re there. Is that the only way to cut through polarization, Governor Hogan, is when the other party goes too far and then the voters create a check? Is that about it? Because it doesn`t seem as if the middle ground gets represented unless one party goes too far.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, it`s an interesting -- it`s an interesting idea. I think that may be possible. Certainly in our state, you know, they had raised taxes 43 times in a row even though we`re the most democratic state in America. Even the Democrats got frustrated with that.\par \par \uc2 And so -- but I think right now in America, there are certainly people on both ends of the extremes, on the left and the right, who get all of the attention. The ones that are making the most noise. And that`s what we hear about when we`re watching television. But about 70 percent of the people actually just are somewhere in the middle. They`re moderate or right of center, left of center. They really want their elected officials to try to figure out a way to do something about this toxic politics and they want us to work together to come up with real bipartisan common sense solutions. It turns out that`s the most popular thing in America with most voters.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Governor Beshear, both you and Governor Hogan have also shared another thing in common. When you`ve had some abortion bills come from your legislature, you chose not to veto them, but you chose not to sign them and they just became law. One had to do with some Medicaid funding with Planned Parenthood, I believe, on Maryland side of things. You, with the current law that`s there, how much do you expect abortion to divide your state going forward? And what is your -- do you have a plan on what to do in a post-Roe world?\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Well, I think it remains to be seen exactly what impact this Texas decision will have, especially with another case coming before the Supreme Court here pretty soon. But as the state`s former top prosecutor, I have seen the worst of the worst situations. You girls raped, impregnated, sometimes by members of their own families. I hope whatever comes down the line provides those victims that have been harmed in ways most of us could never imagine the options that they deserve.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Governor Hogan, do you believe the abortion issue, when it -- if it`s going to be state by state, you seem to basically say you know your electorate is perhaps pro-choice. You may be pro-life and you`ve sort of taken a hands-off approach. Do you believe it should be the sort of public sentiment that should dictate where we go on abortion laws?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, you know, I happen to be personally opposed to abortion but -- and I believe that states do have rights to pass some reasonable restrictions. But certainly in this case, this bill in Texas seems to be a little bit extreme with this problem of bounties for people that turn in somebody that drove someone to an abortion clinic.\par \par \uc2 So, look, I think the courts -- the Supreme Court will take this up. They haven`t made the final determination on it. Legislatures have rights to pass bills. Governors have the right to sign them into law or not. And the court gets to make the ultimate decision.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, I understand. But do you think there should be something -- if it`s by the states, should it reflect the will of the people or the will of the elected officials?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, you know, the way our system works is we elect officials and they make decisions for us. The people get to decide whether they want to keep them in office or not.\par \par \uc2 In our state the voters actually made this determination long before I became governor. My legislature is 70 percent -- more than 70 percent Democratic. And I chose not to try to overturn the will of the legislature or the voters.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , Democrat from Kentucky, \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , Republican from Maryland, again, appreciate you both coming on. Believe it or not this used to be a very common practice here on MEET THE PRESS. It`s hopefully something we can keep going. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, we`re going to turn to the issue of abortion and whether the Supreme Court just signaled it`s ready to overturn Roe v. Wade. Former Democratic Senator Claire McCaskill and former Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Barbara Comstock\uc2  join me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:51:10]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The enactment of the Texas abortion law is perhaps a game changing moment in the nearly 50-year battle over Roe v. Wade. When the Supreme Court declined to block what is now the most restrictive abortion law in the country, it sent a signal that the court`s conservative majority may be ready to unwind or overturn Roe. And while abortion opponents cheer the move abortion rights supporters are hoping that the far-reaching law will energize Democratic voters in the next midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now are former Democratic Senator Claire McCaskill of Missouri and former Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Barbara Comstock\uc2  of Virginia. Welcome to you both. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Senator McCaskill -- Claire, let me start with you on this. And that is this -- how much of -- what should the Biden administration be doing right now? And is there anything that Democrats in Congress or the Biden administration should be doing that can be done before the next election?\par \par \uc2 FMR. SEN. CLAIRE MCCASKILL (D-MO): Well, I`m not so sure that there`s a huge role for Biden and/or Congress right now other than shouting from the rooftops that this law that the Supreme Court blessed -- and you can try to dress it up and put a bow on it, but they blessed a law that embraces vigilantism, that embraces the most extreme position, no exception for a 13-year-old who has been repeatedly raped by her father who didn`t know she was even pregnant until after six weeks. No exception for that.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Chuck, 63 percent of America supports Roe v. Wade. I guarantee you 80 percent of America does not want this law with vigilante embraced by the Supreme Court to become the way of life in America. And I really think they`ve gone too far. And I will not accept both sides on this. This is one party that is doing this, not both parties. This is not the place for, oh, both sides are a problem. No, one side is a problem. I got reelected because of an extreme position on abortion. I believe a lot of Democrats will get elected over this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Former Congresswoman \uc2 Barbara Comstock\uc2 , do you accept that, that this is being driven more by one party at this time, that you can`t have the both sides conversation on this particular topic?\par \par \uc2 FMR. \uc2 REP. BARBARA COMSTOCK\uc2  (R-VA): Well, this is obviously a polarizing issue and this is a polarizing bill. As someone who is pro-life and has always wanted to, you know, change hearts and minds and, you know, focus on creating a culture of life and respecting the dignity of women and the children, I don`t think this is a good bill. And I agree with the "Wall Street Journal" editorial board that said it was a blunder.\par \par \uc2 And so I do think it`s important to point out, because I don`t agree with Claire that this majority and minority have clearly said this does not overturn Roe v. Wade. And secondly a Texas county judge has already blocked the enforcement of it against Planned Parenthood who didn`t have a good legislative strategy. And this is a flawed bill that I expect because these similar bills have been blocked before and I think fairly quickly this will be.\par \par \uc2 So I think neither side is going to be pleased with where the court may come down. I think the key issue on abortion from the original Roe decision is the viability issue. And that`s where you`ve had Republicans usually focus, is on -- we had -- I think it was in 2016 a bill on banning abortions after 20 weeks which is sort of the viability area now. And the original Roe decision highlighted viability as part of that.\par \par \uc2 So I think there`s going to be -- there needs to be some calm conversation on this. I think the Supreme Court, when you look at whether it`s, you know, Bush v. Gore or the Trump election, these judges who got attacked as being -- they were going to be Trump judges -- they upheld the Obamacare decision.\par \par \uc2 [10:55:09]\par \par \uc2 Let them take up these cases, which they will, I think, shortly. And I think they will -- people will be pleasantly surprised where they end up, whether -- you know, I don`t think pro-life or pro-choice are going to like the ultimate, where they come down, maybe where the people are in the middle.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well and, Claire, this is something -- you brought up the fact you got reelected because you had an opponent that was -- went to -- had an extreme position on the idea of rape, if legitimate rape is something that Todd Akin at the time called it. But what -- you look at this Texas law, what is the slippery slope if we go down this idea where you take enforcement and put it into the hands of the people? I mean, I`ve already seen some folks on the right who don`t like what Texas did fear that this could turn into some -- that a blue state could do this on gun issues.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Well, let`s be clear about what the Republican Party is right now. It`s a stewing pot of grievance. And what they have done with this law -- and by the way, they`ve done it with other laws, too. Republicans. Not Democrats. They`ve done it on what is taught at school. In my state, they`ve done it on whether or not local law enforcement enforces federal gun laws.\par \par \uc2 In other states, they`ve done it on what rights people have that are trans. So this vigilantism, our courts have always operated on a concept of standing. That you are in court because you have standing.\par \par \uc2 What the Republicans are doing is they`re giving standing to anybody who`s got a social grievance. They`re taking the culture wars to a place that our country has never gone. It is dangerous. It is wrong.\par \par \uc2 And the Democratic legislatures are not doing this. These are just Republicans doing this. And I think this goes in the column of, they are only listening to their calcified circle of disinformation and conservative media.\par \par \uc2 They think everybody agrees with them. And it`s only about 20 percent of the country. Eighty percent of the country is not going to like this. And I do believe -- I don`t want the Democrats to start doing this kind of BS. It`s wrong. We should not be empowering vigilantes to enforce social grievance.\par \par \uc2 COMSTOCK: Well, Justice Roberts --\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Whether it`s guns, climate, or anything else.\par \par \uc2 COMSTOCK: Well, Justice Roberts pointed out in his dissent -- he was in the minority here --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Correct. No longer in control of the court --\par \par \uc2 COMSTOCK: -- that this case of whether you can outsource to private parties is a very important issue. And I am confident that will not stand. So that`s where all the focus and the inflammation is here. But as you just had that discussion with those governors we have had a lot of polarization on whether it`s January 6 or COVID. And these issues have always been polarizing.\par \par \uc2 We need calm right now. We don`t need another January 6 at the Supreme Court. The Supreme Court knows how to handle these issues and will be doing it quickly, I believe. And then you`re going to have -- I mean, look at \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , who`s the Attorney General, who will be defending this. He lost in court on Obamacare. He lost in three days. The Supreme Court kicked him to the curb on his bogus Trump things.\par \par \uc2 Remember, there were a lot of Democrats saying, oh, these judges are going to uphold Trump. Trump even said it. What did they do? They kicked him to the curb.\par \par \uc2 These are judges who follow the law. And that`s why they will take up these cases, look at what the established law is, and have them briefed. And I think since the judge in Texas has already said Planned Parenthood can go forward, this is going to go through the normal process.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you a big picture question, though, on the Republican Party. Can you have Governor Hogan`s position? He`s personally pro-life, but he essentially allowed pro-choice legislation to pass. Can you have that position and be viable in a Republican primary?\par \par \uc2 COMSTOCK: Well, look. I think Governor Hogan has been very pro-life throughout the COVID case. Look how he`s brought people together. He`s got his state vaccinated. And he`s worked very well on this --\par \par \uc2 TODD: You get what I`m asking.\par \par \uc2 COMSTOCK: I know. But he understood that his bill would be overridden. I mean, the bill if he had vetoed it. So yes, I do believe. Because everybody understands there are different positions on this, which is why instead of having extremes drive this from both, you know, whether it`s a Democrat, right.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look. Claire was attacked by AOC in 2018 when she pointed out that abortion and guns were polarizing the country and that`s why she lost. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  was attacked by Planned Parenthood in 2016 when she said, I`m not for abortion on demand for the whole nine months, when we were discussing the 20 -- and then she had to come back and apologize to Planned Parenthood. We have to be able to discuss this.\par \par \uc2 I got attacked for an over the counter birth control bill that Republican women supported. And it wasn`t just opposed by right wing men. It was opposed by Planned Parenthood who didn`t want the competition in the market. Fortunately, technology has taken that over and you can get it online.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Claire, is there a reasonable middle ground for the Democratic party on -- it`s hard to say sometimes there`s a middle ground on abortion.\par \par \uc2 [11:00:04]\par \par \uc2 But the public seems -- there is a middle ground there, which is they don`t want it completely banned, and they don`t want it without some limitations. What does the middle ground look like in a place like Missouri?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Well, all the noise is going to be on the far extremes. And I - - listen, I have great respect for \uc2 Barbara Comstock\uc2  and who she is and how she held office. But women of America are not calm right now. The Supreme Court had an opportunity to stay this bill. If it was so bad, all they had to do was stay it. And they didn`t. And that is telling.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why women are not calm right now. They are very upset over the idea that this is going to be allowed in any state in the United States.\par \par \uc2 So is there middle ground? Yeah. And the more extreme the Republican Party keeps becoming -- and the next thing they`re going do -- Missouri says they`re going to copy it.\par \par \uc2 They`re going to probably do this on immigration. They`re going to have vigilantes going out with a tip line. You can get $50,000 if you turn in the employer who has hired somebody who is illegal. That`s going to happen unless we do something to stop this extremism in the name of vigilantism in our court system. You know --\par \par \uc2 BARBARA COMSTOCK\uc2 , FORMER U.S. CONGRESSWOMAN: Well, I mean --\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: -- this is the Republican Party that used to hate trial lawyers.\par \par \uc2 COMSTOCK: Well, listen. Exactly --\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: They used to hate the courts --\par \par \uc2 COMSTOCK: Well, and this -- that`s why it`s bad --\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: And now they`re using the court.\par \par \uc2 COMSTOCK: That`s -- it`s bad policy and it`s bad law. And if Republicans are going to try and be Todd Akin Republicans, Claire is right. They will not succeed.\par \par \uc2 But we have a lot more Larry Hogans, people like \uc2 Jaime Herrera Beutler\uc2  who`s pro-life and is the leader on helping women with tough pregnancies and -- and kids who have disabilities. And that`s the kind of pro-life community that I`ve always worked with. And I think that`s the one that needs to stand up now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I will say this. In the spirit of our polarization broadcast today, Claire McCaskill, \uc2 Barbara Comstock\uc2 , you guys for appearing together -- in a very high minded -- discussion. I appreciate both of you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 COMSTOCK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has just come off one of the worst weeks of his presidency. And he has the sinking poll numbers to prove it.\par \par \uc2 But before we go to break, we want to note the passing of an NBC and "Today Show" legend. We learned yesterday that weatherman, funny man, and every man Willard Scott died this week. Scott was the "Today Show" weatherman from 1980 until his semi-retirement in 1996. He was a big personality on and off screen.\par \par \uc2 Trust me, when you`d see him in the halls of the D.C. bureau, he always had a big smile on his face. He was perhaps best known, though, for his birthday wishes for people who had turned 100 and beyond. Willard Scott was 87.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [11:05:32]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 The panel is here: Yamiche Alcindor, moderator of "Washington Week" on \uc2 PBS\uc2 , "Washington Post" contributing columnist Matt Bai, Republican strategist Brendan Buck and Betsy Woodruff Swan of "Politico" -- Brendan being the only happy college football fan at this table this morning. Congratulations to your Bulldogs there.\par \par \uc2 Someday, Miami will be SEC caliber.\par \par \uc2 But I want to start with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and where things stand in the Democratic Party. Yamiche, you cover this White House very closely. I want to put up his poll ratings here. Forty-three, 44, 46. We had him at 49, and it was clear two weeks ago that it was, he was still going down, that he hadn`t hit bottom. Perhaps this is bottom here.\par \par \uc2 Does the White House think they have an Afghanistan problem or a COVID problem?\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I`ll say this -- these numbers underscore a real fact, which is that presidents try to create agendas, but that real-life events and circumstances actually define their agendas. And here you had a president who took such fierce criticism over his withdrawal from Afghanistan. Plus, the idea that he declared independence of the virus or something close to it, talking about the idea of a return to normalcy on July 4th, only to then have Americans say, we have to put our masks back on. We have to see children have surging rates of COVID.\par \par \uc2 So, this is the problem that the president is faced with, it`s this idea that he had been talking about solving these problems. He ran on this idea of being a clear-headed, experienced president. And the, and the White House is saying, we`re trying to do this. We understand our number one job is still COVID.\par \par \uc2 But the American people are looking and saying, OK, all of this experience means what exactly for my lived experience?\par \par \uc2 Add to that, of course, the economics of this, right?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: We have to remember that the economy is the thing that is driving this. And our original sin of this pandemic was polarization and lies. And that is leading people not to get the vaccine. That`s also leading people to die of the virus and take dewormers.\par \par \uc2 And now the president and White House officials tell me that it really is about explaining to people that science evolved, explaining to people that challenges are part of the job. And then these poll numbers are really challenging for him.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s interesting, I want to put up our poll of just the independent voters because, Betsy, this is who`s moving -- the only people that move now in our polarized climate. Democrats don`t move up or down. Republicans don`t move up or down, right? It`s the color of their jersey. It`s this small slice of independents. You can see his approval rating sitting at 36 percent now.\par \par \uc2 In April, it was over 50. And that`s -- they`re the, maybe they`re, call them the competency voters. They actually, they react to what`s happening. They`re not happy with how COVID`s going.\par \par \uc2 Whether it`s Biden`s fault or not, he`s the guy at the wheel.\par \par \uc2 BETSY WOODRUFF SWAN, NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT, POLITICO: It`s a nightmare number for the White House. I talked with a Republican strategist about this whole situation yesterday and what that person said is that Afghanistan -- that horror, in a vacuum, probably would not have significantly damaged Biden`s poll numbers the way we`ve seen them change over the last few months.\par \par \uc2 But the point that this particular strategist made is that it`s the totality of everything that`s happened since April. It`s COVID. It`s inflation. There`s some evidence voters are concerned about the immigration system.\par \par \uc2 And it gives Republicans an opening to argue that things are spiraling out of control for the White House. Whether or not that`s accurate, this number kind of tells you what you need to know, at least in terms of the way that this administration and this White House are communicating to the American people and to these persuadable independents how exactly they`re governing and their ability to shape circumstances rather than be shaped by them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Matt, Biden`s campaign was defined by, hey, I`m, this was -- speaking of chaos, Trump was chaos.\par \par \uc2 MATT BAI, CONTRIBUTING COLUMNIST, THE WASHINGTON POST: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m not going to be chaos."\par \par \uc2 BAI: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`re going to be science focused. We`re going to be this.\par \par \uc2 And I wonder if it`s, and some might say, maybe the expectations are too high that he could somehow govern a polarized environment.\par \par \uc2 BAI: Well, he wasn`t going to stay where he was. And independents, you know, we`ve seen in the last several administrations, independents will come back to their general skeptical outlook.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAI: I don`t think this is a panic number. It`s not a good number. It`s not what they want. They`ve got a lot of time, you know, things are going to change.\par \par \uc2 And I think, look, I think what he`s doing, it`s important. To your point, right, he was elected, and he was given a very wide margin because he did represent a return to normalcy and dignity and directness and honesty in the presidency. That`s -- that`s what people wanted. He held himself out as some return to stability and candor.\par \par \uc2 He has gone out throughout this, and he has spoken directly to the American people several times. He`s taken responsibility. He`s explaining things. I think that`s going to stand him well over time.\par \par \uc2 If that were eroding, then I think you might see a more permanent slide.\par \par \uc2 [11:10:01]\par \par \uc2 But, you know, I don`t think it`s time to conclude that the presidency is hobbled.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Brendan, is, is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  being hurt by the fact that Donald Trump`s on mute? You know, I mean, he`s not there every day reminding people why they ended up supporting \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, FORMER ADVISER TO SPEAKERS RYAN & BOEHNER: Yeah, actually, there was a relatively low bar for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . You know, he was basically elected to end chaos. And things were supposed to be better by now, you know?\par \par \uc2 This -- I mean, let`s stop. This poll was conducted during the Afghanistan evacuations, so it`s not surprising, necessarily, that it -- that he took a hit right then. But there is still a real level of anxiety in this country. And I think a lot of it is from COVID, but I think when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was elected, you know, you have your partisans on one side or on both sides, but the middle set, I just want to go back to feeling better about my life.\par \par \uc2 And I think a lot of people -- you`ve got kids going back to school right now, you`ve got COVID spiking and people are, the anxiety is back. And they just thought that things would be a lot better by now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: An end to this morass is going to be this fight for the rest of his agenda. Joe Manchin, let me put up what Joe Manchin said, Yamiche. He wants to see a strategic pause, he calls it, on the big $3.5 trillion Build Back Better half of this proposal.\par \par \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , pretty blunt, no infrastructure bill without the $3.5 trillion reconciliation bill.\par \par \uc2 The White House is going to have to be the leader here -- not Schumer, not Pelosi, not Pete DeFazio. It`s going to have to be \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Are they ready to figure out how to get Manchin and Sanders to come together?\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Based on my conversations at the White House, this is what they`re going to be focused on, specifically in September.\par \par \uc2 Now, August, we all can in some ways agree, August was an ugly month. There were so many different challenges. But September could be even worse because you`re going to have to have the president on the phone with his own party, saying, we need to get on the same page when it comes to this reconciliation bill.\par \par \uc2 There is this idea of climate change having come to the front door of the east and the west.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Fires on one side of the country, floods in New Jersey. The idea that more people have died of the storm in New Jersey than in other parts of the country, really, I think, scares some people into saying, we need to be taking action in Congress to deal with this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: But this is going to be \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He ran on the idea that he could bring people together. He ran on the idea that, I know these people. I`ve been three decades in Congress, three decades in the government and public service. And as a result, I can bring these people together.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to be a test for this. And White House officials tell me he`s going to be personally involved. He`s going to be calling folks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: He`s going to be spending his time doing this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Boy, Betsy, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  could use a signing ceremony right now. There`s a bill that deals with infrastructure that`s ready to go, and you see what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, I get the strategy behind the scenes, but if you`re a person with a flooded basement, you`re like going, we have to do something.\par \par \uc2 WOODRUFF SWAN: If only there were a way.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WOODRUFF SWAN: And this, I talked about this with a Democratic strategist yesterday, a Democratic Hill aide, who said part of the problem here is the tension within the House and Senate Democratic conferences.\par \par \uc2 But also one reason that this person found optimism is that both progressives and moderates, who are kind of at each other`s throats right now, they both need both of these bills. Progressives need to go back to their districts and say, we got this spending bill done. We expanded the social safety net. Moderates need to be able to go back to their districts, many that are red-leaning, and say the two most wonderful words that everybody wants to hear, bipartisan infrastructure. And unless they both get done, it won`t happen.\par \par \uc2 And that puts a lot of pressure on House Dems that, in some ways, is generating a bit of quiet optimism.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Very quickly.\par \par \uc2 BAI: This is where the, the agenda hits the poll ratings, I think, is that, you know, for Democrats there`s always this feeling of more money solves problems. Put more money. Government solves the problem, you`re good.\par \par \uc2 For voters, there`s a skepticism. There`s a trust factor there. Do I know you`re going to spend that money well? Do I know you`re going to know how to solve that problem with that money?\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s where the approval ratings and the dissatisfaction with what the Biden administration is doing right now may affect their ability to move that agenda.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Very quickly, Brendan.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: New majorities get greedy. There is an infrastructure bill that the president could sign right now if they would just take it. What they need to be able to do is take a win, take a half-loaf if that`s what it requires.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Scoreboard.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, scenes like these in the Northeast, as we were just talking about last week, are a sign of what is a changing climate and what that climate has in store for us. After the break, we`re going to look at the fastest growing counties in America and how they`re at the center of the climate change bullseye.\par \par \uc2 Stick with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [11:17:08]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 It`s Data Download time.\par \par \uc2 This week we saw, once again, the issue of climate change is not a hypothetical. It is here and it is deadly. And with this year`s new census numbers, we`re learning where people are choosing to move is going to mean that more and more Americans are going to be in places where climate change is having a pretty devastating impact.\par \par \uc2 Let me show you where our movements are. Obviously, the fastest growing cities in America, look at it, they`re in the Southwest and in the West. And you already can see where we`re headed.\par \par \uc2 The number one thing that brings up is the issue of water and drought issues, as you can see here. Some of the fastest growing counties are in places that are basically water stressed. We already know, with the Colorado River and some restrictions are going there, yet the fastest growing areas are in Phoenix. Are they going to be able to have enough water?\par \par \uc2 Then you`ve got the issue of rising heat issues. Well, guess what? Some of the fastest growing places in the Southwest also are going to be places where we`re going to have some extreme heat. And we already saw what happened with extreme cold weather and the Texas power grid. That`s just going to have more implications there.\par \par \uc2 Then, of course, we saw this week what happens when you get extreme rainfall in the Northeast and whether the infrastructure there could hold up, but where you have some fast-growing populations in places that are also getting extreme rainfall, whether it`s in the Pacific Northwest or down here in the Texas Gulf Coast.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, where are you going to have more people in line for potentially devastating hurricanes? Those fast-growing areas in Texas and the Gulf Coast mean more people in line for that.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is this -- our population shifts in this country are going to places where we have a lot of climate-related problems.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, could the abortion ban in Texas, possibly followed by other states, energize the abortion rights advocate vote in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 Stick with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [11:22:37]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 All right, the politics of abortion. Here`s where the American public is. When should abortion be legal? The American public is in a very mushy middle.\par \par \uc2 Thirty-one percent believes abortion should always be legal; 23 percent say it should be legal most of the time; 34 percent illegal with some exceptions; and only 8 percent believe it should be illegal without any exceptions.\par \par \uc2 Brendan Buck, is the Texas -- if abortion is at the top of mind in a political campaign, is this good or bad for the Republican Party in 2022?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Oh, absolutely bad. There has been this -- we have taken for granted for a number of years now that abortion politics animates Republicans and it is good for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And I just don`t know that that holds true anymore. Look at the Kavanaugh fight. We lost in the midterms. We lost the House.\par \par \uc2 And look at 2020. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  nominated and confirmed three Supreme Court justices. He lost.\par \par \uc2 I just don`t know that we can assume that Republicans are going to get animated in the way that Democrats are. You are seeing incredible energy among Democrats coming out saying that they`re upset. And I just don`t know that this is going to be a conversation that any Republicans want to have.\par \par \uc2 And the evidence of that, do you see any Republicans talking about this right now? No one has --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I had formers on today to talk about it for a reason, because you didn`t want to get any currents.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: No one is celebrating this on the right. And it`s an uncomfortable conversation that they don`t want to have. And I think Democrats are eager to take advantage of it.\par \par \uc2 WOODRUFF SWAN: One thing I would add to that point is if you talk to the leaders of the conservative legal movement in Washington, including leaders who want abortion laws in this country to be more restrictive, they`ll tell you two things about this Texas law. The first thing they`ll say is they think it`s clownish.\par \par \uc2 And the second thing they`ll say is that they think it`s strategically useful to them because the biggest long-term threat to Roe v. Wade is not this Texas law; it`s Mississippi`s 15-week abortion ban. That ban will be debated at the Supreme Court in the fall. The Court will rule on it next year. And the fact that Texas has this six-week vigilante justice law makes Mississippi`s law look like the moderate compromise position.\par \par \uc2 And it puts Kavanaugh and Amy Coney Barrett, those two justices, in perhaps a much more comfortable spot than it would if Texas hadn`t come first.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: The other thing, though, is that Democratic women are horrified, in particular. And women who believe in some sort of abortion are horrified. And that is, at least based on numbers, the majority of women in this country.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you that when the Supreme Court ruling came through, I got calls in the middle of the night from people who wanted to go on the record immediately to say how upset they were. That`s not a regular sort of response to a Supreme Court ruling. And what it tells you is that Democrats are going to start to get animated about this issue. That`s not what Republicans want.\par \par \uc2 And Republicans, while they might not want to talk about it, this is the consequences of the GOP working for decades to get to this moment. They took over state legislatures. They now have the Supreme Court majority. So, this is also, while they don`t want to talk about it, this is what Republicans wanted, even if it`s not this exact law, it is this idea that abortion will somehow be curtailed in a real, real way.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats, they feel as though this seems like the thing that could galvanize people on the midterms when they badly need to win the midterms and gain seats.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Matt, \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  and \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , both who have got their own issues right now.\par \par \uc2 BAI: Yes, that tells you something.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Boy, they grabbed onto this immediately.\par \par \uc2 BAI: Well, yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And in the Virginia race, Glenn Youngkin, to back up Brendan`s point, was like, can we talk about something else?\par \par \uc2 BAI: Well, let`s forget base mobilization. If we want to just talk about base mobilization, will it gin up the Democrats or will it gin up the Republicans? This is a party, the Republican Party, that lost women in the last election by what, 15 points? Lost independents by what, nine, ten points?\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is -- they can`t afford -- this only helps them in places they`re already winning big.\par \par \uc2 I will say though, I do agree with what Congresswoman Comstock was saying. That court has not said -- I don`t think the court has signaled they`re going to overturn Roe v. Wade. I think a majority of the court has basically said, it`s a procedural issue here and we`re going to wait until there`s a case in front of us.\par \par \uc2 I would be shocked if this stood up.\par \par \uc2 WOODRUFF SWAN: That said, from looking at that ruling that came out from this five justices, where they declined to pause the Texas law, one piece of that, one thing that`s missing from that very, very brief order that several legal folks noted to me, is that it didn`t say explicitly that the Texas law was unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 Those justices could have said, this law is blatantly unconstitutional given current Supreme Court precedent. They could have said that --\par \par \uc2 BAI: They could have.\par \par \uc2 WOODRUFF SWAN: -- while also pausing the law, but they didn`t. And that`s something that is an ominous sign to folks who want to protect abortion.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Brendan, legitimate rape became a sticky phrase. And it took down not one Republican Senate candidate, but also a second one in Indiana.\par \par \uc2 This bounty thing, feels like no matter what, it may make Mississippi look reasonable to some people, but the bounty thing has this stickiness to it where even the average person is going to hear about this.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: It`s an asinine way to set up a law. And you asked the right question of Claire McCaskill, what stops a California from banning guns by saying you can sue anybody who owns one?\par \par \uc2 There`s nothing conservative about how this is set up. And so, I think it will end up failing, but there are going to be other challenges. And if this ends up being the issue that we`re talking about in November, it`s not just bad for moderates; it`s bad for everybody. Claire McCaskill got elected in red state Missouri on this issue. And if this is the conversation we`re having, it`s going to be a bad midterm.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: It`s also a conversation that the White House wants to be having. They`re scrambling and the president put out that forceful statement immediately saying, we`re going to have an immediate response to this. We`re going to have the Department of Justice and the Health and Human Services look at this.\par \par \uc2 The president is standing up, as a Catholic, saying, I support Roe v. Wade. He doesn`t want to be talking about Afghanistan and he doesn`t want to be talking about some of the things that are making August and September so challenging for him.\par \par \uc2 But this is something that he can stand up and say, I believe this and I will try my best to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Here`s what`s going to happen: every elected official is going to have to go on the record with specifics now on their abortion position. And they`ve ducked that for 30 years. So, we shall see.\par \par \uc2 Terrific panel. Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 That`s all we have for today. Enjoy the rest of your Labor Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching, and we`ll be back next week because, if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet The Press".\par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020210905eh9500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Supreme Court Declining To Block Texas Restrictive New Abortion Law\b0\par\par\uc2 Tom Llamas, Catie Beck \par \uc2 418 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS: Now, to another breaking story in Washington where the Supreme Court just cleared the way for a controversial abortion law in Texas. It`s a major blow for the abortion rights movement. For other states though, the divisive ruling could be a blueprint towards their own laws. Catie Beck reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: A divided Supreme Court decision causing more divide across the country.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Everyone deserves to have the ability to decide if and when to carry a child.\par \par \uc2 BECK: A late-night five-four vote allows the country`s most restrictive abortion law to stand in Texas. Justice is denying the request to block the law, saying abortion providers failed to make their case that the court should step in. The law forbids abortions after cardiac activity is detected. Typically, it`s six weeks and allow citizens to sue anyone who assisted someone to get an abortion after that time.\par \par \uc2 President Biden vowing to challenge the law with powerful federal resources saying it unleashes unconstitutional chaos. Meanwhile, defenders of the law call it incredible progress. Florida and South Dakota already making moves toward adopting their own.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:03]\par \par \uc2 WILTON SIMPSON, FLORIDA REPUBLICAN STATE SENATOR: It clearly is going to send a signal to all the states that are interested in banning abortion.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Advocates of abortion rights predict a firestorm of similar legislation.\par \par \uc2 NANCY NORTHUP\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS\uc2 : Roe vs. Wade is a dead letter in Texas today.\par \par \uc2 BECK: You use the word sneaky to describe the Texas law. What`s sneaky about it?\par \par \uc2 NORTHUP: So, what they`ve done is said we`re not going to enforce the law, we`re going to empower individuals to be able to sue people to stop them from having abortions. We`re going to try to get someone else to do our dirty work for us.\par \par \uc2 BECK: The issue again heads to the Supreme Court in coming months. Catie Beck, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020210904eh920005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Texas` Sweeping New Abortion Law Now In Effect\b0\par\par\uc2 Tom Llamas, Catie Beck \par \uc2 555 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS: Geoff Bennett with that new reporting tonight, Geoff, thank you. Texas` sweeping new abortion law now in effect. The Supreme Court deciding not to block the measure which bans abortions after six weeks. President Biden today slamming the law calling it a blatant violation of a constitutional right. NBC`s Catie Beck reports.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:11]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My body is my own.\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Tonight, outrage in Texas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s going to be nearly impossible for folks to access the care that they need.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Protest over what is now the most restrictive abortion law in the country going into effect after the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  did not act on opponents request to block it.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): The bill that I`m about to sign that ensures that the life of every unborn child who has a heartbeat will be saved from the ravages of abortion.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Passed by the legislature in May, it forbids abortion in Texas after cardiac activity is detected, typically around six weeks before many women know they`re pregnant. Instead of state officials enforcing the law, it allows individuals to take legal action, permitted to sue any person who might have helped someone get an abortion after six weeks. Providers, relatives, even a taxi driver can face damages up to $10,000.\par \par \uc2 AMY HAGSTROM MILLER, FOUNDER AND CEO, WHOLE WOMAN`S HEALTH: It is a very sad day.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Amy Hagstrom Miller oversees four abortion clinics in Texas which are complying with the new law already turning large numbers of women away.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: These politicians don`t hear the anguish from the patients. They don`t hear these patients` stories about why they need access to safe abortion today.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Opponents of abortion rights call the new law incredible progress, clearing the way for similar restrictions.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER, PRESIDENT, \uc2 SUSAN B. ANTHONY\uc2  LIST: What we hope is in the future is that abortion limits -- consensus on abortion will be allowed to make their way into the law.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BECK (on camera): The Supreme Court could still weigh in on the Texas law. Meanwhile, the justices are set to hear a challenge to Roe vs. Wade out of Mississippi where a new law bans most abortions after 15 weeks. Tom?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Catie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, to news that could hit a lot of Americans` wallets. Social Security benefits could be slashed by the year 2034 unless Congress comes up with a fix. A new government report saying COVID unemployment and the recession have taken a massive economic toll on funds available for both older Americans and those with disabilities.\par \par \uc2 And when we come back tonight, an exclusive look at what parents are most worried about as kids head back to school.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020210902eh910008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 What`s Next In The Wake Of Decisions Including The Overturning Of Roe V. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Tom Llamas, Peter Alexander \par \uc2 723 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS: Now to the end of what was a tumultuous and historic term at the Supreme Court in the wake of key decisions that have changed the landscape of the country and sparked new legal fights. The first black woman has now been sworn in as justice. NBC`s chief White House correspondent Peter Alexander joins us now with more. And what`s next for this court. Peter, good morning.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Tom, good morning. Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  ushers in a new era for the nation`s highest court where white men no longer make up a majority on the bench. But she also joins a court that`s at least as divided as the country itself with the enormous fallout from its ruling to reverse Roe v. Wade still reverberating. And today President Biden is going to meet virtually with Democratic governors who have moved quickly to protect women`s access to abortion in their states.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): The Supreme Court`s monumental term may be over but the fight over its impact on American life rages on. President Biden before returning to Washington condemning the court`s abortion ruling.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The one thing that has been destabilizing is the outrageous behavior of the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  in overruling not only Roe v. Wade, but essentially challenging the right to privacy.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Now he`s urging Congress to guarantee the right to an abortion calling for a limited change to the Senate rule known as the filibuster. But even though Democrats control the Senate, they don`t have the votes for that. Abortion is now prohibited or likely to be banned or restricted in roughly half the country. In other states, the future of abortion rights remains uncertain. Virginia`s Republican Governor Glenn Youngkin is pushing lawmakers to ban abortion after 15 weeks, with exceptions for rape, incest, and the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-VA): Virginians want fewer abortions not more. And I think this is a chance for us to come together as Virginians.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): The court now delivering the Biden administration another gut punch in its efforts to fight climate change. With a six-three ruling limiting the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2 `s ability to reduce greenhouse gas emissions produced by power plants. The President who`s pledged to shift energy production away from coal burning plants toward renewable sources like wind and solar slamming the ruling as another devastating decision that takes our country backwards.\par \par \uc2 Still, the court also handed the White House a political victory, ruling that President Biden can end a Trump era program that forces migrants seeking asylum in the U.S. to remain in Mexico. All of it as the court begins a historic new chapter.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: So help me God.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Newly sworn in Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  becomes the first black woman on the nation`s highest court replacing retiring \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I am pleased to welcome Justice Jackson, to the court and to our common calling.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): While the court will look more like America, Justice Jackson won`t change its conservative majority.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The court is set to consider a new round of controversial cases when its next term begins. That`s this October from the use of race and college admissions to gay rights and a Republican backed appeal that could have huge implications for the next presidential election with the justices set to decide whether state legislatures should have more power over how federal elections are run in their states. Tom?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right, Peter Alexander for us at the White House this morning. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220704eh7100002\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Simple New Way To Help Take The Frustration Out Of Getting That Appointment\b0\par\par\uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, HODA KOTB, VICKY NGUYEN, DR. JOHN TORRES \par \uc2 1,889 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 February 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:13:22): We are back now with a closer look at the coronavirus vaccine and the rollout as the pace of distribution of the vaccine quickens up, we know a lot of folks have questions.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB (08:13:31): We do indeed. So this morning we brought in our team of experts to help you get some answers. NBC senior medical correspondent John Torres-- Doctor John Torres and investigative consumer correspondent Vicky Nguyen.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:13:42): But before we get to the questions, Vicky, we are unveiling, a new digital tool that we`re really hoping is going to help people especially as the vaccine becomes more widespread.\par \par \uc2 VICKY NGUYEN (NBC News Investigative and Consumer Correspondent) (08:13:51): It is so cool. We know the eligibility requirements vary state by state and the path to vaccination has been filled with frustration and confusion. So what if you could get an alert so you know when it`s your turn and where to go to get that shot. Well, now you can. This morning along with our parent company Comcast NBC Universal, we`re launching a one- stop destination to help you. It`s called planyourvaccine.com. And here`s how it works.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VICKY NGUYEN (08:14:17): Plan Your Vaccine, the interactive destination with everything you need to know about getting vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 (08:14:22): Let me show you how it works. First, select your state. Then your age group. Then you want to click on your occupation and make sure you click whether you have any high risk health concerns.\par \par \uc2 (08:14:32): Easily learn the distribution timeline in your state. Who is eligible now and who is up next. Hover over your state and you`ll see the percentage of people who`ve been vaccinated and how much of the allotted vaccines have been used.\par \par \uc2 (08:14:44): Another useful features this interactive map, all you have to do is plug in your zip code and it will automatically show you the closest vaccination sites, just be sure to call ahead to make sure they have supply available.\par \par \uc2 (08:14:57): One more important feature, you can enter your email to sign up for alerts as changes are made to vaccination plans in your state. (08:15:03): And you can do that for as many states as you need. For example, if you want to help your parents plan their vaccine and they`re in another state, you can sign up and also get alerts for them.\par \par \uc2 (08:15:11): The site also breaks down all the information on how to make an appointment, what to bring, and any rules you need to know.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VICKY NGUYEN (08:15:19): And we also want to mention this tool is available in English, Spanish, and Mandarin. And using this site you can check on your state`s progress as well toward reaching herd immunity. That depends on a lot of variables. All the information you need to know about the vaccine, though, is now in one easy to navigate place. And you may have noticed the QR code on your screen. You can just use your smartphone to scan it and then that`ll take you right to the website. You know, we`re aiming to take away some of the confusion. And again that`s planyourvaccine.com. Definitely go there and play with that site and let us know what your questions are and how-- how you do.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:15:51): Yes.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB (08:15:51): By the way, it`s really, really simple.\par \par \uc2 VICKY NGUYEN (08:15:54): Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB (08:15:53): I was just clickety-clacking for my mom in the commercial. And it-- it`s simple, simple, simple. All right. We`re going to get to our questions. Let`s start with Doctor John. Doctor John, a lot of people have this question. Two vaccines, Moderna and \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  both authorized. If you had a choice, is there one that is better or do you just take what you get and you don`t get upset? DR. JOHN TORRES (08:16:13): You know I tell people is the best vaccine you can get is the first one you can get. And if you look at these two vaccines Moderna and the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine, they were made in similar fashions and they have a lot of similarities. They`re both around ninety-five percent effective. They both take two shots to get. But the big difference is the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine you take them twenty-one days apart. The Moderna vaccine, you take that twenty-eight days apart. And so that`s basically the hugest difference. You are going to get some side effects. They`re going to be essentially the same. You can get arm soreness, maybe low grade fever, muscle aches, fatigue. That`s what most people complain about. But that usually goes away in a day or two. And again these two are very similar and especially considering how hard it is to get the vaccine, having this new planningvaccine.com website is going to be very, very helpful.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:16:57): Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DR. JOHN TORRES (08:16:58): And the first one when you can get is the one you need to get.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:16:59): Okay, we`ve got a question from Michelle in Florida. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE (08:17:04): If you have severe allergies like a peanut allergy, will you be able to get the vaccine?\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:17:10): Doctor John.\par \par \uc2 DR. JOHN TORRES (08:17:13): Yeah. This is a great question. Because a lot of people have these types of-- types of allergies. If you have a severe allergy to food, oral medications, pets, those types of things, that does not preclude you from getting the vaccine, you can go ahead and get the vaccine. But you might want to wait around thirty minutes instead of fifteen minutes just to make sure. If you have a severe reaction to prior vaccines or other injectable medications, then talk to who`s ever giving you the vaccines about that. So you can talk about the risks and benefits, your particular circumstances. Now the only cause and the CDC says if you had-- if you have a reaction to prior COVID vaccine, then you don`t want to get this one, especially if it`s a reaction to what`s called PEG, the polyethylene glycol which is an ingredient in those vaccines then you don`t want to get this. But for everybody else, even with the severe allergies, you can go ahead and still get the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB (08:17:58): Doctor John, another one for here a lot of women are either pregnant, trying to get pregnant, they`re breast feeding and they`re concerned about the vaccine. Is it safe for them?\par \par \uc2 DR. JOHN TORRES (08:18:07): Yeah, this is a huge question I get. And American College of obstetricians and Gynecologists, the OB/GYN experts actually came out, and they said, you know, if you`re pregnant, you can still get the vaccine. And what they recommended is get the vaccine in your phase. And that`s when you would get it normally. Whether you were pregnant or not, as if you`re a health care worker or somebody with prior existing medical conditions. But talk to your doctor beforehand to understand the risks and benefits. Because studies were not done on pregnant women although women did get pregnant during the studies. And there was no issue found on that as far as breast feeding they come out and they say yes if you`re breast feeding you just still get the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:18:42): Let`s get, we`ve got another one, Heidi, on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , this one`s for you, Vicky. Let`s roll it.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI (08:18:48): If you get the vaccine, can you still carry coronavirus?\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:18:51): That`s the question.\par \par \uc2 VICKY NGUYEN (08:18:52): Yeah. And the short answer is, yes. Right before you get the vaccine and even after you`re vaccinated, you could get the coronavirus, the one that causes COVID-19. That`s because it takes a few weeks according to the CDC for your body to develop that immunity. So the recommendation is even after you`re fully vaccinated, you do want to continue wearing your masks and washing your hands and social distancing. The good news is this morning the CDC is saying that if you are fully vaccinated and you are exposed to the coronavirus, you don`t have to quarantine as long as you`re not showing symptoms. So until everybody is vaccinated, we still need to take precautions and be careful.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB (08:19:27): All right. Vicky, thanks. Doctor John, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (08:19:29): And, by the way, these guys are going to stick around for the 3rd Hour of TODAY to answer more questions about the vaccine. And the CEO of Rite Aid will join us for an exclusive interview on how drugstores are getting ready for this.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:19:39): And again if you want to learn more about your state vaccination plans, your eligibility, go to planyourvaccine.com. Hoda has even got have for it, okay. You can scan the QR code too at the bottom of your screen if you like.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB (08:19:50): All right. Let`s over to Mister Roker get a check of weather. Hey, Al.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (08:19:53): All right. It helps you plan your weather. We are looking at another storm coming in the Pacific Northwest. We`ve got icy conditions through the lower Mississippi and Ohio River Valley is pushing into the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic States. For today, below zero high temperatures through the plains. That western storm comes on in. It`s going to be a mess from Central Texas, all the way into the Central Mississippi River Valley with heavy rain down through the Southeast, cloudy skies in the northeast and single digits as you get into the upper Midwest and the Eastern Plains.\par \par \uc2 (08:20:22): That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (Weather follows)\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (08:20:56): That is your latest weather. Guys.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (08:20:57): All right.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:20:58): Al, thank you.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (08:20:58): Thank you, Mister Roker. Time for POPStart.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB (08:21:01): Yeah, come on Carson.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020210317eh2b000dx\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs